Borouge,,,,Exa,"Borouge International is excited to contribute to the launch of On’s new Cloud X5, featuring the world’s first commercial-scale EVA shoe midsole made with captured CO₂ for the Swiss premium sportswear brand. | Borouge International",www.linkedin.com (Borouge International),2026-07-16T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-16 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.linkedin.com/posts/borougeinternational_essentialmaterials-circulareconomy-sustainability-activity-7483532926979321857-7n2k,"# Borouge International is excited to contribute to the launch of On’s new Cloud X5, featuring the world’s first commercial-scale EVA shoe midsole made with captured CO₂ for the Swiss premium sportswear brand. | Borouge International · LinkedIn · 2026-07-16 ... **Borouge International**: Borouge International has 1,000-2,000 employees, founded in 2026. Its workforce is distributed across 30 countries (including Canada, Austria, Belgium, Finland, and Sweden). ... Borouge International is excited to contribute to the launch of [On](https://linkedin.com/company/on-ag)’s new Cloud X5, featuring the world’s first commercial-scale EVA shoe midsole made with captured CO₂ for the Swiss premium sportswear brand. ... Using [Infinium](https://linkedin.com/company/infiniumco)’s ISCC PLUS-certified eNaphtha, a more sustainable, mass-balanced alternative to fossil-based naphtha, we produce EVA for a high-performance midsole with a lower carbon footprint. ... This collaboration demonstrates the power of partnerships across the value chain to accelerate innovation, scale more sustainable materials, and deliver high performance without compromise ... **Reshared from [On](https://linkedin.com/company/on-ag):** ... > This week, we’re reintroducing the Cloud X 5, now featuring a CleanCloud™ midsole – made using captured carbon emissions. First announced in 2022 as a proof-of-concept, today it’s scaled to over a million pairs, four-years ahead of schedule. > ... > While traditional sports shoe midsoles rely heavily on fossil fuels, CleanCloud™ disrupts the cycle by using captured CO2 emissions as raw material – intercepting the emissions before they enter the atmosphere and combining them with green hydrogen, produced using renewable energy. Together, these elements are transformed into e-naphtha, which serves as the base ingredient to produce the final EVA foam, with the resulting material sharing the same Technical Data Sheet (TDS) as standard EVA. > ... > But breakthroughs are not created in a silo – the entirely new value chain relies on strong technical partnerships – with [Infinium](https://linkedin.com/company/infiniumco) Energy and Borouge International. > ... > In the words of Jean-philippe Romain, Director of Polymer Science & Engineering at [On](https://linkedin.com/company/on-ag), “This initiative came purely from a raw innovation mindset: Can we take a resource that is abundant in the atmosphere and do something positive with it? Can we make the problem part of the solution?” >"
Borouge,,,,Exa,Dermal Fillers Market to Reach USD 363.06M by 2032 at 7.8% CAGR – News24 Philippines,www.news24.ph (News24 Pinoy),2026-07-08T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-08 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.news24.ph/biz/74298/,"PW Consulting’s latest industry primer frames the global dermal fillers market from a strategic, decision-oriented vantage point ahead of 2026. The market has shown resilient expansion through the 2020–2025 base period and, under our central-case assumptions, continues on a structurally upward trajectory through the 2026–2032 forecast window at a compound annual growth rate of 7.8%. This preview explains why that growth matters to corporate strategy, where exposure and risk concentrate, and which levers deliver outsized commercial and clinical value — while deliberately withholding the granular split tables that are available in the full study to encourage direct engagement with the primary report. Dermal Fillers Market ... Between 2020 and 2025 the market expanded steadily, and 2025 serves as our analytical base year. Our modeling shows the market passes notable scale inflection points in the mid-2020s before accelerating into the latter half of the decade. Forecast outputs for 2026–2032 reflect the interaction of several structural drivers: demographic tailwinds in ageing populations, wider clinical indications approved by regulators, improving accessibility through new channel models, and product innovation that extends clinical durability and safety profiles. For companies building 3–5 year plans, the combination of a stable CAGR and these structural shifts creates predictable revenue upside for focused investment, while concentrated competitive dynamics elevate strategic options such as distribution partnerships, portfolio differentiation, and selective M&A. Dermal Fillers Market ... The dermal fillers market exhibits high concentration at the top. The three- and five-firm concentration ratios are meaningful strategic signals: incumbents retain scale advantages in regulatory dossiers, clinical evidence, and commercial coverage. That concentration compresses margin opportunity for generic entrants but creates predictable corridors for premium product positioning and clinical-combination strategies (e.g., filler + energy-based device protocols). For 2026 resource allocation, companies should balance investments in defensive ... data for core indications with offensive plays that target underserved use-cases or service models — for example, combinations of longer-lasting formulations with anesthetic-enhanced offerings for premium clinics. Dermal Fillers ... Regulatory approvals since 2021 have broadened the labeled indications for injectable fillers — including approvals for anatomical sites and reconstructive uses that lift addressable patient populations. This regulatory momentum creates new reimbursement and KOL-engagement opportunities but also raises the bar for post-market surveillance and adverse-event governance. ... Injectable dermal fillers are classified as higher-risk devices across major jurisdictions, meaning compliance with device-specific regulatory regimes (e.g., U.S. PMA pathways, EU MDR requirements) is both costly and strategically consequential. Firms should anticipate multi-year clearance timelines for new indication claims and build regulatory timelines into product-launch roadmaps. ... Major legacy and regional players remain active across product launches, geographic expansion, and messaging innovations. Leading global manufacturers continue to invest in differentiated portfolios (biostimulators, crosslinked HA variations, anesthetic-enhanced syringes) and in clinical programs that elevate safety and longevity claims. Recent strategic moves illustrate market dynamics: ... Targeted launches across geographies and new SKU introductions have accelerated in 2025–2026, demonstrating a push for therapeutic breadth and premium positioning in advanced aesthetic markets. ... Large incumbents are amplifying campaign strategies emphasizing natural-appearing outcomes and undetectability as a defensive premium narrative in mature markets — a trend likely to intensify as patient expectations shift from dramatic correction to subtle rejuvenation. ... Regional manufacturers and specialist innovators are leveraging nimble R&D and cost-efficient supply chains to contest mid-market clinic channels and to serve markets with faster regulatory cycles. ... New product launches and regional introductions indicate two parallel plays: premiumization through formulation innovation, and penetration through localized product versions. Both require distinct commercialization investments and post-launch evidence plans. ... Public-facing programmatic themes (e.g., “undetectability,” anesthetic-enabled formulations) suggest marketing is migrating toward outcomes language that maps directly to patient experience metrics — an axis that payer conversations and clinic adoption decisions increasingly reference. ... Regulatory approvals for new anatomic indications increase the addressable clinical use-cases, but they also increase regulatory diligence. The number and variety of PMAs and approvals since 2021 underscores a landscape in which timing and regulatory capital are competitive advantages. ... M&A and partnering playbooks that use quantitative screens and qualitative scorecards to identify high-impact targets by capability, geography, and intellectual property fit. ... Prioritize regulatory and clinical investment where the return horizon matches corporate planning cycles. Approvals that broaden labeled indications materially change addressable markets but require early and sustained investment in evidence generation. ... Lock in upstream supply agreements or diversify material suppliers to hedge raw-material volatility and protect gross margins. Procurement action now insulates 2026–2028 rollout economics. ... Segment go- ... will reward premiumized, evidence-backed innovations; emerging ... are more price-sensitive and ... Beyond these tactical moves, strategic options — from bolt-on acquisitions that add procedural breadth to partnering for commercial scale — should be evaluated using the scenario stress-tests included in our models. ... To maximize strategic value and to respect competitive sensitivities, this preview deliberately does not publish the report’s granular regional and application split tables, nor individual SKU-level revenue forecasts. These detailed datasets are central to investment memos, capex planning, and M&A valuation workstreams; we provide them in the full deliverable together with interactive model files so that clients can run customized sensitivity analyses and scenario permutations. The high-level concentration metrics, CAGR, and aggregated market trajectory shared above are sufficient to orient initial strategy, while the detailed tables enable execution planning and diligence. ... If your 2026 planning cycle includes new product introductions, geographic expansion, or M&A screening in aesthetics, allocate time in Q1–Q2 2026 to integrate the full PW Consulting dataset and scenario workstream into board-level discussions. Our practice can embed a tailored strategic workshop, supply-chain stress-test, or regulatory-clinical roadmap sprint to translate the analysis into firm-level commitments and a one-page investment thesis aligned to your risk appetite. ... The dermal fillers market combines steady structural growth with concentrated competition and increased regulatory complexity. Those factors create attractive opportunity for organizations that align regulatory diligence, supply resilience, and clinically credible differentiation. PW Consulting’s full study provides the forward-looking, operationally actionable detail required to convert the macro upside into executable 2026 plans; this preview identifies the priority levers and the timing considerations that will determine who captures disproportionate value as the market scales. For access to the complete dataset, interactive models, and tailored advisory engagements, please consult PW Consulting’s market report hub."
Borouge,,,,Exa,Dermal Fillers Market to Reach USD 363.06M by 2032 at 7.8% CAGR - Amic News,www.amicnews.com (admin),2026-07-08T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-08 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.amicnews.com/press/dermal-fillers-market-to-reach/,"market expanded steadily ... and 2025 serves as ... analytical base year. Our ... market passes notable scale inflection points in ... outputs for 202 ... –20 ... reflect the interaction of ... structural drivers: demographic tailwinds in ageing populations, wider clinical indications approved by regulators, improving accessibility through new channel models, and product innovation ... extends clinical durability and safety ... a stable CAGR and ... revenue upside for ... concentrated competitive dynamics ... Regulatory approvals since 2021 have broadened the labeled indications for injectable fillers — including approvals for anatomical sites and reconstructive uses that lift addressable patient populations. This regulatory momentum creates new reimbursement and KOL-engagement opportunities but also raises the bar for post-market surveillance and adverse-event governance. ... Injectable dermal fillers are classified as higher-risk devices across major jurisdictions, meaning compliance with device-specific regulatory regimes (e.g., U.S. PMA pathways, EU MDR requirements) is both costly and strategically consequential. Firms should anticipate multi-year clearance timelines for new indication claims and build regulatory timelines into product-launch roadmaps. ... Major legacy and regional players remain active across product launches, geographic expansion, and messaging innovations. Leading global manufacturers continue to invest in differentiated portfolios (biostimulators, crosslinked HA variations, anesthetic-enhanced syringes) and in clinical programs that elevate safety and longevity claims. Recent strategic moves illustrate market dynamics: ... Targeted launches across geographies and new SKU introductions have accelerated in 2025–2026, demonstrating a push for therapeutic breadth and premium positioning in advanced aesthetic markets. ... Regional manufacturers and specialist innovators are leveraging nimble R&D and cost-efficient supply chains to contest mid-market clinic channels and to serve markets with faster regulatory cycles. ... New product launches and regional introductions indicate two parallel plays: premiumization through formulation innovation, and penetration through localized product versions. Both require distinct commercialization investments and post-launch evidence plans. ... Regulatory approvals for new anatomic indications increase the addressable clinical use-cases, but they also increase regulatory diligence. The number and variety of PMAs and approvals since 2021 underscores a landscape in which timing and regulatory capital are competitive advantages. ... M&A and partnering playbooks that use quantitative screens and qualitative scorecards to identify high-impact targets by capability, geography, and intellectual property fit. ... Beyond these tactical moves, strategic options — from bolt-on acquisitions that add procedural breadth to partnering for commercial scale — should be evaluated using the scenario stress-tests included in our models. ... To maximize strategic value ... If your 2026 planning cycle includes new product introductions, geographic expansion, or M&A screening in aesthetics, allocate time in Q1–Q2 2026 to integrate the full PW Consulting dataset and scenario workstream into board-level discussions. Our practice can embed a tailored strategic workshop, supply-chain stress-test, or regulatory-clinical roadmap sprint to translate the analysis into firm-level commitments and a one-page investment thesis aligned to your risk appetite. ... North America maintained market leadership with a 45% revenue share in 2025, supported by high procedure volumes, strong consumer awareness, favorable purchasing power, and rapid regulatory approvals. ... The United States remains the largest regional contributor, benefiting from advanced aesthetic medicine infrastructure and continuous launches of innovative filler technologies. ... Europe represents the second-largest regional market, supported by increasing demand across Germany, France, Italy, and the United Kingdom. Growing research investment and expanding physician expertise continue strengthening regional competitiveness. ... Asia Pacific is emerging as a high-growth opportunity due to rising disposable incomes, expanding middle-class populations, medical tourism, and increasing awareness of cosmetic procedures. South Korea, China, and other regional markets continue driving innovation in aesthetic medicine. ... Latin America and the Middle East & Africa continue experiencing gradual expansion as healthcare infrastructure improves and cosmetic treatments become increasingly accessible. ... Strategic collaborations, regulatory approvals, and geographic expansion continue reshaping competition as companies pursue broader clinical indications and premium treatment categories. ... - February 2025: Evolus received U.S. FDA approval for Evolysse Form and Evolysse Smooth, expanding its hyaluronic acid portfolio. - March 2025: Laboratoires VIVACY partnered with Burgeon Biotechnology to combine STYLAGE fillers with NOVUMA calcium hydroxyapatite technology. - May 2025: Teoxane launched its dermocosmetics portfolio directly to U.S. consumers, supporting its Resilient Hyaluronic Acid (RHA) platform. - October 2025: Croma-Pharma signed an exclusive Canadian distribution agreement with Renellence for its saypha filler range. - January 2026: Galderma expanded the clinical indications for Sculptra following European MDR certification. - February 2026: Revance Therapeutics and Teoxane secured U.S. FDA approval for RHA Dynamic Volume, targeting midface volume loss and cheek contouring."
Borouge,,,,Exa,Exclusive: Moomoo to Launch Unified 'Moomoo Engine' Toolkit in Brazil · BBI,www.brazilbusinessinsights.com,2026-07-03T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-03 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.brazilbusinessinsights.com/news/moomoo-engine-launch-futu-xp-nubank-brazil,"Exclusive: Moomoo to Launch Unified 'Moomoo Engine' Toolkit in Brazil · BBI ... # Exclusive: Moomoo to Launch Unified 'Moomoo Engine' Toolkit in Brazil ... Moomoo is launching 'Moomoo Engine,' a unified trading toolkit designed to capture active retail and professional traders in Brazil, challenging incumbents. ... - ▸ Global user base: Over 22 million users - ▸ Launch date: July 2, 2026 - ▸ Target market: Active retail & pro traders - ▸ Primary region: Brazil expansion ... The Bottom Line: - Moomoo, the digital brokerage subsidiary of Futu Holdings ($FUTU), is set to launch 'Moomoo Engine,' a unified suite of advanced trading tools designed to consolidate market analysis, charting, and execution. - The rollout represents a strategic push to capture high-value retail and professional traders in emerging markets, particularly Brazil, where digital brokerage competition is intensifying. - While the launch enhances $FUTU's technological moat, it poses direct competitive pressure on local incumbents such as XP Inc. ($XP) and Nubank ($NU). Strategic Expansion in Latin America: Futu Holdings ($FUTU), through its international trading platform Moomoo, is accelerating its expansion into Latin America with the exclusive announcement of 'Moomoo Engine.' This unified toolkit is designed to streamline the trading lifecycle for retail and professional investors. By integrating advanced charting, real-time data analytics, and algorithmic execution into a single, cohesive interface, Moomoo aims to address the fragmentation that has historically plagued active traders in emerging markets. The Brazilian market, characterized by a highly sophisticated retail investor base and a rapidly growing interest in global equities, serves as the primary launchpad for this technological deployment. Technological Disruption and the 'Moomoo Engine': The 'Moomoo Engine' represents a significant upgrade to the platform's existing infrastructure. Historically, active traders in Brazil have had to rely on multiple third-party platforms for technical analysis, order routing, and portfolio tracking. This fragmentation often leads to latency issues, higher subscription costs, and execution inefficiencies. Moomoo's unified toolkit addresses these pain points by offering proprietary, low-latency charting algorithms and institutional-grade data feeds directly within the trading application. This integration is expected to lower the barrier to entry for advanced trading strategies, enabling retail investors to execute complex multi-leg options strategies and automated technical trades with greater precision. Competitive Dynamics in the Brazilian Brokerage Sector: The introduction of the 'Moomoo Engine' comes at a time of intense competition within the Brazilian financial services sector. Incumbents like XP Inc. ($XP) and BTG Pactual have traditionally dominated the affluent and active trader segments through extensive advisory networks and proprietary platforms. Concurrently, digital-first platforms like Nubank ($NU) and Inter have successfully democratized access to basic investment products for the mass market. Moomoo's strategy bypasses the traditional advisory model, focusing instead on self-directed, tech-savvy traders who prioritize execution speed, analytical depth, and low transaction costs. By offering institutional-grade tools without the premium price tag, Moomoo is positioning itself to capture market share from both high-end incumbents and mass-market digital banks looking to upsell their user bases. Regulatory and Operational Considerations: Operating a foreign-backed digital brokerage in Brazil requires navigating a complex regulatory framework overseen by the Securities and Exchange Commission of Brazil (CVM) and the Central Bank of Brazil (BCB). Moomoo's expansion strategy relies heavily on seamless integration with local clearing systems and compliance with strict foreign exchange (FX) regulations. For Brazilian investors trading international assets, tax compliance—specifically the calculation of capital gains and the monthly 'Carnê-Leão' tax filing—has historically been a major friction point. To gain widespread adoption, the 'Moomoo Engine' must integrate localized tax reporting tools that simplify these compliance obligations for domestic users. Furthermore, fluctuations in the USD/BRL exchange rate remain a critical variable, as currency volatility can significantly impact the net returns of Brazilian investors trading on US exchanges through the platform. Implications for Futu Holdings ($FUTU): For Futu Holdings ($FUTU), the successful rollout of the 'Moomoo Engine' in Brazil is vital for its broader international diversification strategy. Facing mature markets and regulatory headwinds in mainland China and Hong Kong, Futu has increasingly looked to Southeast Asia and Latin America to drive its next phase of growth. Brazil represents one of the largest retail investment markets in the developing world, making it a high-stakes testing ground for Moomoo's global scalability. If the 'Moomoo Engine' succeeds in capturing a meaningful share of Brazil's active trading volume, it could serve as a blueprint for further expansion into other Latin American markets, such as Mexico and Colombia, thereby bolstering Futu's long-term revenue growth and reducing its geographic concentration risk. ... Market Impact: The launch of the 'Moomoo Engine' has distinct implications for the key players in the digital brokerage space: - Futu Holdings ($FUTU): Bullish. The successful deployment of advanced trading tools in Brazil accelerates Futu's international diversification, reducing its reliance on Asian markets and opening a high-margin revenue stream from active Latin American traders. - XP Inc. ($XP): Bearish. XP faces direct technological competition in its high-value active trader segment. While its advisory network remains a strong moat, Moomoo's low-cost, high-tech offering could pressure XP's commission margins and active client retention. - Nu Holdings ($NU): Neutral. Nubank's primary investment audience consists of mass-market retail investors rather than sophisticated active traders. However, as Nubank seeks to upsell its client base into more complex financial products, Moomoo's advanced platform represents a long-term competitive hurdle. ... > analyst consensus: Bullish on Futu Holdings ($FUTU) international expansion; bearish on XP Inc. ($XP) active trader retention; neutral on Nu Holdings ($NU). > asset_class: Equities"
Borouge,,,,Exa,"Chemical Recycling of Disposable Diapers: Woosh, Borouge, BlueAlp Partnership - News and Statistics - IndexBox",www.indexbox.io,2026-07-01T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-01 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.indexbox.io/blog/circular-system-chemically-recycles-disposable-baby-diapers/,"Chemical Recycling of Disposable Diapers: Woosh, Borouge, BlueAlp Partnership - News and Statistics - IndexBox https://www.indexbox.io/cdn-cgi/content?id=FImuHvxYIBFsVLgjbR5p7_Sy_1XbmWxESip.v6w9O4w-1782905082.976611-1.2.1.1-kvHdW5gpKRvcGIhfNZJAfKsxOC5Y3NeAxgFt_epAnJc ... A three-company collaboration has proven a closed-loop system for chemically recycling used baby diapers. The initiative brings together Woosh, a diaper manufacturer based in Ghent, Belgium; Borouge International, a polyolefin producer headquartered in Vienna, Austria; and BlueAlp, a plastics recycling firm from Eindhoven, the Netherlands. Together, they convert materials from post-consumer diapers into raw material for new polymers. ... Woosh operates a program that delivers diapers to childcare centers and homes, then collects the used items to establish a dedicated, trackable supply for recycling. Borouge and BlueAlp collaborated with Woosh engineers to specify the quality standards the recovered plastic must meet for BlueAlp's chemical recycling process. Woosh subsequently refined its proprietary mechanical separation technique to yield plastic fractions that satisfy those criteria. ... The collected diapers are now processed at a BlueAlp site in Oostende, Belgium. BlueAlp's technology uses low-temperature pyrolysis for chemical recycling of mixed plastic waste. According to BlueAlp CEO Valentijn de Neve, the method consumes less energy and gas while generating more total oil and a higher proportion of premium-grade oil. He added that the continuous operation improves reactor efficiency and process stability, simplifying management and cutting energy use by eliminating repeated heating and cooling cycles. ... The pyrolysis oil from BlueAlp holds International Sustainability & Carbon Certification (ISCC)-PLUS status, a widely acknowledged certification for bio-based, renewable, and circular materials. It also meets the quality benchmarks needed for conversion into new polymers, including those suitable for fresh diaper production. ... Woosh intends to extend its model into France and the Netherlands, boosting the quantity of recovered plastic accessible for recycling. ... 35 and ... demand and supply to macroeconomic ... and technology shifts ... scale, geographic footprint, product mix ... and market positioning. ... identify competitive pressure ... routes to differentiation ... 1. Market Structure and Concentration 2. Competitive Archetypes 3. Segment-by-Segment Competitive Intensity 4. Portfolio Breadth and Product Positioning 5. Capability Matrix 6. Strategic Moves, Partnerships and Expansion Signals ... United States' Sanitary ... and Diaper Market Forecast Shows Slowing ... With a +0.8% CAGRFeb 27, 2026 14"
Borouge,,,,Exa,"Saudi Arabia, the UAE, and Qatar are intensively laying out new petrochemical projects, with polyolefin production capacity seeing a concentrated release. - ChemNet",news.chemnet.com,2026-06-30T00:00:00.000Z,2026-06-30 00:00:00+00:00,,https://news.chemnet.com/news-6916.html,"(I) UAE: Borouge 4 Project to Reach Full Production in Q4, Creating the World's Largest Single-Site Polyolefin Production Base ... The Borouge 4 project, a joint venture between the Abu Dhabi National Oil Company (ADNOC) and Borealis, has completed the commissioning work for all units and plans to officially achieve full-load operation in October 2026. The project involves constructing a new 1.5 million tons/year ethane cracking ethylene unit, supporting 1.4 million tons/year of polyethylene capacity, and simultaneously adding 100,000 tons/year of high-end cross-linked polyethylene capacity. The products focus on high-value categories such as cable specialty materials and high-end packaging materials. ... After full production, Borouge's overall polyolefin annual capacity will increase to 6.4 million tons. Through subsequent technical modifications and optimization of units, the company's total capacity is expected to exceed 6.6 million tons by 2028. Relying on the significant cost advantage of the ethane route, over 90% of the company's products are directed for sale to Asian and European markets. Starting from the second half of the year, it will continuously transport large volumes of low-cost polyethylene goods to China and Southeast Asia. ... Previously, Borouge completed the integration with Borealis and Nova Chemicals to form Borouge International Group. After integration, the total polyolefin capacity reached 13.6 million tons, making it the world's fourth-largest polyolefin producer. In the future, the company will also tap into existing capacity redundancy through unit technical modifications to continuously expand the scale of external export supply. ... , potentially suppressing ... From a medium-to-long-term perspective, there are still several heavyweight projects in the region waiting to be implemented. The Yanbu refinery expansion project, a cooperation between Saudi Aramco and Sinopec, plans to build a new 1.8 million tons/year ethylene unit and supporting polyolefin production lines. The total investment exceeds 10 billion US dollars, and it is planned to be completed and put into production before 2030; Saudi Sipchem is simultaneously planning a new 1.5 million tons/year ethane cracking project, which will continuously release polyolefin increments in ... long term and suppress global chemical prices over the long term. ... QatarEnergy, in collaboration with Chevron Phillips, is advancing the Ras Laffan Petrochemical Complex project. It plans to build a 2.1 million tons/year ethane cracking ethylene unit, which is the largest single-unit ethylene production facility currently planned in the Middle East and is expected to be put into production at the end of 2027, along with supporting high-density polyethylene capacity. After the project is implemented, it will further strengthen the Middle East's dominant position in supply in ... global polyethylene market. ... Kuwait has released its 2025-2030 petrochemical industry development plan, planning to invest a total of 15 billion US dollars for the expansion and upgrading of the refining downstream chain. The goal is to increase domestic total ethylene capacity to 6 million tons and polyethylene capacity to exceed 3 million tons by 2030. The new capacity added in this plan is entirely positioned for export, continuously replenishing the source of goods for the global polyolefin market. ... Second, aiming at the Asian ... consumer market to seize trade ... is concentrated in ... region, with ... world's top ... markets. As ... transfer capacity outward due ... Finally, regional industrial policy support promotes industrial chain upgrading. Saudi Vision 2030 and the UAE Industrial Revitalization Plan both regard the petrochemical downstream industry as a core grip for regional economic transformation. Through multiple support policies such as raw material directed quotas, tax breaks, and industrial park supporting subsidies, they vigorously promote the implementation of ethane cracking and polyolefin integration projects, assisting the local industry in transforming from a single crude oil export to a high value-added fine chemical product export. ... In the short term, the domestic polyolefin market will face the pressure of concentrated arrival of imported goods in the second half of 2026. Starting from the third quarter, new polyethylene units in the UAE and Saudi Arabia will successively go into production and ramp up, and a large volume of low-cost Middle Eastern goods will pour into the domestic market. Coupled with the recovery of operating rates after the completion of maintenance of large domestic refining units such as Shenghong and Wanhua, the market will present a pattern of simultaneous expansion in supply and demand. Prices of general-grade products such as LLDPE and HDPE lack momentum for a trend rise, and the space for industry profit repair is relatively limited. ... From a medium-to-long-term dimension, the global polyolefin overcapacity pattern will further solidify, and homogeneous competition in the low-end general-purpose material market tends to become white-hot. Data shows that global polyethylene capacity is expected to increase by 8 to 8.5 million tons in 2026, with the Middle East region accounting for over 40% of the new capacity; in the next five years, the Gulf region plans to add nearly 20 million tons of polyolefin capacity, which will continue to suppress the upward height of the industry boom cycle. Facing the impact of low-cost goods from the Middle East, domestic oil-based and coal-based polyolefin enterprises urgently need to accelerate industrial upgrading and transform to high value-added differentiated products such as metallocene polyethylene, high-end cable materials, and medical specialty materials, relying on product competitiveness to avoid homogeneous price competition and accelerate the market clearing of backward capacity. ... At the global trade level, the regional supply chain pattern welcomes a reconstruction. The Middle East is gradually replacing Europe to grow into the world's largest polyolefin export base; European chemical companies are shrinking general-purpose polymer capacity and focusing on high value-added tracks such as fine chemicals and specialty new materials. Relying on scale and low-cost capacity advantages, the Middle East region's pricing discourse power in the global polyolefin market continues to rise, and the import source structure of plastic raw materials in the Far East market will further tilt towards the Middle East in the future."
Borouge,,,,Exa,Borouge International launches polyolefin elastomer for greater flexibility and processing,www.specialchem.com,2026-06-29T00:00:00.000Z,2026-06-29 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.specialchem.com/plastics/news/polyolefin-elastomer-with-good-performance-and-processability-by-borouge-international,"Borouge International launches polyolefin elastomer for greater flexibility and processing ... # Polyolefin elastomer with good performance and processability by Borouge International ... Last update on ... 29, 2026 ... A new polyolefin elastomer (POE) grade designed for TPO compounds, hot melt adhesives (HMA), and highly filled systems is now available for evaluation. Borouge International has introduced Queo™ 7030LA-P, a grade developed to address a known formulation challenge: achieving both flexibility and processing efficiency within a single material profile. ... According to the supplier, the POE segment offering low density and high melt flow has historically presented limited choices for demanding compound and blend applications in automotive, construction, and industrial markets. Queo™ 7030LA-P is positioned as a reliable option within that segment. ... The launch reflects Borouge International’s ongoing commitment to expanding its portfolio with high-performance, customer-focused solutions, supporting a wide range of industries. ... - Enables high‑quality T ... production for automotive, construction, and industrial markets ... temperature impact performance thanks ... . - Superior flow and bonding behavior in HMA applications ... high extrusion output ... with talc ... massing during logistics."
Borouge,,,,Exa,XRG and OMV Expand US Plastic Manufacturing in $60B Borouge Deal | UAE Preferred,www.uaepreferred.com,2026-06-29T00:00:00.000Z,2026-06-29 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.uaepreferred.com/perspectives/xrg-and-omv-expand-us-plastic-manufacturing-in-60b-borouge-deal,"XRG and OMV Expand US Plastic Manufacturing in $60B Borouge Deal | UAE Preferred ... # XRG and OMV Expand US Plastic Manufacturing in $60B Borouge Deal ... April 20 ... ADNOC's XRG and OMV have finalized a deal to create the world's fourth-largest polyolefins producer—Borouge Group International. The $60 billion merger of Borouge and Borealis brings North American polyethylene producer NOVA Chemicals into XRG's expanding US portfolio. NOVA Chemicals operates major production facilities in Indiana and Louisiana, employing over 250 people, with additional offices in Pennsylvania. ... This deal creates a global chemicals powerhouse with a significant American footprint. Including NOVA Chemicals' assets, Borouge now operates eight manufacturing sites and corporate offices across New Jersey, Texas, Louisiana, Pennsylvania and Indiana, managing the full plastics value chain from production to distribution. ... Polyolefins—including polyethylene and polypropylene—are among the world's most consumed plastics, essential to packaging, automotive, construction and medical supplies. The merger deepens UAE investment in critical US chemical infrastructure while expanding domestic production capacity in materials vital to American manufacturers and consumers. ... > “Borouge International is exceptionally positioned to meet growing global demand for advanced materials, while supporting industrial development, driving economic diversification, and reinforcing the UAE’s role as a trusted leader in the global energy and chemicals landscape.” – HE Dr. Sultan Al Jaber, Executive Chairman of XRG and Managing Director and Group Chief Executive of ADNOC ... # XRG and OMV Expand US Plastic Manufacturing in $60B Borouge Deal ... April 8, 2026 ... ADNOC's XRG and OMV have finalized a deal to create the world's fourth-largest polyolefins producer—Borouge Group International. The $60 billion merger of Borouge and Borealis brings North American polyethylene producer NOVA Chemicals into XRG's expanding US portfolio. NOVA Chemicals operates major production facilities in Indiana and Louisiana, employing over 250 people, with additional offices in Pennsylvania. ... This deal creates a global chemicals powerhouse with a significant American footprint. Including NOVA Chemicals' assets, Borouge now operates eight manufacturing sites and corporate offices across New Jersey, Texas, Louisiana, Pennsylvania and Indiana, managing the full plastics value chain from production to distribution. ... Polyolefins—including polyethylene and polypropylene—are among the world's most consumed plastics, essential to packaging, automotive, construction and medical supplies. The merger deepens UAE investment in critical US chemical infrastructure while expanding domestic production capacity in materials vital to American manufacturers and consumers. ... > “Borouge International is exceptionally positioned to meet growing global demand for advanced materials, while supporting industrial development, driving economic diversification, and reinforcing the UAE’s role as a trusted leader in the global energy and chemicals landscape.” – HE Dr. Sultan Al Jaber, Executive Chairman of XRG and Managing Director and Group Chief Executive of ADNOC ... ## ADNOC Expands US Manufacturing Presence with NOVA Chemicals"
Borouge,,,,Exa,NOVA Chemicals acquisition 24-06-2026,www.polyestertime.com (Polyestertime),2026-06-23T00:00:00.000Z,2026-06-23 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.polyestertime.com/nova-chemicals-acquisition-borouge-international-joffre/,"NOVA Chemicals acquisition 24-06-2026 ... The acquisition of NOVA Chemicals by Borouge Group International marks one of the most significant shifts in the global polyolefins industry in recent years. For Alberta, the transaction is more than a corporate ownership change. It places the Joffre petrochemical complex, one of Canada’s most important manufacturing assets, inside a much larger international platform spanning North America, Europe and the Middle East. ... NOVA Chemicals, including its major Joffre site in central Alberta, now operates as part of Borouge International, the global polyolefins company formed through the combination of Borouge, Borealis and NOVA Chemicals. While NOVA and its subsidiaries continue to operate under their legal registered names, Borouge International has become the broader brand identity for the newly combined business. ... The NOVA Chemicals acquisition gives Borouge International a stronger position in North America, one of the world’s most important petrochemical and plastics markets. NOVA brings long-established polyethylene expertise, integrated production assets and access to customers across packaging, industrial and consumer-goods supply chains. ... For Borouge International, the strategic logic is scale. Combining Borouge, Borealis and NOVA creates a broader production and technology base, with assets spread across key geographies. This geographic diversification is important in an industry shaped by feedstock costs, energy pricing, logistics, regional demand and trade policy. ... For NOVA, the transaction places its Canadian and North American operations inside a larger global company with deeper access to technology, capital and international markets. That could strengthen the long-term competitiveness of assets such as Joffre, provided the company continues to invest in reliability, emissions performance, product innovation and operational safety. NOVA Chemicals acquisition ... The Joffre site is one of the most important pieces of the NOVA Chemicals portfolio. Located in central Alberta, the large integrated petrochemical complex includes ethylene and polyethylene manufacturing units and supports hundreds of permanent employees as well as a large contractor workforce. ... The recent planned turnaround at the Ethylene One facility also shows the scale and complexity of the Joffre operation. Turnarounds are major maintenance events that require detailed planning, inspections, cleaning, repairs and equipment upgrades. They are essential for safety, reliability and long-term asset performance. ... The NOVA Chemicals acquisition comes at a time when the global plastics sector is under pressure to become more efficient, more circular and more transparent. Polyethylene remains essential for modern life, but producers face rising expectations around recycling, emissions, product design and waste reduction. ... This is where Borouge International’s scale could become important. Larger companies can invest more heavily in advanced materials, recycling-compatible resins, lower-carbon production pathways and technical support for customers trying to reduce packaging waste. ... However, scale alone is not enough. The combined group will need to prove that it can balance growth with environmental responsibility. For customers, regulators and communities, the key question will be whether a larger polyolefins company can deliver better performance, not only larger volumes. ... For Alberta, the deal reinforces the province’s role as a petrochemical manufacturing center. The Joffre site gives Borouge International a major foothold in Canada and strengthens the link between Alberta’s energy resources and higher-value chemical production. ... This could support long-term employment, supplier activity and industrial investment if the new ownership structure continues to prioritize the site. It could also increase the importance of Alberta within global decisions about polyethylene production, export markets and future capital allocation. ... At the same time, local communities will watch closely for continuity. Workforce stability, contractor demand, environmental performance, turnaround planning and community engagement will all be important indicators of how the acquisition affects the region in practical terms. ... The global polyolefins market is highly competitive. Producers must manage feedstock access, energy costs, customer requirements, logistics, regulatory pressure and changing demand patterns. Packaging remains a major market, but producers are also targeting infrastructure, healthcare, mobility, energy and advanced industrial applications. ... By adding NOVA Chemicals, Borouge International gains a stronger North American position and a more balanced global footprint. That may help it compete against other large petrochemical producers, especially in markets where customers want reliable supply, specialized grades and technical support. ... wider trend: major energy and chemical companies are repositioning toward materials that remain in demand even as fuel markets evolve. Petrochemicals, especially polymers, are increasingly viewed as a long-term growth area ... companies with access to ... feedstocks and global distribution networks. ... The most important developments will be operational and strategic rather than symbolic. Investors, customers and local communities should watch whether Borouge International increases investment in Joffre, expands technical capabilities, improves circular plastics offerings, or changes how NOVA’s North American assets are integrated into the wider group. ... Another key issue will be reliability. Large petrochemical complexes depend on consistent maintenance, safe operations and efficient turnarounds. If the combined company can use its broader resources to strengthen asset performance, the acquisition could become a long-term advantage for both the company and the region. ... The NOVA Chemicals acquisition is therefore not simply a change of ownership. It is a sign that Alberta’s petrochemical assets are now part of a larger global contest over scale, technology, feedstock advantage and sustainable plastics production. ... For Joffre, the message is clear: a site built around ethylene and polyethylene has become an even more important node in the global polyolefins industry. ... Nova Chemicals Strengthens Sustainable Packaging Future With Two Advanced Recycled Polyethylene Grades Powering North America’s Circular Plastics Economy Growth ... Borouge International has introduced a bio-based version of a polypropylene grade used in drinking water and irrigation fittings, offering lower-carbon performance without changing processing requirements."
Borouge,,,,Exa,Borouge International offers bio-based variant of PP grade for drinking water fittings,www.specialchem.com,2026-06-23T00:00:00.000Z,2026-06-23 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.specialchem.com/plastics/news/bio-based-pp-grade-for-drinking-water-fittings-drop-in-from-borouge-international,"Borouge International offers bio-based variant of PP grade for drinking water fittings ... Bio-based PP grade for drinking water fittings: Drop-in from Borouge International ... # Bio-based PP grade for drinking water fittings: Drop-in from Borouge International ... Last update on Jun 23, 2026 ... Pipe fitting manufacturers and compounders working with polypropylene for drinking water and irrigation systems can now specify a bio-based variant of Borouge International's established PP grade BA160E-8229-01. The updated grade, now part of the Bornewables™ portfolio, incorporates 10% bio-based feedstock sourced from waste and residue streams while retaining the same performance, processing behavior, and drinking water approvals as the conventional version. ... BA160E-8229-01 is a fully formulated, drinking water–approved compound based on a beta-nucleated PP heterophasic block copolymer, designed for injection molding of pipe compression fittings and mechanical joints. According to Borouge International, the grade provides optimized mechanical properties alongside controlled crystallization behavior. The supplier reports that this controlled crystallization enables higher demolding temperatures and shorter cooling times, helping reduce cycle times and increase manufacturing productivity. ... The updated version of BA160E-8229-01 is produced using 10% bio-based feedstock attributed via the mass balance approach. According to Borouge International, Bornewables™ versions of materials deliver the same quality and consistency as fossil-based alternatives, with a significantly lower carbon footprint. ... The bio-based feedstock content is certified under the ISCC PLUS system. Borouge International states that the sourcing and traceability of the renewable feedstock are independently audited across the entire supply chain, from raw material to final product. The certification is based on mass balance accounting and establishes a verified chain of custody for bio-based materials."
Borouge,,,,Exa,"Lubrizol bets $150 million on India CPVC market, eyes doubling revenue in five years - The Economic Times",economictimes.indiatimes.com (Nikita Periwal),2026-06-23T00:00:00.000Z,2026-06-23 00:00:00+00:00,,https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/news/company/corporate-trends/lubrizol-bets-150-million-on-india-cpvc-market-eyes-doubling-revenue-in-five-years/articleshow/131949413.cms,"Lubrizol bets $150 million on India CPVC market, eyes doubling revenue in five years - The Economic Times ... Lubrizol bets $150 million on India CPVC market, eyes doubling revenue in five years ... bets $15 ... Warren Buffett-backed Lubrizol Corporation is making its largest India investment in six decades, a $150 million facility in Gujarat, to tap into the booming CPVC resin market. This move, in partnership with Grasim Industries, aims to meet surging demand driven by urbanization and infrastructure growth. Lubrizol anticipates doubling its Indian revenues within five years, highlighting the region's significant economic potential and its commitment to local production. ... ANIBharuch [Gujarat], Jun 16 (ANI): Gujarat Chief Minister Bhupendra Patel speaks during the inauguration of Phase-1 of the world's largest state-of-the-art CPVC resin manufacturing plant by Lubrizol and Aditya Birla Group's Grasim Industries at Vilayat, in Bharuch ... Mumbai: Warren Buffett-backed Lubrizol Corporation's largest investment in India in six decades, $150 million, along with Aditya Birla Group's Grasim Industries, is aimed at being a sizeable presence in India's nearly ₹6,000-crore chlorinated polyvinyl chloride (CPVC) resin market. The investment is for an integrated 1,00,000-tonne CPVC resin facility at Vilayat in Gujarat.""India is one of the largest growth regions in the world, and in five years we should double our revenues here,"" Rebecca Liebert, CEO of Lubrizol Corporation, said, estimating growth in the region in high single digits, compared to low single digits in other regions.Also Read: India’s scale, stability make it a bridge for South Asia, Middle East, Africa: Lubrizol’s Anant Pant ... Demand for CPVC, which is gaining traction for plumbing pipes and fittings, is projected to grow at 10-12% annually over the next few years, doubling the market in five to six years. India consumes around 2,50,000 tonnes of CPVC each year. This growth is expected to be underpinned by rapid urbanisation, government infrastructure initiatives, and the replacement of galvanised iron pipes with CPVC in plumbing and fire sprinkler applications.""Most of the output is aimed for the local market. We could export a little, but it is going to be very small,"" Liebert told ET in an exclusive interaction. ""Our strategy has been to be local for local for the region we are operating in. And being in India is the best way to be competitive in India."" ... In its first phase, the CPVC plant in Vilayat will have an output of 50,000 tonnes. Demand for CPVC is seen growing in tandem with the plumbing market in India, which is estimated to grow at 10%. ""If the plumbing market continues to grow at that rate, there is a need for more capacity to come online,"" Liebert said. ... Apart from setting up this plant, Lubrizol has also doubled capacity at its CPVC compound manufacturing plant at Dahej in Gujarat. It has expanded its speciality chemical additives facility at Turbhe in Navi Mumbai and inaugurated a medical tubing facility at Chennai in collaboration with Polyhose. The US-based speciality chemicals major has invested around $350 million in India in the last few years.""The Indian economy, people and growth are driving this optimism,"" Liebert said. ""We have been servicing this market from the US for several years. But it is difficult to understand what is needed on the ground in India by a team in the US, and that is why we have also invested in R&D here."""
Borouge,,,,Exa,World's 4th-largest polyolefins producer enters transformative phase | CESIS,cesis.center (Icesis),2026-06-20T00:00:00.000Z,2026-06-20 00:00:00+00:00,,https://cesis.center/worlds-4th-largest-polyolefins-producer-enters-transformative-phase/,"Headquartered in Abu Dhabi, Borouge operates one of the world’s largest integrated polyolefin complexes in Al Ruwais Industrial City. The company was founded in 1998 as a venture between Abu Dhabi National Oil Company and Austria-based Borealis GmbH and listed on the Abu Dhabi Exchange in 2022. ... The recent creation of Borouge Group International, combining the assets and technologies of ADNOC and Borealis’ parent company OMV, has formed the world’s fourth-largest polyolefins producer by nameplate capacity. ... The transaction, completed in March 2026, significantly expands Borouge’s geographic reach, product portfolio and scale, reducing reliance on any single market while improving resilience through diversification. Management expects the expanded platform to benefit from operational synergies, technology sharing and stronger access to growth markets. ... Even before the transformative change, the Abu Dhabi-based polyolefins producer delivered a record financial performance in 2025, posting a net profit of around U.S.$ 1.1 billion on revenues of U.S.$5.85 billion, supported by the highest annual sales volumes in its history and an EBITDA (earnings before interest, taxation, depreciation and amortisation) margin of 37%. Production exceeded nameplate capacity, while sales reached 5.4 million tonnes, demonstrating the company’s ability to generate earnings even amid a challenging global petrochemicals cycle. ... The momentum has continued into 2026. In the first quarter, Borouge reported revenues of U.S.$ 1.2 billion and a net profit of U.S.$156 million despite disruptions to logistics routes and operational challenges. Production remained resilient at 98% of nameplate capacity, underscoring the strength of its operating model and business continuity planning. Strategic growth drivers The Borouge 4 project is another significant growth engine. The facility is expected to add 1.4 million tonnes of annual production capacity, materially increasing earnings potential and strengthening Borouge’s position in high-growth polyolefin markets. Commissioning activities have already begun for the first facility within the complex, while the broader project is expected to become a core earnings contributor over time. ... Importantly, Borouge recently secured operational control and marketing rights for Borouge 4 under an agreement with ADNOC and OMV. Management estimates the arrangement could generate approximately U.S.$ 400 million in cumulative net profit over the next three years, representing roughly 10% annual earnings accretion once the project reaches full ramp-up. ... Beyond new capacity, Borouge continues to expand margins through technology and innovation. Its AI, Digitalisation & Technology programme generated U.S.$ 717 million in value during 2025, significantly exceeding internal targets. In the first quarter of 2026 alone, the programme contributed a further U.S.$ 143 million in value creation through efficiency improvements, digitisation initiatives and advanced manufacturing applications. ... The company is also shifting its product mix toward higher-margin specialised and premium products. Infrastructure solutions accounted for 38% of sales volumes in 2025, while Borouge continued launching differentiated products across healthcare, automotive and circular economy applications. These higher-value segments support pricing premiums above industry benchmarks and reduce exposure to commoditised pricing pressures. ... Finally, improving industry fundamentals could provide an additional tailwind. Global polyolefin prices rose sharply in March 2026 amid supply shortages, with stronger pricing extending into April. If demand remains firm while new global capacity additions moderate, Borouge’s large-scale production base could benefit from higher realised prices. ... Investment outlook On the income side, shareholders approved dividend payments totalling U.S.$ 1.32 billion for fiscal year 2025, equivalent to around 16.2 fils per share. ... The growth outlook is also attractive. Capacity expansion through Borouge 4, the scale advantages of Borouge International, continued AI-driven efficiency gains and a growing portfolio of premium products all point toward rising earnings power over the medium term. ... Risks remain. Polyolefins markets are cyclical and sensitive to global industrial activity, while regional geopolitical disruptions can affect logistics and operations, as demonstrated during the first quarter. The transition to a fully operational Borouge Group International and successful execution of Borouge 4 will also be critical determinants of future performance. ... Borouge enters this next phase from a position of strength. Record production, industry-leading margins, and visible capacity growth demonstrate strong fundamentals."
Borouge,,,,Exa,Honeywell introduces Experion Cognition for Borouge International | Hydrocarbon Engineering,www.hydrocarbonengineering.com (Ellie Brosnan),2026-06-10T00:00:00.000Z,2026-06-10 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.hydrocarbonengineering.com/petrochemicals/10062026/honeywell-introduces-experion-cognition-for-borouge-international/,"Honeywell introduces Experion Cognition for Borouge International | Hydrocarbon Engineering ... Honeywell has introduced Experion Cognition, an AI-enabled control system platform designed to advance autonomous operations by making recommendations and automated decisions that optimise production and increase safety within industrial facilities. The live proof-of-concept was demonstrated at Borouge Group International AG’s Ruwais facility in Abu Dhabi, UAE. ... “Representing the petrochemical industry’s first AI autonomous operations, this collaboration sets a new industry standard for efficiency and innovation, upskilling our people, as well as boosting our performance and competitiveness,” said Dr. Hasan Karam, Chief Operating Officer of Borouge International. “This initiative is a core component of Borouge International’s AI, Digitalisation & Technology programme reflecting our continued focus on disciplined execution, operational impact and long-term value creation.” ... As part of Honeywell’s Experion PKS distributed control system network, Experion Cognition is designed to seamlessly integrate into existing control room environments. Experion Cognition will be commercially available in 3Q26."
Borouge,,,,Exa,"Supporting a more sustainable, low-carbon future for mobility - smart_molding",smart-molding.com,2026-06-08T00:00:00.000Z,2026-06-08 00:00:00+00:00,,https://smart-molding.com/news/4522,"Borouge International announces the global launch of Borcycle™ GE2331SY, a new high-performance, recycled polypropylene (PP) compound designed for demanding structural automotive components. The material is a finalist in the Plastics Recycling Awards Europe (PRAE) 2026, reflecting its role in supporting a more sustainable, low-carbon future for mobility. ... Established in March 2026 by XRG – ADNOC’s international investment arm – and OMV, Borouge International is a leading global polyolefins producer that designs and manufactures essential materials for modern life. The company brings together Borouge plc, Borealis GmbH and NOVA Chemicals Corporation – three regionally strong, operationally distinct businesses into a single global platform. ... Borcycle GE2331SY, is another example of Borouge International’s commitment to delivering materials that enable greater efficiency, durability, and circularity across critical applications—the glass-fiber reinforced compound combines 40% post-consumer recycled material with 20% glass fibers to deliver the strength required for interior structural parts. It is already being used by several original equipment manufacturers (OEMs) for high-end structural automotive components, including center console carriers and dashboard carriers, demonstrating its suitability for premium applications."
Borouge,,,,Exa,Borouge International & Lanxess advance circular acetone with Borvida B‑acetone,chemxplore.com,2026-06-02T00:00:00.000Z,2026-06-02 00:00:00+00:00,,https://chemxplore.com/news/borouge-international-lanxess-acetone-sustainability,"Borouge International & Lanxess advance circular acetone with Borvida B‑acetone ... - Borouge International supplies Lanxess with Borvida™ B‑acetone, an ISCC PLUS‑certified acetone derived from non‑food waste and residue biomass. - Borvida™ B‑acetone is chemically identical to conventional acetone and is delivered using a Mass Balance chain‑of‑custody model. - Mass‑balanced acetone has been in industrial production for several years and can cut carbon footprint by up to 175% per kilogram versus regular acetone. - Borouge International was established in 2026, combining Borouge, Borealis and NOVA Chemicals into a global polyolefins platform to support supply and technical collaboration. ... Borouge International supplies Lanxess with Borvida™ B‑acetone, an ISCC PLUS‑certified acetone produced from non‑food waste and residue biomass. The product is chemically identical to conventional acetone and is part of the Borvida portfolio of renewable and circular base chemicals. ... Bio‑based, renewable content is tracked using a Mass Balance chain‑of‑custody model from feedstock to final product. Mass‑balanced acetone produced with Borvida™ B‑acetone has been used in industrial production for several years and offers the same properties and performance as conventional acetone. ... The company cites a carbon footprint reduction of up to 175% per kilogram compared with regular acetone, based on a Life Cycle Assessment for a cradle‑to‑gate system boundary including biogenic carbon removals and emissions. ... Lanxess positions the shift to more sustainable value chains beginning with raw materials: “Thanks to our partnership with Borouge International, we can offer bio‑based circular, mass‑balanced acetone solutions that help our customers accelerate their sustainability ambitions.” ... Borouge International frames the deal as part of its strategy to provide secure supply, technical expertise and flexibility: “By working together across the value chain, we can unlock the full value of sustainable acetone from bio waste. In collaboration with Lanxess, we are developing innovative, high‑performance solutions that support customers’ sustainability goals and help them prepare for future sustainable transformation.”"
Borouge,,,,Exa,"Our recycled PE mechanical recycling facility in Connersville, Ind., celebrated one year of operations this May. Since commissioning, SYNDIGO1 has been proving that circularity at scale is possible — and already underway. | NOVA Chemicals",www.linkedin.com (NOVA Chemicals),2026-06-01T00:00:00.000Z,2026-06-01 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.linkedin.com/posts/nova-chemicals_syndigo1-activity-7467246361785032704-gFMC,"# Our recycled PE mechanical recycling facility in Connersville, Ind., celebrated one year of operations this May. Since commissioning, SYNDIGO1 has been proving that circularity at scale is possible — and already underway. | NOVA Chemicals · LinkedIn · 2026-06-01 ... **NOVA Chemicals**: NOVA Chemicals is reshaping plastics for a better, more sustainable world. They are leading the development of and transition to circular plastics, and shaping and sustaining a low carbon, zero-plastic-waste future. NOVA Chemicals has 1,000-2,000 employees (-10% YoY) and has annual revenue in the $4B-$5B range, founded in 1975. Headquartered in Calgary, Alberta, Canada, with presence in United States, China, and Switzerland. Its workforce is distributed across 12 countries (including Canada, United States, India, China, and United Arab Emirates). Has $883.3M in total funding, with 2 prior funding rounds. ... Our recycled PE mechanical recycling facility in Connersville, Ind., celebrated one year of operations this May. Since commissioning, SYNDIGO1 has been proving that circularity at scale is possible — and already underway. ... Some of SYNDIGO1’s key milestones over the past year include: ... ✅ Receiving an FDA Letter of Non-Objection confirming food-contact suitability for post-consumer rLLDPE ... 🏆 Achieving Recycled Material Standard (RMS) certification from GreenBlue, independently verifying that SYNDIGO™ recycled polyethylene is made from 100% post-consumer recycled content ... 🚀 Launching two new grades of SYNDIGO recycled polyethylene resin (SYNDIGO rPE-IN3 and PE-IN4) ... 🛤️ Delivering first commercial order — a 200,000-pound railcar shipment of SYNDIGO rPE-IN3 ... 💚 Donating $25,000 to the Fayette Community Foundation, establishing the NOVA Chemicals Community Impact Fund to support residents and initiatives ... 👷 Creating more than 125 new jobs in the Connersville community ... As [Borouge International](https://linkedin.com/company/borougeinternational), we are excited to see the continued progress SYNDIGO1 will make toward recycling polyethylene and advancing the world through essential materials. #SYNDIGO1"
Borouge,,,,Exa,Borouge signs strategic logistics agreements | GPCA,gpcachem.org (AJ),2026-05-22T00:00:00.000Z,2026-05-22 00:00:00+00:00,,https://gpcachem.org/2026/05/22/borouge-signs-strategic-logistics-agreements/,"Borouge signs strategic logistics agreements | GPCA ... The company signed a series of agreements with key UAE logistics partners at Make it in the Emirates 2026 ... GPCA full member, Borouge Plc, a leading petrochemical company, announced on 7 May a series of strategic agreements with key UAE logistics partners at Make it in the Emirates 2026. ... Through partnerships with Gulftainer at Khor Fakkan Port, Gulftainer Shipping and Etihad Rail, Borouge will expand its access to multiple, diversified export gateways across the UAE. ... The partnerships are aimed at enhancing logistics efficiency and port connectivity, enabling faster, more reliable cargo movement across Borouge’s export network, supporting flexible routing across sea and rail and maintaining stable, consistent supply flows. ... Hazeem Sultan Al Suwaidi, CEO, Borouge Plc, and Board Member, GPCA, noted that these partnerships mark an important step forward in strengthening Borouge’s logistics network and supporting the company’s growth ambitions. By developing a flexible, multi‑modal system in close collaboration with national partners, Borouge is reinforcing its position as a dependable global supplier, while contributing to the UAE’s industrial development, Al Suwaidi said. ... The successful formation of Borouge International, completed on 30 March 2026, creates the fourth-largest polyolefins producer globally as measured by nameplate capacity."
Borouge,,,,Exa,📣 Key milestone at Borouge 4: first batch of Cross Linkable Polyethylene (XLPE) materials from the new XLPE unit is set to deliver! | TECNIMONT,www.linkedin.com (TECNIMONT),2026-05-20T00:00:00.000Z,2026-05-20 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.linkedin.com/posts/tecnimont_tecnimont-meet-borouge-activity-7462783684123914240-un0b,"# 📣 Key milestone at Borouge 4: first batch of Cross Linkable Polyethylene (XLPE) materials from the new XLPE unit is set to deliver! | TECNIMONT · LinkedIn · 2026-05-20 ... 📣 Key milestone at Borouge 4: first batch of Cross Linkable Polyethylene (XLPE) materials from the new XLPE unit is set to deliver! ... At Borouge 4, the fourth expansion of Borouge’s world scale polyolefins complex in Ruwais, Abu Dhabi (UAE), #TECNIMONT is delivering three EPC packages, with an integrated scope spanning polyolefin units, the XLPE unit, and utilities & offsites. ... The new XLPE unit will add 100,000 tons per year of production capacity, supporting the manufacturing of high voltage underground and underwater power cables for power transmission and distribution networks. ... This milestone is more than a technical achievement. It reflects our ability to deliver complex, world scale facilities with execution excellence and expertise - and a concrete outcome of integrated execution supporting the UAE’s power infrastructure and industrial development. ... > Congratulations to the Tecnimont team on this milestone. The XLPE unit at Borouge 4 adds 100,000 t/y of capacity specifically for high-voltage underground and submarine power cables – which are essential for offshore wind and grid interconnection projects. From a project controls perspective, delivering three EPC packages (polyolefins, XLPE, utilities) on an integrated site like Ruwais requires very careful interface management. The XLPE unit’s first batch is a clear sign that the execution strategy is working. Well done."
Borouge,,,,Exa,AD Ports Group and Borouge Collaborate to Explore Alternative Export Hub on the UAE East Coast | Abu Dhabi Ports,www.adports.ae (naser bakeel),2026-05-19T00:00:00.000Z,2026-05-19 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.adports.ae/ad-ports-group-and-borouge-collaborate-to-explore-alternative-export-hub-on-the-uae-east-coast/,"AD Ports Group and Borouge Collaborate to Explore Alternative Export Hub on the UAE East Coast | Abu Dhabi Ports ... AD Ports Group and ... ouge Collaborate to Explore Alternative Export Hub on the UAE East Coast ... Abu Dhabi, UAE – 19 May 2026: AD Ports Group (ADX: ADPORTS), a leading global enabler of integrated trade, industry and logistics solutions, has signed a partnership agreement with Borouge Plc (ADX: BOROUGE), a leading petrochemical company providing innovative and differentiated polyolefin solutions, to explore expanded collaboration opportunities aimed at enhancing export resilience and operational flexibility. ... The agreement paves the way for both parties to collaborate on the establishment of an alternative international export hub on the UAE’s East Coast, as an essential element of Borouge’s long-term export strategy. This initiative aims to strengthen export continuity while embedding resilience by design across Borouge’s logistics network. ... The collaboration will leverage AD Ports Group’s strong marine connectivity and access to its comprehensive port services of Fujairah Terminals and Eastern ports facilities in the UAE, while assessing the development of dedicated polyolefins infrastructure on the East Coast. ... Both parties will assess ways to reduce reliance on constrained maritime passages and enhance flexibility in vessel scheduling and routing through joint engagement with shipping lines to enable new routes via Fujairah Terminals. Together, these efforts aim to support stable and reliable export flows across a diversified portfolio of ports and logistics facilities. ... Hazeem Sultan Al Suwaidi, Chief Executive Officer of Borouge Plc, added: “This strategic partnership marks a significant step forward in Borouge Plc’s logistics capabilities, supporting our continued growth. Together with AD Ports Group, we are building a more flexible and diversified network that enhances reliability, ensures continuity of supply, and reinforces our position as a trusted global supplier, supporting the UAE’s industrial strategy.” ... Borouge Plc is the largest exporter through AD Ports Group. This collaboration builds on a longstanding partnership between the two companies established in 2001, and reflects the shared ambition of AD Ports Group and Borouge Plc to strengthen supply chain resilience and reinforce the UAE’s position as a globally competitive logistics and industrial hub."
Borouge,,,,Exa,AD Ports Group and Borouge Collaborate to Explore Alternative Export Hub on the UAE East Coast,onlinemediacafe.com (Neel Achary),2026-05-19T00:00:00.000Z,2026-05-19 00:00:00+00:00,,https://onlinemediacafe.com/business/ad-ports-group-and-borouge-collaborate-to-explore-alternative-export-hub-on-the-uae-east-coast/,"AD Ports Group and Borouge Collaborate to Explore Alternative Export Hub on the UAE East Coast ... # AD Ports Group and Borouge Collaborate to Explore Alternative Export Hub on the UAE East Coast ... May 19, 2026 Abu Dhabi, UAE – 19 May 2026: AD Ports Group (ADX: ADPORTS), a leading global enabler of integrated trade, industry and logistics solutions, has signed a partnership agreement with Borouge Plc (ADX: BOROUGE), a leading petrochemical company providing innovative and differentiated polyolefin solutions, to explore expanded collaboration opportunities aimed at enhancing export resilience and operational flexibility. The agreement paves the way for both parties to collaborate on the establishment of an alternative international export hub on the UAE’s East Coast, as an essential element of Borouge’s long-term export strategy. This initiative aims to strengthen export continuity while embedding resilience by design across Borouge’s logistics network. ... The collaboration will leverage AD Ports Group’s strong marine connectivity and access to its comprehensive port services of Fujairah Terminals and Eastern ports facilities in the UAE, while assessing the development of dedicated polyolefins infrastructure on the East Coast. ... Both parties will assess ways to reduce reliance on constrained maritime passages and enhance flexibility in vessel scheduling and routing through joint engagement with shipping lines to enable new routes via Fujairah Terminals. Together, these efforts aim to support stable and reliable export flows across a diversified portfolio of ports and logistics facilities. ... Hazeem Sultan Al Suwaidi, Chief Executive Officer of Borouge Plc, added: “This strategic partnership marks a significant step forward in Borouge Plc’s logistics capabilities, supporting our continued growth. Together with AD Ports Group, we are building a more flexible and diversified network that enhances reliability, ensures continuity of supply, and reinforces our position as a trusted global supplier, supporting the UAE’s industrial strategy.” ... Borouge Plc is the largest exporter through AD Ports Group. This collaboration builds on a longstanding partnership between the two companies established in 2001, and reflects the shared ambition of AD Ports Group and Borouge Plc to strengthen supply chain resilience and reinforce the UAE’s position as a globally competitive logistics and industrial hub."
Borouge,,,,Exa,AD Ports Group and Borouge Collaborate to Explore Alternative Export Hub on the UAE East Coast | Biz News Desk,biznewsdesk.com (admin),2026-05-19T00:00:00.000Z,2026-05-19 00:00:00+00:00,,https://biznewsdesk.com/business/ad-ports-group-and-borouge-collaborate-to-explore-alternative-export-hub-on-the-uae-east-coast/,"AD Ports Group and Borouge Collaborate to Explore Alternative Export Hub on the UAE East Coast | Biz News Desk ... # AD Ports Group and Borouge Collaborate to Explore Alternative Export Hub on the UAE East Coast ... Posted on May 19, 2026 by admin Abu Dhabi, UAE – 19 May 2026: AD Ports Group (ADX: ADPORTS), a leading global enabler of integrated trade, industry and logistics solutions, has signed a partnership agreement with Borouge Plc (ADX: BOROUGE), a leading petrochemical company providing innovative and differentiated polyolefin solutions, to explore expanded collaboration opportunities aimed at enhancing export resilience and operational flexibility. The agreement paves the way for both parties to collaborate on the establishment of an alternative international export hub on the UAE’s East Coast, as an essential element of Borouge’s long-term export strategy. This initiative aims to strengthen export continuity while embedding resilience by design across Borouge’s logistics network. ... The collaboration will leverage AD Ports Group’s strong marine connectivity and access to its comprehensive port services of Fujairah Terminals and Eastern ports facilities in the UAE, while assessing the development of dedicated polyolefins infrastructure on the East Coast. ... Both parties will assess ways to reduce reliance on constrained maritime passages and enhance flexibility in vessel scheduling and routing through joint engagement with shipping lines to enable new routes via Fujairah Terminals. Together, these efforts aim to support stable and reliable export flows across a diversified portfolio of ports and logistics facilities. ... Hazeem Sultan Al Suwaidi, Chief Executive Officer of Borouge Plc, added: “This strategic partnership marks a significant step forward in Borouge Plc’s logistics capabilities, supporting our continued growth. Together with AD Ports Group, we are building a more flexible and diversified network that enhances reliability, ensures continuity of supply, and reinforces our position as a trusted global supplier, supporting the UAE’s industrial strategy.” ... Borouge Plc is the largest exporter through AD Ports Group. This collaboration builds on a longstanding partnership between the two companies established in 2001, and reflects the shared ambition of AD Ports Group and Borouge Plc to strengthen supply chain resilience and reinforce the UAE’s position as a globally competitive logistics and industrial hub."
Borouge,,,,Exa,AI Ain Farms signs deal with plastics resin supplier Borouge,ecoplasticsinpackaging.com,2026-05-18T00:00:00.000Z,2026-05-18 00:00:00+00:00,,https://ecoplasticsinpackaging.com/resins-raw-materials/ai-ain-farms-goes-local-with-borouge-supply/,"AI Ain Farms signs deal with plastics resin supplier Borouge ... # AI Ain Farms goes local with Borouge supply ... May 18, 2026 ... Hassan Safi (left), group chief executive of Al Ain Farms Group, signs the deal with Hazeem Sultan Al Suwaidi, chief executive of Borouge ... Petrochemical firm Borouge has scaled up its longstanding partnership with Al Ain Farms Group (AAFG) to build up the supply of locally produced resin used in the food manufacturer’s packaging operations. ... According to a memorandum of understanding (MoU), Borouge will supply approximately 3,500 tonnes of HDPE resin annually to support AAFG’s high purity caps and closures business. ... It will also provide development support for AAFG’s expansion into in-house blow moulding. ... The latest agreement is said to bolster the volume currently sourced by AAFG by 600 per cent, underpinning growing packaging operations across its dairy and juice portfolios. ... The partnership also supports the UAE’s wider industrial strategy under Operation 300bn and the Make it in the Emirates initiative, which prioritise local manufacturing, supply chain resilience, and reduced reliance on imports across strategic sectors, the businesses said. ... The company’s collaboration with Borouge supports a ‘wider vertical integration strategy in packaging’, which reportedly began with the installation of its bottle plant in 2018. ... This continued in 2023 with the launch of its preform and cap plant designed to secure quality, cost, and supply. ... The group said it is now progressing on-site HDPE bottle production, alongside new robotic palletisation systems at its ultra-hight treatment plant to reduce manual handling and improve operational precision. ... “Borouge is proud to support the UAE’s leading food producers with locally manufactured resin to help them grow reliably and sustainably,” said Hazeem Sultan Al Suwaidi, Borouge chief executive. ... “Our partnership highlights the strength of UAE-to-UAE industrial collaboration and the critical role that local supply plays in enabling food security and assembling a more resilient national economy.” ... Hassan Safi, group chief executive of AAFG, said local sourcing is fundamental to how the group operates. ... “Our partnership with Borouge demonstrates our commitment to working with UAE-based businesses to support national capability,” Safi said. ... “Our expanded collaboration allows us to strengthen the local supply chain that supports our products. This supports UAE’s in-country value as well as the UAE’s ambition to produce more of what it consumes within its own borders, contributing to local self-sufficiency targets.”"
Borouge,,,,Exa,Borouge International recycling investments 14-05-2026,www.polyestertime.com (Polyestertime),2026-05-13T00:00:00.000Z,2026-05-13 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.polyestertime.com/borouge-international-recycling-investments/,"# Borouge International Expands Global Growth and Recycling Investments By Polyestertime Polyestertime May 14, 2026 May 13, 2026 # Borouge International recycling investments ... ## Borouge International Highlights Global Expansion and Recycling Investments ... Borouge International used the PRSE event to showcase its growing role in the circular plastics economy and its expanding international presence following the merger of Borealis, Borouge and Nova Chemicals. ... Baseley explained that the creation of Borouge International through the merger of Borealis, Borouge and Nova Chemicals gives the business a broader global footprint across Europe, North America and the Middle East. ... According to Baseley, the larger international scale will strengthen the company’s ability to support customers with more advanced circular economy solutions and improved market reach. ... The company also emphasized its continued investment in chemical recycling technologies. Baseley pointed to Borealis’ 10% stake in Dutch recycling company BlueAlp as part of its strategy to process difficult-to-recycle plastic waste that would otherwise end up in landfill sites or incineration facilities. ... Borouge International said investments in both mechanical and chemical recycling will remain central to its long-term sustainability and growth plans as demand increases for recycled plastics and lower-carbon material solutions. ... Borouge International reshapes global polyolefins market with 13.6 million ton capacity"
Borouge,,,,Exa,Borouge International launches GF reinforced PP for structural automotive components,www.specialchem.com,2026-05-11T00:00:00.000Z,2026-05-11 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.specialchem.com/plastics/news/new-gf-reinforced-recycled-pp-for-automotive-components-by-borouge-international,"Borouge International launches GF reinforced PP for structural automotive components ... New GF reinforced recycled PP for automotive components by Borouge International ... Combines 40% PCR polypropylene with 20% glass fiberSupported by a globally aligned manufacturing strategy ... New GF reinforced recycled ... for automotive components by Borouge International ... Last update on May 11, 2026 ... Integrating post-consumer recycled content into structural automotive components without compromising mechanical performance has remained a persistent constraint for manufacturers developing interior parts such as center console carriers and dashboard carriers. A new glass-fiber reinforced polypropylene compound from Borouge International is designed to address that gap directly – and to support compliance with incoming regulatory requirements on recycled material content. ... Borcycle™ GE2331SY combines 40% post-consumer recycled polypropylene with 20% glass fiber reinforcement. According to Borouge International, the compound delivers the mechanical strength required for demanding interior structural applications. It is already in use by several original equipment manufacturers (OEMs) for high-end components, including center console carriers and dashboard carriers, demonstrating validated suitability for premium vehicle programs. The grade forms part of the Borcycle™ M portfolio – Borouge International's range of high-performance mechanically recycled polyolefin solutions. ... The compound is positioned to support manufacturers in preparing for the upcoming EU End of Life Vehicles (ELV) regulation, which mandates the use of recycled materials from post-consumer waste streams. ... Elisabeth Dedl, global head of Marketing, Mobility, Borealis Polymere GmbH, commented, ""The automotive industry is striving to reduce its environmental footprint, and as a trusted partner, we are helping to make that possible. Borcycle demonstrates that recycled content and high performance can go hand in hand. By enabling automakers to integrate circular materials into their vehicles, we can advance sustainable mobility on a global scale."" ... Produced at Borouge International facilities across Europe, China, and the United States using locally sourced raw materials, the grade is supported by a globally aligned manufacturing strategy designed to deliver consistent quality at scale, strengthen local supply chains, and reduce transport-related emissions. ... Celia Chen, vice president Mobility of Borouge, said, ""With Borcycle GE2331SY we're helping our global customers meet growing demand for sustainable mobility solutions. Developed in collaboration with industry partners, it shows how circular innovation can deliver the performance required to meet real market needs."" ... Borcycle™ GE2331SY has been selected as a finalist in the Automotive, Electrical or Electronic Product of the Year category at the Plastics Recycling Awards Europe (PRAE) 2026. The grade builds on the Borcycle™ GD3600SY, introduced in 2024 with 65% PCR content, extending the Borcycle M portfolio's global reach. ... Borouge International was established in March 2026 through a transaction between XRG – ADNOC's international investment arm – and OMV, consolidating Borouge plc, Borealis GmbH, and NOVA Chemicals Corporation into a single global platform."
Borouge,,,,Exa,"Al Ain Farms, Borouge expand UAE packaging resin deal",www.packaging-gateway.com (Vidhya Edwards Munnangi),2026-05-11T00:00:00.000Z,2026-05-11 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.packaging-gateway.com/news/al-ain-farms-borouge-packaging-resin/,"Al Ain Farms, Borouge expand UAE packaging resin deal ... Al Ain Farms Group and petrochemicals company Borouge have signed a memorandum of understanding (MoU) to expand supplies of locally made resin used in the food producer’s packaging operations, according to a press release posted on Zawya. ... Signed during the Make it in the Emirates event, the MoU states that Borouge will supply 3,50tpa of high-density polyethylene resin for Al Ain Farms Group’s caps and closures unit. ... The petrochemicals company will also support the group’s planned move into in-house blow moulding. ... Borouge CEO Hazeem Sultan Al Suwaidi said: “Borouge is proud to support the UAE’s leading food producers with locally manufactured resin solutions to help them grow reliably and sustainably. ... “Our partnership highlights the strength of UAE-to-UAE industrial collaboration and the critical role that local supply plays in enabling food security and assembling a more resilient national economy.” ... The new arrangement lifts the volume sourced by Al Ain Farms Group by 600% from current levels, supporting packaging activity across its dairy and juice lines. ... The partnership forms part of Al Ain Farms Group’s broader push to bring more packaging processes in-house. ... That process started with the installation of a bottle plant in 2018 and continued in 2023 with the opening of a preform and cap facility aimed at improving control over quality, costs and supply. ... Al Ain Farms Group CEO Hassan Safi said: “Local sourcing is fundamental to how we operate, and our partnership with Borouge demonstrates our commitment to working with UAE-based businesses to support national capability. ... “Our expanded collaboration allows us to strengthen the local supply chain that supports our products. This supports the UAE’s in-country value, as well as the UAE’s ambition to produce more of what it consumes within its own borders, contributing to local self-sufficiency targets.” ... The group is also advancing on-site high-density polyethylene (HDPE) bottle manufacturing and introducing robotic palletisation systems at its ultra-high temperature (UHT) plant to reduce manual handling and improve operating accuracy. ... The companies said the arrangement also fits with the UAE’s Operation 300bn programme and the Make it in the Emirates initiative, which focuses on domestic manufacturing, stronger supply chains and cutting dependence on imports in strategic sectors."
Borouge,,,,Linkup,World's 4th-largest polyolefins producer enters transformative phase,TradingView News,2026-07-23T00:00:00Z,2026-07-23 00:00:00+00:00,,"https://www.tradingview.com/news/reuters.com,2026-07-23:newsml_Zaw8VM0hR:0-world-s-4th-largest-polyolefins-producer-enters-transformative-phase/","Borouge secured operational control and marketing rights for Borouge 4 under an agreement with ADNOC and OMV, forming the world's fourth-largest polyolefins producer. In Q1 2026, Borouge reported revenues of $1.2 billion and net profit of $156 million despite logistical challenges."
Borouge,,,,Linkup,Borouge reports 23% rise in second-quarter net profit as Ruwais operations recover,Energy Connects,2026-07-01T00:00:00Z,2026-07-01 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.energyconnects.com/news/utilities/2026/july/borouge-reports-23-rise-in-q2-net-profit-as-ruwais-operations-recover/,"Borouge Plc reported a 23% increase in net profit to $191 million in Q2 2026, overcoming disruption at its Ruwais complex by using alternative logistics routes. The Borouge 4 expansion project advanced with the new XLPE plant reaching commercialisation and delivering its first materials to customers."
Borouge,,,,Perplexity,Borouge Delivers 23% Increase in Net Profit in Q2 2026 Despite Regional Disruption,Borouge,2026-07-31T00:00:00Z,2026-07-31 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.borouge.com/en/media/Pages/News/Borouge_news.aspx,"Borouge reported Q2 2026 revenue of $1.4 billion, adjusted EBITDA of $401 million, and net profit of $191 million, supported by sales volumes of 0.9 million tonnes. The release emphasized operational resilience, supply chain agility, and financial discipline despite adverse regional developments, making it relevant for assessing counterparty stability, logistics risk, and margin resilience."
Borouge,,,,Perplexity,Borouge and AD Ports Group Collaborate to Explore Alternative Export Hub on the UAE East Coast,Borouge Investor Relations,2026-07-31T00:00:00Z,2026-07-31 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.borouge.com/en/investor-relations/Pages/announcements.aspx,Borouge announced a collaboration with AD Ports Group to explore an alternative export hub on the UAE east coast. This is relevant to supply chain risk management because it suggests contingency planning for export routing and resilience against disruption at existing logistics corridors.
Borouge,,,,Perplexity,"Borouge International Officially Inaugurates New Mumbai Office, Strengthening Presence in High-Growth Market",Borouge International,2026-07-28T00:00:00Z,2026-07-28 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.borougeinternational.com/news-and-insights/borouge-international-officially-inaugurates-new-mumbai-office-strengthening-presence-in-high,"Borouge International opened a new Mumbai office to deepen its presence in India, one of its fastest-growing markets. For procurement and supplier strategy, the move signals a stronger commercial and customer-support footprint in a key demand center, which may improve responsiveness on pricing, service, and supply coordination."
Borouge,,,,Perplexity,World's 4th-Largest Polyolefins Producer Enters Transformative Phase,Reuters via TradingView,2026-07-23T00:00:00Z,2026-07-23 00:00:00+00:00,,"https://www.tradingview.com/news/reuters.com,2026-07-23:newsml_Zaw8VM0hR:0-world-s-4th-largest-polyolefins-producer-enters-transformative-phase/","Reuters described Borouge as entering a transformative phase, noting its record 2025 performance and highlighting the strategic context around the company’s evolution. For procurement strategy, the article is useful as market context on Borouge’s scale, financial strength, and longer-term positioning in polyolefins supply."
