sinesses to transition to clean power through long-term energy solutions. ... The company has commissioned more than 3.6 GW renewable energy capacity across India and overseas, while more than 800 MW of wind-solar hybrid projects are under development. The company has also executed battery energy storage system (BESS) projects totalling more than 50 MW capacity and aims to expand its installed base to 9 GW by 2031. ... Some of its biggest clients include Meta, Walmart, Unilever, Hyundai, Tata Motors, ITC, Nestle, TCS, Wipro, UltraTech Cement, Skoda, and D-Mart.Norfund currently holds a 30% stake in Fourth Partner Energy, while TPG Rise Fund owns 10%. IFC, ADB and DEG together own about 55%, with the founders retaining the remaining 4%, according to sources.Spokespeople at Fourth Partner, Norfund, DEG and BII declined to comment. TPG, IFC and ADB didn't respond to email queries.In 2021, Norway-owned PE fund Norfund invested $100 million in Fourth Partner Energy to acquire a majority stake, while existing shareholder, The Rise Fund, TPG's global impact investing platform, infused $25 million, adding to its $70 million investment in 2018.In 2024, a consortium comprising IFC, ADB and DEG invested $275 million in Fourth Partner Energy.Fourth Partner Energy reported 37% rise in consolidated revenue at $101.7 million in FY25, compared to $75.6 million in FY24, per the latest available data from Tracxn. EBITDA more than doubled to $56 million from $25.3 million. Despite the improved operating performance, the company posted a net loss of $32.5 million in FY25, though narrower than $41.9 million loss in FY24.India's C&I renewable energy market is dominated by a handful of specialised developers, including Fourth Partner Energy, CleanMax, Gentari, Avaada Energy and Ampin Energy Transition. This segment is expected to see renewable energy (RE) capacity rise to 57 GW by fiscal 2028 from 40 GW expected by the end of fiscal 2026, Crisil Ratings said in a recent report."
HPCL Mittal Energy,,,,Exa,Private equity firm Creador in talks to buy into generic drug co Leeford Health - The Economic Times,economictimes.indiatimes.com (Reghu Balakrishnan),2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-03 00:00:00+00:00,,https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/industry/healthcare/biotech/pharmaceuticals/private-equity-firm-creador-in-talks-to-buy-into-generic-drug-co-leeford-health/articleshow/132840847.cms,"Private equity firm Creador in talks to buy into generic drug co Leeford Health - The Economic Times FEATURED FUNDS★★★★★Motilal Oswal Midcap Fund Direct-Growth5Y Return22.91 % Invest Now Business News› Industry› Healthcare/Biotech› Pharmaceuticals›Private equity firm Creador in talks to buy into generic drug co Leeford Health ##### The Economic Times daily newspaper is available online now. # Private equity firm Creador in talks to buy into generic drug co Leeford Health SECTIONS Private equity firm Creador in talks to buy into generic drug co Leeford Health By , ET BureauLast Updated: Aug 04, 2026, 01:04:00 AM IST Rate Story Follow us Share Font Size AbcSmall AbcMedium AbcLarge Save Synopsis Private equity firm Creador is in advanced talks to acquire a stake. The firm expects to invest ₹800 crore in Leeford Healthcare. This acquisition would value the generic drugmaker at ₹8,000-₹8,500 crore. Leeford Healthcare is a diversified pharmaceutical and wellness company. This deal marks the company's first ownership dilution to an external investor. IANS Kuala Lumpur-based private equity firm Creador is in advanced talks to acquire around a 10% stake in Ludhiana-based Leeford Healthcare. Mumbai: Kuala Lumpur-based private equity firm Creador is in advanced talks to acquire about a 10% stake in Ludhiana-based Leeford Healthcare, said people familiar with the matter. Creador is expected to invest around ₹800 crore in Leeford, valuing the generic drugmaker at ₹8,000-₹8,500 crore ($900 million), the people said.Incorporated in 2006 and promoted by Jiwan Lal Gupta, Leeford Healthcare is a diversified pharmaceutical and wellness company with a portfolio of more than 2,000 products across prescription medicines, generic formulations, nutraceuticals, personal care and specialty therapies.The proposed investment follows Creador's November 2025 purchase of a nearly 7% stake in homegrown drugmaker La Renon Healthcare for about ₹770 crore. Leeford is currently wholly-owned by the promoter Gupta family. Chairman Jiwan Lal Gupta holds about 88% stake, while the remainder is held by family members Amit Kumar Gupta, Neha Gupta, Sarika Gupta, and others. ET Bureau A Kuala Lumpur-based company’s proposed 10% stake purchase would value Leeford at around ₹8,500 crore. (Infographic Image) If consummated, the deal will mark the first dilution of ownership by the nearly two-decade-old company in favour of an external investor. Leeford and Creador didn't respond to email queries. The company has delivered steady financial growth in the past five years, clocking 5% revenue growth at $204.7 million in FY25, and 27% rise in net profit at $28.4 million. This was helped by a 24% increase in Ebitda at $40.5 million, according to Tracxn data.Leeford runs multiple businesses including its core healthcare division, wellness division, Cosmacia personal care business, and Leeford Medi Sciences, a super-specialty business focused on nephrology and urology. Leeford also has a growing orthopaedics and mobility aids business and exports products to several international markets, according to the company website. Under this division, it sells cervical collars, kneecaps, lumbar belts, posture correctors, wrist supports, and sports recovery aids. ### Live Events In fiscal 2014, the company launched beauty products under its own brand names-Meglow and Alite. Leeford has 10 manufacturing facilities producing oral solids, liquids, topical formulations, cosmetics, and Ayurvedic products, supported by an inhouse formulation and development centre in Himachal Pradesh. Add as a Reliable and Trusted News Source Add Now! (You can now subscribe to our Economic Times WhatsApp channel) Read More News on (Catch all the Business News, Breaking News and Latest News Updates on The Economic Times.) Subscribe to The Economic Times Prime and read the ET ePaper online. ...moreless (You can now subscribe to our Economic Times WhatsApp channel) Read More News o"
HPCL Mittal Energy,,,,Exa,"Oil PSUs' June quarter losses miss govt's ₹75,000 crore estimate by wide margin - The Economic Times",economictimes.indiatimes.com (Sanjeev Choudhary),2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-02 00:00:00+00:00,,https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/industry/energy/oil-gas/oil-psus-june-quarter-losses-miss-govts-75000-crore-estimate-by-wide-margin/articleshow/132813264.cms,"Oil PSUs' June quarter losses miss govt's ₹75,000 crore estimate by wide margin - The Economic Times FEATURED FUNDS★★★★★Motilal Oswal Midcap Fund Direct-Growth5Y Return22.91 % Invest Now Business News› Industry› Energy› Oil & Gas›Oil PSUs' June quarter losses miss govt's ₹75,000 crore estimate by wide margin ##### The Economic Times daily newspaper is available online now. # Oil PSUs' June quarter losses miss govt's ₹75,000 crore estimate by wide margin SECTIONS Oil PSUs' June quarter losses miss govt's ₹75,000 crore estimate by wide margin By , ET BureauLast Updated: Aug 03, 2026, 05:00:00 AM IST Rate Story Follow us Share Font Size AbcSmall AbcMedium AbcLarge Save Synopsis State-run oil firms reported combined net losses of ₹18,149 crore in the June quarter. This figure was significantly lower than the government's initial projection of nearly ₹75,000 crore. Industry executives cited retail price increases and central tax reductions as key cushioning factors. Higher LPG prices also helped offset cooking gas sales losses for these companies. The divergence highlights the political sensitivity surrounding fuel price adjustments and public perception. Listen to this article in summarized format Listen Loading... × × Subscribe to Unlock AI Briefing and Premium Content New Year Offer 24 Hours Left Subscribe Now Already a member? Sign In ### What's Included - Exclusive Stories - Daily ePaper Access - Smart Market Tools - Curated Investment Ideas - Ad-lite Experience - Subscription ET Bureau New Delhi: The impact of supply disruptions due to the Iran war on state-run oil marketing companies' financial performance has been far milder than initially projected, with IndianOil, Bharat Petroleum, and Hindustan Petroleum reporting combined net losses of ₹18,149 crore in the June quarter, well below the government's estimate of nearly ₹75,000 crore.Oil minister Hardeep Singh Puri said July 2 that three firms incurred losses of ₹74,781 crore in June quarter.Also Read: Petrol sales rise 8.6%, diesel demand jumps 9.4% in July; LPG, jet fuel consumption declines Pump pricesPuri was responding to questions on whether the government would cut pump prices after global crude rates retreated from their peaks following a temporary Iran-US truce. ### Live Events Industry executives said a Rs 8 per litre increase in retail fuel prices, coupled with about Rs 10 per litre in tax reductions on petrol and diesel by the Centre, significantly cushioned the impact of the crude price spike due to the war. Higher LPG prices also helped companies to offset losses on cooking gas sales. While the total quarterly losses of Rs 18,149 crore marks a sharp reversal from these companies’ combined net profits of Rs 16,184 crore a year earlier, it reflects only a fraction of the government’s projection. ET Bureau The wide divergence between the actual losses and the government’s estimate underscores the political sensitivity around fuel prices, said industry executives. Raising pump prices or even resisting demands for price cuts when crude eases is politically difficult. As a result, the government often adopts a more pessimistic narrative on oil companies’ finances to build public acceptance for higher retail prices, the executives said.“It’s about political communication,” one executive said.Also Read: Indian Oil reassesses ₹33,000 crore Tamil Nadu refinery, eyes petrochemicals complex https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/industry/energy/oil-gas/indian-oil-reassesses-33000-crore-tamil-nadu-refinery-eyes-petrochemicals-complex/articleshow/132812527.cms Another executive said the government’s messaging shifted noticeably in May after state elections. During the early weeks of the conflict, when elections were underway in some states, officials largely avoided highlighting losses at oil companies. Once the elections got over, the emphasis on companies’ financial stress got more pronounced.Ahead of the fuel price increases, Sujata Sh"
HPCL Mittal Energy,,,,Exa,"Indian Oil reassesses ₹33,000 crore Tamil Nadu refinery, eyes petrochemicals complex - The Economic Times",economictimes.indiatimes.com (Sanjeev Choudhary),2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-02 00:00:00+00:00,,https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/industry/energy/oil-gas/indian-oil-reassesses-33000-crore-tamil-nadu-refinery-eyes-petrochemicals-complex/articleshow/132812527.cms,"Indian Oil reassesses ₹33,000 crore Tamil Nadu refinery, eyes petrochemicals complex - The Economic Times FEATURED FUNDS★★★★★Motilal Oswal Midcap Fund Direct-Growth5Y Return22.99 % Invest Now Business News› Industry› Energy› Oil & Gas›Indian Oil reassesses ₹33,000 crore Tamil Nadu refinery, eyes petrochemicals complex ##### The Economic Times daily newspaper is available online now. # Indian Oil reassesses ₹33,000 crore Tamil Nadu refinery, eyes petrochemicals complex SECTIONS Indian Oil reassesses ₹33,000 crore Tamil Nadu refinery, eyes petrochemicals complex By , ET BureauLast Updated: Aug 05, 2026, 02:48:00 PM IST Rate Story Follow us Share Font Size AbcSmall AbcMedium AbcLarge Save Synopsis Indian Oil Corp is reassessing its Tamil Nadu refinery project due to economic viability concerns. The company is now exploring a standalone petrochemicals complex instead of the original refinery. This shift reflects industry trends favoring integrated refining and petrochemical ventures. Standalone petrochemicals complexes require lower investments and offer higher long-term returns. Listen to this article in summarized format Listen Loading... × × Subscribe to Unlock AI Briefing and Premium Content New Year Offer 24 Hours Left Subscribe Now Already a member? Sign In ### What's Included - Exclusive Stories - Daily ePaper Access - Smart Market Tools - Curated Investment Ideas - Ad-lite Experience - Subscription ReutersIndian Oil Corporation New Delhi: Indian Oil Corp is reassessing its proposed ₹33,000 crore greenfield refinery in Tamil Nadu after an internal evaluation raised concerns about the project's economic viability, said people familiar with the matter. The state-run refiner is instead exploring the possibility of converting the venture into a standalone petrochemicals complex, the people said.Indian Oil's board approved in January 2021 the construction of a 9 mtpa greenfield refinery at Nagapattinam in Tamil Nadu at an estimated cost of ₹29,361 crore. The project was to be developed through a joint venture, with Indian Oil and Chennai Petroleum Corporation Ltd(CPCL) each holding 25%, and financial institutions 50%.Also Read: Petrol, diesel sales soar on weak monsoon rains, LPG demand shifts to piped gas Three years later, in March 2024, Indian Oil revised the project cost to ₹33,023 crore and approved raising its stake in the JV to 75%, with CPCL holding the remaining 25%. The companies already have the land for the project.Indian Oil also sought financial support from the Centre for the project, but it wasn't approved, the people said. ### Live Events ET Bureau Standalone complex needs lower investments, gives higher long-term returns Industry executives said standalone refineries are becoming increasingly difficult to justify due to demand uncertainty, high capital costs and long payback periods. This is leading companies to increasingly integrate refining projects with petrochemicals. A standalone petrochemicals complex would require lower investments besides offering higher long-term returns than a conventional refinery, the people said. Greenfield refinery projects also typically rely on government support, such as tax incentives or subsidised land from the Centre or state governments, to make their economics viable. Also Read: IndianOil set for board revamp as govt proposes four MD positions BPCL, meanwhile, is moving ahead with its proposed 9-11 MTPA greenfield refinery-cum-petrochemical complex in Andhra Pradesh, backed by a generous incentive package from the state government. The project, conceived just two years ago, is expected to cost about ₹1 lakh crore, with likely participation from Saudi Aramco. HPCL recently commissioned a 9 mtpa greenfield refinery-cum-petrochemical project in Rajasthan. Add as a Reliable and Trusted News Source Add Now! (You can now subscribe to our Economic Times WhatsApp channel) ( Originally published on Aug 02, 2026 ) Read More News on (Catc"
HPCL Mittal Energy,,,,Exa,"Patanjali bets on insurance, green energy in unrelated diversifications beyond Ayurveda, consumer goods | Business News - The Indian Express",indianexpress.com,2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-02 00:00:00+00:00,,https://indianexpress.com/article/business/patanjali-bets-on-insurance-green-energy-in-unrelated-diversifications-beyond-ayurveda-consumer-goods-10814361/,"Patanjali bets on insurance, green energy in unrelated diversifications beyond Ayurveda, consumer goods | Business News - The Indian Express skip to content # Patanjali bets on insurance, green energy in unrelated diversifications beyond Ayurveda, consumer goods ## The transaction, valued at around Rs 4,500 crore, marks Patanjali’s entry into the general insurance business at a time when the sector is witnessing regulatory push and expanding health and motor insurance markets. Baba Ramdev's Patanjali Group has made an aggressive entry into India’s renewable energy sector. Make us preferred source on Google Whatsapp twitter Facebook Reddit PRINT From ayurveda products and packaged foods to general insurance and renewable energy, Baba Ramdev-promoted Patanjali Group is expanding into sectors completely unrelated to its mainstay consumer goods business, signalling a broader diversification strategy. The Insurance Regulatory and Development Authority of India (IRDAI) has recently approved the acquisition of Magma General Insurance by Patanjali Ayurved and the Dharampal Satyapal (DS) Group. The transaction, valued at around Rs 4,500 crore, marks Patanjali’s entry into the general insurance business at a time when the sector is witnessing regulatory push and expanding health and motor insurance markets. At around the same time, Haridwar-headquartered Patanjali Group has made an aggressive entry into India’s renewable energy sector, emerging as a bidder in multiple standalone Battery Energy Storage System (BESS) tenders. Expand Advertisement Aug 03: Latest News 01 Giant-slayer Sakshi makes her left jab count to arrive in style in 51kg 02 Pregnant with a Copper-T: What it could mean for you and baby 03 Mirabai Chanu: Weightlifting legend who wins medals, helps teammates win too 04 This painter keeps Pune’s wadas alive – one portrait at a time 05 Inside Saudi Crown Prince MBS’ phone call with Trump before Iran strike pause Advertisement Live Blog Bankipur, Datia, Manjalpur By-Election Results Live Updates: Prashant Kishor leads by over 5,000 votes in Bankipur, BJP loses lead in Madhya Pradesh10 minutes ago Spider-Man Brand New Day Box Office Collection Day 4 Updates: Tom Holland film eyes $1 billion as it smashes records; earns Rs 300 cr in India15 minutes ago India news Live Updates, 3 August 2026: Open to probe, says CJP’s Abhijeet Dipke over study-in-US questions16 minutes ago NEET Protest Case Hearing Live Updates: Supreme Court likely to constitute SIT17 minutes ago Parliament Monsoon Session 2026 Day 11 Live: Opposition protests stall both Houses, 2 Bills tabled in Lok Sabha28 minutes ago Latest Comment Post Comment Read Comments Edition SUBSCRIBE FOR FULL ACCESS Install IE App for better experience Featured Trending Topics News Multimedia Follow Us Link Subscription"
HPCL Mittal Energy,,,,Exa,"BPCL launches Bharatgas Lite ZIP, a 10kg fibre composite LPG cylinder - The Hindu",www.thehindu.com (The Hindu Bureau),2026-08-01T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-01 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.thehindu.com/news/cities/Mangalore/bharat-petroleum-launches-bharatgas-lite-zip-a-10kg-fibre-composite-lpg-cylinder/article71293923.ece,"BPCL launches Bharatgas Lite ZIP, a 10kg fibre composite LPG cylinder - The Hindu ... # Bharat Petroleum launches Bharatgas Lite ZIP, a 10kg fibre composite LPG cylinder ... ## Bharatgas Lite ZIP is BPCL’s next generation of customer-centric LPG solutions, combining advantages of Bharatgas Lite cylinder with a digitally enabled premium service model focused on speed, convenience and superior customer experience ... Updated - August 02, 2026 02:18 pm IST - MANGALURU ... Bharat Petroleum Corporation Limited (BPCL) launched Bharatgas Lite ZIP – a premium Free Trade LPG (FTL) with instant connection and express delivery – in Mangaluru on July 31. The Lite ZIP comes with a 10kg fibre composite LPG cylinder. ... Bharatgas Lite ZIP is BPCL’s next generation of customer-centric LPG solutions, combining advantages of the lightweight composite Bharatgas Lite cylinder with a digitally enabled premium service model focused on speed, convenience and superior customer experience. ... Under Lite ZIP, customers can obtain a new LPG connection quickly through a simplified onboarding process while the cylinders are delivered within four hours of booking. The cylinders have lightweight design for easy handling, corrosion-free composite body, vsible LPG level through the transparent cylinder body, among other things. ... Besides Mangaluru, BPCL launched the product in 29 other cities on July 31 while it plans to cover 70 more cities in 24 States by August 15. Subsequently, Lite ZIP will be rolled out across all other major cities in the country. ... The rollout at Mangaluru was jointly organised by Anupama Gas Distributors and Kamadhenu Gas Agency at the former’s premises. Marking the inauguration, the first batch of 10kg composite cylinders was handed over to 15 customers. ... BPCL Territory Manager (LPG) Sandeep Kumar Raina emphasised the Corporation’s commitment to delivering customer-centric, technology-enabled solutions that enhance everyday life. Kamadhenu Agency proprietor Dinesh Kumar Shetty, Anupama Distributors proprietors Amrutha and Suresh Kumar, were among those present at the event. ... As on July 31, the cost of the 10 kg cylinder was ₹4,812."
HPCL Mittal Energy,,,,Exa,"Avaada gets ₹12,800-cr loan to fund projects in Gujarat and Maharashtra - The Economic Times",economictimes.indiatimes.com (Joel Rebello),2026-07-30T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-30 00:00:00+00:00,,https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/industry/renewables/avaada-gets-12800-cr-loan-to-fund-projects-in-gujarat-and-maharashtra/articleshow/132750124.cms,"Avaada gets ₹12,800-cr loan to fund projects in Gujarat and Maharashtra - The Economic Times FEATURED FUNDS★★★★★Motilal Oswal Midcap Fund Direct-Growth5Y Return23.2 % Invest Now Business News› Industry› Renewables›Avaada gets ₹12,800-cr loan to fund projects in Gujarat and Maharashtra ##### The Economic Times daily newspaper is available online now. # Avaada gets ₹12,800-cr loan to fund projects in Gujarat and Maharashtra SECTIONS Avaada gets ₹12,800-cr loan to fund projects in Gujarat and Maharashtra By , ET BureauLast Updated: Jul 31, 2026, 02:06:00 AM IST Rate Story Follow us Share Font Size AbcSmall AbcMedium AbcLarge Save Synopsis Avaada Group secured ₹12,800 crore financing from three major lenders. This funding will support 2.15 GW of renewable energy projects in Gujarat and Maharashtra. These new projects will commence commercial operations starting March 2027. The financing covers hybrid renewable energy and solar capacity developments. The loans have an aggregate tenure of 21.5 years. Listen to this article in summarized format Listen Loading... × × Subscribe to Unlock AI Briefing and Premium Content New Year Offer 24 Hours Left Subscribe Now Already a member? Sign In ### What's Included - Exclusive Stories - Daily ePaper Access - Smart Market Tools - Curated Investment Ideas - Ad-lite Experience - Subscription IANSRepresentative image. Mumbai: Renewable energy company Avaada Group has secured about ₹12,800 crore ($1.3 billion) from State Bank of India(SBI), Canara Bank and Rural Electrification Corp(REC) to finance projects in Gujarat and Maharashtra. This is one of the largest term-financing deals in the country this year.Avaada founder and chairman Vineet Mittal confirmed to ET that the three lenders have started the loan disbursement for a 1.35 GW hybrid renewable energy projects and 800 MW of solar capacity in Gujarat and Maharashtra. These projects will begin commercial output starting March 2027. ""These are new projects which are coming up and which have ready power purchasing agreements (PPAs) signed,"" Mittal said. ""This is all fresh funding for these projects, some of which will start in March 2027 and March 2028 in Gujarat and Maharashtra.""The projects are across four special purpose vehicles (SPVs), with a total renewable energy portfolio of 2.15 GW. That includes 1.35 MW of hybrid renewable energy capacity and 800 MW of solar capacity contracted with Maharashtra State Electricity Distribution (MSEDCL) and Gujarat Urja Vikas Nigam (GUVNL). Mittal said the aggregate tenure of the loans is 21.5 years. He declined to give the pricing of the loans.""All these four SPVs are 100% subsidiaries of the group. They have received the finest pricing possible for this term loan. The three lenders are part of the loan as we speak today,"" he said. ""It is possible that these lenders downsell the loan to a wider section of banks and financial institutions in the next few months."" ### Live Events ET could not ascertain the demarcation of the loan among the three current lenders. SBI, Canara and REC did not respond to mailed queries seeking comment. Add as a Reliable and Trusted News Source Add Now! (You can now subscribe to our Economic Times WhatsApp channel) Read More News on (Catch all the Business News, Breaking News and Latest News Updates on The Economic Times.) Subscribe to The Economic Times Prime and read the ET ePaper online. ...moreless (You can now subscribe to our Economic Times WhatsApp channel) Read More News on (Catch all the Business News, Breaking News and Latest News Updates on The Economic Times.) Subscribe to The Economic Times Prime and read the ET ePaper online. ...moreless Find this comment offensive? Choose your reason below and click on the Report button. This will alert our moderators to take action Name Reason for reporting: Foul languageSlanderousInciting hatred against a certain communityOthers Your Reason has been Reported to the admin. X #"
HPCL Mittal Energy,,,,Exa,Mahindra Last Mile raises ₹322 crore at unicorn valuation - The Economic Times,economictimes.indiatimes.com (Shally Mohile),2026-07-30T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-30 00:00:00+00:00,,https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/industry/auto/auto-news/mahindra-last-mile-raises-322-crore-at-unicorn-valuation/articleshow/132748127.cms,"Mahindra Last Mile raises ₹322 crore at unicorn valuation - The Economic Times FEATURED FUNDS★★★★★Motilal Oswal Midcap Fund Direct-Growth5Y Return23.19 % Invest Now Business News› Industry› Auto› Auto News›Mahindra Last Mile raises ₹322 crore at unicorn valuation ##### The Economic Times daily newspaper is available online now. # Mahindra Last Mile raises ₹322 crore at unicorn valuation SECTIONS Mahindra Last Mile raises ₹322 crore at unicorn valuation By , ET BureauLast Updated: Jul 30, 2026, 11:45:00 PM IST Rate Story Follow us Share Font Size AbcSmall AbcMedium AbcLarge Save Synopsis Mahindra Last Mile Mobility has achieved unicorn status after a substantial funding round. This investment solidifies their leadership in India's electric commercial vehicle market. The company plans an initial public offering in the second half of this financial year. Their electric three-wheelers have seen significant sales growth and market penetration. Mahindra aims to deploy one million electric vehicles on Indian roads by 2031. AgenciesRepresentative Image Mumbai: Mahindra Last Mile Mobility Ltd (MLMML) achieved unicorn status after raising about ₹322 crore in a funding round led by global impact investor Lightrock at a valuation of ₹10,822 crore.The Mahindra and Mahindra subsidiary manufactures electric, CNG, petrol, diesel three and four-wheeler passenger and cargo vehicles.The round also saw participation from existing investors International Finance Corporation (IFC) and the India-Japan Fund (IJF), managed by the National Investment and Infrastructure Fund (NIIF), underscoring rising investor appetite for India's rapidly expanding electric commercial vehicle market. The fundraise comes at a time when electrification in the L5 electric three-wheeler segment has accelerated sharply, with penetration rising to about 40% from 12% in the past two years. MLML currently has around 40% market share in the category, making it the country's largest electric commercial vehicle maker for the fourth consecutive year.The company said sales of its electric three-wheelers have grown sixfold in four years. Volumes surged 85% year-on-year in the June quarter of FY27. It also became the first electric commercial vehicle maker in India to cross cumulative sales of 100,000 electric three-wheelers in FY26. ### Live Events ""This investment brings us closer to our goal of deploying one million EVs on Indian roads by 2031 and solidifies our leadership in the electric commercial vehicle sector,"" said Anish Shah, Mahindra Group CEO and Managing Director. The group has identified the last-mile mobility business as one of its ""growth gems"" and plans to launch MLML’s initial public offering (IPO) in the second half of this financial year, Shah said during M&M’s post-earnings media interaction Thursday. The latest funding round is expected to further strengthen the business ahead of the proposed listing.Rajesh Jejurikar, executive director and CEO at M&M’s auto and farm business, said the investment reflects investor confidence in the business and the structural shift underway in electric three-wheelers. The company said its vehicles have cumulatively clocked more than six billion electric kilometres. Add as a Reliable and Trusted News Source Add Now! (You can now subscribe to our Economic Times WhatsApp channel) Read More News on (Catch all the Business News, Breaking News and Latest News Updates on The Economic Times.) Subscribe to The Economic Times Prime and read the ET ePaper online. ...moreless (You can now subscribe to our Economic Times WhatsApp channel) Read More News on (Catch all the Business News, Breaking News and Latest News Updates on The Economic Times.) Subscribe to The Economic Times Prime and read the ET ePaper online. ...moreless Find this comment offensive? Choose your reason below and click on the Report button. This will alert our moderators to take action Name Reason for reporting: Foul languageSland"
HPCL Mittal Energy,,,,Exa,"PGCIL, EnerGrid formally acquire project SPVs of ISTS-TBCB schemes - Your Gateway to Power Transmission & Distribution",www.tndindia.com (T&D India),2026-07-30T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-30 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.tndindia.com/pgcil-energrid-formally-acquire-project-spvs-of-ists-tbcb-schemes/,"S-T ... Power Grid Corporation of India Ltd (PGCIL) and EnerGrid, on July 29, 2026, formally acquired project SPVs of ISTS schemes that the two developers had won under the tariff-based competitive bidding (TBCB) mechanism. ... Project SPV: Bhadla Ramgarh Power Transmission Ltd Winner: Power Grid Corporation of India Ltd (PGCIL) Scheme: Augmentation at Bhadla-III, Ramgarh PS and Kanpur (PG) Project location: Rajasthan, Uttar Pradesh Winning tariff: Rs.1193.45 million per year Project cost: Rs.1,035 crore (estimated) Project scope: Augmentation of the 400/220kV Bhadla-III Pooling Station, 765/400kV Ramgarh Pooling Station and 765/400kV Kanpur Pooling Station, along with associated works. Completion: January ... Project SPV: Shongtong Power Transmission Ltd Winner: Energrid (through “Terralight Solar Energy Tinwari Pvt Ltd”) Scheme: Transmission system for evacuation of power from Shongton Karcham HEP (450 MW) and Tidong HEP (150 MW) Project location: Himachal Pradesh Winning tariff: Rs.5426.32 million per year Project cost: Rs.2,286 crore (estimated) Project scope: Setting up of 400/220kV Jhangi GIS Pooling Station in Himachal Pradesh, 400kV Jhangi PS to Wangtoo double-circuit line of 50 kms, 400kV Panchkula to Wangtoo line of 175 kms, along with other associated works. Completion: By January 2029 (30 months from SPV transfer date) ... As reported by tndindia.com on April 8, 2026, five bidders were shortlisted for the Shongtong scheme, which, incidentally, a case of re-bidding. In the first round of bidding initiated in June 2023, PGCIL was selected as the winning developer and had also received the letter of intent, before the bidding process was annulled. ... The Shongtong scheme is the second ISTS-TBCB scheme acquired by EnerGrid. In February 2026, EnerGrid, through its subsidiary “Enerica 1 Infra Pvt Ltd,” acquired“Morena 1 SEZ Transmission Ltd.” ... Solar Energy Tinwari ... Ltd,” in turn ... Infra Pvt Ltd ... (EnerGrid ... EnerGrid is a new investment platform co-promoted by IndiGrid Infrastructure Trust (IndiGrid), British International Investment (BII) and the Norwegian Climate Investment Fund (Norfund). IndiGrid would henceforth be bidding for power transmission and BESS projects, under TBCB, through EnerGrid, and not by itself. ... PGCIL, in the coming weeks, is set to acquire project SPVs for two ISTS schemes, housed under “Fatehgarh II Transmission Ltd” and “Krishnagiri REZ Transmission Ltd,” for which the Central PSU has already received the letters of intent earlier in July 2026."
HPCL Mittal Energy,,,,Exa,LPG under-recovery on cylinders narrowed to Rs 490 in July from Rs 680 in June: HPCL CMD,psuwatch.com (Shalini Sharma),2026-07-29T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-29 00:00:00+00:00,,https://psuwatch.com/newsupdates/lpg-under-recovery-on-cylinders-narrowed-to-rs-490-in-july-from-rs-680-in-june-hpcl-cmd,"LPG under-recovery on cylinders narrowed to Rs 490 in July from Rs 680 in June: HPCL CMD ... # LPG under-recovery on cylinders narrowed to Rs 490 in July from Rs 680 in June: HPCL CMD ... HPCL's LPG cylinder under-recovery narrowed to Rs 490 in July from Rs 680 in June, its CMD told investors ... LPG under-recovery on cylinders narrowed to Rs 490 in July from Rs 680 in June: HPCL CMDVikas Kaushal takes over as Chairman and MD of HPCL ... New Delhi: Hindustan Petroleum Corporation Ltd's (HPCL) under-recovery on domestic LPG cylinders narrowed to Rs 490 a cylinder in July from Rs 680 in June, Chairman and Managing Director (CMD) Vikas Kaushal said on the company's Q1 FY27 earnings call on July 23, a transcript of the call released on Wednesday showed. The loss averaged Rs 510 a cylinder over the April-June quarter, according to HPCL's management. ... Kaushal attributed the narrowing to a fall of about USD 200 per tonne in the Saudi contract price, a key LPG marker, and to a diversification of sourcing away from the Strait of Hormuz — a market in which, he said, the industry was ""once bitten twice shy."" HPCL had been close to 90 percent dependent on LPG cargoes routed through Hormuz before the West Asia crisis and is now using Indian time-chartered vessels to lift cargoes from the United States (US), ""which never happened in the past,"" he said. ... ## Rajasthan refinery ramp-up ... HPCL set out a ramp-up schedule for its Rajasthan refinery (HRRL), which began commercial operations in June. Its crude distillation unit is running at 60 percent capacity, and Kaushal guided to about 50 percent utilisation this quarter, 80 to 85 percent from October, and close to full utilisation on the refining section by the fourth quarter. ... A full run, including petrochemicals, is expected in FY28. The pet fluid catalytic cracking unit (PFCCU), which Kaushal called a ""money-spinner,"" was hours from commissioning at the time of the call. ""It's a matter of hours by which we will be able to commission it,"" he said. The sulphur recovery unit is the last block, expected by the end of the quarter. The refinery has begun producing motor spirit and LPG, Director (Refineries) S Bharathan said, and HPCL has stationed 150 staff from its Vizag and Mumbai refineries at the site. ... Kaushal laid out a shift away from buying fuel from third parties to meet HPCL's marketing demand, a dependence he said had hit the company during the quarter. Taking diesel as an example, HPCL sourced about 50 percent from its own refineries in the first quarter, 27 percent through the HMEL joint venture and bought out 24 percent. By next year, he expects 56 percent from its own units, including the Vizag residue upgradation facility, 40 percent from joint ventures as HRRL comes on stream, and only the balance from third parties, leaving HPCL ""almost self-sufficient, maybe surplus on some products."" He said the change would ""structurally alter our numbers"" from the third quarter. ... ## Vizag residue unit still ... The residue upgradation facility (RUF) at Vizag is still in its stabilisation phase after what Kaushal described as unexpected technological challenges. ""Even Lummus as a technology provider has not run,"" he said, adding it was the first time the licensor had run a unit this large. He expects the unit to stabilise next quarter and lift the refinery's distillate yields into the 80s. ... ## HP Navya premium LPG piloted ... HPCL has launched HP Navya, an on-demand premium LPG product it is piloting through Swiggy in Bengaluru and its own channel in Mumbai. Kaushal said the product, launched on July 15, would be extended to 200 to 250 cities by Diwali. ... HPCL posts Rs 11,526-crore Q1 loss ... it holds down fuel and LPG prices through West Asia crisis ... ## HRRL refinancing, JV accounting ... Director (Finance) Srividya Venkataraman said HPCL would refinance HRRL's high-cost rupee term loans using the RBI's fully hedged external commercial borrowing (ECB) window, worth a saving of at least 1.5 percent, and reprice bank debt now that the refinery is commissioned. At the parent level, interest cost was ""not a concern,"" she said, with the focus on reducing the size of the debt itself. She said HRRL would be accounted for as a joint venture, reflected as a share of profit rather than consolidated. Full-year capex, guided at up to Rs 9,700 crore, is likely to come in lower as the company conserves cash, Kaushal said."
HPCL Mittal Energy,,,,Exa,Tata Power picks Odisha over Andhra Pradesh for Ingot plant - The Economic Times,economictimes.indiatimes.com (Kalpana Pathak),2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-27 00:00:00+00:00,,https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/industry/energy/power/tata-power-picks-odisha-over-andhra-pradesh-for-ingot-plant/articleshow/132670042.cms,"Tata Power picks Odisha over Andhra Pradesh for 10 GW ingot plant - The Economic Times ... Tata Power will build a ten gigawatt ingot-and-wafer manufacturing plant in Odisha. The company chose Odisha over Andhra Pradesh for better incentives. This new facility will be constructed in two five gigawatt phases. Operations are expected to begin in January twenty twenty-eight. Tata Power is also exploring nuclear power projects in three states. ... Mumbai: Tata Power will set up its ₹6,675-crore, 10 GW ingot-and-wafer manufacturing plant in Gopalpur, Odisha, opting for the eastern state over Andhra Pradesh after evaluating incentives and infrastructure support, a senior company executive said Monday.ET reported in November that Tata Power was in talks with the Odisha government to establish the facility.""Ultimately based on the type of incentives and the type of support that is being provided in terms of lower costs of power and other benefits, it was decided that we will set up the plant in Odisha,"" Praveer Sinha, CEO & MD, Tata Power, told ET, after the company reported June quarter earnings. ... Sinha said the project also benefited from ready land availability at Gopalpur, where Tata Steel is developing a special economic zone. ""The land had already been acquired and was available, allowing us to move ahead with the project quickly,"" he said.The facility will be built in two 5 GW phases, with the first phase expected to start operations in January 2028, followed by the second about six months later. ... Tata Power recorded an 11% rise in consolidated net profit in the first quarter of FY27 at ₹1,401 crore compared to ₹1,262 crore a year earlier. Revenue from operations rose 5.6% to ₹19,051.26 crore from ₹18,035.07 crore. ... Operating profit, or EBITDA, slipped 3% to ₹4,013 crore. ... Sinha said the company is also in advanced discussions to finalise land for nuclear projects across the three states of Madhya Pradesh, Odisha and Gujarat, with preliminary site studies, including soil and geotechnical testing, already underway. A final decision is expected in six to eight months.On reactor sizing, the company is evaluating options with state-run Nuclear Power Corporation of India Ltd (NPCIL), spanning small 220-megawatt units-which typically come in pairs-as well as a standard 700-megawatt design."
HPCL Mittal Energy,,,,Exa,"Sterling & Wilson eyes Rs 1,500-crore IPO for generator manufacturing arm - The Economic Times",economictimes.indiatimes.com (Reghu Balakrishnan),2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-27 00:00:00+00:00,,https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/markets/ipos/fpos/sterling-wilson-eyes-rs-1500-crore-ipo-for-generator-manufacturing-arm/articleshow/132650400.cms,"Sterling & Wilson eyes Rs 1,500-crore IPO for generator manufacturing arm - The Economic Times FEATURED FUNDS★★★★★Motilal Oswal Midcap Fund Direct-Growth5Y Return22.53 % Invest Now Business News› Markets› IPOs/FPOs›Sterling & Wilson eyes Rs 1,500-crore IPO for generator manufacturing arm ##### The Economic Times daily newspaper is available online now. # Sterling & Wilson eyes Rs 1,500-crore IPO for generator manufacturing arm SECTIONS Sterling & Wilson eyes Rs 1,500-crore IPO for generator manufacturing arm By , ET BureauLast Updated: Jul 27, 2026, 06:37:00 AM IST Rate Story Follow us Share Font Size AbcSmall AbcMedium AbcLarge Save Synopsis Sterling & Wilson plans a ₹1,500 crore IPO for its generator unit. Proceeds will fund the company's significant global and domestic expansion plans. The generator business has seen strong momentum, driven by data centre growth. Its order book nearly doubled in FY26, reaching ₹2,900 crore. This move aims to strengthen its market position and meet rising demand. Listen to this article in summarized format Listen Loading... × × Subscribe to Unlock AI Briefing and Premium Content New Year Offer 24 Hours Left Subscribe Now Already a member? Sign In ### What's Included - Exclusive Stories - Daily ePaper Access - Smart Market Tools - Curated Investment Ideas - Ad-lite Experience - Subscription AgenciesSterling Green was previously a subsidiary of Shapoorji Pallonji & Company, which held a 56% stake as of March 31, 2024, with the remaining shares owned by chairman Khurshed Daruwala and his family. Mumbai: Engineering, procurement and construction company Sterling & Wilson is preparing to launch an initial public offering (IPO) of about ₹1,500 crore for its generator manufacturing subsidiary, Sterling Green Power Solutions(formerly Sterling Generators), according to multiple people familiar with the development. ICICI Securities and IIFL Capital have been appointed lead managers to the issue. The proceeds will primarily be used to fund the company's expansion, the people said. Headquartered in Silvassa, Sterling Green assembles and distributes diesel generator sets with capacities ranging from 250 kilovolt-amperes (kVA) to 5,000 kVA. It operates across India, the Middle East, Africa, Australia and Malaysia, and plans to strengthen its global footprint by setting up a facility in Dubai in FY27 to serve customers in Europe and the US. Agencies The company also plans to establish a manufacturing plant in Pune to meet rising domestic demand. It has earmarked capital expenditure of ₹90-100 crore over FY27 and FY28 for the expansion. August Rush: Over 2 dozen companies plan Street debut next month ### Live Events Queries sent to a Sterling & Wilson spokesperson did not elicit a response till press time. Sterling Green was previously a subsidiary of Shapoorji Pallonji & Company, which held a 56% stake as of March 31, 2024, with the remaining shares owned by chairman Khurshed Daruwala and his family. According to a CareEdge Ratings report, Shapoorji Pallonji's holding fell to 42% by March 31, 2025, after a secondary share sale to a group of investors, resulting in Sterling Green ceasing to be its subsidiary. The company has seen a sharp improvement in business momentum, driven largely by the rapid expansion of the data centre industry. According to a recent Crisil Ratings report, Sterling Green's order book nearly doubled in FY26, taking its order book-to-revenue ratio to around 3x. The order book stood at about ₹2,900 crore as of May 1, 2026, with nearly 80% of orders from marquee customers including Adani, AirTrunk and DAMAC. Provisional revenue for FY26 rose to ₹1,167 crore from ₹868 crore in FY25, while operating margin remained healthy at 11.5%, compared with 12% a year earlier. Add as a Reliable and Trusted News Source Add Now! (You can now subscribe to our ETMarkets WhatsApp channel) Read More News on (What's moving Sensex and Nifty Track latest market n"
HPCL Mittal Energy,,,,Exa,"HPCL’s Q1 FY27 under recovery on petrol, diesel and LPG hits ₹26,000 crore - The HinduBusinessLine",www.thehindubusinessline.com (Rishi Ranjan Kala),2026-07-23T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-23 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.thehindubusinessline.com/companies/hpcls-q1-fy27-under-recovery-on-petrol-diesel-and-lpg-hits-26000-crore/article71258303.ece,"HPCL’s Q1 FY27 under recovery on petrol, diesel and LPG hits ₹26,000 crore - The HinduBusinessLine # HPCL’s Q1 FY27 under recovery on petrol, diesel and LPG hits ₹26,000 crore ## For the entire Q1, HPCL’s LPG loss stood at around ₹510 per cylinder, says CMD ### By Rishi Ranjan Kala Updated - July 23, 2026 at 07:46 PM. | New Delhi, July 23 Vikas Kaushal, CMD, HPCL | Photo Credit: cueapi State-run Hindustan Petroleum Corporation (HPCL) on Thursday said that its cumulative under recovery on petrol, diesel and liquefied petroleum gas (LPG) stood at around ₹26,000 crore in the April-June quarter as the conflict in West Asia. Responding to an analyst query at a post-results call, HPCL CMD Vikas Kaushal said: “For the whole quarter, there is a significant under-recovery on this… Just to give you a sense, the marketing under-recovery for the whole quarter was upwards of ₹26,000 crore... It was a significant under-recovery for the whole quarter. Every litre which you and I were buying from petrol pump was being subsidised by one of the three OMCs.” HPCL reported a consolidated net loss of around ₹12,265 crore in Q1 FY27 as the West Asia conflict pushed up crude oil prices while PSU OMCs sold petrol, diesel and LPG at below market cost impacting margins. ## West Asia conflict Due to the closure of the Strait of Hormuz (SoH), crude oil prices surged as West Asian cargoes got stranded. Besides, it also impacted supply of LPG and LNG with India among the major impacted countries. PSU oil marketing companies (OMCs) have incurred high under recoveries as they sold peril, diesel and LPG at below market rates. The OMC attributed the impact to suppressed marketing margins on certain petroleum products. According to Equirus Securities, the average Brent price: stood at $97 per barrel, an over 25 per cent growth Q-o-Q and around 45 per cent Y-o-Y in Q1 FY27. The average Saudi propane prices stood at $682 per tonne, growing by over 26 per cent Q-o-Q and over 17 per cent Y-o-Y. HPCL expects higher LPG under recoveries in January-March 2026 HPCL’s under recoveries on LPG as on June 30, 2026 were around ₹16,405.92 crore, against ₹12,798.67 crore as of March 31, 2026 and ₹13,042.56 crore on June 30, 2025. ## LPG loss To another analyst query, Kaushal said that for the entire Q1 FY27, HPCL’s LPG loss stood at around ₹510 per cylinder. The loss is around ₹680 crore in June 2026 and ₹490 per cylinder in July 2026. HPCL’s standalone and consolidated debt increased sharply by ₹ 25,000 crore Q-o-Q to ₹72,600 crore and ₹75,900 crore, respectively. Increase reflects significant working-capital and funding burden created by elevated crude costs and marketing under-recoveries, Equirus added. The HPCL management said that debt reduction is a top priority, with a goal of bringing the debt-to-equity ratio back toward 0.8 over time. The OMC has also initiated measures to optimise its operations through project Samriddhi. In FY26, HPCL launched Samriddhi 1.0, which is a structured EBITDA improvement programme that delivered accruals of ₹1,691 crore. Building on this success, HPCL has now launched Samriddhi 2.0, an enterprisewide programme targeting at an EBITDA improvement by ₹1,500 crore, out of which ₹1,000 crore is targeted as accrual for FY27. Published on July 23, 2026"
HPCL Mittal Energy,,,,Exa,HPCL shares fall on margin woes; BPCL reverses loss - The HinduBusinessLine,www.thehindubusinessline.com (BL Bengaluru Bureau),2026-07-23T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-23 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.thehindubusinessline.com/markets/hpcl-shares-slide-as-west-asia-conflict-hits-margin-bpcl-reverses-loss/article71256581.ece,"HPCL shares fall on margin woes; BPCL reverses loss - The HinduBusinessLine ##### SENSEX 76,571.72 -183.33 ##### NIFTY 23,934.85 -61.40 ##### CRUDEOIL 8,569.00 + 159.00 ##### GOLD 144,916.00 -764.00 ##### SILVER 225,700.00 -1,298.00 ##### SENSEX 76,571.72 -183.33 ##### NIFTY 23,934.85 -61.40 ##### NIFTY 23,934.85 -61.40 ##### CRUDEOIL 8,569.00 + 159.00 ##### CRUDEOIL 8,569.00 + 159.00 ##### GOLD 144,916.00 -764.00 # HPCL shares slide as West Asia conflict hits margin, BPCL reverses loss ## HPCL reported a consolidated net loss of around ₹12,265 crore in Q1 FY27; BPCL posted a consolidated net loss of around ₹1,873 crore in Q1FY27 Updated - July 23, 2026 at 12:37 PM. | Bengaluru × ### Share WhatsApp X Facebook LinkedIn Messenger Reddit Mail https://www.thehindubusinessline.com/markets/hpcl-shares-slide-as-west-asia-conflict-hits-margin-bpcl-reverses-loss/article71256581.eceCopy HPCL shares fell close to 5 per cent on Thursday after it reported a consolidated net loss of around ₹12,265 crore in Q1 FY27, as the West Asia conflict pushed up crude oil prices while PSU OMCs sold petrol, diesel and LPG below market cost, impacting margins. The stock traded at ₹384.50 at 10.30 am on the NSE after hitting a low of ₹376.55, compared with the previous close of ₹395.20. ## Brokerages divided Macquarie maintained its outperform call on HPCL with a target price of ₹490. The brokerage described Q1FY27 as a likely trough quarter and expects near-term earnings to remain volatile for oil marketing companies. Meanwhile, Jefferies maintained its underperform call on HPCL while raising its target price to ₹345 from ₹275. The brokerage highlighted steep losses but said the company benefited from inventory gains. ### Also read NTPC Green Energy shares zoom 9% after Q1 results Jefferies said spot marketing margins are back in the red after the crude rally and built in a PAT loss for FY27. It also noted that HPCL trades at a premium to BPCL despite reporting three times the PAT loss of BPCL in the quarter. ## BPCL in focus BPCL posted a consolidated net loss of around ₹1,873 crore in Q1FY27. The stock traded flat at ₹315.60 on the NSE at 10.32 am after declining nearly 2.8 per cent to ₹305.10 in early trade from the previous close of ₹314. Citi maintained its buy call on BPCL with a target price of ₹350, while Macquarie retained its outperform rating with a target price of ₹370, citing BPCL’s operational strength and refining cushion despite near-term earnings pressure from crude price volatility. ### More Like This Eternal shares gain after Q1 profit; brokerages see strong growth and execution Dr Reddy’s Lab shares hit 52-week low after Q1 profit falls 69%; brokerages cut targets Published on July 23, 2026 Companies to follow - Bharat Petroleum Corporation Ltd READ MORE #### Related Stories Eternal shares gain after Q1 profit; brokerages see strong growth and execution Dr Reddy’s Lab shares hit 52-week low after Q1 profit falls 69%; brokerages cut targets #### Important Stories 1. ""Nothing more important than youth's future"": PM Modi announces fast-track courts for paper leak offenders 2. PM's response insufficient, won’t leave Jantar Mantar until Pradhan resigns: CJP 3. Centre plans sweeping aviation reforms to ease airline entry 4. FCNR (B) deposits: Small, mid-sized private banks reach out to GIFT City IBUs to offer leverage to NRIs 5. Jana Nayagan hits theatres after delays, sparks festive scenes across Tamil Nadu #### Follow Us FOLLOW US ON WHATSAPP #### Home GO BACK TO HOME ### Comments Comments have to be in English, and in full sentences. They cannot be abusive or personal. Please abide by our community guidelines for posting your comments. We have migrated to a new commenting platform. If you are already a registered user of TheHindu Businessline and logged in, you may continue to engage with our articles. If you do not have an account please register and login to post comments. User"
HPCL Mittal Energy,,,,Exa,"HPCL shares fall up to 3% as fuel and LPG retailing losses lead to ₹11,500 crore net loss in Q1 - CNBC TV18",www.cnbctv18.com,2026-07-23T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-23 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.cnbctv18.com/market/hpcl-share-price-q1-result-net-loss-11500-crore-buy-sell-target-lpg-losses-returns-19951983.htm,"HPCL shares fall up to 3% as fuel and LPG retailing losses lead to ₹11,500 crore net loss in Q1 - CNBC TV18 # HPCL shares fall up to 3% as fuel and LPG retailing losses lead to ₹11,500 crore net loss in Q1 ## HPCL booked an LPG under-recovery of ₹5,600 crore in the first quarter, compared to ₹1,400 crore sequentially, with a cumulative net negative buffer of ₹6,400 as of June 2026-end. By Sudarshan Kumar | Shloka Badkar July 23, 2026, 10:09:49 AM IST (Published) Shares of Hindustan Petroleum Corp Ltd. declined up to 3% on Thursday, July 23, after fuel and LPG retailing losses impacted its first quarter earnings. The company reported a loss of over ₹11,000 crore. However, the same was lower than estimates, which stood at ₹13,680 crore. Its revenue growth remained healthy as it increased by 23% sequentially. HPCL, the state-run fuel refiner, reported an earnings before interest, taxes depreciation and amortization (EBITDA) loss of ₹15,500 crore compared to an EBITDA of ₹9,400 in the previous quarter. The cumulative negative buffer on LPG, as of June, is at ₹16,406 crore. The gross refining margin (GRM) was at $23.8 per barrel, 2% above estimates. ## A Divided House Brokerages Jefferies and Nomura have ""underperform"" and ""neutral"" ratings on the stock, while Macquarie maintained its ""outperform"" rating on the stock. Jefferies Is Bearish On HPCL The brokerage has an ""underperform"" rating on HPCL, and a price target of ₹325 per share. This indicates a downside of 17.6% from its previous close of ₹394.35 apiece. It said the company's loss was lower than the brokerage's estimate. Higher refining margins driven by inventory gains cushioned some of those losses. The brokerage has also warned that HPCL may report a net loss for the full financial year as well with spot marketing margins back in the red after the rally in crude oil prices. Another reason why Jefferies is bearish on HPCL is the fact that it trades at a premium to its peer BPCL, despite reporting a net loss triple of the latter. Nomura The brokerage has a ""neutral"" rating on HPCL with a target price of ₹440 per share, indicating an upside of 11.6% from the previous close. It said the HPCL's first quarter EBITDA loss of ₹16,100 crore was driven by significant fuel and LPG retailing losses. The GRM of $23.8 per barrel came in 2% above estimates of $23.3 per barrel, increasing from $14.3 per barrel in the fourth quarter of FY26. Nomura said crude throughput increased 1% sequentially to 6.5 million tonne. The adjusted net loss of ₹11,500 was better than estimates for a ₹12,200 crore loss due to higher tax losses. It added that HPCL booked an LPG under-recovery of ₹5,600 crore in the first quarter, compared to ₹1,400 crore sequentially, with a cumulative net negative buffer of ₹6,400 as of June 2026-end. The LPG compensation loss of ₹1,980 crore was received in the June quarter toward under-recoveries made in FY25, being paid in 12 eqaul monthly instalments, Nomura added. ## Stock reaction Of the 33 analysts who have coverage on HPCL, 15 have a ""buy"" rating, seven have a ""hold"" rating and 11 have a ""sell"" rating. Shares of HPCL are trading 2.6% lower on Thursday after the results at ₹385.4. The stock is down 23% so far this year. Also Read: Explained - Why Jefferies has the highest target but CLSA downgraded IndusInd Bank after Q1 results"
HPCL Mittal Energy,,,,Exa,"Wipro Consumer acquires TTK's Good Home, Eva brands for ₹256 crore in second deal in three days | Company Business News",www.livemint.com (Vaeshnavi Kasthuril),2026-07-23T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-23 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.livemint.com/companies/wipro-consumer-acquires-ttks-good-home-eva-brands-for-256-crore-in-second-deal-in-three-days-fmcg-acquisitions-11784804513570.html,"Wipro Consumer acquires TTK's Good Home, Eva brands for ₹256 crore in second deal in three days | Company Business News # Wipro Consumer acquires TTK's Good Home, Eva brands for ₹256 crore in second deal in three days Wipro Consumer Care's India business spans eight categories: hygiene, home care, personal care, direct-to-consumer, wellness and male grooming, office solutions and food. (Reuters) AI Quick Read"
HPCL Mittal Energy,,,,Exa,"Hindustan Petroleum Corporation reports loss of ₹11,526 crore, revenue jumps 21% QoQ; check Q1 FY27 numbers",upstox.com,2026-07-22T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-22 00:00:00+00:00,,https://upstox.com/news/market-news/earnings/hindustan-petroleum-corporation-reports-loss-of-11-526-crore-revenue-jumps-21-qo-q-check-q1-fy-27-numbers/article-197389/,"Hindustan Petroleum Corporation reports loss of ₹11,526 crore, revenue jumps 21% QoQ; check Q1 FY27 numbers ... 1. Hindustan Petroleum Corporation reports loss of ₹11,526 crore, revenue jumps 21% QoQ; check Q1 FY27 numbers ... # Hindustan Petroleum Corporation reports loss of ₹11,526 crore, revenue jumps 21% QoQ; check Q1 FY27 numbers ... 2 min read | Updated on July ... HPCL Q1 results: It reported an EBITDA loss of ₹14,860 crore in Q1 FY27, in comparison to the EBITDA profit of ₹4,967 crore in the preceding quarter. ... HPCL Q1 earnings: The oil and natural gas PSU, Hindustan Petroleum Corporation Limited (HPCL), on Wednesday, July 22, reported its earnings for the April-June quarter of the 2026-27 financial year (Q1 FY27). ... During the quarter under review, the company slipped into a loss, reporting a standalone net loss of ₹11,526.41 crore, compared with a profit of ₹4,901.50 in the preceding quarter, according to a regulatory filing. ... “The performance reflects the impact of the ongoing West Asia crisis and also HPCL’s resilient performance on refining and marketing even in these challenging times,” the filing said. ... However, its total income advanced by 21.34% quarter-on-quarter (QoQ) to ₹1.46 lakh crore in the first quarter of FY27, as against ₹1.21 lakh crore in the March quarter of the 2025-26 fiscal year (Q4 FY26). ... At an operational level, it reported an EBITDA (earnings before interest, tax, depreciation and amortisation) loss of ₹14,860 crore for the reporting quarter, in comparison to the EBITDA profit of ₹4,967 crore in the preceding quarter. ... Its refineries recorded crude throughput of 6.52 million metric tonnes (MMT) during Q1 FY27, marking a 2.8% QoQ decline from 6.48 MMT in the March quarter of FY26. It operated at 107% of its capacity during the quarter, the company added. ... Its sales, including exports, stood at 13.12 MMT for the quarter, reflecting a 0.9% QoQ fall from 13 MMT in Q4 FY26. ... The company’s capex for the quarter stood at ₹1,734 crore, with a focus on strengthening refining and marketing infrastructure, including investments in subsidiaries and joint venture companies to build additional capacities, new business lines and improve operating efficiencies. ... As of June 30, 2026, its retail outlet count stood at 25,160, LPG distributors were at 6,391, and PNG Connections at 54,586. ... (NSE) ... results were reported ... the markets closed. ... Hindustan Petroleum Corporation Limited has a total market capitalisation of ₹83,910.68 crore as of July 22, 2026, according to data on the NSE."
HPCL Mittal Energy,,,,Exa,"Gabriel India unveils ₹3,166-crore Project Jupiter for Korean-linked HL Mando, Klemove stakes - The HinduBusinessLine",www.thehindubusinessline.com (Amit Vijay Mohile),2026-07-22T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-22 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.thehindubusinessline.com/companies/gabriel-india-unveils-3166-crore-project-jupiter-for-korean-linked-hl-mando-klemove-stakes/article71251030.ece,"Gabriel India unveils ₹3,166-crore Project Jupiter for Korean-linked HL Mando, Klemove stakes - The HinduBusinessLine ... # Gabriel India unveils ₹3,166-crore Project Jupiter for Korean-linked HL Mando, Klemove stakes ... ## Project Jupiter brings steering, braking and ADAS capabilities alongside its suspension business ... Updated - July 22, 2026 at 07:37 PM. ... Gabriel India on Tuesday unveiled Project Jupiter, a ₹3,166-crore plan to acquire 28.99 per cent of HL Mando Anand India for ₹2,231 crore and 30 per cent minus one share of HL Klemove India for $98.44 million, or about ₹935 crore, deepening its Korean technology ties, the company said in a regulatory filing. ... Gabriel is the listed flagship of the $2.2-billion ANAND Group. The investments add HL Mando and HL Klemove to existing Korean partnerships with CY Myutec, Jinhap and SK Enmove. Gabriel’s wider global alliance network includes Dana, Henkel and Inalfa Roof Systems. ... “These investments strengthen Gabriel India’s position as a comprehensive mobility platform by bringing together complementary capabilities across the automotive value chain,” Executive Chairperson Anjali Singh said. ... The strategic shift is towards selling more products to the same carmakers and increasing Gabriel-linked content per vehicle. ... HL Mando supplies steering, braking and suspension systems to Hyundai, Kia, Mahindra, Tata Motors, Maruti Suzuki, MG and Isuzu. HL Klemove adds radars, cameras and ADAS control units, with Hyundai-Kia-linked localisation disclosed in India. ... Maruti, Mahindra and Tata are already Gabriel customers, creating an immediate cross-selling opportunity. Hyundai, Kia, MG and Isuzu broaden the company’s OEM reach. Gabriel can now approach automakers with a broader package: suspension from Gabriel, steering and brakes from HL Mando, and safety electronics from HL Klemove. ... Gabriel and ANAND businesses already supply several of these customers with sunroofs, fasteners, driveline components, fluids and adhesives. The companies will remain separate joint ventures and associates, but Gabriel becomes the common listed platform coordinating the wider product offering. No fresh orders from these OEMs have been announced. ... HL Mando adds more than steering and brakes. Its portfolio includes suspension modules and smart-damping systems. HL Klemove brings radars, cameras, LiDAR, automated-driving and parking-control units, ADAS software, chassis controllers and steering and braking ECUs. ... Together with existing businesses, the platform can address a larger portion of a vehicle: the sunroof through Inalfa Gabriel; structural adhesives and noise-management products through Henkel Anand; fasteners through Jinhap Gabriel; axles, driveshafts and transmission components through Dana Anand and CY Myutec; and coolants, lubricants and EV thermal fluids through Anchemco and SK Enmove. ... Project Jupiter therefore takes Gabriel beyond suspension hardware towards the roof, body, chassis, driveline, fluids and electronic-safety systems of the same vehicle. ... The company has not disclosed a rupee estimate for content per vehicle. Its stated opportunity is to cross-sell a wider component basket through common OEM relationships while acquiring technology that would take years to develop internally. ... HL Mando Anand, which has three plants near Chennai, reported FY26 revenue of ₹5,886 crore, adjusted EBITDA of ₹639 crore and PAT of ₹358 crore. Gabriel expects the investment to deliver about 13 per cent EPS accretion on a pro-forma FY26 basis, incremental to the approximately 38 per cent accretion achieved through Project Rise. ... HL Klemove India, which operates near Chennai with more than 400 employees, reported FY26 revenue of ₹1,049 crore and adjusted EBITDA of ₹129 crore. Gabriel will pay 75 per cent of the Klemove consideration, or about ₹701 crore, by September 15 and the remaining ₹234 crore within 18 months, funding the purchase through internal accruals and debt. ... For HL Mando, Gabriel will pay ₹350 crore in cash and issue ₹1,881 crore of shares to promoter Asia Investments. Promoter ownership will rise from 63.55 per cent to 66.29 per cent, while public ownership will decline from 36.45 per cent to 33.71 per cent. ... HL Klemove will retain control with 70 per cent plus one share. Gabriel will have four of the 10 board seats and affirmative rights over specified decisions. ... ## ₹50,000-crore ambition ... Project Jupiter follows Project Rise, which transferred several ANAND Group automotive businesses and investments to Gabriel. On the 100 per cent portfolio-revenue basis used by the company, businesses under Gabriel represented ₹4,667 crore of FY26 revenue before Rise, ₹9,176 crore after Rise and would reach ₹16,111 crore after Jupiter. ... After Jupiter, Gabriel-associated businesses would represent about 70 per cent of ANAND Group’s ₹23,049-crore automotive revenue, compared with 21 per cent before Rise. ... The move supports ANAND’s ambition of reaching ₹50,000 crore in group revenue by 2030, while positioning Gabriel as its preferred listed vehicle for future automotive investments. ... Gabriel closed FY26 with restated consolidated revenue of ₹5,245.19 crore, up 29.1 per cent, and EBITDA of ₹516.2 crore, up 31.8 per cent. Q1 FY27 revenue rose 15.5 per cent to ₹1,425.68 crore, while EBITDA grew 2.3 per cent to ₹124.2 crore. The margin fell to 8.7 per cent from 9.8 per cent, and PAT increased 2.5 per cent to ₹108.13 crore. ... Alongside the transactions, Gabriel appointed Goyal as Executive Director, Group CEO and Managing Director for five years, subject to shareholder approval. Atul Jaggi was redesignated Managing Director (Ride Control)."
HPCL Mittal Energy,,,,Exa,"Exclusive: HPCL Plans Smart LPG Cylinders With Real-Time Leak Alerts, Remote Notifications | Times Now",www.timesnownews.com,2026-07-21T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-21 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.timesnownews.com/business-economy/companies/exclusive-hpcl-plans-smart-lpg-cylinders-with-real-time-leak-alerts-remote-notifications-article-155144857,"Exclusive: HPCL Plans Smart LPG Cylinders With Real-Time Leak Alerts, Remote Notifications | Times Now # Exclusive: HPCL Plans Smart LPG Cylinders With Real-Time Leak Alerts, Remote Notifications - Authored by: Vikas Kumar - Updated Jul 21, 2026, 14:12 IST ## HPCL aims to improve household LPG safety by expanding its IoT-based leak detection solutions. Executive Director Dhruv Kapil revealed plans for smart LPG devices that offer continuous monitoring and instant leak alerts. Dhruv Kapil, Executive Director – LPG, HPCL. State-run oil marketing company, Hindustan Petroleum Corporation Limited (HPCL) is looking to enhance household LPG safety by expanding its portfolio of leak detection solutions with Internet of Things (IoT)-based technology. In an exclusive interview with Times Now, Dhruv Kapil, Executive Director – LPG, HPCL shared that the OMC plans to introduce smart LPG safety devices capable of continuous monitoring, instant leak alerts and remote notifications, offering consumers greater convenience and improved safety in their kitchens. Kapil further shared about the HPCL's newly introduced HP Navya, India's first on-demand LPG cylinder delivery service through a quick commerce platform, through Swiggy Instamart . Here are the edited excerpts: Q: How HP Navya is placed differently from regular LPG cylinders? Should consumers expect to pay a premium? HP Navya is a lightweight, corrosion-resistant composite cylinder designed to offer customers a safer and more convenient alternative to conventional steel cylinders. With premium brand experience, express delivery, enhanced convenience, safety and portability. Navya offers a different value proposition for New India who wants quick, fast and robust services and are willing to pay premium for the same. While the product incorporates advanced composite technology and premium features, HPCL will strive to ensure that it remains competitively priced and delivers superior value to customers. Q: Are there plans to introduce smart cylinders or digital safety monitoring solutions? We currently market LPG leak detection sensors for domestic kitchens through our allied distributor channel partners. Going forward, we aim to strengthen and expand this portfolio by leveraging IoT-based technologies. These smart solutions will provide features such as continuous monitoring, instant leak alerts, remote notifications, and enhanced safety management, delivering greater convenience and peace of mind to consumers. Q: Can existing HP Gas customers easily switch to HP Navya, or will they need to follow a separate registration process? Yes, existing consumers can also easily enrol in Navya through HP PAY or Instamart. They need a valid Proof of Identity (POI) to complete the process. The onboarding is simple, convenient, and can be completed in just a few easy steps, ensuring a smooth and hassle-free experience. Q: How durable are these cylinders compared to steel cylinders, and what is their expected lifespan? Composite cylinders are manufactured using advanced fibre-reinforced composite materials, making them corrosion-free, lightweight and highly durable. They are designed and tested in accordance with applicable BIS/PESO norms. To ensure continued safety and performance, these cylinders are required to undergo mandatory quality testing every three years, as prescribed by the applicable standards. Q: How do you see the role of authorised distributors evolving in a digitally enabled fulfilment ecosystem? These developments will further strengthen the serviceability, operational efficiency and responsiveness of our existing distributors, who are backbone of HPCL's LPG distribution network. Digital fulfilment models, including quick commerce and online platforms, are intended to complement the distributor ecosystem. Distributors will continue to play a critical role in customer onboarding, inventory management, safety compliance, last-mile fulfilment and after-sales s"
HPCL Mittal Energy,,,,Exa,"BPCL launches premium LPG service in Mumbai, eyes 100-city rollout by Independence Day - The HinduBusinessLine",www.thehindubusinessline.com (BL Mumbai Bureau),2026-07-20T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-20 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.thehindubusinessline.com/markets/stock-markets/bpcl-launches-premium-lpg-service-in-mumbai-eyes-100-city-rollout-by-independence-day/article71244127.ece,"BPCL launches premium LPG service in Mumbai, eyes 100-city rollout by Independence Day - The HinduBusinessLine # BPCL launches premium LPG service in Mumbai, eyes 100-city rollout by Independence Day ## The composite cylinder offers a transparent body that allows users to visually monitor gas levels, along with a corrosion-free build and lighter weight compared to conventional steel cylinders ... Bharat Petroleum Corporation Limited (BPCL) on Monday launched Bharatgas Lite ZIP, a premium free trade LPG offering, in Mumbai, with plans to expand to 100 cities across 24 states by August 15, 2026. ... The product, unveiled by Chairman and Managing Director Sanjay Khanna, combines BPCL’s existing lightweight composite Bharatgas Lite cylinder with two new service features: instant new connections through a simplified onboarding process, and express delivery of LPG cylinders. The offering is positioned at working professionals, students, and mobile urban populations. ... Bharatgas Lite ZIP operates under the free trade LPG segment, meaning it is priced outside the regulated subsidy framework. The composite cylinder offers a transparent body that allows users to visually monitor gas levels, along with a corrosion-free build and lighter weight compared to conventional steel cylinders. ... Director of Marketing Subhankar Sen said the launch incorporates 100 per cent digital booking, OTP-based delivery authentication, and a quality assurance programme called Zero Ka Dum. ... The Maharatna public sector undertaking operates over 6,250 LPG distributorships nationally and holds a combined refining capacity of 35.3 million metric tonnes per annum across its Mumbai, Kochi, and Bina refineries."
HPCL Mittal Energy,,,,Exa,"Time Technoplast Wins Major Order from HPCL for 1.4 Lakh LPG Cylinders, ETEnergyworld",energy.economictimes.indiatimes.com (www.ETEnergyworld.com),2026-07-17T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-17 00:00:00+00:00,,https://energy.economictimes.indiatimes.com/news/oil-and-gas/time-technoplast-wins-major-order-from-hpcl-for-14-lakh-lpg-cylinders/132454085,"Time Technoplast Wins Major Order from HPCL for 1.4 Lakh LPG Cylinders, ETEnergyworld ... # Time Technoplast bags HPCL order to supply 1.4 lakh composite LPG cylinders ... The order, which will be executed within six months, comes as HPCL expands composite LPG cylinder distribution through quick-commerce platforms. ... The order is scheduled for execution within six months. Time Technoplast Limited has secured a Government e-Marketplace(GeM) contract worth approximately ₹38.14 crore from Hindustan Petroleum Corporation Limited (HPCL) to supply 1.4 lakh 10-kg Type IV composite LPG cylinders. The order is scheduled for execution within six months.According to the company, the order reflects the growing adoption of composite LPG cylinders by India's oil marketing companies due to their enhanced safety, lightweight design and improved consumer convenience.The contract further strengthens Time Technoplast's leadership in the composite cylinder segment and supports the transition towards modern LPG distribution, the company said. ... The company said the order assumes added significance as HPCL has recently introduced an innovative distribution model for composite LPG cylinders through leading quick-commerce platforms, enabling on-demand delivery to consumers. The initiative is expected to improve accessibility and accelerate the adoption of next-generation composite LPG cylinders across the country, it said."
HPCL Mittal Energy,,,,Exa,HPCL Launches India's First Quick-Commerce LPG Delivery Partnering with Swiggy Instamart for 10-kg Cylinders,www.sahi.com,2026-07-16T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-16 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.sahi.com/news/hpcl-launches-india-s-first-quick-commerce-lpg-delivery-partnering-with-swiggy-instamart-for-10-kg-cylinders-934-PE1_CORP,"HPCL Launches India's First Quick-Commerce LPG Delivery Partnering with Swiggy Instamart for 10-kg Cylinders Skip to main content Products Sahi Advantage News Breaking News Market Updates Learn Get Free Account Login # HPCL Launches India's First Quick-Commerce LPG Delivery Partnering with Swiggy Instamart for 10-kg Cylinders HPCL has partnered with Swiggy Instamart to pioneer instant delivery of small-format LPG cylinders, starting with a pilot in Bengaluru. The service features the newly launched 10-kg HP Navya composite cylinder and the existing 5-kg Appu metal cylinder. This represents an industry-first channel that bypasses traditional bureaucratic connection procedures, catering to highly mobile urban consumers. Published: 16 Jul 2026, 09:18 AM IST (41 minutes ago) Last Updated: 16 Jul 2026, 09:18 AM IST (41 minutes ago) 3 min read Reviewed by Arpit Seth Share on WhatsApp Share Market snapshot: Hindustan Petroleum Corporation Limited has partnered with Swiggy Instamart to launch India's first on-demand LPG cylinder delivery service on a quick commerce platform. Currently undergoing a pilot in Bengaluru, the program allows consumers to order compact cooking gas cylinders without needing a pre-existing domestic LPG connection. The deliveries are fulfilled directly via HPCL's authorized distributor network. ## Data Snapshot - HPCL's newly launched HP Navya composite cylinder is a lighter, corrosion-resistant alternative with a translucent body weighing 10-kg. - The service is being piloted in Bengaluru for the 10-kg composite cylinder and the company's existing 5-kg metal cylinder. - HPCL recorded its highest-ever annual LPG sales of 9.41 MMT in FY26, representing a growth of 5.2% over FY25, and maintains its position as India's second-largest LPG marketer. ## What's Changed - HPCL is transitioning from traditional dealership-led booking and milk-run logistics models to an on-demand quick commerce delivery model via Swiggy Instamart. - First-time buyers of cooking gas cylinders can now purchase composite or metal cylinders with instant digital identity verification, bypassing the bureaucratic paper connection process. - The partnership marks the commercial rollout of the HP Navya 10-kg composite cylinder, featuring a translucent body that allows users to monitor real-time gas levels, replacing opaque steel cylinders. ## Key Takeaways - HPCL has established an industry-first channel by integrating LPG cylinder distribution with Swiggy Instamart's quick commerce app. - The pilot project is currently live in parts of Bengaluru, including Subramanyapura and Uttarahalli, with city-wide expansion planned within a month. - The direct-to-consumer delivery is fulfilled by HPCL's authorized distributors using trained delivery personnel, maintaining rigorous safety standards. - The initiative targets high-density urban segments such as students, young working professionals, and tenants who prefer smaller cooking gas formats without long-term contracts. ## SAHI Perspective HPCL's partnership with Swiggy Instamart is a brilliant distribution hack that expands the addressable market for small-format cylinders. By targeting urban tenants and students who often bypass traditional domestic LPG connections due to documentation friction, HPCL creates a high-margin retail channel. Historically, small 5-kg cylinders have seen volatile demand from migrant laborers. Standardizing this segment via a trusted quick commerce platform enables transparent pricing and efficient refill collection. Additionally, the rapid scale of quick commerce can bolster HPCL's position as India's second-largest LPG marketer, where it achieved a record 9.41 MMT of LPG sales in FY26. ## Market Implications This partnership could prompt rival OMCs like Indian Oil and BPCL to seek similar quick commerce arrangements to protect their market share in urban centers. For Swiggy Instamart, expanding into regulated, high-ticket household utilities enhan"
HPCL Mittal Energy,,,,Exa,"HPCL Partners With Swiggy Instamart For LPG Delivery, Schedules Q1 Call For July 23",www.sahi.com,2026-07-16T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-16 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.sahi.com/news/hpcl-partners-with-swiggy-instamart-for-lpg-delivery-schedules-q1-call-for-july-23-934-PE1_CORP,"HPCL Partners With Swiggy Instamart For LPG Delivery, Schedules Q1 Call For July 23 Skip to main content Products Futures & Options Stocks IPO ETFs Sahi Advantage News Breaking News Market Updates Partners Pricing Learn Support Get Free Account Login # HPCL Partners With Swiggy Instamart For LPG Delivery, Schedules Q1 Call For July 23 HPCL has partnered with Swiggy Instamart to pilot on-demand deliveries of 5-kg and 10-kg LPG cylinders in Bengaluru, requiring no existing domestic connection. Concurrently, the company has scheduled its Q1 FY27 post-earnings conference call for July 23, 2026, at 10:00 AM IST, which remains a highly anticipated catalyst given previous management warnings of near-term margin pressure. Sahi Markets Published: 16 Jul 2026, 08:55 PM IST (2 days ago) Last Updated: 16 Jul 2026, 08:55 PM IST (2 days ago) 3 min read Reviewed by Arpit Seth Share on WhatsApp Share Market snapshot: Hindustan Petroleum Corporation Limited has forged a strategic partnership with Swiggy Instamart to launch India's first on-demand LPG cylinder delivery service, initially debuting as a pilot project in Bengaluru. Alongside this consumer-facing initiative, the oil marketing giant has officially scheduled its Q1 FY27 post-earnings conference call for July 23, 2026, to present its quarterly performance to the investment community. ## Data Snapshot - Standalone Profit After Tax for Q4 FY26 grew by 46% year-on-year to ₹4,902 crore. - The company's standalone Profit After Tax for the full financial year ended March 31, 2026, reached a record ₹17,175 crore. - Average Gross Refining Margin for Q4 FY26 rose to $14.27 per barrel, up from $8.44 per barrel in Q4 FY25. ## What's Changed - HPCL is digitizing its last-mile delivery through India's first on-demand quick-commerce LPG channel. - The newly launched translucent HP Navya 10-kg composite cylinder is commercially accessible through Swiggy Instamart. - Standalone net profit grew 46% YoY to ₹4,902 crore in Q4 FY26, but the company has scheduled its next earnings review on July 23 under the shadow of projected Q1 margin pressure. ## Key Takeaways - On-demand quick-commerce delivery of utility LPG cylinders represents a major structural shift in retail distribution patterns. - The pilot targets highly mobile urban demographics, lowering access barriers by bypassing traditional long-term cylinder registration rules. - Time Technoplast has been confirmed as the supplier of the newly introduced Type IV composite cylinders for the project. - Immediate stock focus remains strongly on the July 23 earnings call, where market participants will assess the impact of expensive crude oil and sticky retail fuel prices. ## SAHI Perspective HPCL's partnership with Swiggy Instamart is an innovative customer-engagement move that helps push high-margin composite cylinders like HP Navya to retail segments. From a corporate finance standpoint, however, the real driver is the upcoming Q1 FY27 earnings call scheduled for July 23. During its previous analyst meet, management was explicitly cautious about Q1, warning of heavy margin compression and potential losses due to costly global crude and low fuel margins. While the quick-commerce tie-up is structurally excellent, the near-term stock trajectory will be dictated entirely by the financial damage control shown in the upcoming results. ## Market Implications The development expands Swiggy's addressable quick-commerce portfolio and offers high-margin volume opportunities for HPCL's specialized lightweight cylinder segment. However, the wider public sector oil marketing space is trading with caution as rising crude prices threaten refining margins. The earnings call scheduled for July 23 will act as a major sector-wide event, defining the trajectory of oil marketing companies for the remainder of the quarter. ## Trading Signals Market Bias: Neutral While the Swiggy Instamart pilot is a progressive business update, trading bias re"
HPCL Mittal Energy,,,,Exa,Time Technoplast Supplies Type IV Composite Cylinders For HPCL-Swiggy Bengaluru Pilot,www.sahi.com,2026-07-16T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-16 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.sahi.com/news/time-technoplast-supplies-type-iv-composite-cylinders-for-hpcl-swiggy-bengaluru-pilot-3661-PE1_COR,"Time Technoplast is supplying lightweight, blast-proof Type IV Composite LPG Cylinders to HPCL for its on-demand delivery pilot via Swiggy Instamart in Bengaluru. This project enhances digital access to clean cooking fuel without requiring traditional domestic LPG paperwork and accelerates the company's shift toward high-margin composite products. ... Market snapshot: Time Technoplast Limited has confirmed its role as the exclusive supplier of Type IV Composite LPG Cylinders for Hindustan Petroleum Corporation Limited's (HPCL) joint pilot project with Swiggy Instamart. This collaboration marks India's pioneering on-demand quick-commerce LPG delivery service, starting in Bengaluru. The deployment utilizes HPCL's newly introduced 10 kg 'HP Navya' composite cylinders, highlighting a modern shift in clean fuel utility logistics. ... - Time Technoplast consolidated revenue crossed ₹6,105.2 crore in FY26, highlighting a steady top-line growth trajectory. - Consolidated net profit for the company rose to ₹468.72 crore in FY26, indicating solid profitability gains. - The Board of Directors recommended a final dividend of ₹1.5 per equity share of face value ₹1 for the fiscal year ended March 2026. ... - Consolidated FY26 revenue crossed ₹6,105.2 crore, registering ≈11.9% YoY growth (derived: ₹6,105.2 crore vs ₹5,457.04 crore). - Consolidated FY26 net profit rose to ₹468.72 crore, marking ≈20.8% YoY growth (derived: ₹468.72 crore vs ₹388 crore). ... - Pioneering Delivery Model: The HPCL-Swiggy collaboration is India's first on-demand LPG cylinder delivery model via quick-commerce, bypassing traditional gas connection bottlenecks. - Composite Cylinder Superiority: Time Technoplast's lightweight, translucent, rust-proof, and 100% blast-proof Type IV cylinders are key to unlocking quick-commerce viability for heavy cooking gas delivery. - Structural Product Shift: This deployment showcases Time Technoplast's expansion into high-margin value-added products, which grew 18% in FY26 compared to 10% for established packaging products. ... Securing the exclusive cylinder supply role for the groundbreaking HPCL-Swiggy pilot is a stellar validation of Time Technoplast's advanced polymer technology. While industrial packaging still comprises a significant portion of revenues, composite products are growing rapidly. If the Bengaluru pilot expands successfully across tier-1 markets, it will structuralize a highly recurring, high-margin revenue engine for its composite division. ... This initiative validates quick-commerce integration for heavy utilities, which may encourage other public oil marketing companies to launch on-demand services. For Time Technoplast, early integration into these logistics networks establishes a major competitive moat, positioning them as the default hardware supplier for smart energy distribution. ... Time Technoplast's exclusive role as the hardware supplier for the innovative HPCL-Swiggy pilot validates its composite tech. Coupled with solid FY26 earnings where net profit grew ≈20.8% YoY (derived: ₹468.72 crore vs ₹388 crore), the business maintains highly positive structural momentum. ... - Successful expansion of the HPCL-Swiggy LPG delivery pilot beyond Bengaluru to other metros. - Increased commercial orders for clean mobility components (Type IV and Type III composite hydrogen cylinders). - Stable raw material margins for polymer resins and carbon fiber imports. ... The Indian LPG cylinder ecosystem is moving steadily away from heavy steel models to non-corrosive, safe composite solutions. This transition aligns with municipal smart-city pushes and direct-to-consumer convenience. In FY26, Time Technoplast has capitalised on this, backed by PESO approvals for composite hydrogen and specialized CNG cylinders. ... - Regulatory Compliance: Quick-commerce transport of pressurized LPG cylinders demands stringent compliance under PESO and local safety laws, which could restrict expansion if safety issues arise. - Raw Material Exposure: Operating margins, which hovered around 14.37% in Q4 FY26, remain sensitive to fluctuations in polymer and high-performance carbon-fiber pricing. ... In May 2026, Time Technoplast reported strong Q4 FY26 results with consolidated net profit climbing 20.38% YoY to ₹131.84 crore and recommended a final dividend of ₹1.5 per share. Separately, in April 2026, the company secured PESO design approval for its 250-litre Type IV composite hydrogen cylinders aimed at high-pressure clean transportation like buses, trucks, and trailers. ... The partnership with HPCL and Swiggy Instamart is a massive commercial proof-of-concept for Time Technoplast's Type IV composite cylinders. If the pilot successfully expands, it will elevate the company's profile from a packaging manufacturer to an essential clean mobility technology player, driving valuation re-rating over the medium term."
