KRC Custom Manufacturing,,,,Exa,Stainless steel tubing supplier M&M International buys KC Tech,www.medicaldesignandoutsourcing.com (Jim Hammerand),2026-06-29T00:00:00.000Z,2026-06-29 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.medicaldesignandoutsourcing.com/stainless-steel-tubing-supplier-mm-international-buys-kc-tech/,"Stainless steel tubing supplier M&M International buys KC Tech ... # High-precision stainless steel tubing supplier M&M International buys KC Tech ... June 29, 2026 By Jim Hammerand ... M&M International established its stainless steel tubing manufacturing facility in South Korea in 2011 and expanded in 2017. [Photo courtesy of M&M International] ... M&M International has purchased KC Tech in a deal that combines two suppliers of high-precision stainless steel tubing for the medical device industry. ... Founded in 1993, Edison, New Jersey-based M&M produces 304, 316-grade and 17-7 stainless steel small-diameter tubing for high-precision applications including medical devices. The company operates manufacturing facilities in South Korea. ... Stamford, Connecticut-based private equity firm Gainline Capital Partners purchased M&M in July 2024 and hired a U.S.-based management team, including Jeff Robertson as CEO. Robertson was previously president at Secant Group, which won supplier of the year honors in 2023 from Edwards Lifesciences. ... “Our plan from the outset was to drive both organic growth and pursue strategic add-ons to scale the business,” Gainline Partner Rob Dellinger said in a news release announcing the KC Tech deal. “The acquisition of KC Tech is an exciting next step in M&M’s growth.” ... The deal will “significantly expand M&M’s manufacturing capabilities and broaden its product portfolio by adding KC Tech’s complementary line of larger diameter tubing,” Gainline said. ... “This acquisition will increase our ability to provide high-quality, specialized solutions to meet our customers’ evolving needs,” Robertson said. “Having worked with the KC Tech team for over a decade, we are confident not only in the quality of their manufacturing processes, but also in the talent of their management and employees, who will become part of M&M.” ... South Korea-based KC Tech has been a family business for more than 20 years, KC Tech CEO Terry Park said. ... “We share a commitment to innovation and customer service, and together we’ll deliver even greater value to the medical device supply chain,” Park said."
KRC Custom Manufacturing,,,,Exa,M&M Buys Partner KC Tech to Grow Tubing Portfolio,www.mddionline.com,2026-06-29T00:00:00.000Z,2026-06-29 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.mddionline.com/components/precision-tubing-manufacturer-m-m-international-adds-kc-tech-in-strategic-expansion,"M&M Buys Partner KC Tech to Grow Tubing Portfolio ... # Precision Tubing Manufacturer M&M International Adds KC Tech in Strategic Expansion ... Gainline Capital Partners said its portfolio company M&M International has acquired KC Tech, a strategic partner and fellow manufacturer that also focuses on the medical device market. ... The acquisition will significantly expand M&M’s manufacturing capabilities and broaden its product portfolio by adding KC Tech’s complementary line of larger-diameter tubing. ... “This acquisition will increase our ability to provide high-quality, specialized solutions to meet our customers’ evolving needs,” said Jeff Robertson. M&M’s CEO. “Having worked with the KC Tech team for over a decade, we are confident not only in the quality of their manufacturing processes but also in the talent of their management and employees, who will become part of M&M.” ... ""KC Tech has been a family business for over twenty years, and joining M&M marks an exciting new chapter for all of us,"" said Terry Park, CEO of KC Tech. ""We share a commitment to innovation and customer service, and together we'll deliver even greater value to the medical device supply chain."" ... Founded 33 years ago, M&M is a leading producer of 304, 316-grade, and 17-7 stainless steel small-diameter tubing for medical devices and other high-precision applications. Gainline acquired the company in July 2024 and hired an experienced U.S.-based management team to accelerate its next phase of growth. ... “Our plan from the outset was to drive both organic growth and pursue strategic add-ons to scale the business,” said Rob Dellinger, Partner at Gainline. “The acquisition of KC Tech is an exciting next step in M&M’s growth.”"
KRC Custom Manufacturing,,,,Exa,Custom CR Packaging: Concept to Delivery in 90 Days | TPC,www.thepkgco.com (Sam Baldonado),2026-06-26T00:00:00.000Z,2026-06-26 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.thepkgco.com/custom-cr-packaging-concept-to-delivery-90-days/,"Makes It Happen ... - By Sam Baldonado - June 26, 2026 ... The 90-day window from concept to delivered, finished, production-ready child-resistant packaging is not theoretical at TPC. It’s the result of 30 years of doing exactly this — building the relationships, the process, and the institutional knowledge that compress what takes other suppliers six months into a timeline that actually works for a brand’s business. ... This piece is about how that happens and what it means for cannabis, wellness, and regulated product brands evaluating their packaging partner. ... has in-house engineering capability that ... — it’ ... your product needs ... format that doesn’t ... dimensions don’t match any ... tin, we spec the ... Our CR tin line is an example of this in practice. Those formats didn’t exist before TPC developed them. We identified what premium cannabis brands needed, engineered the format, developed the closure mechanism, tested it to certification standard, and brought a product to market that is now one of the most recognizable CR formats in the category. That’s the engineering capability we bring to every project. ... in regulated markets, ... track record is not a soft credential. ... that clears compliance inspection and packaging that creates an operational crisis at the worst possible moment. ... TPC’s operations include wholly-owned capability in China — a team that manages the production process on the ground, coordinates directly with manufacturing partners, oversees quality at the production level, and handles logistics from completion through shipment. ... This is what makes the 90-day timeline reliable rather than optimistic. The variables that cause packaging projects to slip — production drift that doesn’t get caught, logistics delays that compound, documentation that has to be retroactively assembled — are the variables our China-side operation exists to eliminate. ... A packaging partner who can reliably take you from concept to delivered product in 90 days changes what’s possible for a brand’s launch planning. You’re not building a 6-month buffer into every packaging decision. You’re not launching with a placeholder format while the premium version catches up. You’re not finding out that the packaging you ordered doesn’t fit your product two weeks before a retail launch. ... The brands TPC works with in licensed cannabis markets, wellness, and regulated consumer products are brands that have learned — sometimes the hard way — that packaging is not a procurement transaction. It’s a production discipline. The supplier relationship you choose determines whether your packaging program is an asset or a source of ongoing operational friction. ... TPC’s CR packaging line covers tins, tubes, jars, bags, and topical formats— all made to order, all with full certification documentation, all produced with the same process and oversight that makes the 90-day timeline real. ... days realistic for custom decorated ... formats with custom decoration — ... -day window ... start, clean sample approval ... revision, and production-ready artwork files when decoration begins. Projects with custom tooling requirements or significant artwork preparation needs may run longer. ... with your specific format and timeline requirements for an ... What certification documentation does TPC provide with CR packaging orders? Full third-party laboratory test documentation covering the applicable standard (ASTM or 16 CFR §1700.20) is provided with every CR packaging order. This includes the underlying test report confirming child panel and adult panel pass rates for the specific format — not just a Certificate of Compliance. For brands managing compliance registers across multiple SKUs, our audit-readiness guide covers how to maintain documentation currency across reorders. ... Does TPC work with emerging brands or only established ones? TPC works with brands across the volume range. MOQ starts at 5,000 units for standard CR formats, which is accessible for new SKU launches and emerging brands as well as high-volume established programs. The process and the documentation standards are the same regardless of order size. ... What makes TPC’s CR tins different from catalog tins? TPC’s CR tin formats were engineered by TPC — they didn’t exist in the market before we developed them. The snap closure mechanism, the dimensional profile, the insert tray configurations, and the certification were all developed through our engineering process against the specific needs of cannabis pre-roll and infused product brands. That’s a different product than a catalog tin with your artwork applied. ... ### Child-Resistant CBD Packaging: What Hemp and Wellness Brands Need to Know Before Choosing a Format ... Across Bottles, ... ars, and Pumps ... less Packaging Line Planning: Building a Consistent System Across Bottles, ... ars, and Pumps ... Child-Resistant ... : The Complete Guide for Cannabis, Wellness, and ... ### Child-Resistant ... : The Complete Guide for Cannabis, Wellness, and Regulated Product Brands"
KRC Custom Manufacturing,,,,Exa,Inside A 40-Year Contract Manufacturing Playbook And How The Next Generation Is Rewriting It | Beauty Independent,www.beautyindependent.com (BI Staff),2026-06-23T00:00:00.000Z,2026-06-23 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.beautyindependent.com/acrl-40-year-contract-manufacturing-playbook-next-generation-rewriting-it/,"## As private equity activity in beauty picks back up and large players continue acquiring brands and capabilities, contract manufacturing is consolidating just as product cycles are compressing. Beauty brands are rethinking what they need from a lab partner. ... The role of contract manufacturers is changing quickly. Speed, flexibility and collaboration now matter just as much as technical expertise. That shift is playing out at Advanced Cosmetic Research Laboratories (ACRL), a privately owned manufacturer that has grown over four decades from a small operation into a 55-person company producing skincare, haircare and personal care products for emerging and prestige brands. ... What makes the company unusual in a manufacturing landscape increasingly dominated by consolidators is both its structure and leadership. It remains family-run and women-led. The business was founded in 1983 by Kitty Hunter, who unexpectedly found herself running a small cosmetic lab while still in law school. What began as a 10-person operation in a roughly 1,000-square-foot facility has since grown into a 55,000-square-foot manufacturing operation. ... A major turning point came decades later when Hunter’s daughter, Breslin Juan, officially joined the business six years ago. The addition of a second generation brought new energy into the company and a different way of operating. She brought a new perspective on how contract manufacturers need to evolve to support today’s beauty founders. ... Communication was one of the first areas Juan, VP of product development and operations, focused on. While responses were happening internally, they weren’t always happening at the speed founders expect. Juan pushed to reform that, introducing more proactive updates, faster response times and regular touchpoints between clients and project managers. Today’s founders expect visibility into timelines, development progress and production without having to chase for updates, and manufacturers are expected to match that pace. ... Another shift in the manufacturing industry is the speed at which brands are calling for products to go from concept to shelf. For manufacturers, that has made development timelines and operational flexibility far more important than they were even a few years ago. ... At ACRL, initial formulation development typically takes two to four weeks for the first sample, with production timelines for new launches averaging eight to 10 weeks depending on complexity and materials. That urgency reflects broader changes across the beauty landscape. Social media can create overnight desire for a specific ingredient, texture or product category. Brands want to react in real time rather than endure traditional yearlong development cycles. ... At the same time, consolidation is reshaping the contract manufacturing sector, with private equity rolling up brands and capabilities. Still, founders face a wide range of options from global production giants to smaller independent partners. Large manufacturers often prioritize high-volume production runs, which can leave emerging brands competing for production slots and facing longer lead times. ... Smaller labs, by contrast, can offer more flexibility, closer communication and a more collaborative approach, factors that can make a meaningful difference in the early stages of brand building. “Your manufacturer should be an extension of your team,” Juan says. “It’s not just a supplier relationship. It’s a partnership that impacts everything from timelines to product quality.” ... That perspective helps bridge the gap between brand founders and manufacturing teams and has been especially valuable for younger founder-led brands that place a premium on aesthetics, user experience and brand storytelling alongside formulation performance. It also influences where the ACRL concentrates its innovation work. ... Much of the current demand is for high-performance formulas with modern textures: gel-creams, serum-like moisturizers and hybrid products that deliver results but feel lightweight and elegant on the skin. Treatment-driven haircare is another area of growing interest. ... The contract manufacturing landscape will likely continue evolving as consolidation accelerates, ingredient innovation cycles shorten and brand founders push for faster timelines and greater transparency from suppliers. For independent manufacturers, the opportunity lies in becoming true collaborators, partners that help brands move quickly, innovate effectively and scale when demand spikes. For beauty founders navigating their first manufacturing relationship, the takeaway is simple: Choosing the right partner can shape everything that comes next."
KRC Custom Manufacturing,,,,Exa,K-CAB Enters Commercialization Countdown in U.S. - Businesskorea,www.businesskorea.co.kr (Choi Mun-hee),2026-06-23T00:00:00.000Z,2026-06-23 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.businesskorea.co.kr/news/articleView.html?idxno=271866,"-CAB Enters Commercialization Countdown in U.S. - Businesskorea ... ## Sebela is recruiting quality and validation executives in Massachusetts ahead of potential commercialization ... K-CAB, a gastroesophageal reflux disease treatment and South Korea’s 30th novel drug developed by HK inno.N, has finally entered the countdown to commercialization in the United States, the world’s largest pharmaceutical market. ... Observations suggest that K-CAB’s North American supply chain management strategy, which had been veiled in secrecy, is coming to the surface as its local partner moves to inject key executive-level personnel in quality assurance and quality control to spearhead commercial production and local launch following approval from the U.S. Food and Drug Administration. ... According to the industry on June 23, Sebela, which holds full North American rights to K-CAB, recently posted a job opening for a quality control director to work at the Holbrook, Massachusetts site of its subsidiary Braintree. In the U.S. biopharmaceutical industry, a director-level position is a high-ranking executive role that oversees current good manufacturing practices and quality. ... The main responsibilities of the quality control director will include compliance with the U.S. Food and Drug Administration’s current Good Manufacturing Practice, testing management, response to FDA inspections, and support for commercial production and new product introduction. ... Alongside this, the company is also recruiting a commissioning, qualification, and validation engineer and a document management representative. These roles will be responsible for equipment commissioning and qualification, manufacturing and quality document management, and regulatory inspection responses. ... The reason for the attention on this job posting is that it serves as a smoking gun to understand K-CAB’s local finished product supply structure in the U.S., which had been kept thoroughly behind the curtain until now. ... The director’s primary job scope specifies support for commercial production and new product introduction along with FDA inspection response, and the Holbrook work location houses a contract development and manufacturing organization facility owned by Braintree. ... The industry is focusing on the possibility that the expansion of manufacturing and quality personnel is a preparatory step with commercial production and product launch in mind following approval. ... K-CAB is a Potassium-Competitive Acid Blocker class treatment developed in-house by HK inno.N. It has grown rapidly in the domestic market, capitalizing on its faster onset of efficacy compared to existing Proton Pump Inhibitor class drugs and the fact that it can be taken regardless of meals. Last year, its outpatient prescription sales surpassed 200 billion won (about $133 million), making it a core product that accounts for more than 20% of HK inno.N’s total revenue. ... Currently, Phathom’s Voquezna is the only commercialized product in the U.S. Potassium-Competitive Acid Blocker market. Voquezna was developed in-house by Japan’s Takeda, and Phathom licensed the rights for the U.S., Europe, and Canada to handle its approval and sales. ... However, K-CAB’s commercial product manufacturing method in the U.S. remains behind the curtain. While HK inno.N previously stated at the time of its contract with Sebela that it would push to supply active pharmaceutical ingredients for its U.S. expansion, it did not specifically disclose where the finished product would be manufactured. ... In relation to this, an official from HK inno.N stated, “Sebela has full authority and is proceeding with K-CAB’s U.S. business,” adding, “It is difficult to confirm details regarding the production and supply system following approval.” ... Meanwhile, according to pharmaceutical market research firm IQVIA, the U.S. peptic ulcer drug market, which includes gastroesophageal reflux disease, was recorded at $2.8 billion (approximately 4 trillion won) as of 2021. The industry expects it to grow to $5.7 billion (approximately 8.8 trillion won) by 2036."
KRC Custom Manufacturing,,,,Exa,"Kiremko, Idaho Steel and Reyco brands join forces with Tummers and Van Der Giessen – Potato News Today",www.potatonewstoday.com,2026-06-22T00:00:00.000Z,2026-06-22 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.potatonewstoday.com/2026/06/22/kiremko-idaho-steel-and-reyco-brands-announce-the-acquisition-of-tummers-and-van-der-giessen/,"Kiremko, Idaho Steel and Reyco brands announce the acquisition of Tummers and Van Der Giessen – Potato News Today ... The strategic combination creates a stronger global partner for french fry and potato flake processing solutions.The group will reach approximately €400 million annual revenues. ... A leading food processing equipment group held by an independently managed investment company affiliated to Investindustrial, and comprising the Kiremko, Idaho Steel and Reyco brands, has completed the acquisition of 100% of Tummers Group and Van der Giessen, bringing together highly respected specialist companies with complementary capabilities across the potato processing value chain. ... The acquisition marks an important step in building a stronger global platform for the food processing equipment industry. By combining Kiremko, Idaho Steel and Reyco’s deep expertise in frozen potato processing with Tummers’ expertise in fully integrated potato flake lines, drum drying technology, and related industrial solutions, and Van der Giessen’s expertise in equipment installation and on-site services, the enlarged group is now positioned to offer customers a broader portfolio of solutions, stronger innovation capabilities and expanded global support. ... The combination will also accelerate the development of recovery solutions focused on water, waste heat and steam, helping customers improve resource efficiency, reduce energy consumption and lower total cost of ownership, while supporting the industry’s broader need for more sustainable and efficient processing technologies. ... Together, the companies will form a broader and more integrated industrial group serving customers across both french fries and potato flakes, with a greater technical expertise and a stronger ability to invest in automation, digital capabilities, global reach, stronger customer service and faster local responsiveness across Europe, North America and Asia. ... “Today marks an important milestone for our customers, our associates and our future. With Tummers and Van der Giessen joining Kiremko, Idaho Steel and Reyco, we are bringing together complementary technology players in french fries and potato flakes applications into one stronger global group. Our ambition is clear: to be the partner customers trust for high-quality equipment, innovative solutions, reliable service and long-term value creation across the potato processing industry.” ... site service capabilities ... The enlarged group will continue to invest and build on the heritage of each company. Kiremko, Idaho Steel, Reyco, Tummers and Van der Giessen will remain valued specialist brands, carrying forward their technical expertise, customer relationships and strong reputations in the market, while continuing to create value for customers in their respective areas of expertise. ... The Group will announce its new corporate name shortly. The new Group identity will support the group’s long-term growth and vision, while Kiremko, Idaho Steel, Reyco, Tummers and Van der Giessen continue to operate as trusted specialist brands."
KRC Custom Manufacturing,,,,Exa,Kleen-Rite-linked LLC pays nearly $9 Million for R&S Manufacturing property – Columbia Spy,columbiaspy.com (Author SPY),2026-06-22T00:00:00.000Z,2026-06-22 00:00:00+00:00,,https://columbiaspy.com/2026/06/22/kleen-rite-linked-llc-pays-nearly-9-million-for-rs-manufacturing-property/,"Kleen-Rite-linked LLC pays nearly $9 Million for R&S Manufacturing property – Columbia Spy ... A newly formed LLC tied to Kleen-Rite Corp.’s Columbia headquarters has acquired the longtime home of R&S Manufacturing on Mill Street in a deal worth just under $9 million, according to property and corporate records. ... A deed recently recorded in Lancaster County shows R&S Manufacturing LLC and R&S Manufacturing Corp. conveyed the Mill Street property to an entity called 525 Mill St. LLC for $8,959,680. ... Records filed with the Pennsylvania Department of State show 525 Mill St. LLC was formed on April 3, 2026, as a domestic limited liability company. The LLC’s listed address — 257 S. 9th St., Columbia — is the corporate headquarters of Kleen-Rite Corp., the car wash supply and equipment company that has operated out of Columbia for more than 50 years. ... R&S Manufacturing has operated at 525 Mill St. for nearly three decades, making steel rolling doors, dock levelers and related commercial hardware under the R&S Manufacturing and Lasko Metal Products names. ... Neither Kleen-Rite nor R&S Manufacturing has publicly confirmed a connection between the companies or announced plans for the property. It’s not yet clear whether Kleen-Rite intends to use the site for warehouse or distribution space, or whether R&S will continue operating there under a lease arrangement. ... Kleen-Rite, headquartered at 257 S. 9th St., also operates distribution centers in Mount Joy, Pennsylvania, as well as in Nevada, Texas and Missouri."
