Jindal Films,,,,Exa,Jindal (India) Partners with Dongkuk India for Long-Term Coated Steel Supply to Home Appliance Sector,ntmedia.in (Correspondent),2026-06-30T00:00:00.000Z,2026-06-30 00:00:00+00:00,,https://ntmedia.in/jindal-india-dongkuk-india-long-term-coated-steel-supply-home-appliance-sector/,"Jindal (India) Partners with Dongkuk India for Long-Term Coated Steel Supply to Home Appliance Sector August 3, 2026 # Jindal (India) Partners with Dongkuk India for Long-Term Coated Steel Supply to Home Appliance Sector June 30, 2026 ... Mumbai: Jindal (India) Limited has entered into a long-term supply partnership with Dongkuk India to supply coated steel products for India’s expanding home appliance manufacturing industry. ... Under the agreement, Jindal (India), a part of the BC Jindal Group, will supply coated steel products to Dongkuk India’s manufacturing facilities. The products covered under the partnership include Pre-Painted Galvanised Iron (PPGI), Galvanised Plain Skin Passed (GPSP), and Alu-Zinc Coated Steel. ... According to the company, the collaboration is aimed at addressing the increasing demand for coated steel products driven by the growth of India’s home appliance and consumer durables sector. Rising household incomes, urbanisation, and increasing domestic manufacturing have contributed to higher demand from appliance manufacturers. ... A spokesperson for Jindal (India) Limited said the partnership combines the company’s manufacturing capabilities with Dongkuk India’s processing expertise to support a reliable domestic supply chain for appliance manufacturers. The company also stated that the collaboration is expected to strengthen its presence in the Original Equipment Manufacturer (OEM) segment. ... The coated steel products supplied under the agreement will be distributed through Dongkuk India’s existing supply network to leading domestic and international home appliance manufacturers operating in India. ... Jindal (India) Limited stated that the partnership is expected to contribute to its business growth over the coming years while expanding its presence in India’s home appliance manufacturing sector. ... The company operates two manufacturing facilities in Howrah, West Bengal, equipped with colour coating and metal coating technologies. Its product portfolio includes sheets, pipes, and aluminium foil. The company markets colour-coated sheet products under brands such as Jindal Sabrang and Jindal NeuColor+. ... According to the company, coated flat steel products currently account for around 70% of its total production, making the segment a key focus area for future expansion. ... Founded in 1952, Jindal (India) Limited is part of the BC Jindal Group, which operates across packaging films, energy, and steel products. The company said that while a significant share of its revenue has traditionally come from Eastern India, it has expanded its presence across North and West India as part of its pan-India growth strategy, supported by increasing infrastructure and industrial development in these regions."
Jindal Films,,,,Exa,Jindal India seals long-term Dongkuk deal to power India's appliance manufacturing boom | Indiablooms - First Portal on Digital News Management,www.indiablooms.com (Indiablooms),2026-06-30T00:00:00.000Z,2026-06-30 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.indiablooms.com/finance/jindal-india-seals-long-term-dongkuk-deal-to-power-indias-appliance-manufacturing-boom/details,"Jindal India seals long-term Dongkuk deal to power India's appliance manufacturing boom | Indiablooms - First Portal on Digital News Management ... # Jindal India seals long-term Dongkuk deal to power India's appliance manufacturing boom ... | Jun 30, ... 26, at 05:31 pm ... Jindal (India) Limited, part of the BC Jindal Group and one of the leading downstream steel products manufacturers in the country, on Tuesday announced a long-term supply partnership with Dongkuk India to cater to the growing demand for high-quality coated steel products from the country's rapidly expanding home appliance industry. ... The BC Jindal Group, led by Shyam Sunder Jindal, Promoter, BC Jindal Group, as part of the arrangement, will supply premium coated steel products to Dongkuk Steel India's manufacturing facilities. Under the long-term supply framework, Jindal (India) Limited will offer products that will include Pre-Painted Galvanised Iron (PPGI), Galvanised Plain Skin Passed (GPSP) and Alu-Zinc Coated Steel. ... “India's home appliance and consumer durables industry is witnessing strong growth, driven by rising household incomes, urbanization and increasing demand for locally manufactured products. As the country's leading downstream steel product manufacturer, we see significant opportunities to cater to this expanding sector. ... “Our partnership with Dongkuk India enables us to combine our manufacturing strengths with their processing expertise, creating a reliable domestic supply ecosystem for appliance manufacturers. This collaboration will help us strengthen our presence in the OEM segment while supporting India's growing focus on quality manufacturing and supply chain resilience,” said a Jindal (India) Limited spokesperson. ... The products will be supplied to leading Original Equipment Manufacturers (OEMs) through Dongkuk India’s established supply channels, catering to leading global and domestic home appliances brands. ... The partnership is expected to make a meaningful contribution to Jindal (India) Limited's growth trajectory over the next few years and further strengthen its foothold in India's expanding home appliance market. ... The BC Jindal Group’s Jindal (India) Limited has two modern factories located at Howrah, West Bengal, and its capabilities include advanced colour coating and metal coating technologies. ... With strong brands like Jindal Sabrang and Jindal NeuColor+, Jindal (India) Limited dominates the colour-coated sheets market, particularly in Eastern India, and maintains a significant presence across the country. ... Currently, 70% of the company’s total production consists of coated flat steel products, which remains a key area of focus for future growth and expansion. ... (India) ... of the oldest players in the ... steel product industry. ... A significant portion of the company’s revenue has been driven by the eastern region, followed by South India. However, over the years, the company has adopted a pan-India strategy, expanding into North and ... West India through increased capacity growth. Jindal (India) Limited sees immense potential in these regions due to the rapid growth in infrastructure development and industrial projects. ... The group, led by Shyam Sunder Jindal, Promoter, BC Jindal Group is engaged in a diverse portfolio of businesses like packaging films, energy & steel products. ... Jindal India seals long-term Dongkuk deal to power India's appliance manufacturing boom Tue, Jun 30 2026"
Jindal Films,,,,Exa,RFBL secures packaging materials supply order from 3B Films,www.packaging-gateway.com (Vidhya Edwards Munnangi),2026-06-30T00:00:00.000Z,2026-06-30 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.packaging-gateway.com/news/rfbl-3b-films-packaging-materials/,"RFBL secures packaging materials supply order from 3B Films ... # RFBL secures packaging materials supply order from 3B Films ... RFBL Flexi Pack has received an order worth Rs200m ($2m) from 3B Films to supply transparent and metallised films, with execution planned over the next four months. ... The company said the agreement is not a related-party transaction and that promoters have no interest in it. ... In fiscal year 2026, revenue from operations increased to Rs1.48bn, compared with Rs1.35bn a year earlier. ... The order follows other recent moves by the company, including a plan to set up a wholly owned subsidiary in the United Arab Emirates. The proposed unit would back its overseas expansion plans and add to its presence in international markets."
Jindal Films,,,,Exa,Jindal India seals long-term Dongkuk deal to power India's appliance manufacturing boom | Indiablooms - First Portal on Digital News Management,www.indiablooms.com (Indiablooms),2026-06-30T00:00:00.000Z,2026-06-30 00:00:00+00:00,,http://www.indiablooms.com/phoenix/public/index.php/finance/jindal-india-seals-long-term-dongkuk-deal-to-power-indias-appliance-manufacturing-boom/details,"Jindal India seals long-term Dongkuk deal to power India's appliance manufacturing boom | Indiablooms - First Portal on Digital News Management ... India Blooms News Service | @indiablooms | Jun 30, 2026, at 05:31 pm Jindal (India) Limited, part of the BC Jindal Group and one of the leading downstream steel products manufacturers in the country, on Tuesday announced a long-term supply partnership with Dongkuk India to cater to the growing demand for high-quality coated steel products from the country's rapidly expanding home appliance industry. The BC Jindal Group, led by Shyam Sunder Jindal, Promoter, BC Jindal Group, as part of the arrangement, will supply premium coated steel products to Dongkuk Steel India's manufacturing facilities. Under the long-term supply framework, Jindal (India) Limited will offer products that will include Pre-Painted Galvanised Iron (PPGI), Galvanised Plain Skin Passed (GPSP) and Alu-Zinc Coated Steel. ... > “Our partnership with Dongkuk India enables us to combine our manufacturing strengths with their processing expertise, creating a reliable domestic supply ecosystem for appliance manufacturers. This collaboration will help us strengthen our presence in the OEM segment while supporting India's growing focus on quality manufacturing and supply chain resilience,” said a Jindal (India) Limited spokesperson. The products will be supplied to leading Original Equipment Manufacturers (OEMs) through Dongkuk India’s established supply channels, catering to leading global and domestic home appliances brands. The partnership is expected to make a meaningful contribution to Jindal (India) Limited's growth trajectory over the next few years and further strengthen its foothold in India's expanding home appliance market. The BC ... al Group’s ... (India) Limited has two modern factories located at Howrah, West Bengal, and its capabilities include advanced colour coating and metal coating technologies. ... diverse range of products across three major divisions that include sheets, pipes, and aluminium foil ... With strong brands like Jindal Sabrang and Jindal NeuColor+, Jindal (India) Limited dominates the colour-coated sheets market, particularly in Eastern India, and maintains a significant presence across the country. ... > Currently, 70% of the company’s total production consists of coated flat steel products, which remains a key area of focus for future growth and expansion. ... Jindal (India) Limited is one of the oldest players in the downstream steel product industry. A significant portion of the company’s revenue has been driven by the eastern region, followed by South India. However, over the years, the company has adopted a pan-India strategy, expanding into North and ... West India through increased capacity growth. Jindal (India) Limited sees immense potential in these regions due to the rapid growth in infrastructure development and industrial projects. ... Jindal (India) Limited, founded in ... 1952, is part of BC Jindal Group, a leading Indian conglomerate. The group, led by Shyam Sunder Jindal, Promoter, BC Jindal Group is engaged in a diverse portfolio of businesses like packaging films, energy & steel products."
Jindal Films,,,,Exa,New Tampon Line: Premium Care Expansion | AIMISIN,www.newclears.com,2026-06-24T00:00:00.000Z,2026-06-24 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.newclears.com/news/new-tampon-production-line-launched-expanding-premium-feminine-care-capacity-aimisin/,"New Tampon Line: Premium Care Expansion | AIMISIN ... # New Tampon Production Line Launched: Expanding Premium Feminine Care Capacity | AIMISIN 2026-06-24 To meet the fast-growing global demand for high-quality, safe, and eco-friendly feminine hygiene products, our company has officially put a new professional tampon production line into operation. This capacity expansion marks a key strategic upgrade in our feminine care product portfolio, enabling us to deliver more standardized, comfortable, and trustworthy menstrual care solutions for global distributors, retailers, and brand partners. With upgraded automated production equipment and optimized manufacturing workflows, the new line greatly improves production efficiency, product consistency, and product safety standards, laying a solid foundation for large-scale order fulfillment and high-end customized cooperation. As a professional FDA certificated tampon manufacturer, we have always adhered to strict international health and safety standards throughout product research and development, production, and quality inspection. All products produced by the new production line fully comply with FDA regulatory requirements, with rigorous testing implemented for raw material safety, production hygiene, finished product sterilization, and physical performance. Every batch of tampons undergoes systematic quality screening to eliminate potential safety risks such as harmful residues and unqualified materials. This strict compliance system ensures that our feminine care products can stably serve global mainstream markets and meet the stringent access standards of North America, Europe, and other regions. ... Equipped with advanced flexible production systems, the new line focuses on upgrading high-end feminine care products, especially the development and mass production of organic tampon for feminine daily care. Different from ordinary synthetic material tampons, our organic series adopts pure natural organic cotton as the core raw material, free of fluorescent agents, fragrances, chlorine bleaching, and synthetic additives. The ultra-soft and breathable material effectively reduces skin friction and sensitivity, bringing gentler and more comfortable protection for women with sensitive skin. The launch of the new production line solves the capacity bottleneck of organic tampon mass production, enabling stable and continuous supply of high-quality organic feminine care products for global users. ... The completion of the new production line also comprehensively strengthens our service capability for tampon wholesale business. In the past, high-end organic tampon products were restricted by production capacity, making it difficult to meet the growing bulk order demands of cross-border distributors, supermarket chains, and maternal and child stores. The newly launched automated line achieves high-speed, standardized, and large-scale production, greatly shortening order delivery cycles while maintaining stable product quality. Whether customers need conventional standard tampons or customized organic tampons with exclusive packaging and specifications, we can efficiently complete bulk production and timely delivery. ... In addition to capacity improvement, this expansion further enriches our product matrix and market competitiveness. We can now provide diversified product combinations covering daily conventional tampons, high-absorption night-use tampons, and pure organic skin-friendly tampons, fully meeting the differentiated needs of different age groups, usage scenarios, and market positioning. Meanwhile, we continue to support OEM and ODM customized services, helping global partners create personalized feminine hygiene product lines and quickly occupy the high-end feminine care market. Looking ahead, we will continue to invest in production technology upgrading and product innovation, always focusing on women’s healthy and dignified care. By virtue of standardized FDA certification, reliable large-scale production capacity, and professional wholesale and customized service systems, we will further expand global market layout, provide more cost-effective and high-quality feminine hygiene products for global partners and end users, and consolidate our position as a trustworthy supplier in the global feminine care industry."
Jindal Films,,,,Exa,"Minority Shareholders Move Apex Court Against Jindal Poly Over ₹2,000 Crore Arbitration Referral Order",www.sahi.com,2026-06-23T00:00:00.000Z,2026-06-23 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.sahi.com/news/minority-shareholders-move-apex-court-against-jindal-poly-over-2-000-crore-arbitration-referral-order-1618-PE1_COR,"Move Apex Court Against ... Over ₹2,000 Crore Arbitration Referral Order ... # Minority Shareholders Move Apex Court Against Jindal Poly Over ₹2,000 Crore Arbitration Referral Order ... Minority shareholders are challenging an order that referred their class-action suit to arbitration, seeking a direct judicial review of a ₹2,000 crore transaction involving Jindal Poly Films. ... Market snapshot: The Supreme Court of India has been approached to overturn a crucial order regarding a class-action lawsuit against Jindal Poly Films. This legal escalation stems from a long-standing dispute involving minority shareholders who oppose an arbitration referral concerning a multi-billion rupee asset divestment. ... - Disputed Transaction Value: ₹2,000 crore - Promoter Holding: 74.55% as of Q4 FY26 - Minority Shareholder Stake Involved: Substantial retail and HNI block - Legal Jurisdiction: Supreme Court of India ... - Shift from NCLT/Tribunal proceedings to the Supreme Court (Apex Court) level. - Move to overturn the arbitration referral which previously stalled the class-action suit. - Increased legal overhang on the company’s structural integrity and previous asset sales. ... - The Supreme Court's intervention could potentially halt or reverse the arbitration process. - A class-action suit of this scale is rare in the Indian packaging sector, signaling heightened activist pressure. - Investor sentiment may turn cautious due to prolonged legal uncertainty surrounding corporate governance. ... This legal challenge represents a critical test of minority shareholder rights in India. By moving to the Supreme Court, the shareholders are looking to bypass the arbitration route, which they likely perceive as less transparent or restrictive. For JINDALPOLY, this prolongs the shadow over its ₹2,000 crore divestment deal, potentially impacting future capital allocation and valuation multiples. ... The legal dispute creates a 'legal overhang,' often leading to a valuation discount. Sector-wide, it underscores the risks associated with large-scale divestments where minority interests are not aligned. Capital allocation may be frozen until legal clarity is achieved. ... Legal uncertainty at the Supreme Court level regarding a ₹2,000 crore transaction typically leads to selling pressure and reduced institutional participation. ... Underweight: Jindal Poly Films, Holding Companies with Legal Disputes ... Supreme Court admission of ... - Interim stay orders on previous tribunal rulings ... - Quarterly earnings volatility due to legal provisions ... The Indian packaging and film industry has seen consolidation recently. However, legal hurdles regarding asset sales to private equity or global majors can set precedents for how future domestic M&A deals are structured under the Companies Act. ... - Potential reversal of asset sale proceeds if the court finds procedural lapses. - Damage to corporate reputation and institutional investor trust. - Contingent liabilities arising from legal penalties or compensation. ... In the last 90 days, Jindal Poly Films reported a marginal increase in specialty film exports, though EBITDA margins remained under pressure due to raw material costs. The company recently concluded a small-scale capacity expansion in Noida. ... While operational metrics remain stable, the Supreme Court's decision will be the primary driver for the stock’s trajectory, determining if the ₹2,000 crore deal remains legally sound. ... Minority shareholders are contesting a ₹2,000 crore asset sale and the subsequent referral of their class-action lawsuit to arbitration rather than a public court hearing. ... Yes, the current appeal specifically mentions that more minority shareholders wish to join the case, which could increase the collective claim value beyond ₹2,000 crore."
Jindal Films,,,,Exa,Quebec film recycler expands into Mississippi - Resource Recycling,resource-recycling.com (Antoinette Smith),2026-06-18T00:00:00.000Z,2026-06-18 00:00:00+00:00,,https://resource-recycling.com/plastics/2026/06/18/quebec-film-recycler-expands-into-mississippi/,"Quebec film recycler expands into Mississippi - Resource Recycling ... # Quebec film recycler expands into Mississippi ... Montreal-based Gould Industries has acquired a Mississippi plant, giving the plastic film recycler its first US site. ... Founded in 1954, Gould Industries recycles LLDPE film for private-label trash bags and related materials. The former Gigantic Bag plant in Summit, Mississippi, has more than 100,000 square feet of space on 6 acres, and after adding capacity will be able to recycle up to 10 million pounds of plastic annually. The site also has a CN rail siding that can accommodate 30 rail cars. The Mississippi Development Authority valued the investment at US $14 million. ... “Gould Industries has made a conscious decision to invest south of the border at a time of economic uncertainty between Canada and the United States, and when many Canadian companies are changing hands,” the company said. “This strategic decision goes against the grain. It is driven by a vision of long-term growth and the conviction that Quebec’s manufacturing expertise can make its mark across North America.” ... For years, Gould had sought an acquisition opportunity, and the Mississippi plant “just fell in my lap,” president and owner Frederico Panetta told Plastics Recycling Update. ... And in early April, he received “a warm welcome” from Gov. Tate Reeves, who sent him a letter supporting the company’s vision and the 75 jobs retained at the facility, which Panetta described as “distressed.” The state development authority said the plant would also add 65 more jobs. ... With the Montreal plant reaching full production capacity servicing customers such as Costco, Home Depot and most major Canadian retailers, Gould wa to increase production capacity. The company plans to migrate LLDPE trash bag capabilities to the new site, which will exclusively source post-use film from the US. ... In the coming months, Gould will modernize the site and integrate its corporate culture, “notably through initiatives aimed at humanizing workspaces, strengthening the teams’ sense of belonging and visually marking the premises,” including the creation of a mural that reflects Gould’s identity, Panetta said. “Our goal is to build a strong local team while bringing additional opportunities for development and long-term growth in the region.” ... The Montreal operation will become the company’s head office, and Gould will retain all local jobs in Quebec. In addition, the company will establish a single training and development center for employees on both sides of the border. ... In its statement, Gould pointed out that its CFO Marc-Antoine Laberge “coordinated the entire transaction like a chef d’orchestre.” ... Looking ahead, Panetta indicated that this would not be the last acquisition for the company. “I do have a serious appetite for growth.” ... He said he leverages the company’s family-owned approach and touts its status as an increasingly rare privately owned firm. “I’m still the 100% owner of these two factories with this acquisition. We don’t have an investment fund behind us,” he said, “absolutely not.” ... “I walk the floor in the back, I know how to unscrew every machine, every die myself, so I’m very hands-on ... That familiarity with the literal nuts and bolts of the operation enables opportunities with older plants “that are more tired,” which can make offloading more challenging for current owners, he said. ... “We do have the bandwidth to tackle a few more investments in the same field. So I would say that we’re going to get this one under the belt and lock it in, and the next one will follow pretty closely.”"
Jindal Films,,,,Exa,Jindal Poly Films Ltd Locks at Upper Circuit With 4.19% G...,www.marketsmojo.com,2026-06-10T00:00:00.000Z,2026-06-10 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.marketsmojo.com/news/stocks-in-action/jindal-poly-films-ltd-hits-upper-circuit-amid-strong-buying-momentum-4043824,"Jindal Poly Films Ltd Locks at Upper Circuit With 4.19% G... ... # Jindal Poly Films Ltd Locks at Upper Circuit With 4.19% Gain — Buyers Queue, Sellers Absent Jun 10 2026 10:00 AM IST ... At Rs 703.35, the buying was done — not because demand dried up, but because the exchange wouldn't let the stock go any higher. Jindal Poly Films Ltd locked at its upper circuit of 4.19% on 10 Jun 2026, with buyers queuing and no sellers willing to part with shares. ... The stock, trading in the EQ series, hit its upper circuit price band of 5%, closing at Rs 697.95 after touching an intraday high of Rs 703.35. This 4.19% gain represents the maximum allowed daily increase under the current price band rules. The circuit mechanism effectively froze trading at the ceiling price, signalling that demand exceeded what the price band could accommodate. The total traded volume was 0.29 lakh shares, with a turnover of approximately Rs 2.02 crore. This volume is lower than typical trading sessions, a mechanical consequence of the circuit lock that restricts price movement and liquidity. What does the full demand picture look like for Jindal Poly Films Ltd once the circuit unlocks and normal trading resumes? ... Delivery volumes provide the clearest insight into the quality of the buying on a circuit day. On 9 Jun 2026, the delivery volume surged to 32,630 shares, a rise of 110.22% compared to the five-day average. This sharp increase in delivery volume suggests that the shares traded were largely taken into long-term holdings rather than being flipped intraday. Such a rise in delivery during an upper circuit day is a strong signal of genuine buying conviction. However, the total traded volume remains modest, reflecting the circuit's impact on liquidity rather than a lack of interest. Is Jindal Poly Films Ltd's 4.19% surge backed by improving fundamentals or is this a liquidity-driven micro-cap move? The delivery data leans towards conviction, but liquidity constraints remain a factor. ... Jindal Poly Films Ltd closed above its 5-day, 20-day, 100-day, and 200-day moving averages, signalling a generally bullish trend. However, it remains slightly below its 50-day moving average, indicating some resistance at intermediate-term levels. The stock's position relative to these averages suggests that the upper circuit day was not an isolated spike but rather a continuation of an existing upward momentum. The stock has been gaining for two consecutive days, delivering a cumulative return of 9.72% in this period, outperforming its packaging sector by 4.23% and the Sensex by 3.74 percentage points. This trend confirmation adds weight to the quality of the move, although the 50-day average remains a key level to watch for sustained strength. ... With a market capitalisation of approximately Rs 3,056 crore, Jindal Poly Films Ltd is classified as a small-cap stock. Its liquidity profile is moderate, with a trade size capacity of around Rs 0.05 crore based on 2% of the five-day average traded value. While this level of liquidity is sufficient for retail and small institutional investors, it remains limited for larger trades, especially in the context of an upper circuit day where order books tend to thin out. This liquidity constraint is a critical consideration for investors, as entering or exiting sizeable positions could prove challenging without impacting the price. The circuit lock amplifies this effect, as the price ceiling restricts natural price discovery and can mask latent demand or supply. The circuit is hit and buyers are still queuing — but with near-zero liquidity and a Rs 3,056 crore market cap, should you be chasing Jindal Poly Films Ltd? The complete analysis puts the circuit in context. ... The intraday range for Jindal Poly Films Ltd on 10 Jun 2026 was relatively narrow, with a low of Rs 669.90 and a high of Rs 703.35. The stock touched the upper circuit late in the session, indicating a gradual build-up of buying pressure rather than a sudden spike. This pattern is typical for circuit hits, where the price gravitates towards the ceiling as buyers absorb available supply. The narrow range near the circuit price suggests that the market was unable to push the price beyond the regulatory limit, leaving unfilled demand on the buy side. Such price action often precedes a period of consolidation or a breakout once the circuit restrictions are lifted. ... Operating within the packaging industry, Jindal Poly Films Ltd has maintained a steady presence in the small-cap segment. While the company’s fundamentals are not detailed here, the recent price action and delivery volume surge suggest that market participants are responding to factors beyond short-term speculation. The packaging sector itself has shown resilience, with the stock outperforming its peers in the latest session. However, the absence of detailed fundamental data in this analysis means that the price move should be interpreted primarily through the lens of market mechanics and technical signals. ... The upper circuit hit at a 5% price band capped Jindal Poly Films Ltd’s gain at 4.19%, reflecting strong buying interest that exceeded what the market could accommodate within regulatory limits. The surge in delivery volume by over 110% against the five-day average is a compelling indicator of conviction buying rather than mere speculative trading. Coupled with the stock’s position above most key moving averages, the technical backdrop supports the quality of this move. However, the liquidity profile of this small-cap stock remains a cautionary factor. The limited trade size capacity and the circuit-induced price freeze mean that investors face challenges in executing large trades without price impact. After a 4.19% single-day gain at upper circuit, is Jindal Poly Films Ltd still worth considering or has the move already happened? The multi-factor analysis weighs the data."
Jindal Films,,,,Exa,"Jindal Poly Films dispute, India's first class action suit, sent by Supreme Court to arbitration - The Economic Times",economictimes.indiatimes.com (Indu Bhan),2026-06-09T00:00:00.000Z,2026-06-09 00:00:00+00:00,,https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/industry/indl-goods/svs/packaging/jindal-poly-films-dispute-indias-first-class-action-suit-sent-by-supreme-court-to-arbitration/articleshow/131618898.cms,"Jindal Poly Films dispute, India's first class action suit, sent by Supreme Court to arbitration - The Economic Times ... Business News› Industry› Ind'l Goods/Svs› Packaging›Jindal Poly Films dispute, India's first class action suit, sent by Supreme Court to arbitration ... # Jindal Poly Films dispute, India's first class action suit, sent by Supreme Court to arbitration ... Jindal Poly Films ... 's first class action ... , sent by ... Court to arbitration ... The Supreme Court has sent a minority shareholder dispute involving Jindal Poly Films to arbitration. This decision overturns earlier orders from the National Company Law Tribunal and Appellate Tribunal. The case concerns allegations of asset stripping and financial losses to shareholders. The arbitration will take place in Delhi. ... US will hit Iran 'very hard' tonight: Trump plans to take over main Iranian oil terminalsNew Delhi: Setting aside India's first class action suit, the Supreme Court referred the minority shareholder dispute against Jindal Poly Films to arbitration after both sides agreed to a consent order on Monday.The apex court appointed retired chief justice of the Madras High Court, Manindra Mohan Shrivastava, as the sole arbitrator to decide upon the disputes expeditiously. The seat of arbitration will be Delhi, a bench of justices Prashant Kumar Mishra and Atul S. Chandurkar said while setting aside the National Company Law Tribunal's February 5 order and the National Company Law Appellate Tribunal's February 26 order which had admitted and upheld the class action petition, respectively, against Jindal Poly Films filed by its shareholders, who hold 4.99% of the share capital, under Section 245 of the Companies Act, 2013. ... The tribunal had noted that Section 245 explicitly contemplates class actions for acts prejudicial to the interests of the company, not just its members, and that the reliefs available are not limited to ongoing conduct, a decision appealed by Jindal Poly Films in the Supreme Court. ... Ankit Jain, the original petitioner, and other shareholders had moved the tribunal against a transactions undertaken by Jindal Poly Films, which allegedly stripped the company of valuable assets and resulted in monetary losses to minority shareholders. Jain estimated the total loss to the company at more than ₹2,500 crore. ... The shareholders alleged that a complex web of transactions was devised by Jindal Poly Films to cause wrongful loss to itself and them. The three transactions included sale of financial instruments in the nature of 0% optionally convertible preference shares and 0% redeemable preference shares held by Jindal Poly Films in Jindal India Powertech, at squat valuations in 2021-22. The write-off of loans provided by Jindal Poly Films to Jindal India Thermal Power in 2018-19 allegedly resulting in diminution of value of the securities of the company and sale of shares held by Jindal Films India (a wholly owned subsidiary of Jindal Poly Films) in Jindal Thermal to Champak Niketan, a promoter group entity, in which majority shares are owned by a trust belonging to the promoter group of Jindal Poly Films, Gunjan Trust, in 2020-21.Also read | Why IPO valuations, SpaceX’s included, may not conclusively capture the current state of AI play Jindal Poly Films had opposed the shareholders' stand, saying 5% shareholders couldn't run the company and that the allegations made by them related to transactions from earlier years which were concluded by 2022, and hence, the suit was not maintainable. Besides, it said that minority shareholders couldn't use a class action suit as a substitute for filing an oppression and mismanagement complaint. ... Approximately 40,000 public shareholders, whose interests were at the heart of the class action proceedings, were neither consulted nor informed before the matter was referred to private arbitration. By consenting to private arbitration, Jindal Poly Films and Monet Securities have extinguished the rights of approximately 40,000 public shareholders without their consultation or consent. The Company defending the class action and the shareholder prosecuting it suddenly appeared on the same side – united in diverting the matter to a private, confidential proceeding where the vast majority of affected shareholders would have no voice, participation, or remedy.The fundamental concern that demands an answer is: when the dispute is with all public shareholders as a class and even includes SEBI as an intervenor, how can one shareholder – particularly one that was recently substituted into proceedings initiated by others – unilaterally settle the dispute and extinguish the rights of the entire class by converting it into in personam proceedings? A statutory class action remedy intended to protect an entire shareholder class appears to have been effectively settled through the consent of a single substituted shareholder, raising serious questions about representation and due process.The matter was disposed of without examination of the substantive allegations, SEBI findings, or the concerns raised on behalf of minority shareholders, effectively moving the dispute away from a public forum to a private proceeding. The development raises larger concerns about whether class action proceedings under Section 245 of the Companies Act can be rendered ineffective through private settlements or arbitration arrangements, potentially setting a significant precedent for minority shareholder rights in India. The rights of approximately 40,000 aggrieved public shareholders of Jindal Poly Films Limited, whose collective remedy has been so devastatingly snatched away, need immediate protection. The minority shareholders are currently evaluating the implications of the development and considering their next steps and available remedies in accordance with applicable law."
Jindal Films,,,,Exa,Jindal Poly Films sees Supreme Court set aside NCLT order in ongoing dispute | Business Upturn,www.businessupturn.com,2026-06-09T00:00:00.000Z,2026-06-09 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.businessupturn.com/business/jindal-poly-films-sees-supreme-court-set-aside-nclt-order-in-ongoing-dispute/,"Jindal Poly Films sees Supreme Court set aside NCLT order in ongoing dispute | Business Upturn ... Jindal Poly Films Limited has announced that the Supreme Court of India has set aside previous orders from the National Company Law Tribunal (NCLT) and the National Company Law Appellate Tribunal (NCLAT) regarding a dispute with Monet Securities Pvt. Ltd. and others. This development was communicated to the company on 8 June 2026. ... The Supreme Court’s decision, dated 8 June 2026, nullifies the NCLT’s order from 5 February 2026 and the NCLAT’s order from 26 February 2026, which pertained to the maintainability of the case before the NCLT. The matter has now been referred to a sole arbitrator appointed by the Supreme Court of India, as per the company’s disclosure under Regulation 30 of the SEBI (Listing Obligations and Disclosure Requirements) Regulations, 2015. ... The company has stated that the financial implications of this litigation are currently indeterminate, as the matter remains sub-judice. As such, no claims or potential financial impacts have been ascertained at this stage. ... Jindal Poly Films has committed to taking appropriate steps in response to the Supreme Court’s communication but has not provided further details on the specific actions planned. ... The company has not reported any penalties, restrictions, or sanctions imposed as a result of this communication. Additionally, no aberrations or non-compliances have been identified by the authority in the communication."
Jindal Films,,,,Exa,"JINDALPOLY Q2 2025 Earnings: Revenue Surges 37% YoY, EPS at ₹25.07 - Pretax Income Report",www.csba.org (Earnings Report | 2026-06-08 | Quality Score: 92/100),2026-06-08T00:00:00.000Z,2026-06-08 00:00:00+00:00,,http://www.csba.org/expert-time/JINDALPOLY-Q2-2025-Earnings-Revenue-Surges-37-YoY-EPS-at-2507-51-6997,"Jindal (JINDALPOLY.NS) earnings outlook | market leadership, earnings outlook, and institutional support. Jindal Poly Films Limited reported net earnings of ₹25.07 per share for the second quarter of fiscal 2025 (Q2 2025). Revenue surged 37.04% year-on-year to ₹5,237.70 crores, reflecting strong operational momentum. The stock edged up 0.31% on the NSE, indicating cautious investor acknowledgment of the robust top-line performance amid a lack of explicit earnings estimates. ... Jindal (JINDALPOLY.NS) earnings outlook | market leadership, earnings outlook, and institutional support. Access to reliable, continuous market data is becoming a standard among active investors. It allows them to respond promptly to sudden shifts, whether in stock prices, energy markets, or agricultural commodities. The combination of speed and context often distinguishes successful traders from the rest. The sharp 37% revenue growth in Q2 2025 underscores sustained demand for flexible packaging films, particularly BOPET and BOPP grades, across domestic and export markets. Jindal Poly Films has been benefiting from capacity expansion initiatives undertaken in prior periods, which are now contributing to higher volume sales. The company’s product mix—skewed toward value-added specialties such as coated and metallized films—may have supported realisations despite volatility in global resin prices. On the cost side, moderation in crude-linked feedstock costs and a favourable rupee movement likely provided tailwinds to operating margins. Although specific margin data is not disclosed in this release, the combination of robust revenue growth and manageable input costs