Little Island Productions,,,,Exa,"One Great Studio buys another firm, but still operating with low leverage | Business | Jamaica Gleaner",jamaica-gleaner.com,2026-08-07T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-07 00:00:00+00:00,,https://jamaica-gleaner.com/article/business/20260807/one-great-studio-buys-another-firm-still-operating-low-leverage,"One Great Studio buys another firm, but still operating with low leverage | Business | Jamaica Gleaner ... One Great Studio Company Ltd (1GS), the Junior Market-listed digital agency, has acquired FarmHouse Creative Marketing Ltd for $36 million as it continues assembling a portfolio of marketing brands across the Caribbean. ... The transaction, which includes a performance-based earn-out structured over three years, brings FarmHouse founder Vanessa Henderson into the group as director of brand & creative. She will continue to lead FarmHouse, which will operate under its own name as 1GS’s dedicated creative, social media and brand strategy arm. ... FarmHouse Creative, established in 2023, has built a client base across hospitality, real estate, consumer and lifestyle brands. It is the third acquisition completed by 1GS, following High Voltage Digital and DRT Communications. ... 1GS continues to grow via acquisition and still has room to expand, based on its low debt-to-equity ratio at 2.0 per cent to March, comprised of $13 million in loans and $599 million in equity. These acquisitions — which include US-based High Voltage Digital LLC and, more recently, DRT Communications — have grown annual revenue from roughly $128 million in 2021 to peak at $461 million in 2023 and $376 million in 2025. Profit, however, remains subdued from its peak of $59 million in 2023 to a loss of $24 million in December 2025. It, however, rebounded, recording profit of $14.7 million for its March first-quarter 2026. ... “FarmHouse was built on relationships, creativity, and trust ... believing in what we ... As marketing continues to evolve, ... clients need a partner that brings together creativity, strategy, data, technology, and measurable performance. This partnership gives us the scale and expertise to deliver even greater value, while staying true to the principles that have guided FarmHouse from day one.” ... The 1GS brand portfolio now comprises One Great Studio, High Voltage Digital, DRT Communications and FarmHouse Creative, offering services spanning website and app development, search engine optimisation, paid media, branding, video production, public relations, strategic communications and media monitoring. ... The acquisition comes as 1GS enters a period of improved financial performance. The company reported revenue of $102 million for the first quarter of 2026, up 34 per cent from $76 million in the corresponding period a year earlier, and returned to profitability with net profit of $15 million, compared with a net loss of $13 million in the first quarter of 2025. The turnaround followed a difficult 2024, when full-year revenue declined to $342.3 million from $461.1 million and net profit fell to $35.7 million from $79.1 million as management restructured the business around its house-of-brands model. The company’s shares, which listed on the Jamaica Stock Exchange’s Junior Market in September 2023 at $1.00, were recently trading around $0.34."
Little Island Productions,,,,Exa,Bob Gaudette and Kirk Hipkiss launch AI-native Nolia Studios in Toronto - Broadcast Dialogue,broadcastdialogue.com (Connie Thiessen),2026-08-07T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-07 00:00:00+00:00,,https://broadcastdialogue.com/bob-gaudette-and-kirk-hipkiss-launch-ai-native-nolia-studios-in-toronto/,"Bob Gaudette and Kirk Hipkiss launch AI-native Nolia Studios in Toronto - Broadcast Dialogue ... ## Bob Gaudette and Kirk Hipkiss launch AI-native Nolia Studios in Toronto ... Co-founders Bob Gaudette and Kirk Hipkiss of Toronto ... film production company N ... Award-winning filmmaker Bob Gaudette and TV producer and creative director Kirk Hipkiss have launched new Toronto-based, AI-native film studio, Nolia Studios, positioned at the intersection of independent filmmaking, studio-grade production, and next-gen creative technology. ... The studio launch builds on the success of Gaudette’s short film, A Face Only a Mother Could Love, which earned the $50,000 Grand Prix at the 2026 Runway AI Festival, Best Picture at the OMNI International Film Festival in Australia, Best AI Film at the New York International Film Awards, and the David Harold Blackwell Award for AI & Emerging Technology Film at The Eudaimonia Project Film Festival. It was also a finalist at the 2026 Reply AI Film Festival in Venice. ... Building on that momentum, Gaudette and Hipkiss are establishing Nolia Studios “as a dedicated, slate-driven production company focused on original intellectual property across film, television, and emerging formats.” Their creative partnership that has been developing over the past year through AI-driven film and broadcast projects, with Hipkiss also the founder of Toronto’s Radical Point Media, an established animation, visual effects, motion graphics, and generative AI studio focused on CG and AI-driven production work. ... Nolia Studios is currently developing a slate of original projects spanning short-form, feature, and serialized content, including the animated, The Little Cloud Thief, for both feature and episodic adaptation, in addition to an AI-driven animated feature in collaboration with a UK- and Toronto-based production partner. ... “AI offers new pathways into filmmaking, enabling more people to take part in the creative process,” said Gaudette. “Nolia is built around the idea that powerful stories can come from anywhere, and that the tools now exist to realize them at a high level at reduced budgets.”"
Little Island Productions,,,,Exa,"One Great Studio buys another firm, but still operating with low leverage | Business | Jamaica Gleaner",beta2.jamaica-gleaner.com,2026-08-07T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-07 00:00:00+00:00,,http://beta2.jamaica-gleaner.com/index%2ephp/article/business/20260807/one-great-studio-buys-another-firm-still-operating-low-leverage,"One Great Studio buys another firm, but still operating with low leverage | Business | Jamaica Gleaner ... One Great Studio Company Ltd (1GS), the Junior Market-listed digital agency, has acquired FarmHouse Creative Marketing Ltd for $36 million as it continues assembling a portfolio of marketing brands across the Caribbean. ... The transaction, which includes a performance-based earn-out structured over three years, brings FarmHouse founder Vanessa Henderson into the group as director of brand & creative. She will continue to lead FarmHouse, which will operate under its own name as 1GS’s dedicated creative, social media and brand strategy arm. ... FarmHouse Creative, established in 2023, has built a client base across hospitality, real estate, consumer and lifestyle brands. It is the third acquisition completed by 1GS, following High Voltage Digital and DRT Communications. ... 1GS continues to grow via acquisition and still has room to expand, based on its low debt-to-equity ratio at 2.0 per cent to March, comprised of $13 million in loans and $599 million in equity. These acquisitions — which include US-based High Voltage Digital LLC and, more recently, DRT Communications — have grown annual revenue from roughly $128 million in 2021 to peak at $461 million in 2023 and $376 million in 2025. Profit, however, remains subdued from its peak of $59 million in 2023 to a loss of $24 million in December 2025. It, however, rebounded, recording profit of $14.7 million for its March first-quarter 2026. ... to deliver even greater value ... while staying true to ... The 1GS brand portfolio now comprises One Great Studio, High Voltage Digital, DRT Communications and FarmHouse Creative, offering services spanning website and app development, search engine optimisation, paid media, branding, video production, public relations, strategic communications and media monitoring. ... The acquisition comes as 1GS enters a period of improved financial performance. The company reported revenue of $102 million for the first quarter of 2026, up 34 per cent from $76 million in the corresponding period a year earlier, and returned to profitability with net profit of $15 million, compared with a net loss of $13 million in the first quarter of 2025. The turnaround followed a difficult 2024, when full-year revenue declined to $342.3 million from $461.1 million and net profit fell to $35.7 million from $79.1 million as management restructured the business around its house-of-brands model. The company’s shares, which listed on the Jamaica Stock Exchange’s Junior Market in September 2023 at $1.00, were recently trading around $0.34."
Little Island Productions,,,,Exa,"ICv2: Indie Publishers Band Together for Sub-Distribution, Printing, More",icv2.com (Brigid Alverson),2026-08-07T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-07 00:00:00+00:00,,https://icv2.com/articles/news/view/63065/indie-publishers-band-together-sub-distribution-printing-more,"INDIE PUBLISHERS BAND TOGETHER FOR SUB-DISTRIBUTION, PRINTING, MORE ... Critical Forms Indie Alliance, Massive Indies Adds Discount Printing ... Small presses are working together to develop routes to direct market distribution as well as offer affordable publishing services in the post-Diamond world: The small publisher Critical Entertainment is heading up a new industry group, the Indie Comic Alliance, while Massive will begin offering discounted printing to its Massive Indies clients and other small publishers. ... Critical Entertainment is spearheading a new group, the Indie Comic Alliance, which will offer distribution to the direct market via Universal and Philbo, as well as reasonably priced printing, shipping, and storage. Most books distributed via the alliance will be released on the third Wednesday of the month, which will be dubbed Indie Comic Book Day; solicitation will begin in October, and the first releases will arrive in stores on January 20, 2027. ... The alliance is headed by Christopher Reda, Publisher and CEO of Critical Entertainment, and Thomas Cho of STS & Associates, and members include Critical Entertainment, DSTLRY, Red 5 Comics, Wise Acre Comics, Outland Entertainment, BentBox Comics, Forbidden Futures, Patriot Comic Books, Diabolik, 2510 Press, World at War Comics, Monkeygong, Bear Treasure Studios, K-Toon Studios, Phantom Blade Productions, and Narwhal Press. DSTLRY is a member for printing only. ... Massive Indies, which was launched in the wake of the Diamond bankruptcy (see “ Massive Indies”), is a sub-distribution program for small-press and self-published comics that offers access to the direct market via Lunar Distribution. Massive Indies clients now include Heavy Metal, Warrant Publishing Company, AMP Comics, Antarctic Press, Devil’s Due Studios, Red 5, Midnight Factory, Invader Comics, and two recent additions, Insight Editions and the Hero Initiative. ... Massive is now expanding that program by offering discounted printing services that will include standard offset and digital printing and specialty treatments such as foil, embossing, and spot varnish. The discount will initially be offered only to Massive Indies clients but is expected to be extended to the general public by the end of 2026. ... ""Our goal has always been to remove barriers for independent publishers,” Michael Calero, CEO of Massive Publishing, said in a statement accompanying the announcement. “As the comic market continues to evolve, indie publishers need trusted partners that can help them navigate every stage of the production and distribution process. Adding premium printing services to the Massive Indies program gives our partners access to the same level of quality we've become known for while helping them keep production costs competitive."""
Little Island Productions,,,,Exa,Island Daze Launches Reimagined Website to Showcase Full Seasonal Collections and Elite Design Capabilities,www.prnewswire.com (Island Daze),2026-08-05T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-05 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/island-daze-launches-reimagined-website-to-showcase-full-seasonal-collections-and-elite-design-capabilities-302843775.html,"HALLANDALE BEACH, Fla., Aug. 5, 2026 /PRNewswire/ -- Island Daze(the ""Company""), a leading design-led, private-label coastal lifestyle apparel platform, today announced the launch of its reimagined website, located at its legacy URL, www.IslandDaze.com. Designed to offer an enhanced user experience for the Company's retail partners across the U.S. and internationally, the new website officially went live this summer. ... Rooted in the Company's 27-year legacy at the intersection of surf, design, and culture, the site reflects Island Daze's industry-leading position as a private-label partner delivering premium seasonal collections that balance creative credibility with operational precision and disciplined cost architecture. The redesigned platform was developed to reflect the scale, sophistication, and breadth of Island Daze's seasonal assortment and private-label capabilities. ... The relaunch marks the latest milestone in the Company's continued investment in its design platform, operational infrastructure, and retail partnerships. ... ""For nearly three decades, we've built Island Daze around a simple belief: retailers deserve premium design and quality without sacrificing margin,"" said Abe Allouche, Founder & CEO of Island Daze. ""Our new website reflects the depth of our collections and the strength of our in-house design team, while reinforcing our commitment to delivering elevated product with the operational discipline our partners depend on."" ... Island Daze is a globally trusted private-label apparel platform that combines elite in-house design, premium product development, and disciplined cost architecture to deliver full, high-quality seasonal collections across men's and women's categories—preserving margin integrity for customers while sustaining durable, scalable growth. The Company services hospitality, destination, active, and large-format retailers across North America. ... Founded in 1998 by former professional surfer Abe Allouche, Island Daze is a private-label apparel platform rooted in nearly three decades of surf and coastal culture. The company designs and develops full seasonal men's and women's collections for hospitality, destination, active, and large-format retailers across North America, combining elevated design, premium materials, and thoughtful assortment architecture with disciplined cost control that protects customer margin integrity. Backed by an elite in-house design team, long-standing sourcing relationships, and integrated bi-coastal finishing and fulfillment operations in Florida and California, Island Daze pairs creative credibility with operational precision and scalable growth. For more information, visit www.IslandDaze.com."
Little Island Productions,,,,Exa,Island launches enterprise vibe publishing for employee apps,itbrief.co.nz (Joseph Gabriel Lagonsin),2026-08-04T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-04 00:00:00+00:00,,https://itbrief.co.nz/story/island-launches-enterprise-vibe-publishing-for-employee-apps,"Island launches enterprise vibe publishing for employee apps ... # Island launches enterprise vibe publishing for employee apps Tue, 4th Aug 2026 (Yesterday) Island has launched Enterprise Vibe Publishing for internal applications built by employees. The product is aimed at companies that want to apply governance to apps created with AI coding tools. ... The new product lets users publish applications to a central app library with a built-in permissions model. Employees can find approved apps in one place, while administrators can apply security and access rules at publication. ... Enterprise controls including secure access, identity checks, device posture and data protection are attached automatically. The system also provides audit records for compliance, along with monitoring data on how often an app is opened, how quickly it loads and where it fails. ... The company pointed to the risk of malicious model context protocol servers and insecure app connections as examples of the exposure that can arise when software is created and distributed informally. Traditional enterprise review processes, it argued, were designed for a much slower pace of application roll-out and are struggling ... keep up with ... volume created by ... -assisted development. ... A Gartner forecast cited by Island said 40% of new enterprise production software will be created with vibe coding techniques and tools by 2028. The same forecast said 90% of enterprise software engineers will then use AI code assistants, up from less than 14% in early 2024. ... Island said Enterprise Vibe Publishing is designed to fit around AI coding tools that employees already use. Under the process it described, a user builds an app and submits it for evaluation, after which administrators review it, apply policies and approve publication. ... Once approved, the app can be shared through a central library and placed on the browser homepage used by colleagues. Published apps can also be given restricted access to internal systems through Island's private access technology, limiting connections to a specific internal resource rather than exposing a wider network. ... Island said this approach differs from a hosting platform model because it governs applications rather than running them as the underlying platform vendor. In practice, the company is positioning the product as an overlay for identity, browsing and data controls across software created elsewhere. ... Island said it is also using the product for its own AI Usage Dashboard, a tool intended to help staff manage AI usage and costs across platforms including Claude and Cursor. The company presented that internal deployment as evidence that the publishing model can support operational tools as well as departmental applications. ... Mike Fey, Chief Executive Officer and Co-founder of Island, said the launch is intended to close a gap between rapid app creation and enterprise oversight. ... ""Building software has never been faster, and every employee is a potential builder. But sharing their innovations safely hasn't caught up,"" said Fey. ""In the enterprise, you can't sacrifice governance for speed. Island Enterprise Vibe Publishing closes that gap, keeping the speed and simplicity of vibe coding intact while surrounding it with the controls enterprises already trust."""
Little Island Productions,,,,Exa,Island launches enterprise vibe publishing for employee apps,itbrief.asia (Joseph Gabriel Lagonsin),2026-08-04T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-04 00:00:00+00:00,,https://itbrief.asia/story/island-launches-enterprise-vibe-publishing-for-employee-apps,"Island launches enterprise vibe publishing for employee apps ... # Island launches enterprise vibe publishing for employee apps ... Tue, 4th Aug ... 026 ... Island has launched Enterprise Vibe Publishing for internal applications built by employees. The product is aimed at companies that want to apply governance to apps created with AI coding tools. ... The launch addresses a growing problem for technology and security leaders as AI-assisted software creation spreads beyond engineering teams. Tools such as Claude Code and Lovable have made ... easier for knowledge workers to build simple applications, but many organisations still lack a formal way to review, approve and distribute those ... The new product lets users publish applications to a central app library with a built-in permissions model. Employees can find approved apps in one place, while administrators can apply security and access rules at publication. ... Enterprise controls including secure access, identity checks, device posture and data protection are attached automatically. The system also provides audit records for compliance, along with monitoring data on how often an app is opened, how quickly it loads and where it fails. ... The move comes as businesses grapple with the rise of so-called vibe coding, in which users rely on natural language prompts and AI assistants to build software. That trend has widened the pool of potential app creators, but it has also raised concerns that departments are deploying tools outside normal review processes and feeding them sensitive information. ... Island framed the issue as a new form of shadow AI, with employees building apps in a matter of hours and then sharing them through email or chat. In that model, businesses may have little visibility into what data is being entered, which systems are connected or whether the software contains security weaknesses. ... The company pointed to the risk of malicious model context protocol servers and insecure app connections as examples of the exposure that can arise when software is created and distributed informally. Traditional enterprise review processes, it argued, were designed for a much slower pace of application roll-out and are struggling to keep up with the volume created by AI-assisted development. ... A Gartner forecast cited by Island said 40% of new enterprise production software will be created with vibe coding techniques and tools by 2028. The same forecast said 90% of enterprise software engineers will then use AI code assistants, up from less than 14% in early 2024. ... Island said Enterprise Vibe Publishing is designed to fit around AI coding tools that employees already use. Under the process it described, a user builds an app and submits it for evaluation, after which administrators review it, apply policies and approve publication. ... Once approved, the app can be shared through a central library and placed on the browser homepage used by colleagues. Published apps can also be given restricted access to internal systems through Island's private access technology, limiting connections to a specific internal resource rather than exposing a wider network. ... Island said this approach differs from a hosting platform model because it governs applications rather than running them as the underlying platform vendor. In practice, the company is positioning the product as an overlay for identity, browsing and data controls across software created elsewhere. ... ""Teams that used to experiment quietly with small chat assistants are now turning those ideas into real workflows and apps their departments depend on,"" said Eric Lowe, Director of Infosec Risk Management at TaskUs. ""With Island Enterprise Vibe Publishing, we can finally say yes to that creativity. We give employees the sandbox to build, and Island gives us the secure wrapper to deliver those AI apps safely across the enterprise."" ... Island said it is also using the product for its own AI Usage Dashboard, a tool intended to help staff manage AI usage and costs across platforms including Claude and Cursor. The company presented that internal deployment as evidence that the publishing model can support operational tools as well as departmental applications. ... Mike Fey, Chief Executive Officer and Co-founder of Island, said the launch is intended to close a gap between rapid app creation and enterprise oversight. ... ""Building software has never been faster, and every employee is a potential builder. But sharing their innovations safely hasn't caught up,"" said Fey. ""In the enterprise, you can't sacrifice governance for speed. Island Enterprise Vibe Publishing closes that gap, keeping the speed and simplicity of vibe coding intact while surrounding it with the controls enterprises already trust."""
Little Island Productions,,,,Exa,Island Secures Vibe Coding with New Enterprise Vibe,www.globenewswire.com (Island Technology Inc.),2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-03 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.globenewswire.com/news-release/2026/08/03/3337542/0/en/Island-Secures-Vibe-Coding-with-New-Enterprise-Vibe-Publishing.html,"Vibe Publishing ... , 2026 09:00 ET | Source: ... Technology Inc. Island Technology Inc. ... DALLAS, Aug. 03, 2026 (GLOBE NEWSWIRE) -- CIOs and CISOs can now safely open the floodgates to vibe-coded apps. Island, the enterprise agentic control plane, today introduced Island Enterprise Vibe Publishing, a new way for organizations to quickly publish and share internal apps employees build with all their governance built in. ... Island Enterprise Vibe Publishing solves both. Apps are published to a centralized app library, with a built-in permissions model. End users can then easily find the app they are looking for and discover new ones. All the enterprise controls, such as secure access, identity, device posture, and data protection, automatically attach to apps as they are published. ... network enforcement, ... posture checks, ... -layer controls across multiple ... governed access, policy ... ""Building software has never been faster, and every employee is a potential builder. But sharing their innovations safely hasn't caught up,” said Mike Fey, CEO and Co-founder of Island. “In the enterprise, you can't sacrifice governance for speed. Island Enterprise Vibe Publishing closes that gap, keeping the speed and simplicity of vibe coding intact, while surrounding it with the controls enterprises already trust."" ... Island publishes its own AI Usage Dashboard through Enterprise Vibe Publishing. This live tool helps employees manage their AI usage and cost across platforms, including Claude and Cursor. ... Island is the enterprise agentic control plane, where organizations govern people and AI agents as they work across devices, browsers, applications, networks, and data. Island gives agents the identity, access, guardrails, cost controls, and auditability they need to operate safely at scale. Millions of users trust Island to protect sensitive information, simplify enterprise work, and reduce reliance on legacy infrastructure. Island is backed by world-class investors including Coatue Management, Cyberstarts, Insight Partners, and Sequoia Capital. Island can be reached at info@island.io or (866) 832-7114. ... June 17, 2026 10:00 ET | Source: Island Technology Inc. ... Total Economic Impact Study Shows Island Delivered 344% ROI to Enterprises Over Three Years ... DALLAS, June 17, 2026 (GLOBE NEWSWIRE) -- Island, the platform for enterprise work, gave customers a 344% return on investment. According to a commissioned Total Economic Impact™ (TEI) study by... ... June 08, 2026 10:00 ET | Source: Island Technology Inc. ... Island Brings the First Purpose-Built Workspace for SMBs to the Pax8 Marketplace ... Island and Pax8 introduce a unified, secure browser workspace for SMBs, simplifying security and AI management for MSP partners."
