Linklaters,,,,Exa,Linklaters advises on landmark US$650m senior secured repack high-yield notes issuance by Mercury Finance Company,legaldesire.com (Cynthia Lydia Marbaniang),2026-08-06T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-06 00:00:00+00:00,,https://legaldesire.com/linklaters-advises-on-landmark-us650m-senior-secured-repack-high-yield-notes-issuance-by-mercury-finance-company/,"Linklaters advises on landmark US$650m senior secured repack high-yield notes issuance by Mercury Finance Company ... # Linklaters advises on landmark US$650m senior secured repack high-yield notes issuance by Mercury Finance Company ... 6, ... Linklaters has advised the placement agent on the bespoke Reg S issuance of US$650m 14.5% senior secured repack high-yield notes due 2029 by a Mauritius-incorporated special purpose vehicle, Mercury Finance Company, the proceeds of which were used by Mercury to subscribe to rupee-denominated senior secured bonds issued by Eqyizen Investment Private Limited, a promoter group entity of the Shapoorji Pallonji Group. The issuance was one of India’s largest private credit transactions and formed part of Shapoorji’s broader dual-currency refinancing package. ... admitted to trading on the multilateral trading facility operated by ... > “We are pleased to have advised on this landmark transaction, which demonstrates the growing depth and sophistication of India-related private credit and structured capital markets activity. By bringing together offshore US dollar notes, onshore rupee-denominated debt and a comprehensive hedging structure, the transaction created a bespoke solution for international institutional investors to participate in an Indian financing, underlining Linklaters’ proven track record of supporting complex cross-border financings for India-related transactions.” ... With more than 50 years of experience in Asia, the Linklaters team provides clients with practical expertise and commercial insights to capitalise on opportunities. This transaction builds on the firm’s continued work on complex and innovative cross-border financings, including advising on the first cross-border securitisation deal out of India, AM Green’s financing and advising ReNew Energy on its landmark US$600m senior secured green bond issuance, the first issuance by a corporate using a GIFT City entity."
Linklaters,,,,Exa,The new pecking order: key takeaways from the latest magic circle financials – Legal Business,www.legalbusiness.co.uk,2026-08-05T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-05 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.legalbusiness.co.uk/law-firm-data/the-new-pecking-order-key-takeaways-from-the-latest-magic-circle-financials/,"Alex Ryan · 5 August 2026 · 4 min read A&O Shearman, Clifford Chance, and Linklaters have each reported their financial results in recent weeks – and the differences in performance underline the diverging strategies among the three firms. ... ## Linklaters is pushing ahead on profitability ... Linklaters has delivered the strongest profits growth of the three firms over the past five years, with profit per equity partner (PEP) up 40% since 2021, compared to just over 24% for Clifford Chance and 16% for A&O Shearman. ... Linklaters had been behind the other two firms in the years since 2020-21, with PEP of £1.77m in 2021 compared to £1.85m for CC and £1.9m for legacy Allen & Overy. ... However, it nudged ahead for the first time last year, with a 15% increase taking it to £2.2m – the first time it had exceeded £2m. ... The firm then built on this during 2025-26, with an increase of more than 11% taking PEP to £2.48m, above CC on £2.3m and A&O Shearman on £2.2m. ... Allen & Overy’s US merger with Shearman & Sterling in 2024 handed it a major boost to its top line, with revenue going from £2.15bn to £2.86bn in the firm’s first post-merger results – an increase of 33%. ... This put the firm significantly ahead of both CC – with whom it had been broadly level in revenue terms in the years since 2021 – and Linklaters. ... However, that gap has begun to narrow. CC increased its revenue by 9% to £2.6bn during 2025-26, and Linklaters by 6.8% to £2.47bn. But A&O Shearman posted flat revenues in US dollar terms, equating to a slight decrease in GBP, coming out at £2.8bn. ... The firm has argued that this is the result of a strategic pivot into what managing partner Hervé Ekué describes as a ‘streamlined’ operation. ... The Shearman merger did give A&O more heft in the US, while both CC and Linklaters have seen US growth harder to come by. ... For its part, CC aims to build what managing partner Charles Adams called an ‘integrated’ US presence, with plans to expand on the West Coast and a focus on building its brand in the States. ... Linklaters, which recently denied rumours that it had sought its own transatlantic tie-up with Cleary, is further back in terms of US contribution to its top line, with the proportion of revenue generated in the Americas rising from 5.5% in 2020-21 to just over 8% in 2024-25. ... The firm has made some high-profile hires in the market, including a former Shearman team led by Americas chair George Casey, and in its latest results, reported 39% growth in US profits, on the back of 56% growth the previous year. ... It is notable that despite its smaller presence in the US, Linklaters has nonetheless become the most profitable of the three firms. ... While all three firms had steadily increased the size of their partnerships in recent years, that trend changed last year. ... Both CC and Linklaters trimmed the size of their partnerships by around 1% during 2025-26, with CC’s partner count dropping by five to 655 and Linklaters down by four to 530. ... A&O Shearman’s partner exits since the 2024 merger are well documented, with LB analysis published last October finding that more than 130 had exited since the merger completed. ... While the firm declined to provide current partner numbers, its website states it has ‘more than 750 partners’, down from the figure of c.800 reported by the firm when the A&O Shearman merger completed in 2024 – a dip which is in line with the firm’s characterisation of itself as a more ‘streamlined’ operation."
Linklaters,,,,Exa,"Linklaters advises Prologis, Inc. on its recommended offer for SEGRO plc",legaldesire.com (Cynthia Lydia Marbaniang),2026-08-05T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-05 00:00:00+00:00,,https://legaldesire.com/linklaters-advises-prologis-inc-on-its-recommended-offer-for-segro-plc/,"Linklaters advises Prologis, Inc. on its recommended offer for SEGRO plc ... # Linklaters advises Prologis, Inc. on its recommended offer for SEGRO plc ... Linklaters is pleased to be advising Prologis, Inc., the world’s largest logistics real estate company, on its recommended share offer with a partial cash alternative for SEGRO plc. The £14bn offer for SEGRO represents the largest UK public M&A transaction in recent years. The transaction will bring together two premier real estate portfolios into a single global platform, with c.$269bn. of assets under management. ... The transaction is expected to complete in the first half of 2027, subject to receipt of customary regulatory approvals. As part of the transaction, Prologis has committed to implement a secondary listing on the London Stock Exchange concurrently with completion. ... This is the latest in a series of major UK public M&A transactions that Linklaters has advised on in 2026, including Tinicum and Blackstone’s recommended offer for Senior, Tate & Lyle’s recommended offer by Ingredion, EQT’s recommended offer for Intertek, and Mitie’s recommended offer by OCS, further cementing Linklaters’ market leading position in this space. The firm recently achieved a #1 ranking in the UK for M&A in Bloomberg’s H1 2026 league tables. ... Iain Fenn, Corporate Partner, said: “We are very pleased to be advising Prologis, Inc. on this landmark transaction, the largest seen in the UK public markets for many years. This is precisely the type of complex deal where our full corporate offering and global platform come into their own, combining our cross-practice specialist expertise with market-leading commercial advice.”"
Linklaters,,,,Exa,Linklaters Returns to Paul Weiss for Trio of New York Litigators | Law.com,www.law.com,2026-08-04T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-04 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.law.com/international-edition/2026/08/04/linklaters-returns-to-paul-weiss-for-trio-of-new-york-litigators/,"Linklaters Returns to Paul Weiss for Trio of New York Litigators | Law.com ... # Linklaters Returns to Paul Weiss for Trio of New York Litigators ... Less than a month after Linklaters' lateral hiring spree, the firm turns again to Paul Weiss for its New York build-out, with one of the new hires being elevated to partner. ... Linklaters is expanding its New York business, turning again to Paul, Weiss, Rifkind, Wharton & Garrison to hire a partner-led trio, just a month after it made high profile hires from the same firm. ... Lawyer César Rivière joins Linklaters’ litigation, arbitration & investigations practice as a partner in firm's New York office, having been an associate at Paul Weiss for two years, and before that an associate for more than five years at Skadden, Arps, Slate, Meagher & Flom. He joins with fellow associate Grant May, who becomes counsel, and associate Cara Day. It comes within a month of the arrival of partners H. Christopher Boehning and Daniel H. Levi, who departed the same firm. ... Adeel Mangi, the chair of Linklaters' trial practice who joined the firm from Patterson Belknap Webb & Tyler last year, said: “Chris, Dan, César, Grant, and Cara add to our capability in this respect and enhance the platform we are building in New York.” ... According to the latest Law.com Compass data, over the past 12 months, Paul Weiss has lost 2.89% of its headcount, while Linklaters has increased lawyer headcount by 6.10%, with the firm demonstrating an ambition to expand its presence in the U.S. ... Across the U.S., Linklaters is host to more than 400 lawyers. Earlier this summer, the firm reported increased revenue of 6.8% to £2.47 billion in the fiscal year ending April 30, 2026, while profit before tax rose 11.6% to £1.2 billion. The firm's profit growth was concentrated in the U.S., where revenue grew by nearly 40% year over year. ... Adam Lurie, head of U.S. Litigation, arbitration & investigations, said the new arrivals strengthen the firm's ability to support clients on their most complex and business-critical litigation, arbitration, and investigations matters in the U.S. and globally. ... Linklaters has made strategic investments in the U.S. by expanding its litigation, arbitration, and investigations platform over the past two years, including the addition of partners new trial chair Mangi, Muhammad Faridi, Diana Conner, and George LoBiondo, as well as partner Joseph R. Profaizer as global co-head of international arbitration based in the Washington, D.C., office."
Linklaters,,,,Exa,Linklaters Advises on Singapore Green Bond,www.finews.asia,2026-08-04T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-04 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.finews.asia/finance/45026-linklaters-advises-on-singapore-green-bond,"Linklaters Advises on Singapore Green Bond ... # Linklaters Advises on Singapore Green Bond ... International law firm Linklaters has advised the syndicate of managers on Singapore's latest sovereign green bond issuance, a SGD 2,6 billion (approximately USD 2,0 billion) transaction under the government's medium-term note (MTN) programme. ... The Green Singapore Government Securities (Infrastructure) due 2046 were issued under the framework established by the Significant Infrastructure Government Loan Act (SINGA), which enables the government to raise long-term funding for major infrastructure projects. The offering also included a retail tranche of approximately SGD 7.6 million for investors in Singapore. ... According to Linklaters, the issuance attracted strong investor demand. The proceeds will finance eligible expenditures under the Singapore Green Bond Framework, supporting the country's sustainable infrastructure strategy. ... The mandate marks another role for Linklaters on Singapore's sovereign green bond programme, following the firm's advice on the government's previous SGD 2.5 billion green bond issuance under the MTN programme. ... «This latest transaction is another important milestone in Singapore's sovereign green finance journey and reflects the continued development of its sustainable finance market,» said Amit Singh, partner and Head of South and Southeast Asia Capital Markets at Linklaters. «We are proud to have supported this successful issuance, which demonstrates both the depth of investor demand for high-quality green instruments and the strength of our capital markets capability in ... ers team was led by Amit Singh and capital markets partner Xunming Lim, supported by managing associate Alwyn Loy and associates Ashley Loh and Reeve Chia."
Linklaters,,,,Exa,More Paul Weiss Litigators Head to Linklaters to Join FIFA Lawyer,news.bloomberglaw.com,2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-03 00:00:00+00:00,,https://news.bloomberglaw.com/business-and-practice/more-paul-weiss-litigators-head-to-linklaters-to-join-fifa-lawyer,"More Paul Weiss Litigators Head to Linklaters to Join FIFA Lawyer ... # More Paul Weiss Litigators Head to Linklaters to Join FIFA Lawyer ... Aug. 3, 2026, 10:00 AM UTC Roy Strom The FIFA World Cup 2026 Final match between Spain and Argentina on July 19 in East Rutherford, N.J. Linklaters announced it has hired three more Paul Weiss litigators since adding two partners with a focus on sports litigation last month. ... The firm brings aboard New York-based lawyers César Rivière, Grant May and Cara Day in its litigation, arbitration & investigations practice. Rivière is a partner; May is counsel; and Day is an associate. ... The lawyers have worked closely with partners Christopher Boehning and Daniel Levi, who joined Linklaters from ... Beohning has had a long relationship with the global soccer regulatory body FIFA, which he advised during the recent World Cup on matters including the body’s efforts to prevent political statements and flags being brought into matches. The new additions will bolster Linklaters’ sports practice. “Sports is big business, and it now attracts the same sorts of disputes and investments as many other industries,” Boehning said in an interview. “That’s all the more reason why clients are increasingly looking to hire sophisticated lawyers and firms to help them.” ... The new lawyers join a Linklaters sports disputes practice that has advised the English Premier League on regulatory matters and competition laws. The firm represented the NFL Players Association on matters including the New Orleans Saints’ “Bountygate” scandal and an investigation into bullying on the Miami Dolphins. ... Linklaters’ US practice now counts 56 partners and roughly 220 lawyers. The firm last month reported its fiscal 2026 financial results, highlighted by a nearly 7% increase in revenue to $3.3 billion and a 39% increase in US profits. ... “The idea is to make sure that we can offer our clients the complete range of abilities and skills that they may need,” Boehning said. “Sometimes a transaction will raise an issue that needs attention from someone focused on disputes or particular regulations. That’s certainly what we are trying to build at Linklaters.”"
Linklaters,,,,Exa,Linklaters Advises Petroquímica Comodoro Rivadavia S.A. on US$400m Inaugural Bond Offering,legaldesire.com (Cynthia Lydia Marbaniang),2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-03 00:00:00+00:00,,https://legaldesire.com/linklaters-advises-petroquimica-comodoro-rivadavia-s-a-on-us400m-inaugural-bond-offering/,"Linklaters Advises Petroquímica Comodoro Rivadavia S.A. on US$400m Inaugural Bond Offering ... # Linklaters Advises Petroquímica Comodoro Rivadavia S.A. on US$400m Inaugural Bond Offering ... Linklaters advised Petroquímica Comodoro Rivadavia S.A. (“PCR”) and its subsidiary Luz de Tres Picos S.A. (“LDTP”), as co-issuers, on their debut offering of US$400m 8.500% Senior Notes due 2034. The notes were offered and sold pursuant to Rule 144A and Regulation S under the US Securities Act. The offering launched on July 13, 2026, priced on July 22, 2026, and closed on July 28, 2026. The notes are listed on Bolsas y Mercados Argentinos S.A. (ByMA) and admitted for trading on ByMA and on A3 Mercados S.A. This transaction marks PCR’s first international capital markets bond issuance. ... PCR is an Argentine independent energy company with over 100 years of experience in the exploration and production of oil and gas. The company operates across three business segments: (i) upstream oil and gas, with a portfolio of 16 areas in Argentina and Ecuador, as well as a participation in a non-conventional hydrocarbons project in the United States; (ii) renewable energy, which operates one of the largest independent renewable energy platforms in Argentina; and (iii) cement production, where PCR is the leading producer in the Argentine Patagonia. ... Spanning New York and São Paulo, the Linklaters deal team was led by Partners Emilio Minvielle and Matthew Poulter, alongside Counsel Marcelo Arellano, Associate Madeleine Bléhaut, International Associate Francisco Algorta. Additional tax assistance for the deal was provided by Partner Gabriel Grossman and Senior Associate Bernard Yi. ... Linklaters’ lawyers have practiced in Latin America for decades and Linklaters was one of the first international firms to open an office in Brazil in 1997. With a growing team of Latin America-focused lawyers across the firm’s New York, Washington, D.C., and São Paulo, the firm continues to increase its presence in the Latin America region and deepen existing relationships with leading local law firms. Linklaters is highly ranked across key Latin America legal directories including Chambers, Legal 500, and Latin Lawyer."
Linklaters,,,,Exa,"Linklaters advises SWIAT on the establishment of RL1, Luxembourg’s second European Cooperative Society (SCE) - The British Chamber of Commerce for Luxembourg",www.bcc.lu,2026-07-30T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-30 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.bcc.lu/news/linklaters-advises-swiat-on-the-establishment-of-rl1-luxembourgs-second-european-cooperative-society-sce/,"Linklaters advises SWIAT on the establishment of RL1, Luxembourg’s second European Cooperative Society (SCE) - The British Chamber of Commerce for Luxembourg ... ## Linklaters advises SWIAT on the establishment of RL1, Luxembourg’s second European Cooperative Society (SCE) By Véronique Cioli, Head of Marketing Europe for Linklaters LLP, July 30 2026 The RL1 network is built on SWIAT’s DLT infrastructure, which is transferred to the cooperative. The platform has been operational for three years and has facilitated more than 20 transactions with an aggregate volume exceeding €700 million. ... Commenting on the transaction, Corporate Partner Florence Forster said: “We are pleased to have advised SWIAT on this innovative project that sits at the intersection of financial regulation, distributed ledger technology and cooperative law, a combination that demands deep technical knowledge across multiple disciplines. Structuring only the second SCE in Luxembourg required a forward-looking approach led by our Luxembourg team. It further highlights how our innovative legal structuring can support the development of next-generation financial market infrastructure.”"
Linklaters,,,,Exa,"Linklaters, Webber Wentzel End Exclusive 13-Year Alliance in Latest South Africa Reset | Law.com",www.law.com,2026-07-28T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-28 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.law.com/international-edition/2026/07/28/linklaters-webber-wetzel-end-13-year-alliance-in-latest-south-africa-reset/,"ers, Webber Wentzel End Exclusive 13-Year Alliance in Latest South Africa Reset ... # Linklaters, Webber Wentzel End Exclusive 13-Year Alliance in Latest South Africa Reset ... The restructuring follows a series of Africa strategy changes by global law firms, although Linklaters said it remains committed to serving clients across the continent. ... Linklaters and South African law firm Webber Wentzel will end their exclusive alliance in November after more than 13 years, becoming the latest firms to rethink how they operate across Africa. ... In a joint statement on Tuesday, the firms said they will return to the informal relationship that existed before their exclusive collaboration. ... Linklaters Senior Partner and Chair Aedamar Comiskey said the firm remained committed to Africa and would continue serving clients on the continent through its long-standing regional relationships and global team. ... For Webber Wentzel, the change marks the next chapter in its development as a proudly African firm with deep South African roots and a track record of advising on some of the continent’s most significant matters, according to senior partner Gareth Driver. ... “We look forward to continued growth alongside our clients across the continent,"" he said. ... The Linklaters and Webber Wentzel split, which both firms describe as mutual, comes as global law firms rethink their Africa strategies. ... On April 1, Norton Rose Fulbright separated from its South African offices, leaving Deneys to rebuild as an independent firm while NRF shifts to an Africa strategy centered on Casablanca and local referral alliances. Meanwhile, A&O Shearman and legacy Hogan Lovells closed their Johannesburg offices following strategic reviews in 2024. ... Market observers point to lower billing rates in South Africa compared to London and New York, the high cost of maintaining local offices, and South Africa's broad-based black economic empowerment (B-BBEE) framework, which has prompted some international firms to favor alliances or ‘best friends’ relationships over fully integrated local practices. ... Linklaters and Webber Sentzel said the end of their exclusive structure will give each greater flexibility while allowing them to continue collaborating when clients or particular mandates require it. The firm signed their collaboration agreement in 2012, formalizing a relationship that had already existed for more than 40 years. The alliance became operational in February 2013. ... Under the former arrangement, Linklaters had direct access to lawyers in Webber Wentzel’s Johannesburg and Cape Town offices, while Webber Wentzel could draw on Linklaters’ international network for cross-border matters. Both firms also had a shared training program that allowed secondment of lawyers from London to South Africa and vice-versa. ... The firms have worked together for multinational clients on several major transactions. They advised Anglo American on the $11 billion demerger of its platinum business, now known as Valterra Platinum, while Webber Wentzel also advised the mining company on the $53 billion merger with Teck Resources. ... Linklaters and Webber Wentzel were also among the firms advising on fintech company Optasia’s 2025 initial public offering on the Johannesburg Stock Exchange, South Africa’s largest IPO that year. ... A Linklaters spokesperson confirmed that the decision will not affect the firm’s other alliances, including its integrated arrangement with Australian firm Allens."
Linklaters,,,,Exa,Webber Wentzel’s UK ally becomes fourth global law firm to ditch SA in two years | News24,www.news24.com (Garth Theunissen),2026-07-28T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-28 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.news24.com/business/companies/webber-wentzels-uk-ally-becomes-fourth-global-law-firm-to-ditch-sa-in-two-years-20260728-0785,"Webber Wentzel’s UK ally becomes fourth global law firm to ditch SA in two years | News24 ... # Webber Wentzel’s UK ally becomes fourth global law firm to ditch SA in two years ... Webber Wentzel is among four top law firms that challenged new B-BBEE codes for the legal sector in the Pretoria High Court earlier this year. ... - Webber Wentzel and global law firm Linklaters have agreed to end their formal alliance from 1 November 2026. - This means Linklaters is the fourth big global law firm to turn its back on SA in the last two years. - Four of SA’s top law firms – including Webber Wentzel – are challenging new legal sector B-BBEE codes in court. - For more financial news, visit News24 Business. ... SA legal giant Webber Wentzel and its London-headquartered counterpart, Linklaters, have agreed to end their formal alliance after 13 years. ... Linklaters was founded in 1838 and is part of the so-called “Magic Circle”, a group of five leading London-based multinational law firms. ... Linklaters and Webber Wentzel worked together as part of a “collaborative alliance” for 60 years, which was formalised in 2013 when they agreed to jointly pursue opportunities on the continent. ... There wasn’t a financial arrangement between the two firms, and News24 understands that the ending of the alliance from November this year won’t have any financial consequences. ... Webber Wentzel announced the news in an email to clients on Tuesday, saying Africa’s legal and commercial landscape had changed significantly over the last 13 years. It added that the decision to return to a more informal collaboration with Linklaters reflected the “natural progression” of the two firms’ respective strategies. ... “Our alliance is ending, but our commitment to supporting clients on their most complex, cross-border matters remains unchanged,” said Aedamar Comiskey, senior partner and chair at Linklaters. “We look forward to continuing to serve our clients in Africa, drawing on the strength of our global platform and the depth of our relationships, market knowledge and experience across the continent.” ... The announcement means that Linklaters becomes the fourth global law firm to effectively turn its back on South Africa in the last two years. ... Norton Rose Fulbright (NRF) pulled out of the country in November last year, with its local arm reverting to Deneys, a shortened version of the Deneys Reitz moniker it had operated under prior to its 2011 buyout by the UK firm. ... Hogan Lovells, which is dual-headquartered in Washington DC and London, exited SA in September 2024 as part of a strategic decision to focus on its core international markets. It had initially entered SA in 2013 through a merger with local law firm Routledge Modise. ... The exit of global law firms from SA comes at a time when industry heavyweights are at loggerheads with the government over B-BBEE laws. Four of SA’s top law firms – namely Bowmans, Webber Wentzel, Werksmans and Deneys – are challenging legal sector B-BBEE codes in court, arguing they are irrational and unconstitutional. ... The Gauteng High Court heard arguments for and against the new B-BBEE codes for the legal sector in May, though judgment is still pending. The codes, which were gazetted by Trade, Industry and Competition Minister Parks Tau in September 2024, apply only to large law firms with annual revenues above R5 million but impose much stricter black ownership and management control requirements, among other requirements. ... The Legal Practice Council (LPC), which argued in favour of the codes alongside the Department of Trade, Industry and Competition, said at an event in June that the private sector engages in discriminatory procurement practices that favour what it called “white law firms”. ... Data provided by the LPC at the event showed that about 45% of practising attorneys and 40% of practising advocates in SA were white. The LPC also said its data showed that in 2025, some 72% of the partners or directors of SA’s top six law firms were white. ... looms - Premier Foods to shut down R ... ery - Mrs Ball’s ‘back where she belongs’, thanks to ... site upgrade - Webber Wentzel’s UK ally becomes fourth global law firm to ditch SA in two years - Joburg call centre workers lose jobs amid big shift to AI"
Linklaters,,,,Exa,"Linklaters advises Citi on landmark, largest-ever UK Equity Release Mortgages securitisation",legaldesire.com (Cynthia Lydia Marbaniang),2026-07-28T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-28 00:00:00+00:00,,https://legaldesire.com/linklaters-advises-citi-on-landmark-largest-ever-uk-equity-release-mortgages-securitisation/,"Linklaters advises Citi on landmark, largest-ever UK Equity Release Mortgages securitisation ... # Linklaters advises Citi on landmark, largest-ever UK Equity Release Mortgages securitisation ... Linklaters has advised Citibank, N.A., London Branch as sponsor and risk retainer on the acquisition and securitisation of a £1bn+ portfolio of Equity Release Mortgages (ERM) from Prudential Lifetime Mortgages Limited, a group company of leading savings and investment firm M&G Group. ... The landmark transaction is the first ERM securitisation where the junior tranche was marketed publicly rather than being privately placed, and it is the largest-ever ERM securitisation in the UK. ... Senior tranches were structured as matching adjustment eligible and were retained in their entirety by Prudential Assurance Corporation, another M&G Group entity. ... “We are delighted, once again, to support the Citi team on this first of its kind transaction, in an asset class that is seeing ever-increasing innovation and issuance volumes in the UK and European markets. This transaction again demonstrates our capabilities to structure and execute the most-complex and market-first securitisations.” ... The global tier-1 Linklaters teams regularly advise on innovative and landmark structured finance transactions, providing a one stop shop for loans, securitisation, derivatives, repurchase transactions and more, combining vast expertise across all financing products with a deep knowledge of the underlying regulatory framework. ... The international Linklaters team was led by Adam Fogarty and Counsel John McCallion. ... The team also included New York Partner Derek Poon and Counsel Catherine Russ. ... Please find more information on the specifics of this transaction here in the attached article: How Citi remade equity release RMBS with record-breaking Serval."
Linklaters,,,,Exa,Data Center Surges In Global Coverage - Light Mask,lightmask.net (Light Mask Team),2026-07-28T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-28 00:00:00+00:00,,https://lightmask.net/trending/data-center-surges-in-global-coverage/,"Data center mentions worldwide have increased sharply, with GDELT reporting 26 mentions in recent weeks, indicating growing global interest and expansion. ... Recent reports show a sharp increase in global coverage of data centers, with mentions rising from baseline levels. This indicates heightened interest and expansion in the sector, though specific drivers remain unclear. ... Global coverage of data centers has surged significantly in recent weeks, with the GDELT database recording 26 mentions—an increase of 21 times the baseline level. This spike indicates heightened attention from media and industry sources, highlighting the sector’s expanding importance worldwide. ... The GDELT (Global Database of Events, Language, and Tone) monitoring system reported 26 mentions of data centers within a recent time window, compared to a baseline of around 1 mention. This represents a substantial surge in media and online coverage, suggesting increased interest or activity in the sector. ... While the specific reasons for this surge are not yet fully clarified, analysts suggest that factors such as new data center projects, investments in cloud infrastructure, or geopolitical developments could be contributing. Industry experts note that this rise in coverage aligns with broader trends of digital transformation and cloud adoption. ... Officials from major data center companies and industry associations have not yet issued detailed statements regarding the coverage increase, and it remains to be seen whether this is driven by new developments or media attention cycles. ... The surge in global coverage underscores the growing strategic importance of data centers in the digital economy, cloud computing, and data security. Increased media attention can influence investor interest, policy discussions, and public perception, potentially accelerating sector growth or prompting regulatory scrutiny. ... For businesses and governments, heightened awareness may translate into more investment and infrastructure development, but also raises questions about environmental impact and data sovereignty. The sector’s visibility could shape future policies and industry standards. ... Over the past few years, the data center industry has experienced rapid growth driven by the expansion of cloud services, 5G deployment, and digital transformation initiatives. The GDELT database has historically shown low levels of media mentions, so the recent spike indicates a notable shift. ... In particular, recent announcements of new data center projects in key markets, geopolitical tensions affecting supply chains, and increased investments from tech giants have likely contributed to this surge. Industry analysts note that media coverage often correlates with sector expansion, regulatory developments, or major technological breakthroughs. ... However, it remains unclear whether this increase in mentions reflects actual growth and activity or is influenced by media cycles and reporting trends. ... It is not yet clear what specific factors are driving the surge in mentions. While industry activity and new projects are possible causes, there is no confirmed data linking the coverage spike to particular developments or investments. Further analysis is needed to determine whether this is a temporary media cycle or reflects sustained sector growth. ... Industry analysts and market observers will track upcoming project announcements, investment flows, and policy developments to assess whether the coverage surge indicates a genuine expansion trend. Media monitoring and sector reports over the coming months will clarify if this is a transient spike or a sign of longer-term growth. ... stakeholders may issue statements or data releases that shed light on ... underlying drivers of this increased attention. ... The increase suggests heightened interest and possibly expanding investment in data infrastructure, though the actual growth impact remains to be confirmed through sector data. ... It is not yet clear which regions or companies are most involved; further analysis of media sources is required to identify key players. ... ### Could this coverage increase lead to regulatory changes? ... Potentially, as greater visibility might prompt policymakers to consider new regulations related to data security, privacy, and environmental impact, but no such changes have been confirmed yet. ... While possible, there is no direct evidence linking the coverage spike to specific technological breakthroughs or innovations at this stage. ... Exploring the rapid expansion of data centers across Texas and what occurs inside these…"
Linklaters,,,,Exa,"Latham, Linklaters Lead as Nestlé Taps US PE Firm for $5.6B Water JV | Law.com",www.law.com,2026-07-24T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-24 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.law.com/international-edition/2026/07/24/latham-linklaters-lead-as-nestl-taps-us-pe-firm-for-56b-water-jv/,"Latham, Linklaters Lead as Nestlé Taps US PE Firm for $5.6B Water JV | Law.com ... # Latham, Linklaters Lead as Nestlé Taps US PE Firm for $5.6B Water JV ... It is the second multi-billion deal for Linklaters this week as it advises long time client Nestlé. ... Latham & Watkins and Linklaters are leading on the $5.6 billion spin-out of a stake in Nestlé's water unit in a 50:50 venture between the Swiss food giant and U.S. private equity firm Platinum Equity. ... The deal sees Nestlé's break off its water arm into a new entity called Peranel Water, with well-known brands such as San Pellegrino and Perrier spinning out, in another example of U.S. private equity's massive influence on global M&A markets. ... Platinum Equity is being advised by Latham & Watkins while Nestlé has instructed Linklaters. The transaction is subject to regulatory approvals but is expected to close in H1 2027. ... Corporate partner Namrata Shah and TMT partner Greg Palmer are based in London with Pierre Zelenko on antitrust in Paris leading the Linklaters team. ... Nestlé, and its subsidiaries, is also a long-time client of Linklaters; the firm advised the Swiss foods giant on its launch of its ice cream joint venture in 2016 worth €3 billion and the carve out and sale of its skin health brand to EQT for $10.1 billion in 2019. Shah advised on the skin health brand sale as well. ... It is the latest mandate for Linklaters in a week which has already seen them scoop a role on the £13.5 billion bidding war for Segro opposite Slaughter and May. ... Linklaters ranked third for M&A in the U.K. for the first half of this year, advising on $76.9 billion worth of transactions with a 23.1% market share. Latham ranked fourth globally in the rankings. ... ## Clifford Chance, Linklaters and Milbank Lead Largest European IPO This Year"
Linklaters,,,,Exa,Linklaters Germany Breaks €1 Million Revenue-Per-Lawyer Barrier With Lower Headcount | Law.com,www.law.com,2026-07-23T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-23 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.law.com/international-edition/2026/07/23/linklaters-germany-breaks-1-million-revenue-per-lawyer-barrier-with-lower-headcount/,"ers Germany Breaks ... # Linklaters Germany Breaks €1 Million Revenue-Per-Lawyer Barrier With Lower Headcount ... The firm’s revenue per lawyer in Germany increased by 8% in the latest fiscal year with a new managing partner and a strategy that focuses on German corporates and restructuring. ... One year after appointing a new German managing partner, Linklaters says its focus on complex international mandates is delivering results. ... German revenue rose 3% to €288.7 million ($330 million) for the financial year ended April 30, 2026, up from €280 million during the same period the previous year. At the same time, the number of Linklaters' fee earners in Germany fell 5.2% year-on-year—a drop in headcount from 293 to the equivalent of 277.8 fee earners—and revenue per lawyer (RPL) rose to about €1.04 million, surpassing the €1 million mark for the first time, an increase of nearly 9%. ... Country managing partner Staffan Illert, who took charge in the summer of 2025, said the figures reflect Linklaters' year-long strategy, which includes a focus on advising German corporates who are being increasingly targeted by American private equity. ... “These figures result from the consequent implementation of our strategy, focusing on complex mandates for leading international companies, private equity investors and financial institutions,” Illert said. “There is almost not a single DAX 40 company we don't advise with our corporate, finance and litigation practices.” ... Some of Linklaters’ most significant deals during the period include advising on GIC’s €9.5 billion investment in German grid operator TenneT and the $2 billion Lonza acquisition of building materials firm Xella. ... Internationally, Linklaters reported revenue of £2.47 billion for the financial year ended April 30, 2026, a 6.8% rise over the previous year. Pre-tax profit increased 11.6% to £1.2 billion, and average profit per equity partner increased 11.4% to £2.48 million. The number of equity partners fell slightly to 490, a decline of 2.4% from the previous year. ... The figures show that Germany makes up less than 10% of Linklaters’ global revenue**,** although Illert described the country as a key contributor to Linklaters’ international practice. ""Germany is one of the firm's engines. We export a lot of work to other offices, with many clients being true global players.” ... Illert, an M&A and corporate partner, sees a robust pipeline of work ahead for the German offices, despite macroeconomic uncertainty. ... ""The pipeline for M&A, complex financings and restructurings or challenging litigation remains very strong,” he said, adding that structural changes in the German economy continue to generate significant advisory work, particularly in energy and defence. ... ""There are many industrial corporates looking into the defence business,” Illert said. “We have recently advised Heidelberger Druckmaschinen, the market leader in producing high-quality printing machines, on a drone joint venture with an Israeli entity."" ... Linklaters’ future investment in Germany will be concentrated on four major offices after closing the Hamburg office at the end of 2026. ""Our strategic growth investments in Germany are focused on Frankfurt, Dusseldorf, Berlin and Munich, where we see the opportunities to further 'strengthen the quality of our business,"" he said. ... ""Transformation of the economy has always been one of the motors for us,” Illert added, with the firm working on Deutsche Glasfaser's €7billion restructuring, one of the largest German restructurings in recent years. ... Linklaters entered the German market by merging with Oppenhoff & Rädler in 2001, but now the firm's primary geographical priority is the U.S., much like its Anglo-German rival Freshfields. ... ""Our number one regional focus is clearly the U.S. That's where we want to grow,"" Illert said. ... He added that the firm's investment in its U.S. platform is increasingly paying off with international clients. ""We have an offering now that, for example, European clients do their U.S. work with us."" ... At the same time, artificial intelligence is changing the legal market and Linklaters isn't alone in wrestling with the future of Big Law. ... “AI is a core pillar of our strategy,” said Illert, noting “it's essential to respond to some very important questions: How will it change the business model, our role, our size and shape as a firm?"" ... The firm launched a bespoke platform, Applied Intelligence, in May but also uses off-the-shelf products like Legora. He expects the technological changes to accelerate changes in legal pricing. ... ""The future will move away from selling hours—it will be more about value and fixed prices."""
Linklaters,,,,Exa,Linklaters Advises Blackstone On The Sale Of Logistics Asset Portfolio. - Conventus Law,conventuslaw.com (Rohin Pujari),2026-07-23T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-23 00:00:00+00:00,,https://conventuslaw.com/press-releases/linklaters-advises-blackstone-on-the-sale-of-logistics-asset-portfolio/,"Linklaters Advises Blackstone On The Sale Of Logistics Asset Portfolio. - Conventus Law ... # Linklaters Advises Blackstone On The Sale Of Logistics Asset Portfolio. ... July 23, 2026 ... Linklaters, together with other law firms, has advised Blackstone on the sale of a logistics assets portfolio to vehicles invested by CBRE IM and managed by ExCorde Capital. The portfolio included 19 logistics assets across Germany, Italy, the Netherlands, Poland and Spain. The transaction involved a combination of share and asset sales with a total deal value of about €310m. ... A cross-practice Linklaters team advised Blackstone on the German part of the transaction. The team was led by Real Estate Partner Hannes Dietmaier and Managing Associate Barbara Rybka and included Counsels Isabelle-Carmen Petersen Weis(Banking), Anne Katharina Rosenow(Tax), as well as Associates Hanna Dolata and Felix Kolbe(all Real Estate.) ... Linklaters is a leading advisor for logistics asset transactions in Germany. The team has advised Blackstone on several transactions in Germany."
Linklaters,,,,Exa,"Linklaters Revenue Creeps Up as US, Asia Return Soaring Profits",news.bloomberglaw.com,2026-07-21T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-21 00:00:00+00:00,,https://news.bloomberglaw.com/business-and-practice/linklaters-revenue-creeps-up-as-us-asia-return-soaring-profits,"Linklaters Revenue Creeps Up as US, Asia Return Soaring Profits ... UK-founded law firm Linklaters reported an uptick in revenue for fiscal 2026, with bright spots in Asia and the US. ... The firm said its pre-tax profit was £1.2 billion ($1.6 billion) in the year ended April 30, up 11.6% from the previous comparable period. Profit per equity partner was £2.5 million ($3.34 million), up 11.4%. Linklaters generated £2.5 billion ($3.3 billion) in revenue, 6.8% more than in the previous year. ... “Asia has been the story of the year for me,” Paul Lewis, Linklaters’ firmwide managing partner, said in an interview. “Our Asia performance, after a number of years where the entire region’s market was a bit slower, came roaring back and we were strong across the board.” ... The firm reported a 39% increase in its US profits and 36% in Asia, out-distancing performance in other core markets such as Europe at 10% and the UK at 7%. ... Linklaters in May guided Hong Kong billionaire Victor Li’s CK Group in its sale of the UK’s largest power distribution network UK Power Networks to French utility Engie SA for £10.5 billion ($14.2 billion). The transaction capitalized on a surge of demand from data centers and electric vehicles. ... Crossborder deals work is key for the firm as it continues to ramp up in the US market. “We are building a brand in the US rather than having a brand in the US,” said Lewis. “And as things currently stand, we know that it’s building on trajectory.” ... The London “Magic Circle” firm ranked 16 in Bloomberg Law’s M&A league tables for the first half of 2026, placing it among US Big Law firms. A&O Shearman last week posted a flat $3.7 billion in revenue for fiscal 2026, along with profit gains. ... The firm this month poached FIFA’s go-to New York litigators Chris Boehning—who joined as chair of the global sports practice and US strategic disputes—and partner Daniel Levi from Paul Weiss. ... “I don’t think we have had any missteps, certainly not any expensive missteps,” said Lewis about the firm’s US hires. “I think that is something that we’re very focused on.” ... The firm is not looking to expand via a merger with an American firm, according to Lewis, despite a July 13 Semafor report that Linklaters was shot down by New York’s Clear Gottlieb earlier this year. ... “We have not approached anyone for a merger,” he said. “We’re happy pursuing the strategy we’re pursuing.”"
Linklaters,,,,Exa,Linklaters reports record FY26 results,www.linklaters.com,2026-07-21T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-21 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.linklaters.com/about-us/news-and-deals/news/2026/july/linklaters-reports-record-fy26-results,"Linklaters has released its financial results for the year ended 30 April 2026, reporting record revenue and profit across its global platform. ... - Revenue was £2.47bn, +6.8% vs FY25. - Pre-tax profit was £1.2bn, +11.6%, the third consecutive year of double-digit profit growth. - Profit growth in all core markets: Asia +36%, Europe +10%, UK +7%, and US +39%. - Profit per all partners was £2.31m, +10.7%. - Profit per equity partner was £2.48m, +11.4%. ... Our momentum continues to build as we attract and develop top tier talent and deepen relationships with global clients, including in the US, where we have doubled revenue and quadrupled profit over the past four years. We continued to strengthen our leadership in legal AI, launching our applied intelligence team, hiring dedicated AI lawyers and rolling out frontier agentic technology to deliver more, better and faster, for clients. ... The firm has grown its US partnership by more than 75% since 2021 with the addition of elite teams across M&A, disputes and finance. Most recently, in New York, H. Christopher Boehning joined the firm as a partner and chair of the global sports practice and US strategic disputes, and Daniel H. Levi joined as a partner in the firm’s litigation, arbitration and investigations practice, bringing world-class commercial disputes expertise for clients such as FIFA and Regeneron. Linklaters also boosted its New York corporate practice with the addition of Kristina Trauger as a capital markets and M&A partner, and appointed George Casey chair of the Americas and Adeel Mangi chair of the trial practice. ... The firm hired an eight-lawyer restructuring and insolvency team in Paris led by François Kopf, who joined as global chair of restructuring and insolvency, and which included Mathieu Della Vittoria. Also in Paris, the firm boosted its leveraged finance capabilities with the addition of Pierre-Arnoux Mayoly and Shirin Deyhim. Meanwhile, Massimiliano Nitti, and Giovanni Colantuono joined as partners in Italy, with Massimiliano joining as head of corporate there. Both further enhance the firm’s global M&A and private equity offering. ... The firm accelerated its investment in AI this year, rolling out Legora’s market-leading platform in September across all 30 offices and recruiting and training a growing team of AI Lawyers to work alongside global teams. ... In May, the firm launched applied intelligence, a new practice combining Linklaters’ legal, AI and data science expertise, to build bespoke AI-led solutions for clients on their most complex mandates – working in partnership with practice teams and sector experts.Advising on the biggest cross-border deals ... Linklaters’ market-leading corporate team advised on US$167bn worth of deals in 2025 and that momentum continued into H1 2026, with the value of deals advised on more than tripling compared with the same period last year. ... This included advising CK Group on its US$10.5bn sale of UK Power Networks to Engie; Volkswagen and Porsche on the sale of Porsche’s stake in Bugatti Rimac; La Caisse on KKR’s US$1.5bn strategic equity investment in Vertical Bridge; Arise Digital Technology on its US$3.2bn acquisition of a stake in True Corporation; Brookfield on the sale of a stake in its Paris-based data centre platform, Data4 Group, to Arjun Infrastructure Partners; Alphawave IP Group, on its recommended US$2.4bn acquisition by a subsidiary of Qualcomm; Unilever’s demerger of The Magnum Ice Cream Company and subsequent listings in Amsterdam, London and New York; Nexfibre and its investors on the £2bn acquisition of UK’s second largest alternative fibre provider; and Dow on a US$540m investment by Macquarie Asset Management in Diamond Infrastructure Solutions, following the launch of the US$6bn platform in 2024. ... Linklaters experienced strong demand for public M&A. This included advising Tate & Lyle on its £2.7bn cash takeover by US rival Ingredion; Dai-ichi Life on its acquisition of a stake in M&G plc; and Macquarie Asset Management on its recommended cash offer for Renewi plc.Growth in complex global financings and restructurings ... Linklaters advised on many of the market’s most innovative and transformational energy and infrastructure projects, including the UK Government on the financing of the landmark £38bn Sizewell C Nuclear Plant Project. Other landmark infrastructure financing projects included the US$15bn Amiral Petrochemical Complex, one of the largest integrated petrochemical projects ever developed in the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia; the US$10bn Taiwanese Hai Long 2 & 3 and Greater Changhua 2A, 2B & 4 Offshore Wind Farms, the largest offshore wind project financings in Asia to date; and the US$5bn HSAGP Energy Gigafactory, one of the largest EV battery gigafactory investments in the US.At the forefront of private capital fundraising and financing ... Linklaters supported private capital clients across the full spectrum of fundraising, deployment and strategic investment opportunities, advising on fundraising in excess of US$175bn and on the investment of over US$25bn into more than 200 funds, including EQT Group on BPEA Private Equity Fund IX, the largest-ever Asia Pacific-dedicated private equity fund raised to date.The firm advised on some of the largest private capital financings and refinancings, including Apollo-managed funds on their agreement to invest in a 50% stake in Ørsted’s Hornsea 3, the world’s largest offshore wind project; Brookfield-owned data centre operator Data4 on its €3.3bn debt raise, the largest digital infrastructure financing deal of its kind in Europe, and on its refinancing post-acquisition. Linklaters advised the mandated lead arrangers, Apollo, Ares and Blackstone Real Estate Debt Strategies, on a senior-only refinancing of an existing senior/mezzanine financing for a portfolio of Blackstone-owned residential assets in the Testa portfolio in Spain. The firm also advised on the €890m high yield bond offering by Neopharmed Gentili (a portfolio company of Ardian and Renaissance Partners); and Warburg Pincus’ landmark investment in Acclime. Acting on business-critical contentious mandates ... Linklaters’ leading litigation, arbitration and investigations division continued to represent clients across a range of sectors in relation to significant, business-critical issues. This included representing Kelkoo in its high-profile dispute with Google alleging abuse of dominance in the shopping comparison and search advertising markets; Visa in its defence of thousands of complex funded group proceedings and parallel opt in and opt out class actions in the Competition Appeal Tribunal; Sony Interactive Entertainment in a consumer class action relating to the PlayStation Store; and Johnson & Johnson in several pieces of material US commercial litigation. New online safety legislation also led to a significant increase in regulatory and public law mandates across the tech sector.Celebrating significant milestones ... Following highly successful first terms, Linklaters’ global partnership voted in June 2025 to extend Aedamar Comiskey as senior partner and chair, and Paul Lewis as firmwide managing partner until 2029. In March, the firm moved into its new global headquarters at 20 Ropemaker Street in London, a 360,000sq ft landmark development designed to support the firm’s culture of teamwork and high-performance. This year also saw a move to new premises in Shanghai and Singapore. ... - Achieved #1 rankings for M&A in the UK (up from #22 last year) and France – and #3 in Europe – in the Bloomberg H1 2026 league tables, while also ranking #2 in Europe (up from #17 last year) in Mergermarket. ... - Achieved #1 M&A H1 2026 deal value rankings in Asia-Pacific excluding Japan, #1 in Asia excluding Australasia and Japan, and #1 in Greater China in the Mergermarket league tables, underlining the strength of our leading Asia platform."
Linklaters,,,,Exa,No Merger Needed: Linklaters Posts Strong Financial Results - Above the Law,abovethelaw.com (Staci Zaretsky),2026-07-21T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-21 00:00:00+00:00,,https://abovethelaw.com/2026/07/no-merger-needed-linklaters-posts-strong-financial-results/,"No Merger Needed: Linklaters Posts Strong Financial Results - Above the Law ... Linklaters has spent years talking about its international growth strategy. This year, the numbers suggest it’s paying off. ... The Magic Circle firm reported revenue of £2.5 billion ($3.3 billion) for fiscal 2026, a 6.8% increase over the previous year. Pre-tax profit climbed 11.6% to £1.2 billion ($1.6 billion), while profit per equity partner rose 11.4% to £2.5 million ($3.34 million). ... The firm’s strongest performances came from the very markets it’s been working hardest to expand. ... Profits in the U.S. jumped 39%, while Asia posted 36% growth, easily outpacing Europe (10%) and the UK (7%). For firmwide managing partner Paul Lewis, there was no question which region stole the show. As he explained to Bloomberg Law: ... “Asia has been the story of the year for me. Our Asia performance, after a number of years where the entire region’s market was a bit slower, came roaring back and we were strong across the board.” ... The U.S. remains another major focus. Lewis said the firm is continuing to build its brand in the American market as it expands its cross-border practice. “We are building a brand in the US rather than having a brand in the US,” Lewis said. “And as things currently stand, we know that it’s building on trajectory.” ... That strategy has included lateral hiring, with the firm continuing to add prominent lawyers to strengthen its U.S. platform. One thing Lewis says isn’t part of the plan, however, is a merger with an American firm. ... His comments come just days after a Semafor report claimed Linklaters unsuccessfully approached Cleary Gottlieb about a merger earlier this year — a report both firms publicly denied. Lewis flatly rejected the idea: “We have not approached anyone for a merger. We’re happy pursuing the strategy we’re pursuing.” ... If these financial results are any indication, Linklaters doesn’t see a merger as necessary. Its strategy of growing its U.S. practice, capitalizing on cross-border work, and taking advantage of Asia’s rebound appears to be doing exactly what the firm hoped it would. ... Linklaters Revenue Creeps Up as US, Asia Return Soaring Profits [Bloomberg Law]"
Linklaters,,,,Exa,Magic Circle firm Linklaters sees partner profits soar to £2.5m after record year,www.cityam.com (Maria Ward-Brennan),2026-07-21T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-21 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.cityam.com/magic-circle-firm-linklaters-sees-partner-profits-soar-to-2-5m-after-record-year/,"Magic Circle firm Linklaters sees partner profits soar to £2.5m after record year ... Linklaters unveils new global headquarters at 20 Ropemaker Street ... Partners at magic circle law firm Linklaters saw pay packets double after the firm had its third consecutive year of double-digit profit growth. ... For its year to 30 April, 2026, Linklaters reported record revenue of £2.47bn, up nearly 7 per cent, and pre-tax profit of £1.2bn, up over 11 per cent, after hitting the billion-pound mark last year for the first time. ... As a result, profit per equity partner (PEP) rose over 11 per cent to £2.48m, while profit per all partners grew nearly 11 per cent to £2.31m. ... Firmwide managing partner, Paul Lewis, called it “another outstanding year”, adding, “our results reflect the confidence and trust our clients continue to place in us to advise on their most high-stakes matters.” ... Like the rest of its top English firms, Linklaters has been on a punchy international expansion drive and is determined to break into the US market. ... Over the past four years, the results show that the firm has doubled its US revenue and quadrupled profit, increasing its US partnership to around 55 partners. Recently, the magic circle giant snagged two of FIFA’s lawyers from a rival firm, Paul Weiss, to bolster its US strategic disputes. ... ## Firm moved into new HQ ... The firm opened new offices in Shanghai and Singapore and added 37 new elected partners and 12 lateral partner hires across global offices. ... In the City, the firm moved into its new global headquarters at 20 Ropemaker Street, an office that consists of 360,000 sq ft of space. ... Its mandates over the last year include advising the UK government on the £38bn Sizewell C Nuclear Plant Project, representing Unilever on the global demerger and listings of The Magnum Ice Cream Company, and defending Visa against class actions and group proceedings in the Competition Appeal Tribunal (CAT) and Kelkoo in its antitrust lawsuit against Google. ... Like all major law firms, Linklaters has made its own moves on legal AI. Lewis explained: “We continued to strengthen our leadership in legal AI, launching our Applied Intelligence team, hiring dedicated AI lawyers and rolling out frontier agentic technology to deliver more, better and faster, for clients.” ... ### Magic circle Linklaters scores FIFA’s top lawyers in US raid"
Linklaters,,,,Exa,Linklaters advises syndicate of lenders and hedge counterparties on €7bn recapitalisation of Deutsche Glasfaser,legaldesire.com (Cynthia Lydia Marbaniang),2026-07-21T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-21 00:00:00+00:00,,https://legaldesire.com/linklaters-advises-syndicate-of-lenders-and-hedge-counterparties-on-e7bn-recapitalisation-of-deutsche-glasfaser/,"Linklaters advises syndicate of lenders and hedge counterparties on €7bn recapitalisation of Deutsche Glasfaser ... # Linklaters advises syndicate of lenders and hedge counterparties on €7bn recapitalisation of Deutsche Glasfaser ... - Author Cynthia Lydia Marbaniang - Published July 21, 2026 ... Linklaters has advised the full syndicate of lenders and hedge counterparties to Deutsche Glasfaser in relation to the €7 billion recapitalisation of the group by way of a single class UK Scheme of Arrangement."
Linklaters,,,,Exa,"Linklaters Posts Double-Digit Profit Gains, Seeing Nearly 40% Growth in US Arm Profitability | Law.com",www.law.com,2026-07-20T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-20 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.law.com/international-edition/2026/07/21/linklaters-posts-double-digit-profit-gains-seeing-nearly-40-growth-in-us-arm-profitability/,"Linklaters Posts Double-Digit Profit Gains, Seeing Nearly 40% Growth in US Arm Profitability | Law.com ... # Linklaters Posts Double-Digit Profit Gains, Seeing Nearly 40% Growth in US Arm Profitability ... Profit gains were concentrated in the U.S., which grew by nearly 40%, and in Asia, where profitability increased by 36%, according to the firm’s figures. ... - Global law firm Linklaters has posted 11.6% growth in its profits, including a 39% growth in the profitability in its U.S. branch. - Revenue grew by 6.8%, while PEP increased by 11.4% year-over-year, the firm said. ... Global law firm Linklaters secured a double-digit increase in profitability in its most recent fiscal year, with greater corporate demand driving the firm's financial performance as well as growing profit gains in the U.S., according to financial results it released Tuesday. ... Linklaters, which only reports financial performance in pounds, grew revenue by 6.8% to £2.47 billion in its fiscal year ending April 30, 2026, while profit before taxes grew by 11.6% to £1.2 billion. Profit growth was concentrated in the U.S., which grew by nearly 40% year-over-year, and in Asia, where profitability increased by 36%, according to the firm’s figures. ... Average profits per equity partner across the entire firm, meanwhile, grew by 11.4% to £2.48 million. Converted to dollars, revenue grew to about $3.33 billion, pre-tax profit increased to $1.62 billion, and PEP came in at $3.35 million. ... ""Our momentum continues to build as we attract and develop top-tier talent and deepen relationships with global clients, including in the U.S., where we have doubled revenue and quadrupled profit over the past four years,"" firmwide managing partner Paul Lewis said in a statement. ""This has been another outstanding year for Linklaters globally. Our results reflect the confidence and trust our clients continue to place in us to advise on their most high-stakes matters."" ... Linklaters' financial report comes shortly after sources said Linklaters approached Cleary Gottlieb Steen & Hamilton for potential merger talks, an offer that Cleary rebuffed. Both firms, in statements, denied any merger talks. ... Still, Linklaters cultivated 39% growth in profits in the United States, according to the firm's announcement, with the firm touting several hires in the U.S., including global sports practice and U.S. strategic disputes head Christopher Boehning and litigator Daniel Levi, both of whom joined this month from Paul, Weiss, Rifkind, Wharton & Garrison. Total U.S. headcount for the firm is 216 lawyers, including 55 partners, the firm said. ... Compared with the broader performance of the Am Law 100, Linklaters' growth lagged slightly behind the U.S. firms, which benefited from rate increases and increased leverage. The Am Law 100 on average grew revenue by 13% year over year, while net income increased by 16% and PEP rose by 14% year-over-year. ... Despite concerns about U.K.-founded firms struggling to compete in compensation and profits against top American firms, Linklaters told Law.com this month that it is going strong in the U.S. and is continuing to build a ""market-leading"" presence in the country. ... global chair of corporate ... Globally, Linklaters grew its Asia profits by 36%, while Europe and the U.K. saw more moderate profit growth at 10% and 7% respectively. ... Boosting headcount recently, Linklaters hired a group of three regulatory attorneys from Weil, Gotshal & Manges in Paris in June 2026, as well as adding hires bolstering its leveraged finance, credit solutions, and international law teams in London. ... The firm highlighted the launch of its Applied Intelligence practice, a practice meant to create AI tools and workflows for clients. The firm announced last year it had rolled out AI platform Legora across its 30 offices, in addition to recruiting an inaugural cohort of 20 'AI lawyers' to accelerate the development of AI legal solutions for clients. ... Linklaters noted that it advised on a total of $167 billion worth of deals in 2025, with demand continuing into the first half of 2026. The firm also noted that the value of its deals more than tripled in H1 2026 compared with the same period in 2025. ... Key matters for Linklaters in its most recent fiscal year included advising the U.K. government regarding the Sizewell C Nuclear Plant project, valued at about £38 billion; Unilever's separation from the Magnum Ice Cream Company in a complex global carve-out; and the CK Group's $10 billion sale of UK Power Networks to Engie, to name just a few of the firm's deals."
Linklaters,,,,Exa,Linklaters and Slaughter and May Lead in £13.5B Bidding War for Segro | Law.com,www.law.com,2026-07-20T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-20 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.law.com/international-edition/2026/07/20/linklaters-and-slaughter-and-may-lead-in-135b-bidding-war-for-segro/,"Linklaters and Slaughter and May Lead in £13.5B Bidding War for Segro | Law.com ... # Linklaters and Slaughter and May Lead in £13.5B Bidding War for Segro ... Both firms are leaning on prior relationships as the 'put up or shut up' deadline approaches. ... Linklaters and Slaughter and May are advising on Prologis’ rejected £13.5 billion bid for Segro as the intense bidding process continues. ... It is the third offer in as many weeks, with Linklaters advising the U.S. real estate investment trust and Slaughter and May advising long term client, Segro. ... Managing partner of Slaughter and May David Johnson is advising alongside corporate partners Alex Dustan, Sally Wokes and Jack Wharton. ... Slaughter and May has advised Prologis numerous times this year including a development of a data centre in Paris and financing of €1.4 billion in notes. ... The team for Linklaters was led by corporate partners Jonathan Sadler and Iain Fenn. ... Linklaters has advised Prologis numerous times before, including on its €2.4 billion European real estate joint venture with Norges Bank Investment Management and its acquisition of Prologis Park Grodzisk in Poland in 2025. ... Prologis have a deadline of July 22 to submit a final offer. ... The company has said that they are open to exploring a secondary listing on the London Stock Exchange if the deal goes through and there is investor demand. Prologis estimates Segro shareholders would be 36.5% better off if they had accepted an all share offer in March 2024. ... ## Clifford Chance, Linklaters and Milbank Lead Largest European IPO This Year ... rise as Swiss advertising company ... G goes for $5.7 billion."
Linklaters,,,,Exa,Linklaters Smashes Record as Aggressive US Expansion Pushes to Unprecedented FY26 Heights,www.lawfuel.com (Tom Borman),2026-07-20T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-20 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.lawfuel.com/linklaters-smashes-record-as-aggressive-us-expansion-pushes-to-unprecedented-fy26-heights/,"Linklaters Smashes Record as Aggressive US Expansion Pushes to Unprecedented FY26 Heights ... Following a blockbuster run that saw the firm smash the billion-pound profit ceiling in recent years, Linklaters has once again rewritten its own record books. According to the firm’s newly released FY26 financials, the firm has posted unprecedented revenue and profit-per-equity-partner (PEP) figures, confirming what the market has been whispering: Linklaters’ aggressive transatlantic play is paying massive dividends. ... Riding the wave of an unrelenting U.S. expansion strategy and robust cross-border mandates, the firm’s FY26 results stand as a further testament to its aggressive growth, building on its 2025 growth. Linklaters is clearly no longer just defending its European fortress; it is taking the fight directly to the doorsteps of the most profitable American firms. ... But it is also profiting from lucrative Asian work, generating partner profits of up to £2.5 million according to a Financial Times report. ... If you want to understand Linklaters’ recent growth trajectory, look no further than New York. Over the past 24 to 36 months, Linklaters hasn’t just dipped its toes into the American market; it has cannonballed into it. ... The strategic coup of bringing on heavyweights like George Casey to chair the Americas, alongside a top-tier litigation and finance team (including Adeel Mangi, Muhammad Faridi, and David Lucking), signalled a clear departure from the conservative, wait-and-see approach historically associated with London-headquartered firms. ... Firmwide managing partner Paul Lewis said: “We continued to strengthen our leadership in legal AI, launching our applied intelligence team, hiring dedicated AI lawyers and rolling out frontier agentic technology to deliver more, better and faster, for clients.“ ... The expensive lateral raids, which included hires from Paul Weiss last month, have effectively turbo-charged the firm’s U.S. operations. The FY26 numbers reflect a maturing Stateside footprint that is now actively driving top-line growth rather than merely complementing the European network. ... While the headline numbers are a cause for champagne at Silk Street, the past year hasn’t been without its battle scars and strategic pivots. ... - The Brutal Talent War: The transatlantic talent war remains vicious. U.S. firms in London continue to wield massive checkbooks (fueled by the ever-increasing Big Law salary scales), forcing Linklaters to consistently re-evaluate its lockstep model to retain star rainmakers. The firm’s ability to push its PEP figures substantially higher is as much a defensive necessity to ward off poachers as it is a victory lap. ... - Capitalising on the Cycle: The firm has astutely navigated global economic headwinds, leaning heavily on its premier restructuring and insolvency practices. Bolstered by major recent hires across Europe, such as François Kopf in Paris, Linklaters has proven that when markets get choppy, its counter-cyclical practices are virtually bulletproof. ... - Tech and AI Integration: The AI arms race in Big Law is well underway, and Linklaters has spent the past year heavily investing in proprietary legal tech to streamline complex M&A and leveraged finance mandates. The operational efficiency is no longer just a marketing talking point but is materially showing up in the bottom line. ... Linklaters’ FY26 results aren’t just a financial statement. The firm has successfully shaken off the legacy constraints of the traditional Magic Circle to operate as a truly integrated, highly aggressive global powerhouse. ... Building on the back of its £2.32bn revenue and £2.2m PEP milestones from FY25, this latest record-breaking year proves the momentum is real. As they look to FY27, the challenge will be maintaining this blistering pace while fending off elite U.S. rivals on both sides of the Atlantic."
Linklaters,,,,Exa,Linklaters advises Resolution Life on two strategic reinsurance transactions in Hong Kong SAR and Japan,legaldesire.com (Cynthia Lydia Marbaniang),2026-07-17T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-17 00:00:00+00:00,,https://legaldesire.com/linklaters-advises-resolution-life-on-two-strategic-reinsurance-transactions-in-hong-kong-sar-and-japan/,"Linklaters advises Resolution Life on two strategic reinsurance transactions in Hong Kong SAR and Japan ... # Linklaters advises Resolution Life on two strategic reinsurance transactions in Hong Kong SAR and Japan ... Linklaters has advised Resolution Life, a global life insurance group focusing on reinsurance and the acquisition and management of portfolios of life insurance policies, on two significant reinsurance transactions covering policies in Hong Kong SAR and Japan. These transactions mark another milestone in Resolution Life’s expanding presence across Asia and continued commitment to delivering tailored reinsurance solutions to life insurance companies across the region. ... These transactions demonstrate Linklaters’ depth of expertise in the insurance sector, drawing on a cross-disciplinary team across a range of market-leading practice areas. Linklaters’ previous work in Asia includes advising Resolution Life on its acquisition by Nippon Life and on Resolution Life’s landmark first reinsurance transaction in Hong Kong SAR and various block and flow reinsurance transactions in Japan. ... The Linklaters team was led by Tokyo-based corporate partner and global co-head of the insurance sector Tracy Whiriskey, and included amongst others managing associates Takahisa Watanabe, Hirofumi Tsunoda and De Hong Lee, associate Calvin Rokich and legal advisor Rory Dolan. ... Linklaters’ global co-head of the insurance sector Tracy Whiriskey commented: ... “We are delighted to have advised Resolution Life on these two strategic transactions in Asia which mark the latest steps in their impressive growth across Asia and are grateful for the trust they place in our team. These matters reflect the strength of our cross-border insurance sector offering to support clients on complex cross-border insurance transactions as they continue to expand their presence in key markets across Asia.”"
Linklaters,,,,Exa,Linklaters Advised Resolution Life on Strategic Reinsurance Transactions in Hong Kong SAR and Japan,www.legaleraonline.com (Legal Era News Network),2026-07-16T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-16 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.legaleraonline.com/deal-street/linklaters-advised-resolution-life-on-strategic-reinsurance-transactions-in-hong-kong-sar-and-japan-987821,"Linklaters Advised Resolution Life on Strategic Reinsurance Transactions in Hong Kong SAR and Japan ... # Linklaters Advised Resolution Life on Strategic Reinsurance Transactions in Hong Kong SAR and Japan ... By Legal Era News Network| Law Firm Linklaters ... Linklaters Advised Resolution Life on Strategic Reinsurance Transactions in Hong Kong SAR and Japan ... Linklaters has advised Resolution Life, a leading global life insurance group specializing in reinsurance and the acquisition and management of life insurance portfolios, on two strategic reinsurance transactions covering policies in Hong Kong SAR and Japan. The transactions further strengthen Resolution Life's expanding presence in Asia and reinforce its commitment to delivering tailored reinsurance solutions to life insurance companies across the region. ... The mandates demonstrate Linklaters' strong cross-border insurance capabilities and its longstanding relationship with Resolution Life. The firm has previously advised the company on its acquisition by Nippon Life, its inaugural reinsurance transaction in Hong Kong SAR, and multiple block and flow reinsurance transactions in Japan. ... Commenting on the transactions, Tracy Whiriskey said: ... > ""We are delighted to have advised Resolution Life on these two strategic transactions in Asia, which mark the latest steps in their impressive growth across the region. We are grateful for the trust they continue to place in our team. These matters reflect the strength of our cross-border insurance practice in supporting clients on complex insurance transactions as they expand their presence in key Asian markets."""
Linklaters,,,,Exa,‘Organic growth in the US is fraught with risk’ – why transatlantic merger talks aren’t going away – Legal Business,www.legalbusiness.co.uk,2026-07-15T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-15 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.legalbusiness.co.uk/law-firms/organic-growth-in-the-us-is-fraught-with-risk-why-transatlantic-mergers-talks-arent-going-away/,"In an era of mega-mergers, two firms are clear that a rumoured tie-up between them is off the table: Linklaters and Cleary Gottlieb have both denied that they are considering a combination. ... Linklaters approached Cleary to discuss a potential merger, but was rebuffed by the New York-headquartered firm, LB understands. ... The approach is understood to have occurred earlier this year. However, no further meetings took place between the two firms, according to a source familiar with the situation. ... ‘These rumours are ... from the strategically aggressive growth plan our ... has been successfully pursuing ... in key industries ... Meanwhile, a Linklaters spokesperson said: ‘It is not true.’ ... Sources across the market were unsurprised to hear that the firms had engaged in early-stage exploratory discussions around a merger. For many, the story reflects the strategic imperative facing the UK’s leading firms: to break into the US market, the largest and most profitable in the world. ... The UK’s top firms are often larger than many in the US in terms of revenue: in last year’s Global 100, for example, Linklaters sat in 14th place with $2.96bn in revenue, compared to Cleary, in 38th place with $1.7bn in revenue. ... However, UK firms struggle to compete on profit: Linklaters had just $2.8m in profit per equity partner in its most recent financial year, while Cleary had more than $5.2m in 2025. ... ary is so much ... profitable,’ noted one ... it cut itself in half, a combination wouldn’t make sense.’ Comparing it to the tie- ... & Overy and legacy Shearman & Sterling, he added: ... It’d be ... than the Shearman deal. Cleary would have to lead it, and I’m not sure that would work for Linklaters.’ ... Some sources argued that failed merger talks indicated concerns about strategy at Linklaters. ‘On the basis that they’ve had a strategy for the last 35 years that they haven’t been able to execute on, I’m not surprised they’d explore it,’ said one partner. ... ‘There is some unrest within the Linklaters partnership about the need to do something in the US,’ said another partner. ‘They have made some recent hires here and there, but it isn’t enough. Trying to grow organically takes a long time, and is fraught with risk.’ ... The comment perhaps undersells Linklaters’ US investments. In 2024, the firm famously brought on George Casey, former global managing partner at legacy Shearman & Sterling, appointing him to the new role of chairman of the Americas the following June. This February, the firm appointed Maria Zaino, former global business director for corporate and M&A at legacy Shearman, as its new chief operating officer for the Americas. ... Linklaters also scored a coup this week, hiring a pair of sports litigators from Paul Weiss, with Christopher Boehning joining as chair of the global sports practice and US strategic disputes. ... Some observers argued that a potential tie-up would have had notable strengths. ‘I’m surprised Cleary rebuffed Linklaters,’ said one partner. ‘Linklaters is a strong firm.’ ... Another partner added: ‘It doesn’t surprise me that Linklaters would look at a firm like Cleary. They’re international, they have strong lockstep associations, and overlaps in areas including Paris and Brussels. And I’d imagine Linklaters want serious New York corporate presence, as well as high exposure to US tech, AI, and fast-growing industries.’ ... Indeed, part of the impetus for Cleary’s 2020 lockstep reform was the departure the previous year of a four-partner team led by Legal 500 big-ticket M&A Hall of Famer Ethan Klingsberg. The team went to Freshfields, one of Linklaters’ UK-headquartered rivals, where Klingsberg is currently co-head of US corporate and M&A. ... For another source, it would make sense for Cleary to consider options now. ‘Cleary is a great brand, but it’s been underperforming. They don’t want to be another Shearman & Sterling.’ However, they added: ‘it’s not clear if the way for them to up their game means going truly international.’ ... For Cleary’s part, one partner argued that this particular deal would not make sense. ‘I’d be surprised if they were to merge with a firm like Linklaters, because that doesn’t solve their issues,’ the partner said. ‘Cleary has too small a base in the US. It needs a bigger footprint in US M&A and private equity, and Linklaters isn’t going to provide that.’ ... ‘Cleary is thoughtful and cautious. They wouldn’t consider a merger unless they felt there was no other path,’ they continued. ‘And even if they were to consider a merger, the last thing they would do is look at a magic circle firm.’ ... While this particular merger rumour appears to have been stopped in its tracks, many sources said there is little reason to believe the overall story is over. The imperative of scale remains strong. ... ‘The starting gun has been fired on the merger situation,’ said Scott Gibson, director at recruitment consultancy Edwards Gibson, referencing a string of recent merger that have seen tie-ups completed in recent months between Hogan Lovells and Cadwalader, Ashurst and Perkins Coie, and Taylor Wessing and Winston & Strawn. ... Another partner summed up the situation: ‘English firms need to do something, and the solely US firms need to do something too. A transatlantic presence is essential.’ ... And in a world where the top law firms are extending their lead, the imperative only grows stronger. Kirkland & Ellis became the first law firm to break $10bn in revenue in its most recent financial results, with second-place Latham & Watkins hitting $8.3bn. ... ‘Anyone that wants to compete with Kirkland and Latham either needs to be more agile, or if they want to compete at the top of the market, they need a merger,’ said one partner. ... In such an environment, it should not be surprising that law firm leaders are considering their options. As Gibson noted: ‘It’s a managing partner’s duty.’"
Linklaters,,,,Exa,"Linklaters Hits Paul Weiss for New York Partner Duo, Gaining FIFA's Top Counsel | Law.com",www.law.com,2026-07-14T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-14 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.law.com/international-edition/2026/07/14/linklaters-hits-paul-weiss-for-new-york-partner-duo-gaining-fifas-top-counsel/,"Linklaters Hits Paul Weiss for New York Partner Duo, Gaining FIFA's Top Counsel ... # Linklaters Hits Paul Weiss for New York Partner Duo, Gaining FIFA's Top Counsel ... It is a significant boost to the firm's U.S. growth strategy. ... 3 minute read July 1 ... , 2026 at 04:57 AM ... Linklaters is adding two leading sports litigators from Paul, Weiss, Rifkind, Wharton & Garrison in New York in a significant boost to the firm's U.S. growth effort, expanding its sports sector practice at a time when global attention is focused on major tournaments like the World Cup. ... H. Christopher Boehning joins the U.K.-founded law firm as chair of the global sports practice and U.S. strategic disputes, while Daniel H. Levi joins the firm's litigation, arbitration & investigations practice in New York. Boehning spent significant time as lead counsel to FIFA on matters related to the 2026 World Cup, helping secure $201 million in compensation from the U.S. Department of Justice and the dismissal of a landmark antitrust lawsuit. ... Boehning was co-chair of Paul Weiss's sports, international, and insurance practices, as well as chair of its international arbitration practice. Paul Weiss has lost 3.03% of its headcount since July 2025, while Linklaters has increased its headcount by 2.59% over the same period. Levi, meanwhile, has more than 25 years of experience handling product liability litigation, civil and criminal regulatory matters, and internal whistleblower investigations. ... Aedamar Comiskey, Linklaters' senior partner and chair, said their arrivals represent a significant step in delivering the firm's strategy. George Casey, chair of the Americas and global chair of corporate, said the hires are a clear signal of the scale of the firm's ambition in the U.S. ... Casey's 2024 hire from legacy Shearman & Sterling was seen as Linklaters' most significant move yet to advance its position in the U.S., which had previously drawn questions, given the rate of U.S. growth among key rivals such as Freshfields. In the same year, the firm also added senior restructuring and insolvency partner Michael Torkin to expand its U.S. footprint. ... The addition of Boehning and Levi sees the firm build on its global sports expertise, having advised clients such as the English Premier League, Real Madrid, the NFL Players Association, DAZN, and Tennis Australia. ... While Linklaters became the first law firm to generate more than £1 billion in U.K. revenue, its U.S. revenue has more than doubled and its profit has quadrupled since FY2021. The firm's U.S. lawyer headcount has grown by 77% since FY2022, reaching 55 partners in the U.S. business."
Linklaters,,,,Exa,Latest Merger Miss Highlights High Bar for Trans-Atlantic Tie-Ups | Law.com,www.law.com,2026-07-14T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-14 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.law.com/americanlawyer/2026/07/14/latest-merger-miss-highlights-high-bar-for-trans-atlantic-tie-ups/,"# Latest Merger Miss Highlights High Bar for Trans-Atlantic Tie-Ups ... Failed approaches—such as Linklaters' reported inquiry with Cleary—are to be expected, and trans-Atlantic mergers face a unique obstacle: a profitability gap. ... - Linklaters approached Cleary Gottlieb earlier in 2026 to discuss a potential merger, sources say, although Cleary Gottlieb rebuffed Linklaters' overtures. - The ongoing profitability gap between U.S. and U.K. firms is often a major issue for firms on either side of the ocean to reconcile when seeking a trans-Atlantic merger. ... Given the ongoing interest in mergers in ... legal industry, several firms in recent years have had failed or aborted merger conversations. ... In one recent example, London-based Linklaters earlier this year approached Am Law 50 firm Cleary Gottlieb Steen & Hamilton over a potential merger, according to sources familiar with the matter, who added that Cleary turned Linklaters away. ... But Linklaters likely has company in sounding out potential U.S. merger partners. According to a source familiar with the market, several other top-earning U.K.-based peers in recent years have also sought out merger talks in the U.S. and gotten a chilly reception. ... In response to a request for comment regarding the reported attempted merger talks between Linklaters and Cleary, a spokesperson for Linklaters stated that the reports are ""not true."" ... A Cleary spokesperson said, “These merger rumors are categorically false, and they distract from the strategically aggressive growth plan our firm has been successfully pursuing. In addition to seeing impressive year-over-year revenue growth and expansion in key markets and practices, since the beginning of 2026 alone, Cleary has made 11 impressive lateral partner hires in key industries such as capital markets, private equity, and funds.” ... Overall, sources noted that one of the main points of friction in attempted trans-Atlantic mergers is the gap in profitability between U.S. and U.K. firms. ... That profitability gap is apparent in the proposed combination between Linklaters and Cleary: Linklaters, home to about 2,750 attorneys according to Law.com Compass data, posted a profits per equity partner of $2.75 million in its most recent financial report for FY 2024, whereas Cleary, with just over 1,100 lawyers, reported profits per equity partner of more than $5.2 million for FY 2024 and more than $6 million in FY 2025. ... .S. firms of the quality the Magic Circle firms respect are significantly more profitable than Magic Circle firms are, but those U.S. firms are also ... as profitable as some U.S. rivals,"" ... . ""The ... are approaching prioritize growing ... path to doing that ... Nor is a merger the only successful method for growth for U.K. firms interested in expanding in the U.S. Lippman, for one, suggested lateral and organic growth in U.K. firms' American offices as a preferred option over securing a U.S.-based merger partner. ... ""The Magic Circle will have better opportunities to build out separately their New York offices and show they can recruit from top firms and have a New York office as profitable as a Wall Street firm, as opposed to finding a Wall Street firm to merge with,"" Lippman suggested. ""That will be difficult as to the overall profitability of the combined entity."" ... While sources said Linklaters is not the only U.K. firm to face rejection of merger overtures, several stateside merger conversations have also fallen apart, each for varying reasons. ... At the start of 2026, Law.com reported that merger talks between Adams and Reese and New Orleans-founded midsize firm McGlinchey Stafford fell apart, contributing to the dissolution of McGlinchey Stafford. At the time, leaders ... the firm noted lagging collections ""compounded with various internal factors over the years"" led to the firm's closure. ... In late 2025, reported merger talks between Alston & Bird and Cadwalader, Wickersham & Taft ended, with some industry observers suggesting that cultural differences contributed to the conclusion of talks. Cadwalader, which had also previously been in merger talks with King & Spalding in 2017, subsequently ended up merging with Hogan Lovells at the start of July 2026. ... Hogan Lovells, meanwhile, held failed merger talks with Shearman & Sterling in 2023. Shearman & Sterling also felt out a merger with Texas-based Baker Botts, although those talks fell through as well; Shearman & Sterling subsequently merged with U.K. firm Allen & Overy to create A&O Shearman. ... Rounding out the recent failed mergers, now-defunct New York firm Stroock & Stroock & Lavan held several failed merger talks before its dissolution in 2023, with talks spanning firms including Nixon Peabody, McGuireWoods, Steptoe & Johnson, Pillsbury Winthrop Shaw Pittman and Squire Patton Boggs. A major impediment to the merger talks, sources said at the time, was Stroock's pension program for retired partners and its accrual accounting system. ... ## Combination With IP Boutique Gives Lathrop GPM Offices in NYC and London ... Second Hundred firm Lathrop GPM is combining with IP boutique HG Law in a merger expected to add about $40 million to the firm's revenue and will also bolster the firm's California presence. ... Hogan Lovells ... More than 50 ... Cadwalader attorneys and staff are moving into legacy Hogan Lovells’ office space in downtown D.C. this month, following the firms'"
Linklaters,,,,Exa,Linklaters Strengthens Litigation & Sports Offering with Leading New York Team,www.linklaters.com,2026-07-14T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-14 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.linklaters.com/about-us/news-and-deals/news/2026/july/linklaters-strengthens-litigation-sports-offering-with-leading-new-york-team,"Linklaters Strengthens Litigation & Sports Offering with Leading New York Team ... # Linklaters Strengthens Litigation & Sports Offering with Leading New York Team ... 14 July 2026 ... Linklaters is delighted to announce that H. Christopher Boehning has joined the firm as a Partner and Chair of the Global Sports Practice and U.S. Strategic Disputes, while Daniel H. Levi has joined as a Partner in the firm’s Litigation, Arbitration & Investigations practice — both in New York. The team, which joined from Paul Weiss, brings world-class commercial disputes expertise for clients such as leading biotech company Regeneron, as well as a premier sports practice supporting clients such as FIFA in relation to the 2026 World Cup. Their recruitment further strengthens Linklaters’ global offering and marks another milestone in the firm’s transformational growth in the U.S., following the addition of elite teams across M&A, disputes, and finance. ... “We are very focused on having top talent in all our markets. The recruitment of Chris and Dan is a further significant step in delivering that strategy. They are top-of-the-market partners and terrific additions to our global team.” ... “Chris and Dan joining us is a clear signal of the scale of our ambition in the U.S. They strengthen our offering at a time when clients are looking for elite capability across practices, and build on the momentum we have created.” ... “The roster of clients trusting us with their most complex, high-stakes disputes and investigations is growing and the arrival of Chris and Dan will add to this. They bring further depth to our leading Litigation, Arbitration and Investigations team and we are delighted to welcome them to the firm.” ... dismissal of a ... Dan has more than 25 years of experience handling complex litigation in a variety of substantive areas including product liability, insurance litigation, M&A litigation, and general commercial disputes. His accomplished record spans all aspects of pretrial, trial, and appellate work in state and federal ... in international arbitration. He has also handled ... Their arrival further strengthens Linklaters’ U.S. disputes bench following the addition of a leading trial team led by Chair of the Trial Practice Adeel Mangi and Partners Muhammad Faridi, Diana Conner, and George LoBiondo, and a top international arbitration team led by Co-Head of International Arbitration Joe Profaizer. ... Linklaters’ U.S. Litigation, Arbitration & Investigations practice is an elite team of trial lawyers specializing in complex commercial litigation, international arbitration, regulatory and internal investigations, antitrust, securities, business crime, class and derivative actions, fraud, bankruptcy litigation, and lender liability matters. The U.S. group operates seamlessly within the top-ranked global practice comprising 400 lawyers, providing clients with coordinated, high-quality representation across markets. ... Linklaters LLP is a leading global law firm, advising the world’s leading corporates, banks and private capital clients on their most important mandates. We combine legal expertise with a collaborative and commercial approach to help clients navigate rapidly evolving markets and regulatory environments. With offices in major business and financial centers, our teams operate at the forefront of all major practices and sectors."
Linklaters,,,,Exa,Linklaters raids Paul Weiss for senior New York litigation duo - The Global Legal Post,www.globallegalpost.com (Victoria Basham),2026-07-14T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-14 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.globallegalpost.com/news/linklaters-raids-paul-weiss-for-senior-new-york-litigation-duo-1094391568,"Linklaters raids Paul Weiss for senior New York litigation duo - The Global Legal Post ... Linklaters has landed a pair of senior New York litigators from Paul Weiss, in a move the firm described as a “milestone” for its US growth ambitions. ... Christopher Boehning has joined as chair of Linklaters’ global sports and US strategic disputes practices after more than 30 years at Paul Weiss alongside Daniel Levi, who spent more than 25 years at his former firm and has joined as a partner in Linklaters’ 400-lawyer litigation, arbitration and investigations practice. ... Linklaters’ senior partner, Aedamar Comiskey, said the duo’s hire represented “a further significant step” in the firm’s strategy to attract “top talent” in all its markets. ... Their hire continues a growth push by Linklaters, which has seen it add highly regarded teams across M&A and finance as well as disputes. ... Americas chairman George Casey, who joined at the helm of a six-partner M&A group in 2024 from legacy Shearman & Sterling, said Boehning and Levi’s arrival was “a clear signal of the scale of our ambition in the US”. ... “They strengthen our offering at a time when clients are looking for elite capability across practices, and build on the momentum we have created,” he added. ... Their arrival follows Linklaters hiring a four-partner trial team led by chair of the trial practice, Adeel Mangi, from New York litigation boutique Patterson Belknap Webb & Tyler last year. In late 2024, it added an arbitration team from Paul Hastings in Washington DC led by Joseph Profaizer, who joined as global co-head of international arbitration, around the same time it hired a quartet of senior finance partners from A&O Shearman. ... Linklaters said its US revenue had more than doubled and profit quadrupled compared to FY21, with Boehning and Levi taking its US practice to 55 partners, an increase of 77% since FY22. ... Meanwhile, Levi handles litigation in areas including product liability, insurance litigation, M&A litigation and general commercial disputes. ... Their move comes as elite law firms boost their sports offerings amid growing private capital investment in the sector. ... Earlier this week, Paul Hastings launched a dedicated global sports practice led by former Slaughter and May partner Mark Zerdin and other recent recruits, including funds partner Chidi Oteh from Ropes & Gray and former Baltimore Ravens general counsel Brandon Etheridge. ... Simpson Thacher hired three sports partners in April, including new practice co-heads Michael Kuh, who joined from Hogan Lovells, and Eric Geffner, who arrived from Sidley Austin, while Paul Weiss launched its formalised sports practice late last year."
Linklaters,,,,Exa,Linklaters advises Zhipu AI on US$4bn placing of new H shares,legaldesire.com (Cynthia Lydia Marbaniang),2026-07-14T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-14 00:00:00+00:00,,https://legaldesire.com/linklaters-advises-zhipu-ai-on-us4bn-placing-of-new-h-shares/,"Linklaters advises Zhipu AI on US$4bn placing of new H shares ... # Linklaters advises Zhipu AI on US$4bn placing of new H shares ... Linklaters has acted as Hong Kong and US counsel for Knowledge Atlas Technology Joint Stock Company Limited (Zhipu AI) on its placing of new H shares on the Hong Kong Stock Exchange, raising approximately HK$31.41bn (US$4bn). ... The transaction represents one of this year’s largest equity capital markets fundraisings in Asia and by far the largest in the technology and AI sector. It closely follows Zhipu AI’s landmark listing on the Main Board of the Hong Kong Stock Exchange in January 2026 – the first large AI model developer IPO globally – on which Linklaters also acted as Hong Kong and US counsel to Zhipu AI. ... > “We are delighted to have supported Zhipu AI on this significant H-share placing, following its landmark Hong Kong listing earlier this year. We are grateful for the continued trust and confidence Zhipu AI has placed in our team across important milestones of its capital markets journey. This transaction also reflects the important role of Hong Kong’s capital markets in supporting innovative companies with long-term growth ambitions. We look forward to continuing to support clients across the fast-evolving technology and AI sectors.” ... With over 50 years on-the-ground expertise in Hong Kong SAR and the wider Asia region, Linklaters’ highly regarded Greater China ECM team has deep local insights combined with the strength of the firm’s global platform. The team provides seamless legal support on pre-IPO investments, IPOs, post-listing compliance and financing, having advised on a number of high-profile Hong Kong IPOs, including CATL, Chery and SANY Heavy Industry, as well as share placings for CATL, Kelun-Biotech and RoboSense."
Linklaters,,,,Exa,Magic circle Linklaters scores FIFA's top lawyers in US raid,www.cityam.com (Maria Ward-Brennan),2026-07-14T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-14 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.cityam.com/magic-circle-linklaters-scores-fifas-top-lawyers-in-us-raid/,"Magic circle Linklaters scores FIFA's top lawyers in US raid ... Magic circle law firm Linklaters has snagged two of FIFA’s lawyers from a rival firm as part of its push into the US legal market. ... Partner Christopher Boehning has joined as Chair of its global sports practice and US strategic disputes, while Daniel Levi will serve in the firm’s litigation, arbitration and investigations practice, both in the New York office. ... The senior lawyers, who joined from Paul Weiss, have been the go-to US legal heavyweights for FIFA over the last several years, frequently teaming up on the governing body’s most high-profile and sensitive disputes. ... Aedamar Comiskey, Linklaters’ senior partner and Chair, said: “We are very focused on having top talent in all our markets. The recruitment of Chris [Boehning] and Dan [Levi] is a further significant step in delivering that strategy. They are top-of-the-market partners and terrific additions to our global team.” ... The magic circle firm, like many of its English Big Law competitors, has its eyes on expansion into the US market. ... Linklaters has tried for several years to get its foot into the world’s largest legal market, but has recently ramped up its focus by expanding headcount. So far, the firm has 55 partners, up 77 per cent since 2022. ... On Monday, its competitor, Freshfields, was revealed to have forced out senior partners to boost growth and fund its own US expansion. ... Top legal firms have increasingly been drawn to the sports sector due to a surge in private equity investments, complex regulatory frameworks, and lucrative high-profile litigation, transforming sports law from a niche speciality into a major corporate practice area. ... Linklaters’ sports sector clients include the English Premier League, Real Madrid, the National Football League Players Association, DAZN, and Tennis Australia. ... This move comes as on Monday, US giant Paul Hastings announced it has launched a new global sports practice, nabbing former Baltimore Ravens general counsel Brandon Etheridge and former Slaughter and May figure Mark Zerdin, who advised TWG Motorsports on their Cadillac F1 team entry. ... ### Magic circle Freshfields ousts equity partners amid US push"
Linklaters,,,,Exa,Linklaters advises Bayer on Apollo´s €3bn equity capital investment in its long acting reversible contraceptives business,legaldesire.com (Cynthia Lydia Marbaniang),2026-07-14T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-14 00:00:00+00:00,,https://legaldesire.com/linklaters-advises-bayer-on-apollos-e3bn-equity-capital-investment-in-its-long-acting-reversible-contraceptives-business/,"Linklaters advises Bayer on Apollo´s €3bn equity capital investment in its long acting reversible contraceptives business ... # Linklaters advises Bayer on Apollo´s €3bn equity capital investment in its long acting reversible contraceptives business ... Linklaters has advised Bayer AG on Apollo´s €3bn equity capital investment in its long acting reversible contraceptives business. Under the agreement, Apollo-managed funds and affiliates will obtain a minority, non-controlling stake in a newly established entity holding Bayer’s long-acting reversible contraceptives (LARC) business. Bayer will retain a majority stake in the entity and will continue to exercise complete operational control over the business. ... With this transaction, Bayer strengthens its capital structure while preserving full operational control over its core pharmaceuticals business. The transaction is expected to close in the third quarter of 2026, subject to approval by antitrust authorities and customary closing conditions. ... An international cross-practice Linklaters team advised Bayer comprehensively on this high-profile transaction. The team was led by Partners Carl Sanders (Private Capital) and Alexander Schlee (Capital Markets) and Counsel Nikita Selivanov (Private Capital); it included Partners Corporate/M&A Partners Arne Kießling, Guido Portier, Marleen Wessel (both Amsterdam), Oliver Rosenberg (Tax), Counsels Anna-Livia von Lüdinghausen, Isabelle-Carmen Petersen Weis (Banking), Managing Associates Philipp Meyer Reichen, Tobias Buddemeier (Corporate/M&A), Sebastian Plötz, Associates Lena Mühleisen (Private Capital), Ruth van der Haar, Carolien Lode (both Amsterdam, Corporate/M ... A), Pascal Janssen Jägers (Tax)."
Linklaters,,,,Exa,UK law firm Linklaters sought to merge with Cleary Gottlieb | Semafor,www.semafor.com,2026-07-13T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-13 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.semafor.com/article/07/13/2026/uk-law-firm-linklaters-sought-to-merge-with-cleary-gottlieb,"UK law firm Linklaters sought to merge with Cleary Gottlieb | Semafor ... # Exclusive / UK law firm Linklaters sought to merge with Cleary Gottlieb ... Linklaters approached Cleary Gottlieb about a merger earlier this year and was rebuffed, in what would have been the latest tie-up of white-shoe law firms, according to people familiar with the matter. ... Linklaters has more than 3,000 lawyers globally. Cleary is smaller, with about 1,300 attorneys. But Cleary is far more profitable than Linklaters is, with profit per equity partner of more than $5 million last year. There has been a wave of consolidation among top-flight law firms, even as their scaled models face imminent pressure and upheaval from AI. ... The face of law has changed dramatically over the last decade: A fifth of 2015’s top 100 global law firms by revenue have merged with each other or announced their intention to. A drive for international reach, top lawyers’ soaring salaries, and the need to invest in AI systems are pushing firms to chase scale and cut costs. A Linklaters spokesperson denied that it had made an approach to Cleary. A Cleary spokesperson said the firm was focused on a “strategically aggressive growth strategy.” ... Linklaters is the latest of the UK’s elite law firms — a club known as the “Magic Circle” — to attempt a transatlantic merger. Allen & Overy merged with Shearman & Sterling in 2024, and Herbert Smith Freehills merged with Kramer Levin last year. ... There is intense competition among these firms for talent, and any merger presents opportunities for rival firms to pick off hires. McDermott Will & Emery merged with Schulte, Roth,& Zabel earlier this year; Philadelphia-based Dechert promptly mounted a 20-partner raid across multiple practices. ... “In addition to seeing impressive year-over-year revenue growth and expansion in key markets and practices, since the beginning of 2026 alone, Cleary has made 11 impressive lateral partner hires in key industries such as capital markets, private equity, and funds,” the Cleary spokesperson said."
Linklaters,,,,Exa,Linklaters advises Citi on the inaugural digitally native commercial paper issuance on Clearstream’s D7,legaldesire.com (Cynthia Lydia Marbaniang),2026-07-13T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-13 00:00:00+00:00,,https://legaldesire.com/linklaters-advises-citi-on-the-inaugural-digitally-native-commercial-paper-issuance-on-clearstreams-d7/,"Linklaters advises Citi on the inaugural digitally native commercial paper issuance on Clearstream’s D7 ... # Linklaters advises Citi on the inaugural digitally native commercial paper issuance on Clearstream’s D7 ... Linklaters has advised Citibank N.A., London Branch, as Issuing and Paying Agent, and Citigroup Global Markets Europe AG, as Dealer, in connection with the issuance of digitally native commercial paper (DNCP) by the European Investment Bank on Clearstream Bank S.A.’s D7 distributed ledger technology platform (D7). ... The DNCP has an aggregate principal amount of EUR 77,500,000. This transaction marks the first issuance of DLT-native commercial paper on Clearstream’s D7 platform, a significant milestone in the ongoing digitalisation of debt capital markets infrastructure. ... Linklaters has been at the forefront of digital finance and capital markets innovation, advising on the legal and structural frameworks underpinning some of the most significant transactions in this space. The firm’s work on this issuance draws on its deep expertise across tokenisation, digital assets, capital markets regulation, and financial infrastructure — combining the resources of its London, Luxembourg and German practices to navigate the multi-jurisdictional legal considerations inherent in a transaction of this nature. ... Richard Hay, Partner and Global Co-Head of FinTech at Linklaters, comments: ... “The digitalisation of financial assets is gaining real momentum, and this transaction is a clear illustration of that trajectory. We are proud to have advised Citi on this landmark deal and to continue to play a central role in shaping the legal frameworks that make these innovations possible.”"
Linklaters,,,,Exa,Linklaters advises on the acquisition of the company owning the landmark ‘Stairs’ office building in Luxembourg,legaldesire.com (Cynthia Lydia Marbaniang),2026-07-10T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-10 00:00:00+00:00,,https://legaldesire.com/linklaters-advises-on-the-acquisition-of-the-company-owning-the-landmark-stairs-office-building-in-luxembourg/,"Linklaters advises on the acquisition of the company owning the landmark ‘Stairs’ office building in Luxembourg ... # Linklaters advises on the acquisition of the company owning the landmark ‘Stairs’ office building in Luxembourg ... Linklaters has advised the purchaser on the acquisition of all shares in Stairs S.à r.l., the company owning the ‘Stairs’ building, which will become the new headquarters of State Street Bank International Luxembourg. The asset was acquired from Grossfeld, a joint venture between Nextensa and Promobe; the transaction value exceeds €100 million. ... The ‘Stairs’ building is a landmark asset in the Luxembourg real estate market, being the country’s first office building to achieve BREEAM Outstanding certification, complemented by WELL Gold and an A+ energy rating. The 9,999 m² property represents a high-quality, future-proof asset within the dynamic Cloche d’Or district. ... The cross-practice Linklaters team advised on the acquisition of the asset by way of a share purchase throughout the project lifecycle, including corporate governance and real estate matters. ... > “This acquisition marks the successful completion of a landmark project. We are proud to have supported our client from the initial phases through to the delivery of new, state-of-the-art headquarters, a truly pioneering building for its sustainability credentials in Luxembourg. This transaction highlights our deep expertise in the Luxembourg real estate market and our ability to guide clients on complex, long-term developments in the dynamic Cloche d’Or district.”"
Linklaters,,,,Exa,Linklaters advises on the acquisition of the company owning the landmark ‘Stairs’ office building in Luxembourg | Linklaters Luxembourg,www.linkedin.com (Linklaters Luxembourg),2026-07-10T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-10 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.linkedin.com/posts/linklaters-luxembourg_linklaters-advises-on-the-acquisition-of-activity-7481245720826683392-Bc5D,"# Linklaters advises on the acquisition of the company owning the landmark ‘Stairs’ office building in Luxembourg | Linklaters Luxembourg · LinkedIn · 2026-07-10 ... **Linklaters Luxembourg**: Linklaters is a law firm that delivers innovative thinking and streamlined solutions to deliver greater efficiencies and smarter outcomes. They have a proven ability to adapt to all environments. Linklaters Luxembourg has 40-50 employees (+40% YoY). Headquartered in Luxembourg, Luxembourg. Its workforce is distributed across Luxembourg, France, and Germany. ... Linklaters advises on the acquisition of the company owning the landmark ‘Stairs’ office building in Luxembourg ... 📢 A cross-practice team led by Corporate Real Estate Counsel [Anton Cox](https://linkedin.com/in/anton-cox-116845) has advised the purchaser on the acquisition of all shares in Stairs S.à r.l., the company owning the landmark ‘Stairs’ office building in Luxembourg’s Cloche d’Or district from Nextensa and Promobe. ... The building is a benchmark for sustainability in Luxembourg, being the country’s first office building to achieve BREEAM Outstanding certification, complemented by WELL Gold and an A+ energy rating. ... We are delighted to have supported our client in this strategic acquisition, which will become the new headquarters of State Street Bank International Luxembourg. This is further cementing our firm's position as a trusted advisor for significant real estate investments in Luxembourg."
Linklaters,,,,Exa,Linklaters assists on Philippines’ USD2.5bn bond offering | Law.asia,law.asia (Website team 05),2026-07-09T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-09 00:00:00+00:00,,https://law.asia/philippines-usd2-5-billion-global-bond-offering/,"Linklaters assists on Philippines’ USD2.5bn bond offering | Law.asia ... Home Deal digest Linklaters assists on Philippines’ USD2.5bn bond offering ... Linklaters has advised the joint lead managers and bookrunners on the Philippine government’s USD2.5 billion triple-tranche global bond offering registered with the US Securities and Exchange Commission. ... The issuance comprises USD550 million of 5.5-year bonds, USD1.65 billion of 10-year bonds and a USD300 million tap of the government’s 2051 bonds. The offering drew strong demand from international investors, with the order book reaching 4.4 times the amount offered, prompting an increase from the initial USD2 billion target size. ... BNP Paribas, Citigroup, HSBC, JP Morgan, MUFG, and Standard Chartered Bank acted as joint lead managers and bookrunners. ... The Linklaters team was led by the head of South and Southeast Asia capital markets, Amit Singh, and partner Xunming Lim, who had the support of counsel Grace Yumul-Villanueva. ... Offering proceeds will be used for general budget financing."
Linklaters,,,,Exa,Linklaters Advised on Vinpearl US$255M Investment,www.legaleraonline.com (Legal Era News Network),2026-07-09T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-09 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.legaleraonline.com/global-deals/linklaters-advised-private-capital-investors-us255-million-investment-vinpearl-987569,"Linklaters Advised on Vinpearl US$255M Investment ... # Linklaters Advised Private Capital Investors On US$255 Million Investment In Vinpearl ... By Legal Era News Network| Law Firm Linklaters ... Linklaters Advised Private Capital Investors on US$255 Million Investment in Vinpearl ... The leading global law firm, Linklaters advised SeaTown Holdings International (SeaTown), Oman Investment Authority (OIA) and Vietnam Oman Investment (VOI) on their US$255 million investment in Vinpearl Joint Stock Company, one of Vietnam's leading tourism, hospitality and entertainment businesses. ... The investment is expected to support Vinpearl's long-term growth strategy, continued business expansion, and enhancement of its hospitality and tourism offerings. ... The investment was made through highly bespoke Convertible Dividend Preference Shares (CDPS) issued by Vinpearl. The transaction reflects continued investor confidence in Vinpearl's market position and Vietnam's long-term economic fundamentals. It also highlights the increasing depth of private capital investment activity across Southeast Asia, with international investors continuing to seek opportunities in businesses supported by favourable demographic trends, rising consumer demand, and expanding regional tourism flows. ... The mandate further demonstrates Linklaters' leading position in advising clients on complex cross-border investments, private capital transactions, and strategic investments across the region, combining expertise across corporate, financing, and regulatory matters to deliver coordinated advice across jurisdictions."
Linklaters,,,,Exa,"Linklaters advises BNP Paribas, Deutsche Bank and J.P. Morgan on bridge loan financing for Merck KGaA",legaldesire.com (Cynthia Lydia Marbaniang),2026-07-09T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-09 00:00:00+00:00,,https://legaldesire.com/linklaters-advises-bnp-paribas-deutsche-bank-and-j-p-morgan-on-bridge-loan-financing-for-merck-kgaa/,"Linklaters advises BNP Paribas, Deutsche Bank and J.P. Morgan on bridge loan financing for Merck KGaA ... # Linklaters advises BNP Paribas, Deutsche Bank and J.P. Morgan on bridge loan financing for Merck KGaA ... Linklaters has advised BNP Paribas, Deutsche Bank and J.P. Morgan on the bridge loan financing for Merck KGaA, supporting its proposed acquisition of Bio-Techne Corporation (NASDAQ: TECH) at a total enterprise value of approximately US$11.3bn. ... The international cross- ... Linklaters team was led by Marc Trinkaus(Partner) and Urs Lewens(Counsel) (both Banking, Frankfurt). ... Linklaters is a market leader in syndicated lending, acquisition finance, and financing in the context of spinoff and other corporate reorganisations. The team have advised, amongst others, Continental on a €2.5bn syndicated credit facility for AUMOVIO in connection with the spin-off of the automotive segment as AUMOVIO group, E.ON SE in relation to a syndicated credit facility in the amount of €4.7bn and Bayer AG on a syndicated credit facility in the amount of €5bn. ... ##### Linklaters advises on Burjeel Holdings’ Sukuk Programme Establishment and Subsequent Issuance"
Linklaters,,,,Exa,"Linklaters, Freshfields, Sullivan & Cromwell Top H1 M&A Rankings in EMEA, Boosted by German Deals | Law.com",www.law.com,2026-07-08T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-08 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.law.com/international-edition/2026/07/08/linklaters-freshfields-sullivan--cromwell-top-h1-ma-rankings-in-emea-boosted-by-german-deals/,"Linklaters, Freshfields, Sullivan & Cromwell Top H1 ... Rankings in EMEA, Boosted by German Deals ... # Linklaters, Freshfields, Sullivan & Cromwell Top H1 M&A Rankings in EMEA, Boosted by German Deals ... The value of announced EMEA deals jumped 59% year on year, even as the number of transactions fell 14%, LSEG found. ... The M&A results for EMEA H1 2026 are in, with three familiar names among those topping the law firm rankings: Freshfields, Linklaters, and Sullivan & Cromwell. ... M&A surged globally in the first half of 2026, with announced deals reaching a record $2.85 trillion, up 50% from a year earlier. Europe accounted for $676 billion of that, more than double its value in H1 2025 and its strongest performance in 19 years, according to LSEG analytics. ... M&A activity in Europe and the Middle East proved resilient despite a turbulent first half marked by geopolitical tensions, including the conflict involving the US, Israel and Iran. The value of announced deals jumped 59% year on year, even as the number of transactions fell 14%, LSEG found. ... “The surge in M&A activity across Europe in H1 was driven primarily by strategic corporate transactions across a number of key sectors, said Simon Branigan, global head of corporate at top-ranked Linklaters. ... Tech, banking, energy, and industrials all saw strong growth, driving M&A activity, according to LSEG data. ... Freshfields—ranked first in Germany and second for EMEA deals by LSEG during H1—advised on some of Europe’s biggest transactions, including UniCredit’s €35 billion bid for Commerzbank and the €29 billion acquisition of TK Elevator by KONE. ... Cichy said Freshfields’ top ranking was partly a result of deals that were delayed from December, rather than completed in ... 4 2025. After a slow start to the year, activity “started to pick up in the second half,” leaving the firm with a backlog of transactions that closed in H1. ... London was clearly the European hot spot in H1 with $292 billion in targeted M&A—the value of companies being acquired—and Germany in second place with $111 billion, followed by Italy and France. ... Sullivan & Cromwell, ranked third in the EMEA with 34 deals valued at $131 billion, also had a strong showing in Germany where it was fourth-ranked. ... “We have always been very high in the league tables in Germany,” Frankfurt managing partner Carsten Berrar, Sullivan & Cromwell’s head of capital markets, said, noting the firm represented Merck in its $11.3 billion U.S. acquisition and advised on Uber’s $11.6 billion bid for Germany’s Delivery Hero. ... “Overall, this is one of the leading M&A practices in Germany,” said Berrar, one of seven SullCrom M&A partners in Frankfurt. “On the M&A side, we are very optimistic [about] what we can do in the next five years.” ... ## Linklaters Hires Corporate Head in Italy After 27 Years at Chiomenti ... https://www.law.com/international-edition/2025/09/17/linklaters-hires-corporate-head-in-italy-after-27-years-at-chiomenti/"
Linklaters,,,,Exa,LCECOM Boosts Cross-Border Digitalization,www.martechcube.com (GlobeNewswire),2026-07-07T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-07 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.martechcube.com/lcecom-boosts-cross-border-digitalization-to-drive-regional-expansion/,"Boosts Cross-Border Digitalization ... # LCECOM Boosts Cross-Border Digitalization to Drive Regional Expansion ... LUCKCAT E-COMMERCE PTE. LTD. (hereinafter referred to as “LCECOM”), an international company registered in New York, USA, today announced its continued commitment to advancing its global operational strategy. The company’s forward-looking initiatives will focus heavily on regulatory compliance, systemic transparency, and the long-term enhancement of cross-border digital operational capabilities. ... LCECOM stated that its strategic direction will center on strengthening structured digital operational capabilities across multiple regions while ensuring strict alignment with relevant regulatory requirements in each local jurisdiction. ... Operating lawfully within the U.S. legal framework, ... engages in cross-border digital commerce coordination, operational process management, and brand-related support services under the U ... S. federal tax system via its Employer Identification Number (EIN). ... The Company notes that because different countries employ distinct business identification systems, minor localized discrepancies naturally arise between corporate registration networks. LCECOM emphasizes that any inability to query its corporate information on local regional tax systems does not indicate that the Company is unregistered, but rather reflects a normal divergence between disparate jurisdictional data systems. The Company reaffirms that it strictly complies with all applicable local laws and regulatory requirements in every region where it maintains operations. ... LCECOM is continuously expanding its international operational structure through several key ongoing initiatives: ... - Establishing localized operational workspaces in select global regions. - Strengthening cross-regional collaborative operational systems. - Promoting the broad standardization of operational processes across multiple markets. - Gradually exploring alignment with local regulatory frameworks in future phases, subject to localized approvals and operational conditions. ... To support this infrastructure, LCECOM continues to invest heavily in the development of internal digital systems designed to improve consistency, traceability, and scalability. These core components include digital workflow coordination systems, cross-regional task management frameworks, data tracking systems, and standardized communication mechanisms. ... LCECOM’s 2026–2028 strategic plan prioritizes structural growth opportunities within international expansion zones to further refine its global business footprint. The Company identifies these target regions as high-priority sectors due to several critical growth drivers, including a youthful demographic profile, rising digital connectivity, rapid mobile payment adoption, and sustained demand for cross-border e-commerce. ... Within this strategic framework, key regional hubs have been identified as essential strategic nodes for regional positioning, offering significant trade connectivity, a stable business environment, and a growing receptiveness to international digital business models. LCECOM emphasizes that this international strategy will be implemented in phases, with gradual execution based carefully on market conditions and regulatory environments. ... “Global commerce is progressively shifting from traditional geographic structures toward operational models centered on digital collaboration,” the Company noted in a statement. LCECOM’s long-term objective is to lead this transition by advancing cross-border digital operating models that reduce geographical constraints and enhance systemic interconnectivity for teams worldwide."
Linklaters,,,,Exa,Linklaters advises RENK on the acquisition of David Brown Defence,legaldesire.com (Cynthia Lydia Marbaniang),2026-07-06T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-06 00:00:00+00:00,,https://legaldesire.com/linklaters-advises-renk-on-the-acquisition-of-david-brown-defence/,"Linklaters advises RENK on the acquisition of David Brown Defence # Linklaters advises RENK on the acquisition of David Brown Defence ... Linklaters has advised RENK on entering into a binding agreement for the acquisition of David Brown Defence. RENK is the Germany-based leading provider of propulsion solutions for the military sector. David Brown is based in the UK and specializes in high-precision gearboxes for the naval and land defence sectors. ... The acquisition represents a strategically important addition to RENK’s existing portfolio in the naval sector. In particular, RENK is gaining access to high-value naval programs of the next few years and a long-term base of orders across the main naval programs in the UK, Canada and Australia, including aftermarket business opportunities. Furthermore, the acquisition will also enable RENK to tap into significant synergies at operational and technological levels. The transaction is subject to standard regulatory approvals and is scheduled for completion in Q4 2026. ... An international cross-practice Linklaters team is advising RENK on this transaction. The team is led by Corporate/M&A Partners Richard Spedding and Klaus von der Linden and Managing Associates Lisa Gao and Karen Clarke. ... Linklaters advises a wide range of clients in the aerospace, defence and security (ADS) sector, including major defence contractors, suppliers, investors and financial institutions, as well as companies and private equity firms entering this highly dynamic market. Most recently, our team advised Thyssenkrupp on the spin-off and IPO of Thyssenkrupp Marine Systems, the investment banks on IPO of RENK Group AG, Safran.AI on the acquisition of technologies from Kayrros and Heidelberger Druckmaschinen AG on forming a joint venture for drone defence with US-based Ondas."
Linklaters,,,,Exa,Linklaters and Jones Day advise on German firm RENK’s acquisition of David Brown Defence - The Global Legal Post,www.globallegalpost.com (Peter Taberner),2026-07-05T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-05 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.globallegalpost.com/news/linklaters-and-jones-day-advise-on-german-firm-renks-acquisition-of-david-brown-defence-1506974781,"Linklaters and Jones Day advise on German firm RENK’s acquisition of David Brown Defence - The Global Legal Post ... Magic Circle law firm Linklaters was called in to advise German manufacturing company RENK on its acquisition of UK-based David Brown Defence from Stellex Capital Management, advised by Jones Day. ... The transaction combines RENK’s expertise as a leading provider of propulsion solutions for the military sector, along with Huddersfield-based David Brown’s specialisation in high-precision gearboxes for the naval and land defence sectors, including for submarines. ... The deal is expected to be completed by the fourth quarter of this year, subject to standard regulatory approvals. ... According to Bloomberg, the deal is valued at around $200m. ... The Linklaters team advising on the deal was led by corporate and M&A partners Richard Spedding (London) and Klaus von der Linden (Düsseldorf), along with managing associate Lisa Gao. ... Runnicles also advised Stellex on its acquisition of DB Santasalo from the UK’s N4 Partners in March 2023. ... The acquisition will support RENK’s portfolio across the naval sector. The Augsburg-based company will gain access to orders in place across naval programmes in the UK, Canada and Australia, while the purchase will allow it to reap significant synergies at operational and technological levels. ... Late in June, Linklaters also advised the Volkswagen Group over its majority stake sale of its marine engine unit Everllence to private equity firm Bain Capital. ... Kirkland & Ellis advised Bain on the purchase, while Paul Weiss advised the private equity firm on the financing of the transaction, and Gleiss Lutz counselled Volkswagen’s supervisory board on the sale. ... A survey published earlier this month by FTI Consulting showed that M&A activity in the aerospace and defence sector recorded the largest year-on-year improvement compared to any other sector. ... Improved regulatory clarity, higher defence spending and renewed access to financing were all credited for the upturn in momentum."
Linklaters,,,,Exa,Linklaters advises CBRE IM on acquisition of landmark Italian logistics portfolio,legaldesire.com (Cynthia Lydia Marbaniang),2026-07-03T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-03 00:00:00+00:00,,https://legaldesire.com/linklaters-advises-cbre-im-on-acquisition-of-landmark-italian-logistics-portfolio/,"Linklaters advises CBRE IM on acquisition of landmark Italian logistics portfolio ... # Linklaters advises CBRE IM on acquisition of landmark Italian logistics portfolio ... Linklaters has advised CBRE Investment Management (CBRE IM) on the acquisition of a landmark portfolio of eight logistics assets, on behalf of a fund sponsored by CBRE IM. The portfolio comprises approximately 327,000 sqm of gross lettable area and represents a significant step in expanding CBRE IM’s European logistics platform. The assets were disposed of by different real estate funds managed by leading Italian general partners (GPs). ... The portfolio is strategically distributed across four of Italy’s most established logistics submarkets: three assets in the Bologna market, three in the Rome area, one in the Milan market and one in Verona. Each location benefits from direct access to Italy’s primary motorway network, supporting both domestic distribution and cross-border European trade flows. All assets are newly built to Grade A specifications and incorporate strong sustainability features, with all schemes certified LEED Platinum or Gold. ... The international cross-practice Linklaters team was led by Real Estate Partner Francesco De Blasio. The team also included Managing Associates Francesco Calabria and Massimo Schirinzi, as well as the Associates Martina Fariseo, Teodoro Pellegrino Pellegrino and Giulia Ponzo(all Real Estate, Milan). The Italian tax aspects of the transaction were handled by Counsel Fabio Balza, assisted by Managing Associate Luigi Spinello and Associate Giovanni Moretta. Managing Associate Emanuele Aurilia advised on the Italian regulatory aspects. ... Further international team members included Partner Markus Wollenhaupt, Counsel Rocco Beck and Managing Associate Nicola Zeibig(all Investment Funds, Frankfurt), Roman Payre(Managing Associate, Investment Funds, Luxembourg) as well as Ulrich Johann(Counsel, Tax, Frankfurt). ... ##### Linklaters advises Ardian on the sale of its 38% stake in Kallista Energy to APG and Pensionskasse Stadt Zürich (PKZH)"
Linklaters,,,,Exa,Linklaters advises on Masan Group’s landmark US$750m senior unsecured syndicated loan,legaldesire.com (Cynthia Lydia Marbaniang),2026-07-02T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-02 00:00:00+00:00,,https://legaldesire.com/linklaters-advises-on-masan-groups-landmark-us750m-senior-unsecured-syndicated-loan/,"Linklaters advises on Masan Group’s landmark US$750m senior unsecured syndicated loan ... # Linklaters advises on Masan Group’s landmark US$750m senior unsecured syndicated loan ... Linklaters has advised the arrangers on Masan Group’s US$750m senior unsecured syndicated loan, representing the largest syndicated facility raised by a Vietnamese privately owned enterprise and marking Masan Group’s first six-year tenor loan. ... Founded in 1996 and listed on the Ho Chi Minh Stock Exchange in 2009, Masan Group is one of Vietnam’s largest conglomerates. ... This transaction involved a sophisticated cross-border financing with Vietnamese, Singapore and English law elements, setting a new benchmark for Vietnamese privately owned enterprises seeking access to long-dated, unsecured and competitively priced syndicated loans in the international markets. ... The Linklaters team was led by banking partner Dior Lin, with support from Grace Sim, James Lek and Don Loke, as well as Linklaters’ alliance partner Allens, led by partner Linh Bui, in relation to the financing provided to Masan Group entities in Vietnam. ... With more than 50 years of on-the-ground experience in Asia, and drawing on the strength of its fully integrated global platform and global alliances, Linklaters supports clients on the region’s most complex, innovative and multi-jurisdictional financings, playing an instrumental role in shaping the financing landscape across the region."
Linklaters,,,,Exa,Linklaters Loses German Funds Partner to McDermott | Law.com,www.law.com,2026-06-26T00:00:00.000Z,2026-06-26 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.law.com/international-edition/2026/06/26/linklaters-loses-german-funds-partner-to-mcdermott/,"Linklaters Loses German Funds Partner to McDermott | Law.com ... # Linklaters Loses German Funds Partner to McDermott ... McDermott boosted its finance team with a second lateral hire this year but also lost a Munich corporate partner to Bird & Bird as European firms compete aggressively for top talent. ... Frankfurt-based investment funds partner Olena Tokman is joining McDermott Will & Schulte on August 1 after seven years at Linklaters, where she was promoted to partner in 2023. ... Prior to Linklaters, Tokman headed the legal department at Patrizia asset management and was a counsel at Hogan Lovells and a Shearman & Sterling associate. Her move to McDermott reunites Tokman with Frankfurt managing partner Frank Müller, an investment funds partner and former Hogan Lovells colleague with whom she previously worked. ... So far this year, Linklaters has lost two partners including Tokman and a Paris restructuring partner who both departed this week. Linklaters has also added parters in Europe this month, however, including a three-lawyer public law and regulatory team in Paris from Weil Gotshal & Manges. ... Meanwhile, Linklaters is restructuring its continental European offices with plans to close its10-lawyer Hamburg office and relocate partners later this year. Linklaters transferred its 80-lawyer Warsaw office to Addleshaw Goddard last year. ... Markus Wollenhaupt, Linklaters' global co-head of real estate and head of funds in Germany, said the firm continues to have a market-leading team in Germany and ""will further grow the senior ranks”. ... Matthias Kampshoff, McDermott's managing partner in Germany, said the firm is focused on expanding its German investment management practice. Worldwide, McDermott has about 150 investment funds practice lawyers following last year’s Schulte Roth & Zabel merger. ... In Europe, McDermott hired Kirkland Paris investment partner Kalish Mullen earlier this year and a 12-strong team in Milan with five partner s in May led by Vincenzo Giannantonio. It also strengthened its banking and finance practice with the addition of Oliver Sutter, former head of the Norton Rose Fulbright German banking practice, but lost Munich corporate partner Carsten Böhm to Bird & Bird in April."
Linklaters,,,,Exa,Linklaters advises Luxinva as consortium partner to EQT on the £10.9bn takeover of Intertek,legaldesire.com (Cynthia Lydia Marbaniang),2026-06-24T00:00:00.000Z,2026-06-24 00:00:00+00:00,,https://legaldesire.com/linklaters-advises-luxinva-as-consortium-partner-to-eqt-on-the-10-9bn-takeover-of-intertek/,"Linklaters advises Luxinva as consortium partner to EQT on the £10.9bn takeover of Intertek ... # Linklaters advises Luxinva as consortium partner to EQT on the £10.9bn takeover of Intertek ... Linklaters has advised Luxinva S.A., a wholly owned subsidiary of the Abu Dhabi Investment Authority (ADIA), as consortium partner to EQT on the takeover of Intertek for around £10.9bn (including debt). Intertek Group PLC (Intertek) is a leading quality assurance provider to industries worldwide, with a network of more than 1,000 laboratories and offices in more than 100 countries. ... The £61.08 per share cash and dividend deal values Intertek’s equity ⁠at £9.5bn, and marks Britain’s third-largest take-private deal, behind the acquisitions of airport operator BAA in 2006 and pharmacy chain Alliance Boots in 2007, according to LSEG-compiled data. Luxinva and a wholly owned subsidiary of Mubadala will become minority shareholders in Intertek, with stakes of 16% and 8%, respectively. ... An international cross-practice Linklaters team advised Luxinva on this transaction. The team was led by Partners Nicholas Edwards(Abu Dhabi), Richard Spedding(London) and Counsel Claire Neil(Dubai). ... The core team included Partner Rupert Cheyne(Corporate/M&A, London), Partner Alexei Franks(Tax, London), Annamaria Mangiaracina(Antitrust and Foreign Investment, Brussels), Counsel Jack Harrison(Tax, London), Managing Associates Landy Mao(Corporate/M&A, Abu Dhabi) and Karolis Pocius(Antitrust and Foreign Investment, Brussels), and Associates Prerna Handa, Deborah Wong(both Corporate/M&A, London) and Malak Toshani(Tax, London). ... ##### Linklaters advises banking consortium on successful placement of convertible bond by AT&S Austria Technologie & Systemtechnik AG"
Linklaters,,,,Exa,Linklaters Steers Tate & Lyle Through £2.7B US Takeover Approach | Law.com,www.law.com,2026-05-15T00:00:00.000Z,2026-05-15 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.law.com/international-edition/2026/05/15/linklaters-steers-tate--lyle-through-27b-us-takeover-approach/,"Linklaters Steers Tate & Lyle Through £2.7B US Takeover Approach | Law.com ... # Linklaters Steers Tate & Lyle Through £2.7B US Takeover Approach ... mark the latest in a ... . assets. ... Linklaters is advising Tate & Lyle PLC after the London-listed company said it had been approached by U.S. food company Ingredion in a takeover offer. ... According to an announcment by the U.K. company via LSEG, Ingredion has approached Tate & Lyle with a possible offer of up to 615 pence per share. Based on Tate & Lyle's 445,446,940 fully paid ordinary shares in issue, the headline consideration values the target's equity at approximately £2.74 billion. ... London corporate partners Simon Branigan and Lisa Chang were on the deal alongside lead US partners Thomas McGrath, Gabriel Grossman, and John Eichlin ... The mandate continues a long-running relationship between the firm and Tate & Lyle. ... If it succeeds, it would mark the latest in a string of U.S. takeovers of prized U.K. assets in recent years. ... In June 2024, Linklaters advised the company on its US$1.8 billion acquisition of pectin and speciality gums producer CP Kelco from J.M. Huber Corporation, taking control of its three divisions in the US, China and Denmark. ... That cross-practice team was led by global head of corporate Simon Branigan with corporate partners Lisa Chang and Namrata Shah, banking partner Caroline Courtney on the financing, Marly Didizian on commercial carve-out aspects, Sam Lintonbon and New York-based Gabe Grossman on tax, and Thomas McGrath on antitrust. ... The firm had also advised Tate & Lyle a month earlier on the US$350 million sale of its remaining 49.7% interest in Primient to KPS Capital Partners. ... The announcement follows recent press speculation and confirms that the proposal follows a number of earlier approaches from Ingredion to the board. Tate & Lyle and Ingredion are now in discussions over the terms. ... Ingredion has until 5.00 pm on June 11, 2026 to either announce a firm intention to make an offer or announce that it does not intend to make an offer, with any extension requiring Takeover Panel consent. ... Tate & Lyle cautioned shareholders that there can be no certainty that any offer will be made, nor as to the final terms on which any offer might be made. ... ## Clifford Chance, Linklaters and Milbank Lead Largest European IPO This Year"
Linklaters,,,,Exa,"Linklaters launches Applied Intelligence for firepower ""where off-the-shelf tools can't deliver"" - Legal IT Insider",legaltechnology.com (Caroline Hill - Editor-in-Chief),2026-05-11T00:00:00.000Z,2026-05-11 00:00:00+00:00,,https://legaltechnology.com/linklaters-launches-applied-intelligence-for-firepower-where-off-the-shelf-tools-cant-deliver/,"Linklaters launches Applied Intelligence for firepower ""where off-the-shelf tools can't deliver"" - Legal IT Insider ... # Linklaters launches Applied Intelligence for firepower “where off-the-shelf tools can’t deliver” ... - May 11, 2026 ... - Gen AI Latest News ... Linklaters announced last week that it has launched Applied Intelligence, which brings together lawyers and data scientists to co-design and deliver bespoke AI-enabled tech and legal solution for matters involving large and complex data sets and where off-the-shelf tools can’t deliver. We’re told that the team works with the full range of frontier models. ... Legal IT Insider revealed in November last year that Linklaters has created a 20+ dedicated AI lawyers team and naturally wondered how the two programs and individuals interconnect. A spokesperson for the magic circle firm said: “This is the next step in the firm’s ongoing strategy to lead in legal AI and this team will be closely joined up with the 20+ dedicated AI lawyers, so we see the two initiatives as complementary.” ... They added: “The AI Lawyer cohort and firmwide tools (Legora, Harvey, Copilot) embed AI into how our lawyers work day-to-day – they work alongside our practice groups to develop and implement AI solutions tailored to daily practice. Applied Intelligence is a specialist team you bring in when a matter needs more, e.g. algorithmic and AI review across large datasets or bespoke tech and legal solutions that an off-the-shelf tool cannot deliver.” ... Tom Quoroll, co-founder of Applied Intelligence, commented: “We’ve built the team not just for technical capability, but for the collaboration and judgement needed to turn complexity into impact for our clients.”"
