Deloitte,,,,Exa,Deloitte and Cedra Reshape Denmark’s Audit and Tax Advisory Market,www.taxspoc.com (TAXSPOC News Desk),2026-06-23T00:00:00.000Z,2026-06-23 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.taxspoc.com/articles/deloitte-cedra-denmark-audit-tax-market-deal/,"# Deloitte and Cedra Reshape Denmark’s Audit and Tax Market with Strategic Deal ... Deloitte and Cedra Draw a New Line in the Audit and Tax Market Deloitte Denmark’s decision to sell part of its Audit & Assurance business and selected Tax & Legal activities to Cedra Denmark is more than a corporate restructuring. It reflects a broader shift underway across the audit and tax advisory profession. Increasingly, firms… ... Deloitte Denmark’s decision to sell part of its Audit & Assurance business and selected Tax & Legal activities to Cedra Denmark is more than a corporate restructuring. It reflects a broader shift underway across the audit and tax advisory profession. ... The Deloitte-Cedra transaction is one of the clearest examples yet of a major firm deliberately repositioning itself around that divide. ... Following completion of the transaction, Deloitte will sharpen its focus on larger organizations with increasingly sophisticated requirements across audit, tax, legal, technology, risk, and transformation. ... Deloitte stated that it will continue serving clients through its global network while increasing its focus on large corporates, regulated industries, and privately owned businesses with complex operational structures. ... The firm will also maintain its public-sector audit and advisory practice, particularly where specialist expertise and technology-enabled solutions are required. ... For Cedra Denmark, the acquisition provides immediate scale and significantly expands its audit, tax, legal, and advisory capabilities. ... The transaction supports Cedra’s ambition to become a leading Nordic firm focused on SMEs and the mid-market segment, where businesses often require integrated audit, tax, accounting, legal, and business advisory services delivered through local relationships. ... The acquisition follows Cedra’s expansion across Scandinavia, including similar transactions involving Deloitte’s businesses in Norway and Sweden. ... Backed by Adelis Equity Partners, Cedra has been building a regional platform capable of competing with larger international networks while maintaining a local-market focus. ... While much of the attention will focus on the audit business, the transaction also includes selected activities from Deloitte’s Tax & Legal practice. ... The deal highlights how tax advisory businesses are increasingly being reshaped alongside audit practices as firms refine their target client segments. ... Following completion, Deloitte’s remaining Tax & Legal practice is expected to employ approximately 326 professionals and generate around DKK 700 million in annual revenue. ... At the same time, Cedra will gain additional tax capabilities that complement its growing advisory platform for owner-managed businesses and mid-sized enterprises. ... Under the agreement, Cedra Denmark will acquire: ... - Approximately 460 employees - Around DKK 565 million in annual revenue - Deloitte offices in Aalborg, Odense, Silkeborg and Kolding - Selected employees and client portfolios from Copenhagen and Aarhus ... Following the acquisition, Cedra Denmark is expected to employ approximately 730 people. ... The transaction reflects a broader trend emerging across Europe’s professional services market. ... Large firms are increasingly investing in technology, international capabilities, sector specialization, and multidisciplinary advisory services for complex organizations. At the same time, regional and mid-market firms are consolidating to build scale while preserving local client relationships. ... For tax professionals, the deal illustrates how firms are adapting to changing client expectations and how the market for tax advisory services is becoming increasingly segmented. ... Rather than competing for the same clients, Deloitte and Cedra are effectively choosing different parts of the market — and betting that greater focus will create stronger businesses on both sides. ... - Deloitte Denmark is divesting part of its Audit & Assurance practice and selected Tax & Legal activities. - Approximately 460 employees and DKK 565 million in annual revenue will transfer to Cedra Denmark. - The transaction includes Deloitte offices in Aalborg, Odense, Silkeborg and Kolding. - Deloitte will increase its focus on large and complex organizations. - Cedra will strengthen its position in the SME and mid-market audit, tax, and advisory market. - Deloitte will retain approximately 326 professionals in its Tax & Legal practice. - The transaction remains subject to competition authority approval. - Completion is expected during 2026."
Deloitte,,,,Exa,Big Four firms' India operations shine amid global slump - The Economic Times,economictimes.indiatimes.com (Vinod Mahanta),2026-06-21T00:00:00.000Z,2026-06-21 00:00:00+00:00,,https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/industry/services/consultancy-/-audit/big-four-firms-india-operations-shine-amid-global-slump/articleshow/131896673.cms,"India's Big Four accounting firms have achieved remarkable growth, outperforming global peers in fiscal year 2026. PwC and Deloitte reported significant revenue increases, while KPMG and EY also saw substantial gains. Technology consulting is a major driver, contributing significantly to revenue across all firms. Despite reporting variations, all four have surpassed the $1 billion India revenue mark, signaling robust expansion and strategic investments in key service areas. ... The Indian units of the Big Four firms closed fiscal year 2026 as some of the fastest-growing operations within their global networks, at a time when growth remained sluggish in mature markets, the lucrative Middle East market slowed and some regions continued to see layoffs.In FY26, PwC reported around Rs 14,000 crore in gross revenue with 21% growth, Deloitte clocked Rs 14,500 crore with 22% growth. KPMG's revenues crossed Rs 10,000 crore, including royalties, a one-off asset sale and revenue from KDN, its global delivery centre. And EY, whose fiscal year ends on June 30, has surpassed Rs 16,000 crore, according to two senior partners who spoke on condition of anonymity.Comparing the firms, however, is not straightforward. ... The firms, which operate as private partnerships, are locked in a race to appear bigger and have increasingly adopted different revenue reporting styles. Some include out-of-pocket expenses billed to clients, GST, royalties received to global headquarters, subcontracted work, India-centric work executed through global delivery centres and even asset sales, creating a 10%-25% variation in revenue figures.Excluding these additions, the Big Four's core India revenue is estimated at Rs 51,000-52,000 crore. All four firms have now crossed the $1 billion India revenue mark, with KPMG becoming the latest entrant after PwC and Deloitte last year. The firms' chiefs characterised FY26 as a year of steady expansion. ... PwC India Chairman Sanjeev Krishan said the firm maintained “steady growth momentum while continuing to invest”, Deloitte South Asia CEO Romal Shetty described it as ""meaningful growth on a large base"", while KPMG India CEO Yezdi Nagporewalla said the firm was in a “transformative phase.” ... Technology consulting emerged as the biggest growth engine and largest service line across firms. It contributes nearly 50% of PwC's business, 65% of Deloitte's and, along with business consulting, 35%-40% of KPMG's overall revenue. ... ""Consulting has been driving growth, led by cloud, data analytics, automation, digital enterprise services and business transformation, growing 24% and now accounting for 50% of the overall India business,"" said Krishan. ""The key drivers for the next three-to-five-year growth cycle are already in place and we are now pushing for the next phase of scale.”Deloitte has been the biggest scale-up story among the Big Four over the past year, continuing to expand its technology consulting footprint and client base.“Over the last three years, we have kept our heads down and invested heavily in building new growth engines, and those investments are now paying off. In FY26, we generated nearly Rs 19,000 crore in sales and have a pipeline of close to Rs 34,000 crore. Given the momentum, we are still hiring about 1,000 people a month. Demand has softened somewhat, but we are confident of delivering similar numbers next year as well,” said Shetty.KPMG, meanwhile, is leaning harder into advisory including deals , the domestic market and government work while trying to narrow the gap with its larger rivals.""We are focusing more on the domestic market, building new solutions, scaling up indirect tax and expanding our government advisory business. While our tax and audit practices have performed exceptionally well, the firm is making bold, long-term bets on advisory and AI. From the initial 8 to 10 % advisory and deals has now grown to almost 70 % of gross revenue ,” said Nagporewalla.Market leader EY retained leadership in its tax and deals businesses, despite ceding technology consulting leadership to Deloitte and facing margin and working capital pressures in that business. EY did not respond to the ET queries.Over the past year, the firm focused on scaling up its GCC business, strengthening its regional corridors, winning a larger share of work from India's biggest companies and defending its traditional strongholds of tax and deals, where it continues to enjoy a clear edge over rivals, say EY partners quoted above. EY India contributes 3%-4% of EY's global revenue.The leaders said India remains a key investment market, with the firms ramping up investments to support Prime Minister Narendra Modi's Viksit Bharat vision.The firms pursued different priorities over the past year. At PwC, the focus was on expanding in the domestic market and strengthening its global capability centre, or GCC, business, while investing in tax and deals after beefing up consulting over the preceding two years.At Deloitte, which has scaled up the fastest among the Big Four over the past three years, the emphasis was on expanding technology consulting, building new solutions and innovation centres and widening client coverage.The biggest battle among the Big Four is increasingly shifting away from tax and audit and toward GCCs. Some firms remain heavily dependent on their global networks, while others have built stronger ties with clients across multiple geographies.GCCs have also become a key growth driver for technology consulting businesses. Nearly 25% of PwC's technology consulting business comes from GCCs. At Deloitte, GCCs account for about 40% of technology consulting revenue, while at EY they contribute more than 35%.""We have invested in growing our GCC business domestically and want to be the leaders in building a similar value-added GCC proposition for Indian clients,"" said PWC’s Krishan.Tax has also emerged as an unexpected growth engine. PwC crossed Rs 2,300 crore in tax revenue, Deloitte exceeded Rs 2,100 crore and KPMG reached Rs 1,500 crore, with KPMG's tax practice growing 20% year-on-year.Global networks see India as an important growth market but what’s also now really the focus for networks is the use of it as a talent base. For example, EY Global is very bullish on the Ajay Anand led India-based Global Delivery Services unit, which is directly controlled by headquarters. The unit added 25,000 professionals last year— taking its workforce past 74,000— which is nearly half of entire EY India business workforce."
Deloitte,,,,Exa,Deloitte and Google Cloud open London AI studio to scale agentic capabilities,www.channelweb.co.uk (Paul Ferretti),2026-06-17T00:00:00.000Z,2026-06-17 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.channelweb.co.uk/news/2026/deloitte-google-cloud-london-ai-hub,"Deloitte and Google Cloud open London AI studio to scale agentic capabilities ... # Deloitte and Google Cloud open London AI studio to scale agentic capabilities ... ## 1,000 AI professionals will also receive training on Google’s Gemini Enterprise platform ... Deloitte has launched a new AI Studio in London with Google Cloud to help UK organisations move from AI experimentation to deployment. ... Set to open in late July, the facility will reportedly allow Deloitte and its customers to build, test and deploy AI agents across business processes. ... The initiative will focus on sectors including public services, financial services, retail, healthcare, life sciences and telecommunications. ... For retailers, the emphasis will be on modernising customer journeys through agentic AI, while healthcare and telecoms projects will centre on transforming business models and day-to-day operations. ... ### Deloitte and Google: training plan for 1,000 partners ... Alongside the studio, Deloitte announced plans to train 1,000 members of its UK AI and data workforce on Google Cloud’s Gemini Enterprise platform. ... The programme aims to equip consultants, engineers and AI specialists with the technical expertise required to deploy agentic AI solutions using Google’s tech stack. ... Google Cloud said the initiative reflects growing demand from organisations looking to move AI projects into production environments. ... The studio will host regular sessions for testing and assessing agentic AI use cases, some of which are expected to run for around four weeks. ... “With our new AI Studio and the continued investment in our people, we are providing the physical and technical infrastructure necessary to make agentic AI an industrial reality in the UK.” ... Matt Lacey, global chief commercial officer for Alphabet Google at Deloitte, added: “Together, we will bring AI technology to life for a cross-section of industries in a physical setting that facilitates experimentation, collaboration and innovation, complementing our existing Experience Centres in Egypt and India.” ... ### Deloitte’s renewed EMEA focus ... The launch comes as Deloitte has doubled down on its UK and EMEA operations over the past 12 months ... **[In March](https://www.channelweb.co.uk/news/2026/deloitte-darren-graves-uk-ceo)**, the organisation named Darren Graves as UK CEO, replacing Richard Houston after six years in the role. ... Houston transitioned to CEO of Deloitte’s new EMEA structure, which came into effect on 1 June and aims to bolster collaboration across 16 participating firms operating in more than 80 countries. ... These changes follow a mixed year for the consultancy, as global revenue rose 4.8 per cent to $70.5bn (£52.55bn) despite a one per cent decline in UK revenue to £5.7bn."
Deloitte,,,,Exa,"Deloitte, SAP set up AI Bengaluru hub, eye 500 new hires",www.techcircle.in (Shraddha Goled),2026-06-11T00:00:00.000Z,2026-06-11 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.techcircle.in/2026/06/11/deloitte-sap-launch-bengaluru-hub-to-add-up-to-500-ai-specialists,"Deloitte, SAP set up AI Bengaluru hub, eye 500 new hires ... Deloitte India and SAP have launched a dedicated AI-led delivery centre in Bengaluru aimed at accelerating enterprise cloud transformation, intelligent automation and artificial intelligence adoption, as demand for AI-powered business modernization gathers pace across industries. ... The new Deloitte-SAP Delivery Centre will serve as a strategic innovation and delivery hub supporting SAP and joint enterprise customers across the transformation lifecycle, from architecture and engineering to implementation and managed services. ... The launch marks one of Deloitte's first SAP-focused delivery centres in India and reinforces the country's growing role as a global hub for enterprise technology services, cloud transformation and AI innovation. ... The centre will be supported by an ecosystem of more than 1,000 professionals and is expected to expand further with the addition of 300 to 500 specialised AI professionals over the next 12 to 24 months. Recruitment will focus on high-demand skills including generative AI, agentic AI, machine learning, cloud architecture, data engineering and intelligent automation. ... ""The Deloitte-SAP Delivery Centre underscores our shared commitment to driving innovation through AI, cloud and advanced technologies,"" said Sathish Gopalaiah, President, Consulting Businesses, Deloitte South Asia. ""As organisations accelerate digital transformation, this centre will help SAP and our joint clients harness emerging technologies, scale innovation and deliver technology-led outcomes globally."" ... The facility is expected to play a key role in helping enterprises modernise legacy systems, migrate to cloud-based SAP environments and deploy AI-driven business processes. It will focus on enabling organisations to unlock business value through SAP's cloud offerings while building intelligent operating models powered by automation and artificial intelligence. ... A central feature of the centre is a dedicated AI Command Centre Lab, which will function as a prototyping and experimentation hub for enterprise AI use cases. The lab is designed to help accelerate AI adoption by providing access to development tools, mentorship and collaborative environments for both technical and business teams. ... The initiative reflects a broader trend among global consulting and technology firms to invest in AI-focused delivery hubs as enterprises move from pilot projects to large-scale deployments of generative and agentic AI technologies. ... Industry analysts say demand for AI transformation services is rising as organisations seek to integrate AI capabilities into core business operations, modernise enterprise applications and improve operational efficiency. ... Beyond customer implementations, the centre will also focus on developing reusable frameworks, accelerators and industry-specific assets aimed at simplifying complex transformation projects and reducing deployment timelines. ... The announcement comes as India continues to attract investments in global capability centres, AI engineering hubs and cloud innovation facilities, driven by its large technology talent pool and growing strategic importance in enterprise digital transformation initiatives. ... With enterprises increasingly prioritising AI and cloud-led modernization, the Deloitte-SAP Delivery Centre is expected to support the development and deployment of next-generation enterprise solutions for customers in India and global markets."
Deloitte,,,,Exa,AXA XL and Deloitte forge global cybersecurity partnership | Insurance Business,www.insurancebusinessmag.com (Josh Recamara),2026-06-11T00:00:00.000Z,2026-06-11 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.insurancebusinessmag.com/uk/news/breaking-news/axa-xl-and-deloitte-forge-global-cybersecurity-partnership-578508.aspx,"AXA XL and Deloitte forge global cybersecurity partnership | Insurance Business ... # AXA XL and Deloitte forge global cybersecurity partnership ... AXA XL has struck a strategic partnership with Deloitte to deliver cybersecurity and operational technology services to clients across the globe, in a move that carries significant implications for the UK cyber insurance market at a moment when the industry is under intense scrutiny. ... The tie-up, available through the AXA Digital Commercial Platform, expands on AXA XL's existing cyber insurance and risk consulting capabilities. Under the agreement, clients will gain access to a suite of Deloitte-delivered services spanning the full prevention and detection spectrum, from critical asset identification and vulnerability assessments to cyber crisis simulations and continuous threat monitoring. ... Centre, a ... body established by the ... industry, classified ... incidents as a ... total financial impact ... for catastrophic cyber losses, ... Cyber incidents surged to unprecedented levels in 2025, yet premiums fell by an average of 11% and coverage broadened, a rare divergence between underlying risk and insurance pricing driven by aggressive growth targets, new managing general agents, a new syndicate and insurers deploying more net capacity. ... The partnership is also well-timed from a regulatory perspective. The Cyber Security and Resilience Bill was introduced to Parliament in November 2025, aiming to modernise the UK's cyber regulatory regime, widen the scope of regulated entities and strengthen resilience across critical sectors. ... The Association of British Insurers welcomed the legislation, noting that nearly £200 million was paid out in cyber claims last year and describing cyber insurance as ""not just a financial safety net"" but a tool that ""helps to put businesses on the front foot, improving their security processes, providing expert advice and helping with incident response planning."" ... Despite the growing threat environment and regulatory pressure, uptake remains stubbornly low. The 2025 UK Cyber Security Breaches Survey found that only 7% of all UK businesses held standalone cyber coverage, with just 17% of small businesses holding such policies, even though over 43% report cyberattacks annually. ... The Deloitte deal comes weeks after AXA XL unveiled a dedicated prevention business unit led by global chief underwriting officer Libby Benet, designed to accelerate the development and reach of the insurer's risk consulting capabilities. The new unit becomes AXA XL's fifth, sitting alongside Americas, APAC & Europe, UK & Lloyd's and Reinsurance. The Deloitte partnership operationalises that vision on the cyber front, offering clients a direct pipeline to specialist cybersecurity expertise through their insurance relationship. ... AXA XL chief executive Scott Gunter has been clear about the direction of travel. ""Prevention must be at the heart of how we support our clients,"" he has said. ""That is why we are making services a core part of our offering."" With rates soft, losses rising and clients demanding more than indemnity cover, the Deloitte deal suggests that prevention-as-a-service is fast becoming the new competitive frontier in cyber insurance. ... ""As cyber risks continue to grow, prevention, early detection and swift remediation are becoming increasingly important to business resilience, alongside appropriate cyber insurance coverage,"" said Imade Elbaraka, managing partner at Deloitte Cyber. ""Together, we aim to strengthen clients' resilience by helping them better anticipate and prevent cyber-related risks, while contributing to a safer cyber environment for businesses."""
Deloitte,,,,Exa,Deloitte Middle East launches Tax Operate to help organizations strengthen compliance and modernize tax operations,www.deloitte.com,2026-06-10T00:00:00.000Z,2026-06-10 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.deloitte.com/middle-east/en/about/press-room/dme-launches-tax-operate-to-help-organizations-strengthen-compliance-and-modernize-tax-operations.html,"Deloitte Middle East launches Tax Operate to help organizations strengthen compliance and modernize tax operations ... # Deloitte Middle East launches Tax Operate to help organizations strengthen compliance and modernize tax operations ... New technology-enabled model supports end-to-end tax functions and helps organizations manage growing regulatory complexity. ... Middle East, June 10th, 2026 - Deloitte Middle East has announced the launch of Tax Operate in the region, a new offering designed to help organizations manage and transform their tax functions through scalable, technology-enabled operating models. ... As tax environments become more complex and regulatory expectations continue to rise, organizations are under increasing pressure to strengthen compliance while maintaining operational efficiency. Tax Operate supports end-to-end tax operations, helping businesses streamline processes, improve governance, and maintain operational control through sustainable delivery models. ... Unlike traditional advisory engagements focused on one-off transformation projects, Tax Operate is designed for ongoing operational delivery. The offering combines domain expertise with technology-enabled processes to support the full lifecycle of tax operations, including compliance, reporting, process standardization, and operational governance. ... Delivered through flexible co-sourced and outsourced models, Tax Operate enables organizations to access Deloitte’s specialist tax, regulatory and industry expertise while maintaining oversight and control of their tax function. This offering helps businesses strengthen governance, improve efficiency, and manage growing complexity through more scalable and resilient operating models, leveraging Deloitte’s deep industry knowledge. ... Beyond day-to-day operational support, what differentiates Tax Operate is organizations’ access to Deloitte specialists who understand the evolving regulatory landscape and the broader strategic implications of tax transformation across complex business environments. ... The offering also reflects the growing need for organizations to respond to rapidly evolving global tax regulations, including frameworks such as the OECD’s Pillar Two global minimum tax where applicable. By modernizing tax operating models and embedding technology-enabled processes, Tax Operate helps organizations build resilient tax functions that can adapt to regulatory change while enabling continuous improvement. ... Basit Hussain, Partner & Tax Operate Leader, Deloitte Middle East, said: ""Tax functions today face increasing regulatory scrutiny and operational complexity. Organizations need sustainable models that strengthen compliance while improving efficiency and governance. Tax Operate combines deep technical expertise, technology-enabled processes and scalable delivery models to help businesses manage their tax operations with greater confidence and control."" ... since 192 ... Middle East region is established through its affiliated independent legal entities, which are licensed to operate and to provide services under the ... laws and regulations of ... DME’s affiliates and related entities cannot oblige each other and/or DME ... and when providing services, each affiliate and related entity engages directly and independently with ... own clients and shall ... be liable for its own acts or omissions and not those of any other affiliate ... Deloitte provides leading professional services to nearly 90% of the Fortune Global 500® and thousands of private companies. Our people deliver measurable and lasting results that help reinforce public trust in capital markets and enable clients to transform and thrive. Building on its 180+year history, Deloitte spans more than 150 countries and territories. Learn how Deloitte’s over 470,000 people worldwide work together every day to make an impact that matters at www.deloitte.com"
Deloitte,,,,Exa,Deloitte and Ironclad Form Strategic Alliance to Expand Agentic Contract Lifecycle Management Capabilities – Press release | Deloitte US,www.deloitte.com,2026-06-08T00:00:00.000Z,2026-06-08 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.deloitte.com/us/en/about/press-room/deloitte-and-ironclad-form-strategic-alliance.html,"Deloitte and Ironclad Form Strategic Alliance to Expand Agentic Contract Lifecycle Management Capabilities – Press release | Deloitte US ... # Deloitte and Ironclad Form Strategic Alliance to Expand Agentic Contract Lifecycle Management Capabilities ... ## New alliance combines Deloitte’s transformation experience with Ironclad’s AI contracting platform to help enterprises accelerate, govern and optimize the full contracting lifecycle ... NEW YORK and SAN FRANCISCO, June 8, 2026 — Deloitte Tax LLP (“Deloitte”) and Ironclad today announced a new strategic alliance designed to help organizations modernize contracting as AI reshapes how legal and business teams create, negotiate and manage agreements. By combining the broad legal and business transformation experience, industry knowledge and scalable delivery capabilities offered by Deloitte's Legal Business Services (LBS) practice with Ironclad’s AI contracting platform and AI-enabled capabilities, the alliance aims to support clients across the full contracting lifecycle. ... Contracting is a core enterprise consideration that can impact revenue velocity, cost management, compliance and customer and supplier experience. Yet many organizations still rely on fragmented tools and manual workflows that slow execution and limit visibility into obligations and risk. Enterprises are looking to deploy AI-enabled contracting that can accelerate cycle times, improve consistency and surface valuable insights from contract data. Ironclad’s AI-first functionalities and seamless user experience, combined with Deloitte’s systems implementation, integration and optimization capabilities help clients transform their end-to-end contracting with the potential for measurable business value. ... “Contracts sit at the center of how organizations operate and manage risk, yet many still rely on fragmented and manual processes,” said Carin Giuliante, chair and CEO, Deloitte Tax LLP. “Our alliance with Ironclad reflects our focus on helping clients modernize critical business functions by combining leading technology with practical transformation experience to drive faster cycle times, better insight and stronger outcomes.” ... “This alliance with Deloitte is a significant step forward in helping enterprises harness the power of AI contracting,"" said Dan Springer, CEO, Ironclad. ""By combining our AI contracting platform with Deloitte's deep industry knowledge and transformation experience, we empower our joint clients to not just modernize their contracting processes, but to turn them into a strategic advantage that accelerates business with every contract."" ... “AI is rapidly reshaping contracting, but value only materializes when innovation is embedded into the operating model, controls, data foundation and ways of working,” said Teju Deshpande, lead Ironclad Alliance partner, Deloitte Tax LLP. “Together with Ironclad, we can help organizations modernize contracting end to end — deploying AI-enabled workflows with the appropriate governance, human oversight, and adoption strategies to support efficiency, manage risk more effectively and unlock actionable insight from their contracts.” ... The alliance underscores Deloitte and Ironclad’s commitment to helping organizations modernize their legal and business operations through technology-enabled contracting, responsible AI adoption and stronger governance, with the goal of supporting agility, compliance and growth. ... Deloitte provides industry-leading audit, consulting, tax and advisory services to many of the world’s most admired brands, including nearly 90% of the Fortune 500® and more than 9,000 U.S.-based private companies. At Deloitte, we strive to live our purpose of making an impact that matters for our people, clients, and communities. We bring together distinct talents, technologies, disciplines, and an ecosystem of alliances to help tackle today’s most complex business challenges and drive long-term progress. Deloitte is proud to be part of the largest global professional services network serving our clients in the markets that are most important to them. Bringing more than 180 years of service, our network of member firms spans more than 150 countries and territories. Learn how Deloitte’s approximately 470,000 people worldwide connect for impact at www.deloitte.com."
