COLES,,,,Exa,Diageo targets $1 billion savings after FY sales decline - The Spirits Business,www.thespiritsbusiness.com (Joe Rogers),2026-08-07T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-07 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.thespiritsbusiness.com/2026/08/diageo-targets-1-billion-savings-after-fy-sales-decline/,"Diageo targets $1 billion savings after FY sales decline - The Spirits Business ... # Diageo targets $1 billion savings after FY sales decline ... The Johnnie Walker owner unveiled a US$1 billion restructuring plan after reporting a net sales decline of 2% for the year ended 30 June 2026. ... Diageo delivered its preliminary full-year 2026 (F26) results yesterday, reporting a 2% drop in organic sales against predictions of a 2-3% decline. ... The release of these figures came before Diageo announced it is to cut c.$1 billion in costs over the next three years as changes to its operating framework, supply chain and restructuring begin to yield savings. ... This represents a 0.4% fall in volume and an organic net sales decline of 3% to US$19.6 billion, driven by a combination of category softness across spirits, competitive pressure on the Tequila category, and double-digit declines in sales of Chinese white spirits (CWS). ... Organic profit increased by 2%, which the firm has attributed to cost savings despite the impact of tariffs and unfavourable price mix. ... However, the weight of impairment charges and expenditures related to restructuring reflected in the group’s operating profits, which declined by 27.2%. ... In addition to the negative impact of declining organic net sales, the company has cited the impact of disposals including the sales of Guinness Ghana Breweries, Guinness Nigeria, Seychelles Breweries; as well as the Sheridan’s liqueur brand and the transfer of majority ownership of Cîroc vodka in North America to investment firm Main Street Advisors. ... The London-headquartered firm cited the impact of significant exceptional costs relating to restructuring which totalled $0.9 billion in FY26, with roughly $752 million going towards the implementation of a new operating framework, and the remaining c.$148 million accounted for by the firm’s Accelerate programme. ... These expenses were offset somewhat by positive organic growth seen in many regions, with net sales up by 3.4% in Europe, 7.7% in Latin America and the Caribbean (LAC), and 13.3% in Africa. ... However, net sales in North America were down by 8.4% while Asia Pacific fell by 6.3%. ... CFO Nik Jhangiani said: “fiscal 26 has been a mixed year, with good momentum in Europe, Latin America and Africa, but with challenges in North American and Asia Pacific.” ... While Sir Dave Lewis, CEO, added: “We are pleased with our progress in Latin America, Europe and Africa. We are focused on recovering our competitiveness in North America and we are working through the consequences of government policy in Chinese white spirits.” ... US spirits net sales declined by 11.5%, reflecting a 9% drop in volume, driven by competitive pressure and softness in the Tequila category. Tequila sales in the region were down by 21.1%. ... Türkiye grew by 25.5%, aided by double-digit increases in sales of raki, Scotch whisky and gin, with Johnnie Walker benefitting from expanded distribution and increased visibility in the territory. ... Challenges relating to Chinese white spirits (CWS) contributed to a 6.3% decline in organic net sales in the Asia Pacific region. ... In Latin America and the Caribbean, organic net sales grew by 7.7%, with volumes up by 3.1%. ... Diageo’s Scotch whisky portfolio also helped to drive a net sales rise of 5.7% in ... Caribbean, Central America and Venezuela region with volumes up by ... 1.8 ... Africa showed organic net sales growth of 13.3% and organic volume growth of 14.0%, attributable in part to double-digit growth across spirits and RTDs and high-single-digit growth in beer. ... Sales of the key Don Julio and Casamigos Tequila brands fell by 19.2% and 27.7% respectively. Diageo has announced plans to reposition the Casamigos brand with a new marketing campaign to boost competitiveness. ... Ready-to-drink and cocktail net sales grew by 35.1% in the US, which has been attributed to the successful launch of Casamigos read-to-serve formats coinciding with the men’s Fifa World Cup and strong sales of Bulleit and Ketel One cocktails. ... Vodka sales ... region, with flagship brand Smirnoff down by 5%, amid pressure from RTDs and overall category weakness. ... Net sales of Scotch whisky were up by ... 2% worldwide, driven in part by organic net sales growth of 2 ... Jonnie Walker and ... Diageo has predicted flat organic sales growth for FY27, with North American organic net sales expected to fall by mid-single-digits. ... It has further outlined a projected operating profit growth in the low-to-mid single-digits, accounting for savings yielded by ongoing changes to its supply chain and operational framework. ... “This new strategy, executing with a new, more agile, competitive and cost-effective operating model, gives us confidence that we can return Diageo to a business consistently creating value for shareholders,” Lewis added. ... “We remain a business with a very strong premiumisation agenda, but by activating our wider portfolio, we will be able to serve more consumers, across a variety of occasions. There is hard work ahead, particularly in North America, where improving performance is a clear priority, but we are confident we can deliver without taking a step back in operating profit.” ... India bans Diageo brands over added flavourings ... Union says Diageo ‘steam-rolling’ through job cuts ... Diageo appoints new Asia Pacific chief"
COLES,,,,Exa,Supermarket giant Coles to move 1000 back-office roles to India in new Accenture deal,www.afr.com (Edmund Tadros),2026-08-06T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-06 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.afr.com/companies/professional-services/coles-lands-accenture-deal-to-send-hundreds-of-office-jobs-to-india-20260806-p60m2i,"Supermarket giant Coles to move 1000 back-office roles to India in new Accenture deal ... Aug 7, 2026 – 5.00am ... Coles is planning to move as many as 1000 jobs to India as part of a deal with Accenture, allowing the supermarket giant to cut costs and invest in its online operations as it faces intense competition from Amazon. ... The country’s second-largest supermarket retailer will outsource marketing, finance, human resources and technology jobs as part of its expanded multi-year partnership with the consulting and advisory giant."
COLES,,,,Exa,"3 days, 3 supermarkets: the reporting week that will shape ASX consumer staples shares",www.fool.com.au (Mark Verhoeven),2026-08-05T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-05 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.fool.com.au/2026/08/06/3-days-3-supermarkets-the-reporting-week-that-will-shape-asx-consumer-staples-shares/,"Coles Group Ltd (ASX: COL), Woolworths Group Ltd (ASX: WOW) and Wesfarmers Ltd (ASX: WES) are all scheduled to report on consecutive days. ... Their combined consensus net profit runs to more than $5.6 billion. ... As a result, these three companies are a great barometer of Australian consumer behaviour on the calendar. ... Woolworths delivered the best results of the three. ... Group EBIT rose 14.4%, and the board declared a fully franked interim dividend of 45 cents, up 15.4%. ... Coles was more mixed. ... Sales revenue lifted 2.5% to $23.6 billion, and group EBIT excluding significant items rose 10.2% to $1,231 million. ... Supermarkets EBIT grew a strong 14.6%. ... But statutory net profit fell 11.3% to $511 million, hit by a $235 million provision relating to the Fair Work Ombudsman proceedings. ... The interim dividend still rose 10.8% to 41 cents, fully franked. ... Wesfarmers grew revenue 3.1% to $24.2 billion and net profit 9.3% to $1,603 million. ... Its interim dividend rose 7.4% to $1.02 per share, fully franked. ... All three lifted their payouts, which tells you something about how confident these boards were halfway through the year. ... Coles reported supermarket price inflation of 1.9% in the second quarter, or 1.7% excluding tobacco. ... Low inflation is welcome for shoppers. However, it also removes an easy lever for revenue growth, which places all the pressure on volumes and cost control. ... ? Are margins"
COLES,,,,Exa,Salter Brothers cash in on payment platform sale to Commonwealth Bank,www.afr.com (Sarah Thompson),2026-08-05T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-05 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.afr.com/street-talk/salter-brothers-cash-in-on-payment-platform-sale-to-commonwealth-bank-20260805-p60lrj,"Salter Brothers on M&A spree ... Melbourne’s Salter Brothers has gone against the grain to pile into a software-as-a-service platform used by the likes of the English Premier League’s teams. ... The company’s technology fund has cornerstoned a capital raising for platform EngageRM, a global sports and entertainment technology platform whose customers include major sporting leagues like the NBA, Premier League, AFL and NRL, as well as major sporting venues."
COLES,,,,Exa,Coles puts electric prime movers to work on B-double freight routes - Fleet EV News,fleetevnews.com.au (Fleet EV News Team),2026-08-04T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-04 00:00:00+00:00,,https://fleetevnews.com.au/coles-puts-electric-prime-movers-to-work-on-b-double-freight-routes/,"Coles puts electric prime movers to work on B-double freight routes - Fleet EV News ... # Coles puts electric prime movers to work on B-double freight routes ... By Fleet EV News Team August 4, 2026 Coles together with S2 Logistics and Primary Freight are launching their first ever electric truck, pictured at Coles ADC in Kemps Creek. Coles is expanding its electric vehicle trials into heavy freight, introducing three Volvo electric prime movers into its New South Wales distribution network in partnership with S2 Logistics. ... The electric trucks will transport products from suppliers into Coles distribution centres and are capable of pulling B-double trailer combinations. ... Their introduction represents an important operational test for battery-electric heavy vehicles in Australia, where freight tasks involving high payloads, long operating hours and strict delivery schedules are significantly more demanding than urban delivery applications. ... Based on expected annual operations, ... forecasts that each electric prime mover will avoid ... use of more than 2 ... ,000 litres of ... every year when compared with ... Coles Head of Primary Freight, Dwayne Fernandez, said the introduction of the electric prime movers would help the company understand how lower-operational emissions technology could be incorporated into its transport network. ... The ability to pull B-double trailers is a significant part of the trial. ... While battery-electric trucks are increasingly being introduced for metropolitan delivery work, B-double operations create additional demands relating to vehicle mass, energy consumption, route planning, charging infrastructure and driver productivity. ... The Coles project will provide practical information about how electric prime movers perform when completing genuine supply chain tasks rather than controlled demonstrations. ... “These electric prime movers will give us valuable insights into how heavy electric vehicles perform ... day operations, ... double routes,” ... “This will help inform how we invest in and scale transport solutions with lower operational emissions across our network over time.” ... The trial should help Coles and S2 Logistics identify where electric prime movers provide the strongest operational and emissions benefits, as well as where further infrastructure, technology or changes to freight planning may be required. ... and maintenance costs ... Managers will need reliable operating data ... supporting larger investments in ... vehicles and charging ... Projects such as the Coles trial can provide evidence about energy consumption, charging demand, vehicle availability and maintenance performance. This data can then be used to refine financial models and compare electric trucks with diesel alternatives on a like-for-like operational basis. ... The new trucks form part of Coles’ broader work to explore electric vehicle technology across its operations. ... This includes investigating lower-emissions freight transport and providing electric vehicle charging for customers at selected stores. ... The three Volvo electric prime movers will give Coles and S2 Logistics an opportunity to test battery-electric freight technology under genuine operating conditions. ... More importantly, the lessons from the project could help shape how Coles invests in lower-emissions transport across its distribution network in the years ahead."
COLES,,,,Exa,"Coles, Palantir and GetUp; tradie health; IEEFA and solar; CEFC continues SME discounts | Jobs and Biz News | The Fifth Estate","thefifthestate.com.au (Bevin Liu, The Fifth Estate)",2026-08-04T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-04 00:00:00+00:00,,https://thefifthestate.com.au/jobs-and-biz-news/coles-palantir-and-getup-tradie-health-ieefa-and-solar-cefc-continues-sme-discounts/,"# Coles, Palantir and GetUp; tradie health; IEEFA and solar; CEFC continues SME discounts by Bevin Liu and The Fifth Estate 4 August 2026 4 August 2026 Pro ... Palantir Photo: American Friends Service Committee ... ## Coles threatens GetUp with legal action ... Feisty social and environmental justice activist GetUp, which pulled that dramatic stunt at Pauline Hanson’s National Press Club speech a few weeks ago is now under attack from Coles and vowing to fight back. ... The problem is the supermarket giant’s relationship with Palantir, a company GetUp refers to as “a surveillance company founded by a far-right billionaire that builds tools for militaries, spy agencies and ICE”. ... GetUp said in a missive on Tuesday that Coles is fighting to stop GetUp’s advertising campaign by forcing ad companies to pull down unflattering ads relating to the surveillance company and seeking a public apology to all social media and websites. ... “All this matters, because Palantir isn’t an ordinary tech supplier,” a GetUp statement said. ... “It was built to fuse enormous quantities of personal data into a powerful AI system, and it sells that capability to militaries, intelligence agencies and ICE. Coles knows what millions of Australian households buy, when, where, how often and on whose card. It knows where its workers are, what they are doing, and for how long.” ... The organisation said it will be raising funds and seeking support from shareholders participating at the Coles AGM to seek answers on what data Palantir will be touching. ... The Institute for Energy Economics and Financial Analysis (IEEFA) has released notes from its briefing examining the growth of distributed energy resources (DER ... In Australia, solar panels are now installed on 40 per cent of homes, thanks to early support from feed in tariffs and government rebates. This helped grow rooftop solar from near zero to 13 per cent of the total electricity generation in the national electricity market between 2014 and 2025, while coal’s share declined from 75 per cent to 52 per cent. The nation is also experiencing a boom in residential batteries, with almost 10 gigawatt-hour capacity installed less than a year since federal rebates were introduced. ... In Bangladesh, rooftop solar is growing faster than other DERs. Unlike government data, which reports 415.9 megawatts (MW) of total rooftop solar capacity, IEEFA finds the country’s capacity ... have already reached 667 MW, and with small units below ... 5MW included, ... reached 100 ... in power demand during the ... in 2024 to 2026. ... In India, DER growth, particularly solar, has been driven by government-led schemes. Subsidies, policies and easier financing have led to growing rooftop solar and agricultural solarisation, increasing distributed solar capacity from 1.8 GW in the 2018 financial year to 31.5 GW in FY26. ... ## CEFC ups the ante on SME energy efficiency ... The Clean Energy Finance Corporation is committing another $150 million to expand access to ANZ Bank’s small to medium enterprise discounts for energy efficiency technology. ... The corporation and the bank each annually contribute 0.4 per cent towards a total discount of 0.8 per cent, which can be used to reduce total repayment costs and improve cash flow over the life of the asset, supporting decisions that would otherwise be deferred. Technology eligible for the discount includes electric vehicles, rooftop solar, batteries, energy-efficient technology and recycling equipment, helping businesses reduce operating costs while improving energy performance. ... The commitment expands on a CEFC and ANZ co-financing program, which has already helped more than 1600 Australian businesses with more than $444 million in discounted asset finance. The organisation said it provided more than $90 million in finance in the last financial year alone to customers through the program. - Read about"
COLES,,,,Exa,Centuria buoyed by near-record leasing and asset sales,www.businessnewsaustralia.com (Business News Australia),2026-08-04T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-04 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.businessnewsaustralia.com/articles/centuria-buoyed-by-near-record-leasing-and-asset-sales-as-office-market-stabilises.html,"Centuria buoyed by near-record leasing and asset sales ... # Centuria buoyed by near-record leasing and asset sales as office market stabilises ... Australia’s largest listed pure-play office fund Centuria Office REIT (ASX: COF) has benefitted from strong leasing activity, positive valuations and sales of assets at a significant premium to book value to deliver funds from operations of $66.9 million, which is within guidance and a sign that the trust says the sector has begun to stabilise. ... The real estate investment trust says the funds from operations translate to 11.2cs per unit for FY26, which compares with a guidance range of 11.1c to 11.5c per unit - although down from 11.8c per unit in the prior year. ... Centuria leased 40,000sqm across 47 transactions during the year, representing 14.5 per cent of the portfolio's net lettable area and achieving average re-leasing spreads of 5 per cent. ... Queensland assets were the standout performers, recording re-leasing spreads of 14 per cent. ... The trust sold 9 Help Street at Chatswood for $90 million at a 12.5 per cent premium to book value, delivering a 5.5 per cent passing yield, a 12.3 per cent internal rate of return and a 109 per cent capital uplift over the hold period. ... “Conditions across the Australian domestic office markets continued to stabilise during the year, reflected in an improvement in COF’s portfolio valuations, the successful divestment of 9 Help St, Chatswood at a premium to book value, and positive re-leasing spreads across the portfolio,"" says Belinda Cheung, fund manager at Centuria Office REIT. ... ""With limited new office supply across the medium-term due to a rising disparity between replacement cost and prevailing valuations, COF anticipates continuing benefits from stabilising market conditions."" ... Like-for-like portfolio revaluations rose $18 million or 1 per cent on June 2025 book values, supported by market rental growth of 4.4 per cent. ... The weighted average capitalisation rate expanded 16 basis points to 7.04 per cent across the 18-asset portfolio, which was valued at $1.842 billion at year-end. ... The result followed a stronger first half in which valuations rose $43 million on a like-for-like basis, implying a second-half reversal as capitalisation rates expanded further in the back end of the year. ... Portfolio occupancy stood at 91 per cent with a weighted average lease expiry of four years across the portfolio, 97 per cent of which comprises A-grade assets. ... On the balance sheet, Centuria Office refinanced $1 billion of debt during the year, extending its weighted average debt expiry to 4.3 years while achieving a reduction in margins of 30 basis points. ... Gearing sat at 43.7 per cent and net tangible assets were $1.66 per unit. ... For FY27, the trust is guiding to funds from operations of 11.3c per unit and a distribution of 9c per unit, representing a reduction from the 10.1c per unit paid in FY26. ... At the current unit price, the FY27 distribution implies a yield of 10.1 per cent with the lower distribution reflecting in part the impact of the Chatswood disposal on income. ... South-East"
COLES,,,,Exa,"Acom International acquires Barbeques Galore, names CEO - Inside Retail Australia",insideretail.com.au (Harry Booth),2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-03 00:00:00+00:00,,https://insideretail.com.au/stores/openings-closings/acom-international-acquires-barbeques-galore-names-ceo-202608,"Acom International acquires Barbeques Galore, names CEO - Inside Retail Australia ... Melbourne-based wholesaler Acom International has emerged as the buyer of Barbeques Galore, the largest outdoor furniture retailer in the country, which collapsed earlier this year. ... Acom’s acquisition includes a series of brands, including Ziegler & Brown, Ziggy, Turbo, Beefmaster, and Firehawk. The deal will see the closure of all company-owned stores, with independently-owned stores allowed to operate under licence. ... Some 500 jobs were put at risk when Barbeques Galore collapsed; it is unclear how many of those jobs will be saved under the new operating model. The fate of these jobs was dealt an additional blow in early June when a restructuring plan led by investment firm Gordon Brothers was rejected by creditors. ... Longstanding Barbeques Galore executive Mike Ainsworth has been named the new CEO. Acom says he will be joined by an experienced leadership team from the wholesaler’s own operation. ... “Our ambition is simple: To bring Barbeques Galore and some of Australia’s most loved barbecue and wood-heating brands within reach of more Australians,” Ainsworth said. ... “We will do that by supporting our independent Barbeques Galore stores, expanding distribution through wholesale customers and other retailers, growing our export markets, and maintaining a strong national online presence.” ... The new era of Barbeques Galore will centre around a “renewed” online business and wholesale model. Acom said it wants to increase the reach of all the acquired brands while expanding into other retailers and growing exports. ... “This is about building a simpler, more connected business that allows customers to access these brands through a local independent store, another trusted retail partner or online,” Ainsworth added. ... “The strength of Acom gives the portfolio the stability and platform it needs for its next chapter. We can protect nearly 50 years of heritage while building a business designed to grow these brands and support customers, stores, retailers and suppliers for the long term.”"
COLES,,,,Exa,Lactalis CEO Emmanuel Besnier says Australia big in Asia plan,www.afr.com (Andrew Tillett),2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-02 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.afr.com/world/europe/australia-is-a-big-country-for-us-lactalis-ceo-unveils-asia-plan-20260731-p60khj,"Lactalis CEO Emmanuel Besnier says Australia big in Asia plan ... Paris | The boss of the world’s biggest dairy company says its acquisition of Fonterra’s Mainland brands has transformed Australia into one of its “big five” regional beachheads as it pushes into South-East Asia. ... In his first interview with an English-language media outlet, Emmanuel Besnier, chief executive and controlling shareholder of Lactalis, offered a rare insight into the strategy behind a series of acquisitions in Australia and New Zealand."
COLES,,,,Exa,Victoria Secret Co Surges In Global Coverage - Fashionide,fashionide.com (Fashionide Team),2026-08-01T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-01 00:00:00+00:00,,https://fashionide.com/designers-brands/victoria-secret-co-surges-in-global-coverage/,"# Victoria Secret Co Surges ... Global Coverage Victoria’s Secret experiences a notable increase in international media mentions, with 23 reports within a recent window, signaling heightened global interest. Published on 01 August 2026 ... Victoria’s Secret has seen a surge in global media coverage, with 23 mentions recorded recently, reflecting increased international attention. This development highlights the brand’s renewed relevance and strategic shifts. ✔ ... etted by the fashionide.com team ... Victoria’s Secret has experienced a significant increase in global media mentions, with 23 reports recorded within a recent reporting window. This surge indicates a renewed international focus on the brand, which has implications for its market positioning and public perception. ... According to data from the GDELT Project, Victoria’s Secret has been mentioned 23 times in global media outlets during the latest monitoring period. This number represents a notable rise compared to previous baseline levels, suggesting increased media interest. The surge comes amid ongoing efforts by the company to revamp its brand image and expand its international presence. While specific reasons for the coverage increase are not fully detailed, analysts suggest it may relate to recent marketing campaigns, leadership changes, or strategic initiatives aimed at broadening its global footprint. The brand’s spokesperson declined to comment on the media attention, emphasizing instead its focus on upcoming product launches and global expansion plans. At ... The surge in media coverage signals heightened global interest in Victoria’s Secret, which could translate into increased brand awareness and market opportunities. It may also reflect shifts in public perception as the company attempts to reposition itself in a competitive retail landscape. For investors and industry watchers, this increase in coverage could indicate a strategic pivot or a response to recent brand revitalization efforts, potentially impacting its sales and international growth trajectory. ... Victoria’s Secret has historically been a dominant player in the lingerie and apparel market but faced challenges related to brand perception and competition in recent years. Over the past 12 months, the company has launched new marketing campaigns, diversified its product lines, and appointed new leadership to modernize its image. The recent media surge, with 23 mentions, appears linked to these efforts, although specific media outlets or campaigns driving the coverage have not been identified. The company has also announced plans for increased international expansion, which may be drawing more global media attention. Prior to this surge, coverage levels were relatively stable, indicating this is a notable shift. ... > “We are focused on launching innovative products and expanding our global reach. The recent media interest reflects growing awareness of our brand evolution.” > > — Victoria’s Secret spokesperson ... It is not yet confirmed what specific events or campaigns triggered the surge in media mentions. Details about the particular outlets, campaigns, or strategic moves responsible for the increased coverage remain unclear. Analysts suggest possible reasons, but no definitive link has been established. ... Victoria’s Secret is expected to continue its media outreach and promotional activities, which may sustain or further increase coverage. Industry observers will watch for official announcements regarding new campaigns, product launches, or expansion plans that could explain or amplify this media attention. The company may also face increased scrutiny as it seeks to redefine its brand image globally. ... The specific causes are not confirmed, but it is likely related to recent marketing efforts, leadership changes, or international expansion strategies. ... , 23 ... represent a notable increase, indicating heightened global interest, ... While increased coverage can boost brand visibility, its significance depends on the content and tone of the reports. The company is actively working to improve its image and market reach. ... It is uncertain. Media coverage can influence consumer perception and sales, but tangible results depend on subsequent marketing and product strategies."
COLES,,,,Exa,TPG left holding the leash after Coles ditches Greencross buyout,www.afr.com (Carrie LaFrenz),2026-07-29T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-29 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.afr.com/companies/retail/denied-a-billion-dollar-plus-exit-tpg-works-on-plan-b-for-greencross-20260720-p60gtq,"TPG left holding the leash after Coles ditches Greencross buyout ... For the past three years, supermarket giant Coles watched from the sidelines with a serious case of FOMO. It wasn’t enough to just sell cans of pet food and rubber squeak-toys in the supermarket aisle – not when Australians were treating their pets like family and spending up big. ... Across the country, two-thirds of households now share their homes with a furry or feathered family member, opening their wallets for premium treats and accessories and doggy day care – even in a crippling cost-of-living crunch."
COLES,,,,Exa,Coles shuts store on crime-stricken Melbourne CBD street - Inside Retail Australia,insideretail.com.au (Harry Booth),2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-27 00:00:00+00:00,,https://insideretail.com.au/sectors/supermarkets/coles-shuts-store-on-crime-stricken-melbourne-cbd-street-202607,"Coles shuts store on crime-stricken Melbourne CBD street - Inside Retail Australia ... Supermarket giant Coles is planning to shut a store on Elizabeth Street in Melbourne, a location notorious for its challenges with crime and antisocial behaviour. ... The street – which has once again become the focus of outcry after witnessing two separate machete attack incidents over the weekend – sits opposite Melbourne’s Flinders Street station. The Coles Central store on it is just a few doors down from a Woolworths Metro. ... A company spokesperson told The Daily Telegraph that the decision to exit at the end of its lease in 2027 is strictly commercial. The move would see the supermarket operate just one site in the CBD, at Melbourne Central. ... Coles recently announced the extension of its partnership with Crime Stoppers Victoria to combat retail crime. “Across the retail sector, rising incidents of theft, abuse, and aggression are creating increasingly challenging environments for frontline workers,” a supermarket spokesperson said in a statement. ... Martin Smithson, Coles’ GM of supermarket operations, added: “The growth in retail violence is absolutely unacceptable, and our partnership with Crimestoppers is just one of the ways we’re addressing it. Industry, retailers, government, police, we all need to work together.” ... Victoria, especially Melbourne, has been at the centre of Australia’s growing retail crime problem. In 2025, 95,181 criminal incidents in retail locations were recorded in the state, a 25.7 per cent increase over the past decade. ... “The trend is clear. Retail crime unfortunately continues to escalate in Victoria. It is persistent, it is widespread, and it continues to put frontline workers and customers at risk,” said the Australian Retail Council’s CEO, Chris Rodwell."
COLES,,,,Exa,"‘Deeply disturbing’: Expert says Leggo’s, Coles and Aldi have a ‘major problem’ after shock find in tomato products | news.com.au — Australia’s leading news site for latest headlines",www.news.com.au,2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-27 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.news.com.au/finance/business/retail/deeply-disturbing-expert-says-leggos-coles-and-aldi-have-a-major-problem-after-shock-find-in-tomato-products/news-story/ffd624ef057203282768068ee7ba38e6,"‘Deeply disturbing’: Expert says Leggo’s, Coles and Aldi have a ‘major problem’ after shock find in tomato products | news.com.au — Australia’s leading news site for latest headlines ... # ‘Deeply disturbing’: Expert says Leggo’s, Coles and Aldi have a ‘major problem’ after shock find in tomato products ... Two supermarkets and one of Australia’s biggest tomato brands have a “major problem”, after a “disturbing” discovery was made. ... Two supermarkets and one of Australia’s biggest tomato brands have a “major problem” on their hands, after a “disturbing” discovery was made about their products. ... Coles Italian tomato paste also failed the testing, along with Aldi’s home brand Remano paste. ... The ABC reports that 22 per cent of the products failed country-of-origin testing, while a further 6 per cent were flagged for further testing. Another product that failed was Rosso Gargano passata, available in Coles. ... Last week the Trump administration slapped a 12.5 per cent tariff on Australian goods, citing Australia’s failure to effectively ban forced-labour imports. ... Well-known tomato brands that passed country-of-origin testing include Mutti, SPC, Woolworths, Providore D’Italia and Annalisa. Diced tomato cans and passata from Leggo’s and Coles also passed. ... In statements sent to news.com.au, US food giant Simplot (which owns Leggo’s), Aldi and Coles have all rejected the findings in the report by calling into question the testing method. ... , a consumer trust expert at Macquarie University, said the brands highlighted in the report will now face a “major backlash” as customers will be “deeply disturbed” by ... findings, particularly ... the links to forced labour in China ... findings, testing questioned ... Simplot, Aldi and Coles have all rejected the findings made in the Four Corners investigation. ... Leggo’s owner Simplot said in a statement it takes product integrity, provenance and responsible sourcing “extremely seriously”. ... “Any impact on the reputation of the Leggo’s brand arising from being named in this program is a matter of significant concern to us,” a spokesman said ... in relation to its upcoming Four Corners program regarding our supply chain and ... tomato paste products ... were provided by the ... who maintains sophisticated ... is proud to support ... Coles and Aldi also rejected the test results, saying they backed their suppliers. ... “Coles takes food integrity, food labelling and the protection of customers extremely seriously. ... Coles has no evidence that Chinese tomatoes, including tomatoes from Xinjiang, were used in the Coles Italian Tomato Paste products identified by Four Corners,” a spokesman said. ... “We strongly reject any suggestion that Coles knowingly misled customers, engaged in food fraud or profited from food fraud. ... “We do not accept that the testing results presented by Four Corners establish that Coles Italian Tomato Paste contains tomatoes sourced from China or Xinjiang. ... “Coles has had a trusted relationship with the supplier in question for over 20 years. The product specification requires the tomatoes used in this product to be sourced exclusively from Italy, and the supplier has advised that the product is manufactured in accordance with that specification. ... “Our supplier has provided detailed batch-specific traceability records, production documentation and supporting evidence to confirm the Italian origin of the tomatoes used, supporting the confidence we have in the integrity of the product and its manufacturer. ... “As we take food integrity seriously and to confirm the provenance of our own brand tomato paste, we will be commissioning independent testing.” ... Aldi also issued a statement. ... “ALDI rejects the claims of Four Corners and Source Certain,” a spokesman said. “ALDI partners with reputable suppliers, who we require to have full evidence of traceability of both raw materials and production of these two products, which are sourced from Italy and Australia. ... “Our suppliers have assured ALDI that the batch numbers provided contain tomatoes that are sourced from these locations.” ... The importer of Rosso Gargano also rejected the findings and said it had access to detailed traceability data."
COLES,,,,Exa,Coles to vacate Melbourne CBD store as retail crime surges | news.com.au — Australia’s leading news site for latest headlines,"www.news.com.au (Harrison Christian less than 2 min read July 23, 2026 - 10:43AM)",2026-07-23T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-23 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.news.com.au/finance/business/retail/coles-to-vacate-melbourne-cbd-store-as-retail-crime-surges/news-story/b84c3bc4a4091693d1b049b138782528,"Coles to vacate Melbourne CBD store as retail crime surges | news.com.au — Australia’s leading news site for latest headlines ... # Coles to vacate Melbourne CBD store as retail crime surges ... A supermarket giant will vacate a store in one Aussie CBD after a surge in theft and violent assaults against retail workers. ... Coles will shut down a store in Melbourne’s CBD as Victoria is branded a “national hotspot” for surging theft and violent assaults against retail workers. The Elizabeth St store will pack up at the end of its lease in mid-2027, the supermarket giant confirmed to The Herald Sun, maintaining it was a commercial decision. The property is located on one of Melbourne’s busiest CBD thoroughfares and has been listed for lease on commercial property sites. A Coles spokesperson said the company was “constantly evaluating our store network to ensure we are delivering the best shopping experience for our customers”. ... “As part of this process, we are currently exploring opportunities for a potential new supermarket location in Melbourne’s CBD,” the spokesperson said. ... “Our Elizabeth St supermarket will continue to trade as normal until mid-2027. It comes after Coles released shocking footage showing a worker being attacked. Picture: Supplied ... An Australian Retail Council (ARC) spokesperson said retail crime was at elevated levels across the country but Victoria remained the “national hotspot”. “Many retailers are repeatedly targeted and simply can’t sustain the ongoing impact on their teams, their stores or their products,” the spokesperson said. ... It comes after Coles released shocking CCTV footage in May showing a staff member being attacked and dragged across the floor by her hair, along with thieves leaving with trolleys full of food."
COLES,,,,Exa,Electric Bidet Seats Market to Reach USD 394.2 Million by 2032 (9.2% CAGR) – Big News,www.bignews.uk,2026-07-23T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-23 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.bignews.uk/business/electric-bidet-seats-market-to-reach-usd-394-2-million-by-2032-9-2-cagr,"As PW Consulting’s Senior Strategic Advisor and Lead Industry Analyst, I present a focused preview of our latest market study on electric bidet seats. The market is entering a decisive growth phase: our base year is 2025, the forecast window runs from 2026–2032, and the industry is projected to expand at a compound annual growth rate (CAGR) of 9.2% through the forecast period. By the end of the horizon the market is expected to approach the high hundreds of millions (USD), demonstrating clear runway for product innovation, channel expansion, and institutional adoption. This briefing highlights the strategic implications for corporations planning investments, partnerships, or go-to-market moves in 2026—while intentionally withholding granular segment tables to preserve the “preview” purpose and to encourage access to the full report for transaction‑level detail. Electric Bidet Seats Market ... Regulatory and reimbursement tailwinds: Recent policy moves—most notably Medicaid waiver programs increasingly recognizing electric bidet seats as durable medical equipment—create a runway into healthcare procurement channels (notably long‑term care and assisted living). Early institutional adopters report reductions in infection rates, driving evidence-based procurement decisions. Electric Bidet Seats Market ... Growth profile: The market posted steady expansion through the 2020–2025 period and is forecast to continue expanding at ~9.2% annually over 2026–2032. That pace implies meaningful scale opportunities for players that can execute on product differentiation, cost control, and distribution reach by 2026. ... Regulatory compliance and product positioning: GFCI outlet requirements and hygiene performance standards (e.g., UV/nozzle sterilization) are now market norms. Compliance is table stakes; differentiation will depend on user experience, integrated services (warranty, subscription maintenance), and demonstrable clinical outcomes for institutional buyers. ... benchmarks (instant ... , self‑ ... strategic planners, ... OTO’s ... engineering, global brand leverage, and deep ... particularly in regions where ... Kohler Co. — The January 2026 launch of the PureWash E930 with dual-wand, app connectivity, and voice control underscores Kohler’s strategy to blend design-led premium positioning with smart-home integration. Expect Kohler to push through both retail and specification channels (high-end residential and boutique commercial projects). ... Brondell Inc. — Recent distribution expansions indicate a channel-driven growth strategy. By broadening retailer availability and offering a range of Swash models, Brondell is pursuing volume growth through mainstream shelf presence and competitive product breadth. ... New product introductions and refreshed model lineups (early 2026) indicate that incumbents are accelerating product roadmaps to capture share among smart-home consumers and commercial specifiers alike. ... Distribution expansions suggest retailers and online marketplaces see sufficient demand elasticity to justify broader assortments—this will intensify pricing competition but also help normalize install costs through bundled promotions with plumbing and renovation services. ... Policy/coverage updates that explicitly recognize bidet seats as necessary medical equipment for certain populations can catalyze institutional procurement contracts and third-party reimbursement pathways—an attractive growth lever for vendors that can demonstrate clinical evidence and compliance. ... Actionable M&A and partnership screening criteria for bolt-on acquisitions, OEM relationships, and white‑label opportunities. ... Invest in compliance and clinical evidence: Vendors that can demonstrate reduced infection rates, validated safety compliance (GFCI and sterilization standards), and cost-savings in care settings will unlock institutional contracts and reimbursement channels. ... Manage cost-to-serve aggressively: Material sourcing strategies, installation partnerships, and bundled service offers (installation + extended warranty) will be decisive in protecting margins as competition intensifies. ... Choose channel strategy by cohort: Premium brands should double down on specification channels and design partnerships; value brands should expand big-box and e-commerce presence; D2C players should prioritize subscription and recurring-revenue services for aftermarket filters/wash cycles. ... Explore partnerships with healthcare integrators and O&P providers: Integration into durable medical equipment catalogs and hospital procurement frameworks requires both product certification and care pathway alignment. ... Fragmented standards: Divergent regional safety and hygiene regulations could raise compliance costs for global players. ... The electric bidet seat market in 2026 represents a compelling blend of growth and strategic complexity. With a clear CAGR trajectory and visible institutional adoption pathways, firms that act now to align product architectures, compliance programs, and go-to-market channels will secure advantaged positions for the rest of the decade. Our study provides the operational playbooks, financial models, and competitive maps to inform M&A diligence, product roadmaps, and channel investments."
COLES,,,,Exa,How the ACCC is using its new powers to penalise Coles - Inside FMCG,insidefmcg.com.au (Tahlia Whitfield),2026-07-22T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-22 00:00:00+00:00,,https://insidefmcg.com.au/2026/07/22/how-the-accc-is-using-its-new-powers-to-penalise-coles/,"How the ACCC is using its new powers to penalise Coles - Inside FMCG ... # How the ACCC is using its new powers to penalise Coles ... The regulator is examining both where supermarkets open and what they charge. (Source: Supplied) ... Coles and Woolworths are facing a new level of regulatory scrutiny. Since July 1, the ACCC has gained sweeping new powers to examine Australia’s largest supermarket chains. Under the excessive grocery pricing prohibition, it can scrutinise prices, revenue, quantities sold, product margins, operating costs and supplier rebates, then dig deeper when the figures raise questions. On the very same day the law came into effect, the regulator also blocked Coles’ proposed lease of a Ka ... Coles and Woolworths are facing a new level of regulatory scrutiny. Since July 1, the ACCC has gained sweeping new powers to examine Australia’s largest supermarket chains. Under the excessive grocery pricing prohibition, it can scrutinise prices, revenue, quantities sold, product margins, operating costs and supplier rebates, then dig deeper when the figures raise questions. On the very same day the law came into effect, the regulator also blocked Coles’ proposed lease of a Kalgoorlie site for a new supermarket and Liquorland. Together, the decisions signal an ACCC increasingly willing to scrutinise supermarkets from site acquisition through to shelf pricing. The pricing prohibition applies to supermarket retailers earning more than $30 billion annually in Australia, which currently consists of the archetypal supermarket duopoly – Coles and Woolworths. A grocery price may breach the rule when it is significantly excessive compared with the cost of supplying that product plus a reasonable margin. However, there is no automatic percentage at which a price becomes unlawful. Suzanne Howari, a special counsel at Holding Redlich with a remit in Corporate and Commercial Law, says the regulator is taking “a more active role in overseeing both market structure and market conduct in the supermarket sector”. The ACCC may also consider supplier costs, freight, wages, rent and other legitimate expenses. Rises can therefore be commercially justified, but the problem begins when the retailer’s numbers struggle to explain the distance between cost and checkout. That dual interest was evident in Kalgoorlie, a small mining town of about 30,000 people, where Coles proposed a 2800-square-metre full-line supermarket and liquor store. It became the first acquisition prohibited under Australia’s new mandatory merger regime, which requires businesses to notify the ACCC of qualifying acquisitions and wait for clearance before proceeding. Upon the decision, ACCC Deputy Chair Mick Keogh noted the significance of independent supermarkets and their role as an important competitive constraint on the major supermarket chains. “They provide consumers with meaningful choice, competition on service, quality and range, and competition on price for some products.” Competition advice should be the first priorityFor Howari, the case highlights the importance of competition advice towards the beginning of the development process, besides rent, planning, construction costs and projected sales. “Ideally, before sites are secured and key commercial terms are agreed,” she said. The risk is especially pronounced in regional markets, where a single opening can divert a sizable share of household grocery spending. As Howari explained, competition law cannot be treated as a regulatory issue to be addressed shortly before completion.The ACCC’s new reach also extends to promotions; in its guidance, the regulator imagines a soft drink that regularly alternates between full price and 20 per cent off. A supermarket then doubles the full price and advertises a 30 per cent discount, which leaves customers paying far more than before. The watchdog can examine the entire pricing history, including supplier rebates, allowances and promotional funding behind the “sale”. It may also compare prices across regions and investigate whether higher charges in areas with fewer competitors genuinely reflect greater supply costs. Coles and Woolworths must retain relevant pricing information for at least three years, while false or misleading responses to compulsory information requests can create further legal trouble. The modern supermarket may still live by the weekly special, but the ACCC now wants to see how it works.Regulatory scrutiny can begin with the land, continue through the lease and follow a product all the way to the checkout basket. Developers may require a competitive analysis before securing an anchor tenant, and subsequently, pricing teams will need records that stand the test of time. As Howari explains, businesses that can be proactive by identifying and managing competition risks early will be far better positioned to avoid lengthy investigations. “Strengthen governance, competition law compliance and internal risk assessment processes, particularly in areas that have become ACCC enforcement priorities,” she said. Kalgoorlie proved the ACCC can prevent a supermarket from opening, and the pricing prohibition will test how closely it can examine one already trading.Further reading: When ‘Down Down doesn’t add up: How the ACCC caught Coles."
COLES,,,,Exa,Coles dumps $4bn merger move,www.realcommercial.com.au (James MacSmith),2026-07-20T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-20 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.realcommercial.com.au/news/coles-dumps-4bn-merger-move,"Coles dumps $4bn merger move ... # Coles dumps $4bn merger move ... Coles has turned its back on a mooted $4bn merger. ... Coles has turned its back on a mooted $4bn merger that would have given it a foothold in one of Australia’s fastest growing industries. ... In a surprise move, the supermarket giant has reneged on a $4bn deal to buy the owner of Petbarn, Greencross. ... Coles has initially indicated its interest in bringing Greencross, which runs veterinary chains across the country alongside well-known pet retailer Petbarn, which boasts around 150 Aussie stores. ... However Coles has now decided to turn its back on any potential merger or acquisition of the pet businesses. ... “Coles advises that it has ceased discussions with TPG Capital private equity group regarding the potential acquisition of Greencross Pet Wellness Company,” Coles announced in a statement. ... “Coles applies a disciplined approach to acquisitions, and as one of Australia’s leading retailers, regularly assesses strategic opportunities that may complement its existing business.” ... The supermarket retailer which has over 1862 stores across Australia, did not give a reason for the move. ... But it appears Coles has decided to stay away from the pet industry in Australia, an industry which is worth an estimated $21.3bn annually and has grown 35 per cent since 2022. ... Coles has operated its own pet care business before it made the decision to shut down Swaggle in March this year. ... And its initial interest in Petbarn was viewed as a counter to rival Woolworths’ purchase of a majority stake in Petstock in 2024 for $586m. ... Coles and TPG had been in discussions re Greencross for about nine months before the deal fell apart. ... “As one of Australia’s leading retailers, with a strong balance sheet and debt capacity, Coles regularly assesses strategic opportunities that may complement its existing business and create value for shareholders,” the supermarket had previously said in confirming the discussions. ... “Coles will only pursue an acquisition where it is satisfied that the transaction is strategically compelling and capable of delivering attractive shareholder returns.” ... TPG bought Greencross in 2019 for about $1 billion."
COLES,,,,Exa,Investors bark. Coles backpedals on ‘peak pooch’ price for $4b Greencross pet business,www.watoday.com.au (Elizabeth Knight),2026-07-20T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-20 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.watoday.com.au/business/companies/investors-bark-coles-backpedals-on-peak-pooch-price-for-pet-business-20260720-p60gux.html,Investors bark. Coles backpedals on ‘peak pooch’ price for $4b Greencross pet business ... It is difficult to deny that the growth in the provision of pet goods and services is a trend that retains some steam.
COLES,,,,Exa,Coles walks away from $4bn deal,au.finance.yahoo.com (Cameron Micallef Fri 17 July 2026 at 3:10 am GMT+2 2 min read),2026-07-17T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-17 00:00:00+00:00,,https://au.finance.yahoo.com/news/coles-walks-away-4bn-deal-003533794.html,"Coles walks away from $4bn deal ... # Coles walks away from $4bn deal ... Coles has announced it will not buy Petbarn. Picture ... NewsWire / Nicholas Eagar ... Supermarket giant Coles has walked away from a $4bn deal to buy Petbarn's owner Greencross. ... In an update before the market opened on Friday, Coles said it had ceased talks with private equity firm TPG Capital over a potential buyout of pets and vets business Greencross Pet Wellness Company. ... As part of the deal, Coles could have taken ownership of major Australian brands including Petbarn, City Farmers and Greencross Vets. ... Greencross operates 247 Petbarn stores across Australia, alongside 143 veterinary clinics and 28 speciality and emergency hospitals. ... Shares in Coles soared 4.90 per cent to $23.66 during early trading following the announcement. ... Coles originally announced talks with Greencross on July 1, but stated at the time ""there was no certainty that a transaction will proceed"". ... On Friday, Coles says the matter is closed with no further negotiations or due diligence to take place. ... Coles did not give a reason for ending the talks with Greencross. ... While figures were never publicly released, the deal was reportedly set to be worth $4bn including debt. ... But Coles hasn't completely closed the door to new opportunities in the future. ... ""Coles applies a disciplined approach to acquisitions, and as one of Australia's leading retailers, regularly assesses strategic opportunities that may complement its existing business,"" the statement said. ... The retail giant previously operated its own pet care business before it made the decision to shut down Swaggle in March this year. ... Coles' move into the pet industry was widely touted as a counter to major supermarket rival Woolworths' move who bought a majority stake in Petstock in 2024 for $586m. ... The market had a negative view of the deal, as Coles shares slumped 3.3 per cent on July 1 shortly after the potential deal was announced."
COLES,,,,Exa,Coles walks away from $4b-plus deal to acquire Greencross,www.businessnewsaustralia.com (Business News Australia),2026-07-17T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-17 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.businessnewsaustralia.com/articles/coles-walks-away-from-4b-plus-deal-to-acquire-petbarn-owner-greencross.html,"Coles walks away from $4b-plus deal to acquire Greencross ... # Coles walks away from $4b-plus deal to acquire Petbarn owner Greencross ... Supermarket giant Coles Group (ASX: COL) has abandoned plans to acquire pet care company Greencross Pet Wellness from private equity group TPG Capital, ending discussions around a deal reported to be worth more than $4 billion including debt. ... Coles this morning confirmed in an ASX announcement that it has ceased discussions with TPG regarding the potential acquisition. ... The brief statement indicates that Coles hit a stumbling block during its discussions which were first confirmed in a statement to the ASX on 1 July this year. ... ""Coles applies a disciplined approach to acquisitions, and as one of Australia’s leading retailers, regularly assesses strategic opportunities that may complement its existing business,"" says Coles today. ... Coles shares have eased since the company confirmed it was in discussions with TPG Capital which acquired Greencross in 2019 for $675 million. ... Greencross operates 247 Petbarn stores, 143 veterinary clinics and 28 specialty and emergency hospitals across Australia and when it was acquired by TPG Capital the deal was based on an enterprise value of close to $1 billion. ... The abandoned acquisition comes on the heels of the closure in April this year by Coles of its Swaggle online pet supplies business after just two years of operation. ... The push by Coles into the pet market with a potential Greencross deal follows supermarket rival Woolworths (ASX: WOW) acquiring a 55 per cent stake in PetStock for $586 million four years ago, a deal that gave it a significant foothold in the fast-growing pet care market. ... The deal was was approved by the Australian Competition and Consumer Commission on the condition that Petstock divest a portion of its store network. ... The Greencross deal would have represented a far larger bet on the sector, dwarfing Woolworths' PetStock investment and positioning Coles as the dominant supermarket-backed player in Australian pet retail. ... With Coles now stepping back, market analysts expect TPG to pursue potential alternative exit options for Greencross. ... The private equity group has previously flagged a potential ASX listing and in 2023 was reported to have pursued a sale to EBOS Group, a major player in the animal health and pet care industry. Both options failed to proceed. ... ## Coles walks away from $4b-plus deal to acquire Petbarn owner Greencross ... Coles walks away from $4b-plus deal to acquire Petbarn owner Greencross ... Supermarket giant Coles Group (ASX: COL) has abandoned plans to ac ... 1. Coles walks away from $4b-plus deal to acquire Petbarn owner Greencross"
COLES,,,,Exa,"Coles ends talks with TPG Capital over Greencross buy, shares jump | KELO-AM",kelo.com (Thomson Reuters),2026-07-17T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-17 00:00:00+00:00,,https://kelo.com/2026/07/16/coles-ends-talks-with-tpg-capital-over-greencross-buy-shares-jump/,"Coles ends talks with TPG Capital over Greencross buy, shares jump | KELO-AM ... # Coles ends talks with TPG Capital over Greencross buy, shares jump ... July 17 (Reuters) – Australia’s Coles Group said on Friday it had ended discussions with U.S. private equity firm TPG Capital over a potential acquisition ‌of Greencross Pet Wellness, sending its shares up as much as 5%. ... The announcement came weeks after Australia’s No. 2 grocer confirmed talks ‌were underway for the purchase ⁠of the pets and ⁠vets business, a disclosure that had sent its shares down more than 4%. ... “We believe this is at least partly due to the negative investor feedback surrounding the deal,” Citi analysts said in ⁠a note. ... Local media reports ⁠had said TPG was likely aiming for an A$4 billion ($2.80 billion) deal, the valuation it had planned to seek ‌for the business in a potential float. ... Brokerage Citi had several concerns about the deal, including the potential funding structure and ⁠a weakening outlook for the pet care industry. ... Coles shares rose as much as 5% to ⁠A$23.68 on ‌Friday and were on track ⁠for their biggest one-day gain since ‌early March, pushing the staples sub-index more ⁠than 2% higher."
