CHEP,,,,Exa,Cheech and Chong's Global Holding Company Supports Bipartisan Effort Toward Responsible Federal Hemp Regulation,www.accessnewswire.com (Cheech and Chong's Global Holding Company),2026-07-28T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-28 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.accessnewswire.com/newsroom/en/consumer-and-retail-products/cheech-and-chongs-global-holding-company-supports-bipartisan-effort-to-1196948,"Cheech and Chong's Global Holding Company Supports Bipartisan Effort Toward Responsible Federal Hemp Regulation ... # Cheech and Chong's Global Holding Company Supports Bipartisan Effort Toward Responsible Federal Hemp Regulation ... LOS ANGELES, CA / ACCESS Newswire/ July 28, 2026 / Cheech and Chong's Global Holding Company is encouraged by new bipartisan efforts to establish a responsible federal framework for America's hemp industry, creating renewed optimism around an approach that protects consumers while supporting the businesses, jobs, farmers, and communities that have helped build the industry. ... Introduced by U.S. Representatives Angie Craig (D-Minn.) and Andy Barr (R-Ky.), the Lawful Hemp Protection Act seeks to establish a federal regulatory structure for hemp products, including standards around product safety, testing, labeling, age restrictions, manufacturing, and responsible commerce. ... For Cheech and Chong's Global Holding Co., the bipartisan effort offers a path beyond the extremes of an unregulated marketplace and outright prohibition, toward a responsible framework that protects consumers while allowing legitimate businesses to operate under clear, enforceable rules. ... Since the passage of the 2018 Farm Bill, hemp has evolved from an emerging market into an established part of the American economy. The hemp-derived cannabinoid market alone represents more than $28 billion in consumer demand, supporting hundreds of thousands of jobs across agriculture, manufacturing, distribution, retail, hospitality, and other sectors. That growth has been driven not only by industry innovation, but by Americans embracing new ways to enjoy and experience hemp-derived products. ... Cheech and Chong's Global Holding Co ... recognize both realities: ... to invest, innovate, employ people, and plan for ... The proposed framework comes at a critical moment for the industry, with significant changes to federal hemp policy currently scheduled to take effect in November. Industry organizations, farmers, businesses, and policymakers have increasingly called for a comprehensive federal framework that distinguishes responsible, compliant operators from bad actors while establishing consistent standards for the marketplace. ... Cheech and Chong's Global Holding Co. supports strong consumer protections, including appropriate age restrictions, product testing, transparent labeling, responsible marketing, and clear manufacturing standards-standards the company has implemented and advocated for since entering the hemp industry. The company believes thoughtful federal regulation can provide these safeguards without eliminating consumer access or dismantling the legitimate businesses that have developed under the existing legal framework ... ""Good regulation creates accountability, predictability, and trust,"" said Ted Whitney, Chief Beverage Officer at Cheech and Chong's Global Holding Co. ""The responsible operators in this industry are not asking for a marketplace without rules. They are asking for clear, workable rules that protect consumers, hold bad actors accountable, and give compliant businesses the certainty they need to operate responsibly. Prohibition doesn't eliminate consumer demand; it risks pushing consumers toward an illicit market where the very safeguards we all want-testing, transparency, and accountability-are absent. This bipartisan framework offers an opportunity to move in the opposite direction: toward responsible regulation, higher safety standards, and greater consumer protection."" ... As the legislation moves forward, Cheech and Chong's Global Holding Co. believes continued collaboration among policymakers, regulators, farmers, industry leaders, public-health stakeholders, and consumers can help create a lasting framework that prioritizes safety while preserving access, consumer choice, and economic opportunity. ... ""This is bigger than ... one company or any one product category,"" added Harshbarger. ... There are a lot of ... counting on us to get ... industry to move ... that we can do ... Cheech and Chong's Global Holding Co. looks forward to continued collaboration with policymakers, industry organizations, and stakeholders to advance responsible federal hemp regulation that protects consumers, supports American businesses and farmers, and creates long-term certainty for the industry. ... Cheech and Chong's Global Holding Company is the parent company and master licensor behind a growing portfolio spanning CHEECH & CHONG brand licensing, hemp-derived consumer products, regulated cannabis ventures, and retail experiences, rooted in more than 50 years of advocacy, culture, and education. Built on the legacy of Cheech Marin and Tommy Chong, as the world's premier heritage cannabis brand, the company works with partners across multiple industries to expand responsible access, support consumer choice, and create products and experiences that bring people together."
CHEP,,,,Exa,Champion Breweries' revenue more than doubles after Bullet acquisition - Businessday NG,businessday.ng (Chinwe Michael),2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-27 00:00:00+00:00,,https://businessday.ng/companies/article/champion-breweries-revenue-more-than-doubles-after-bullet-acquisition/,"Champion Breweries' revenue more than doubles after Bullet acquisition - Businessday NG ... # Champion Breweries’ revenue more than doubles after Bullet acquisition ... Champion Breweries Plc has delivered the strongest financial performance in its 52-year history, with first-half revenue more than doubling after the brewer’s acquisition of the Bullet energy drink and ready-to-drink (RTD) alcoholic beverage portfolio transformed it from a regional Nigerian brewer into a pan-African beverage company with euro-denominated earnings. ... The Uyo-based brewer reported group revenue of N35.73 billion for the six months ended June 30, 2026, representing a 124.2 percent increase from N15.93 billion recorded in the corresponding period of 2025. Profit after tax rose by 15.6 percent to N2.65 billion from N2.29 billion a year earlier, according to the company’s unaudited half-year financial statements. ... The results are the first to fully reflect Champion Breweries’ consolidation of EnjoyBev B.V., the Netherlands-based holding company for the Bullet beverage portfolio, following the completion of its acquisition on February 26, 2026. ... The transaction marks one of the most ambitious overseas acquisitions undertaken by a Nigerian consumer goods company in recent years, giving Champion control of a portfolio distributed across 14 African markets while introducing hard-currency earnings into a business previously dependent almost entirely on the Nigerian market. ... Total assets expanded by 58.6 percent to N130.57 billion from N82.34 billion at the end of December 2025, while shareholders’ equity surged by an extraordinary 428 percent to ₦69.08 billion from N13.08 billion. ... The brewer successfully raised approximately N60 billion through a N15.9 billion rights issue and a N42 billion public offer, allowing it to finance the acquisition while simultaneously strengthening its balance sheet. ... As a result, total liabilities declined to N61.49 billion from N69.26 billion despite the sharp increase in assets, underscoring the company’s decision to pursue expansion without materially increasing financial leverage. ... The capital raising exercise also resolved a long-standing regulatory issue. ... Champion’s free float increased to 25.72 percent, valued at about N39.9 billion, from 16.96 percent previously, enabling the company to regain compliance with the Nigerian Exchange’s minimum listing requirements after previously being flagged for falling below the required threshold. ... Beyond the acquisition-driven jump in revenue, the brewer’s underlying operating performance also strengthened considerably. ... Gross profit increased by 59.3 percent to N13.14 billion, while operating profit rose by 59.9 percent to N6.17 billion. ... Net cash generated from operating activities climbed 286.7 percent to N8.45 billion compared with N2.19 billion recorded during the same period last year, indicating that earnings growth translated into stronger cash flows rather than remaining purely accounting gains. ... Quarterly performance showed an even faster pace of growth. Second-quarter revenue rose 186.8 percent year-on-year to N21.37 billion, while quarterly profit after tax increased 35.4 percent to N1.76 billion, reflecting almost three full months of contribution from the newly acquired Bullet business. ... most strategically significant development is the change in Champion’s earnings profile. ... With EnjoyBev generating revenue from European production and pan-African distribution, Champion now derives part of its income in euros, reducing its exposure to the naira depreciation that has significantly eroded earnings across much of Nigeria’s consumer goods sector over the past three years. ... The benefits were already visible in the half-year accounts. The group recorded a N1.38 billion foreign currency translation gain within other comprehensive income alongside a N182 million net foreign exchange gain under finance income, highlighting how foreign currency exposure has become an earnings advantage rather than a liability. ... The acquisition structure also allows Champion to retain strategic control while preserving operational continuity. ... Champion owns 80 percent of EnjoyBev B.V., while Belgian company Vinar N.V., the long-standing manufacturing partner behind the Bullet brand, retains the remaining 20 percent stake. ... The group recognised N3.49 billion in goodwill arising from the acquisition and disclosed that no impairment indicators had been identified as of June 30. ... Finance costs rose sharply to N3.90 billion from N403.7 million in the corresponding period of last year, reflecting interest expenses associated with acquisition financing. ... Consequently, the standalone parent company reported an after-tax loss of N943.5 million compared with a N2.29 billion profit in the first half of 2025. ... Basic earnings per share declined to 16 kobo from 26 kobo after the company’s outstanding shares increased by 26.5 percent following the rights issue and public offer, diluting per-share earnings even as total profit increased. ... Management indicated that the second half of the year is expected to reflect the acquisition’s earnings potential more fully, as it will include six months of complete operational contribution from EnjoyBev. ... The brewer’s strategy also reflects a broader shift among Nigerian consumer goods manufacturers seeking geographic diversification and foreign currency earnings to reduce dependence on domestic demand, where consumers continue to struggle with elevated inflation, declining purchasing power, rising electricity tariffs, and higher logistics costs following the removal of petrol subsidies. ... By acquiring an established international beverage portfolio instead of relying solely on organic expansion, Champion has positioned itself among a small group of Nigerian listed consumer companies generating meaningful offshore revenue. ... Champion Breweries closed at N13.70 per share on June 30, 2026, giving the company a market capitalisation of approximately N155.1 billion based on its enlarged share capital of 11.32 billion shares. ... The stock has traded as high as N21.49 during the past 52 weeks, reflecting investor optimism surrounding the Bullet acquisition. ... EnjoyCorp Limited, which acquired a controlling interest in Champion Breweries in 2024, remains the company’s largest shareholder with a 64.08 percent stake, while Akwa Ibom Investment Corporation holds 10.15 percent"
CHEP,,,,Exa,ITE expands intermodal chassis unit with NACPC purchase - TT,www.ttnews.com (Keiron Greenhalgh),2026-07-22T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-22 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.ttnews.com/articles/ite-nacpc-acquisition,"ITE expands intermodal chassis unit with NACPC purchase - TT ... # ITE expands intermodal chassis unit with NACPC purchase ... ## Regional pool provider, OEM joins Milestone, Trend in chassis division ... NACPC offers regional pools of intermodal ... , allowing drayage companies to avoid owning their own equipment. ... NACPC) ... - ITE Management acquired North American Chassis Pool Cooperative for an undisclosed sum July 21, expanding its intermodal chassis portfolio. - NACPC has about 20,000 chassis, while ITE’s Milestone Chassis had around 36,000 when acquired in December. - NACPC CEO Darren Hawkins will lead Milestone, Trend and NACPC as ITE combines the three intermodal brands. ... Transportation infrastructure investment group ITE Management bought North American Chassis Pool Cooperative for an undisclosed sum, the firm said July 21, adding to a growing portfolio of assets in the buoyant intermodal segment of the freight market. ... NACPC offers regional pools of intermodal chassis, allowing drayage companies to avoid owning their own equipment. The supplier began manufacturing its own chassis after acquiring Pratt Industries Intermodal Chassis in December 2021. Formed in 2012, NACPC has about 20,000 chassis. ... “We see significant opportunity to create long-term value in the chassis market through focused expertise and operational excellence,” said Jennifer Polli, head of intermodal at ITE Management. “Scaling our chassis leasing platform strengthens our commitment to this essential segment and reinforces ITE’s core strategy.” ... ITE — which now owns and operates nearly $12 billion of infrastructure assets across rail, intermodal, air and other transportation verticals — bought chassis leasing specialist Milestone Chassis in December. ... Milestone Chassis had around 36,000 chassis when the deal closed. Milestone Equipment Holdings sold the division to focus on its core trailer and container leasing operations. Also, in 2023, ITE bought Trend Intermodal Chassis Leasing. ... “Milestone, Trend and NACPC have each built strong businesses with their own identities, but they’re united by a common approach: deep industry expertise, responsive service and a commitment to quality,” said Darren Hawkins, NACPC CEO. “Bringing these companies together creates a more powerful platform that expands our reach while continuing to deliver the reliable equipment and service our customers expect.” ... Hawkins will head the leadership team for all three ITE intermodal brands. The executive, who joined NACPC in 2024, was the second CEO of the chassis pool after former American Trucking Associations Chairman Dave Manning. He was previously at the helm of bankrupt less-than-truckload carrier Yellow Corp. when the nearly 100-year-old company filed for court protection."
CHEP,,,,Exa,"Logistics expansion: ITE adds 20,000 chassis to intermodal platform via NACPC deal - FreightWaves",www.freightwaves.com (Stuart Chirls),2026-07-21T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-21 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.freightwaves.com/news/logistics-expansion-ite-adds-20000-chassis-to-intermodal-platform-via-nacpc-deal,"Logistics expansion: ITE adds 20,000 chassis to intermodal platform via NACPC deal - FreightWaves ... Transportation asset manager ITE Management L.P. announced the acquisition of North American Chassis Pool Cooperative, expanding its premium chassis platform to national scale with broad market coverage for intermodal customers. ... Privately-held NACPC of Nashville operates 20,000 chassis serving major intermodal markets across North America. The deal brings New York-based ITE’s fleet to 60,000 units, which it said would strengthen its presence across key freight corridors and expand its range of leasing solutions. ... “Milestone, Trend, and NACPC have each built strong businesses with their own identities, but they’re united by a common approach: deep industry expertise, responsive service, and a commitment to quality,” said Darren Hawkins, chief executive of NACPC, in a release. “Bringing these companies together creates a more powerful platform that expands our reach while continuing to deliver the reliable equipment and service our customers expect.” ... The strategic consolidation gives ITE a roughly 30% share of the 200,000 chassis operated as part of cooperative pools; chassis availability has been a recurring bottleneck in drayage and port operations. NACPC gains a larger capital base and broader operating footprint; it has long pursued a core model of pooled, interoperable chassis operations to reduce cost and improve availability. NACPC in 2021 acquired chassis maker Pratt. ... West Coast chassis pools in Southern California are moving from a “pool of pools” model to a more fragmented structure as shipper seek reliable availability. The original Los Angeles-Long Beach pool has shrunk by nearly 70%, from 80,000 to approximately 25,000, according to published reports. ... ITE emphasized its fleet offers coast-to-coast coverage, and a broad range of solutions spanning term leases, daily rentals, chassis pools, and customized programs. All three businesses will initially continue to operate under their existing brands led by Hawkins. ... “We see significant opportunity to create long-term value in the chassis market through focused expertise and operational excellence,” said Jennifer Polli, head of Intermodal at ITE Management. “Scaling our chassis leasing platform strengthens our commitment to this essential segment and reinforces ITE’s core strategy.”"
CHEP,,,,Exa,Odyssey Acquires Majority Stake in Family-Owned TransPak,peprofessional.com,2026-07-17T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-17 00:00:00+00:00,,https://peprofessional.com/2026/07/odyssey-acquires-majority-stake-in-family-owned-transpak/,"Odyssey Acquires Majority Stake in Family-Owned TransPak ... # Odyssey Acquires Majority Stake in Family-Owned TransPak ... ## The majority investment marks the first outside capital for a business the Inch family has owned since 1952 ... TransPak designs and builds packaging, crating, and logistics programs for products that are complex, fragile, and difficult to ship, from semiconductor and data center hardware to aerospace, defense, and medical equipment. ... Odyssey Investment Partners has made a majority investment in TransPak, a Silicon Valley provider of packaging engineering, crating, testing, and supply chain services for fragile and high-value equipment. This is the first institutional capital taken on by the Inch family, which has owned the business for generations. ... TransPak designs and builds packaging, crating, and logistics programs for products that are complex, fragile, and difficult to ship, from semiconductor and data center hardware to aerospace, defense, and medical equipment. The company combines custom crate and container engineering, packaging design and testing, and coordinated global logistics so that sensitive equipment reaches its destination intact. ... The company’s work centers on engineered protective packaging validated through testing, paired with logistics management that spans an international footprint across North America, Asia, and Europe. That combination has made TransPak a partner to many of the world’s technology and cloud infrastructure companies as they move high-value hardware between manufacturing sites, data centers, and customers. ... TransPak was founded in 1952 and has spent more than seven decades helping customers transport hard-to-ship products, building a customer base weighted toward blue-chip technology accounts. TransPak, led by CEO Bert Inch, is headquartered in San Jose, California. ... The business has been owned by the Inch family for generations, most recently under Arlene Inch, former owner and chairwoman, and will continue to operate under Mr. Inch and the existing executive team following the majority investment by Odyssey. The transaction represents the company’s first institutional partnership in a history stretching back to 1952. ... Now in partnership with Odyssey, TransPak plans to accelerate spending on its global operations, manufacturing capabilities, engineering expertise, and technology infrastructure, and to expand geographically as demand for AI infrastructure, advanced semiconductors, and high-performance computing grows. Odyssey pointed to the company’s exposure to those end markets and its record with family businesses as central to the deal. ... “TransPak is an industry leader with specialized capabilities, deep blue-chip customer relationships and meaningful exposure to fast growing segments of the technology infrastructure landscape,” said Craig Staub, a senior managing principal at Odyssey. “The transaction builds on Odyssey’s decades of packaging industry experience and recent first-hand insights into the data center market, and we are excited to utilize our significant industry expertise and relationships to support the management team as they capitalize on a wide range of attractive growth opportunities.” ... Protective packaging is being reshaped by the same data center and semiconductor demand that drives TransPak’s core accounts. The U.S. protective packaging market was valued at roughly $38.4 billion in 2026 and is expected to grow at a compound annual rate of about 4.2%, according to Global Market Insights, with electronics shipments and anti-static cushioning cited as growth drivers. Demand for high-density semiconductor and advanced packaging tied to AI, high-performance computing, and networking is expanding at a faster clip, supporting specialized providers that move and protect fragile, high-value hardware. ... Odyssey is a New York-based private equity firm founded in 1997 that makes majority-controlled investments in the industrial and business services sectors using a buy, build, and integrate approach. The firm targets companies with EBITDA of roughly $20 million to $100 million and enterprise values of about $100 million to $1 billion. Odyssey is investing out of its $3.25 billion sixth fund which closed in 2020. ... and service that ... ir Industries Acquires"
CHEP,,,,Exa,RealPage Acquires Cherre To Build The Data Foundation For AI | Inman Real Estate News,www.inman.com,2026-07-17T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-17 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.inman.com/2026/07/17/realpage-acquires-cherre/,"RealPage Acquires Cherre To Build The Data Foundation For AI | Inman Real Estate News ... RealPage has completed its acquisition of Cherre, betting that real estate’s AI ambitions hinge on cleaner, governed data across the capital stack. ... RealPage has completed its acquisition of Cherre, a real estate data intelligence company trusted by institutional owners, investment managers and operators worldwide. The companies are framing the deal as the connective tissue between property-level operations and portfolio-level intelligence. ... “AI can transform real estate only if it understands real estate,” Dirk Wakeham, president and CEO of RealPage, said in a statement. “Cherre has built the kind of trusted, governed intelligence that institutional owners and asset managers depend on. Bringing that expertise into RealPage means every customer, whether they manage one property or a global portfolio, gets access to a stronger, more trustworthy foundation for their decisions.” ... Cherre founder and CEO L.D. Salmanson framed it similarly, arguing that the industry needed to move from reporting to reasoning and that joining RealPage would enable Cherre to bring that shift to the broader real asset ecosystem more quickly. ... “This is the gap now stalling AI across the industry,” the companies said in the press release. “Models cannot reason across data that does not agree on what it describes. The industry’s AI ambition is sound, but the data infrastructure beneath it was built to report on the past, not to support the decisions that come next.” ... RealPage’s strength has always been property-level operations, while Cherre’s client base skews toward owners, asset managers and lenders making fund-level decisions. ... The companies are billing the combination as unifying data, trust and infrastructure across the full real estate capital stack, spanning all asset classes. It’s a broader ambition than RealPage’s traditional focus on multifamily and single-family operations. ... Cherre customers get RealPage’s scale and global delivery capacity, including expanded engineering and deployment resources to move from data readiness to AI in production, while RealPage customers gain a layer built to bridge property-level data with portfolio- and fund-level context. ... Both companies emphasize continuity. Cherre says it will remain an open hub that works across any property management system or data source a customer already uses, under existing permissions and governance standards. ... RealPage has made dozens of acquisitions over the past two decades, but its strategy has shifted since Thoma Bravo took the company private in 2021, from bolting on property-management features to building out data intelligence and AI infrastructure. ... The 2025 acquisition of Rexera, an agentic AI platform for real estate transactions, was an early signal. Cherre extends that same trajectory. ... Platforms with property-level reach are buying up the data-layer companies that institutional capital already trusts, rather than building that trust from scratch. It’s the same logic driving consolidation elsewhere in real estate tech. Scale plus credibility is faster to acquire than to build. ... The deal also lands against the backdrop of RealPage’s ongoing antitrust exposure. The DOJ settled its algorithmic pricing case against RealPage in November 2025, barring the company from training its models on active-lease or forward-looking data from unaffiliated properties and requiring a court-appointed monitor with oversight authority for three years. ... The settlement doesn’t directly affect Cherre’s business, but it raises the stakes for how RealPage handles sensitive data going forward and puts extra weight on the “governed” and “trusted” language both companies used in Tuesday’s announcement."
CHEP,,,,Exa,"Kempower: ""The market is moving to large-scale rollout""",www.worldcargonews.com (Jasmina Ovcina Mandra),2026-07-16T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-16 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.worldcargonews.com/news/2026/07/kempower-the-market-is-moving-to-large-scale-rollout/,"Kempower: ""The market is moving to large-scale rollout"" ... # Kempower: “The market is moving to large-scale rollout” ... Battery-electric container handling equipment (­­BE-CHE) is approaching a commercial tipping point as operating costs fall and large-scale deployment accelerates across container terminals. ... Despite grid constraints and space limitations at some ports and terminals, the strong commercial case for electric CHE is now driving demand for high-capacity charging infrastructure. Finnish charging technology company Kempower is positioning ­itself at the centre of the transition. ... The company has completed around 80 projects in the port sector over the past couple of years. “Most of these are smaller installations, with customers interested in piloting a charger,” Jesse Makkonen, Head of Marine and Ports at Kempower, told WorldCargo News during an interview at TOC Europe in Hamburg. Makkonen said the market is now moving beyond pilot projects to large-scale rollout. ... A clear example is Kempower’s flagship project at DP World’s London Gateway in the UK, where the company has installed 11.2 MW of charging capacity across two phases. Phase I included eight Combined Charging System (CCS) dispensers rated at 350 kW each, while Phase II, recently completed, added 12 Megawatt Charging Systems (MCS) rated at 560 kW each. ... The charging infrastructure at DP World has so far delivered around 2.9 million kWh (2,900 MWh) of energy to eight Kalmar electric straddle carriers that are equipped with charging interfaces for CCS2. ... At London Gateway, Kempower worked with Kalmar to ensure the machines and chargers were aligned on communication protocols and MCS standards. At this stage in the industry’s electrification journey, equipment charging profiles vary across OEMs. This means every electrification project has different requirements and system design is an “­­ecosystem effort”, requiring coordination between charger suppliers, OEMs, terminal operators and engineering partners. ... , Makk ... noted that the market for automatic connection devices (ACDs) ... of standardisation ... some systems follow ... integrated relatively easily ... others are fully bespoke and require tailor ... made engineering for ... ­customer. ... is where analytics and real-time ... are becoming increasingly important, providing ... basis for operational decision-making. ... ’s charging management ... , can collect ... communication interfaces, giving ... visibility into charging behaviour, energy use and battery condition. The ... schedules, avoid unnecessary full ... Modern Kempower charging systems are designed for a service life of around 15 years, supported by scheduled maintenance. Deployment options include containerised charging units to avoid civil works and protect equipment from the harsh port environment. ... In Ghana, for example, ­Kempower, in partnership with UK-based ­Paterson Simons, has delivered a DC charging system at Meridian Port ­Services (MPS), the operator of Terminal 3 at the Port of Tema. The installation includes 1.8 MW of installed power and six CCS2 connectors able to provide maximum power for the fleet, ­including terminal tractors and empty container handlers. The power units have been separated from the charging plugs and are housed in protected, air-­conditioned environments, built to IP55 standards with corrosion-­resistant materials and active cooling. Initially grid-powered, the setup is also being positioned for the future integration of renewable energy sources such as solar. ... A similar approach is also being used at PSA Genova in Italy, where civil works constraints led to the deployment of a containerised solution integrating both substation and distribution equipment within a single footprint. ... While Europe has led the first wave of port equipment electrification, activity is now expanding into Africa, as well as North and Latin America. ... “This year, North America will be one of the most interesting markets,” Makkonen said. The EPA Clean Ports Programme funding is finally starting to roll out and generate orders for equipment and charging systems. Some grants include a Build America, Buy America (BABA) requirement, which Makkonen said Kempower is prepared to meet by producing equipment at a factory in North Carolina. ... During TOC Europe, Kempower signed a three-year framework agreement with APM Terminals to support the rollout of BE-CHE. The deal covers the supply of high-power charging systems to multiple terminals over the next three years, with pilot projects already underway in Morocco, Mexico and Peru to support the introduction of electric terminal vehicles. APM Terminals intends to place individual purchases in line with the company’s investment plans and operational needs."
CHEP,,,,Exa,RealPage Acquires Cherre in Major Proptech Deal – Commercial Observer,"commercialobserver.com (Philip Russo July 14, 2026 9:00 am)",2026-07-14T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-14 00:00:00+00:00,,https://commercialobserver.com/2026/07/realpage-cherre-acquisition/,"# RealPage Acquires Cherre in Major Proptech Deal ... ## Multifamily software and data analytics giant absorbs data intelligence platform for institutional investors, commercial operators and asset owners ###### ... By [Philip Russo](https://commercialobserver.com/author/philip-russo/) July 14, 20269:00 am ... Multifamily software and data analytics giant [**RealPage**](https://www.realpage.com/) announced Tuesday that it has acquired institutional portfolio intelligence firm [**Cherre**](https://cherre.com/). ... Richardson, Texas-based [RealPage](https://commercialobserver.com/company/realpage/) acquired [Cherre](https://commercialobserver.com/company/cherre/) in a cash and money deal, said **Dirk Wakeham**, president and CEO of RealPage. Wakeham declined to provide specific figures on the acquisition. ... Manhattan-based Cherre will retain its brand as part of RealPage, said **L.D. Salmanson**, co-founder and now the former CEO of Cherre. Salmanson will remain with Cherre as the company’s senior vice president, he said. ... “We are not going away — in fact, the very opposite,” Salmanson said of Cherre, a data platform that connects decision-makers to property investment, management and market information. “We will continue to grow the RealPage ecosystem and continue to grow with our clients. I&#8217;m very excited about that. My role essentially is the same, which is to help insurance companies in the world have access to governed trusted data to be able to power their agentic future. And now we have an extra continued mission together, which is to bring that across the entire capital stack, the entire ecosystem, and leverage the existing RealPage product line, which is very broad.” ... Founded in 2016, Cherre’s platform involves unifying and analyzing more than 4 billion entities and $4 trillion in real assets globally to create a vetted and compliant environment for institutional owners and investors. ... In acquiring Cherre, RealPage customers will gain access to a layer of intelligence built specifically to bridge property-level data with portfolio- and fund-level context, according to the announcement. ... the opportunity for the 1 plus ... here is pointing the Cherre technology ... unified data models and data, ... In becoming part of RealPage’s platform, Cherre customers will gain the scale, resources and global delivery capacity of one of the industry’s largest technology providers, including expanded engineering and deployment capabilities, the announcement added. That in turn will mean a faster, clearer path from data to AI. ... on that specific problem ... on as something that we ... would be a game-changer in ... able to grow at the scale ... we have. ... The immediate future for RealPage in acquiring Cherre is to provide a better data foundation for clients’ agentic solutions, said Wakeham. ... “Everybody’s announcing their AI agentic solution,” he explained. “When we talk to customers, it’s not the models that are the problem. It’s sometimes the change management, but it’s mostly that they don’t have accurate, reliable data that they can base these decisions on. So they point their decision support tool at a subpar and unreliably reconciled set of data. They can’t use it to make decisions. I think the immediate thing that we’re going to see is our customers are going to have a uniform source of truth that they can use to power their agentic solutions. We have these products as well, and we think having this capability is going to advance that very quickly. ... “The other thing we’re seeing is that we’re able to connect what has been largely focused on a single property asset performance. The way our products primarily work is ‘how did this property do?’ Now we are able to uplevel that, not only to the aggregate portfolio level but to the capital allocator level. That will allow clients to have global visibility into all their operators, which is a very unique feature set.”"
