GANDHAR OIL REFINERY,,,,Tavily,Gandhar Oil Share Price Falls After Q1 FY27 Results | Kalkine India,,,,,https://www.kalkine.co.in/article/news/gandhar-oil-refinery-india-limited-nse-gandhar-shares-slide-after-q1-fy27-results-despite-profit-growth,"Highlights Overview Gandhar Oil Refinery (India) Limited manufactures white oils, lubricants, liquid paraffin, petroleum jelly, transformer oils, and other specialty products for domestic and international markets. The company's shares declined more than 13% to ₹244.81 during Monday's trading session following the announcement of its Q1 FY27 financial results. Despite reporting higher profitability, the stock witnessed a sharp correction as investors evaluated the latest earnings after a strong rally in recent sessions. Fundamental View [...] Fundamental View For the quarter ended 30 June 2026, the company reported consolidated total income of ₹1,73,495.00 lakh. Profit before tax stood at ₹26,376.00 lakh, while net profit came in at ₹20,590.00 lakh. Earnings per share was reported at ₹19.65. Gandhar Oil continues to strengthen its presence across personal care, healthcare, automotive, industrial, and process oil segments. Going forward, investors are likely to monitor export demand"
IMPACT RETAIL,,,,Exa,Retail: Academy Sports Launches Retail Media Network | invidis,invidis.com (John Berkovich),2026-08-08T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-08 00:00:00+00:00,,https://invidis.com/news/2026/08/retail-academy-sports-launches-retail-media-network/,"Retail: Academy Sports Launches Retail Media Network | invidis ... Academy Sports + Outdoors has launched Academy Retail Media, an omnichannel advertising platform that connects in-store and digital campaigns (Image: Academy Sports+Outdoors) ... The U.S. sporting goods retailer is expanding its omnichannel strategy with a retail media platform that links in-store and online advertising to measurable sales results. August 8, 2026 by John Berkovich ... Academy Sports + Outdoors has launched Academy Retail Media (ARM), a new retail media network designed to help brands deliver targeted advertising across the retailer’s stores, website and digital channels while measuring the impact on both online and in-store sales. ... The platform combines Academy’s physical store network with its e-commerce business and first-party customer data to create advertising campaigns that can be tracked from media exposure through to purchase. According to the company, ARM supports objectives ranging from product launches and customer acquisition to sales growth and brand awareness. ... Academy said the network reaches more than 52 million verified customers through its more than 320 stores across 21 U.S. states, enabling brands to activate campaigns across digital and in-store touchpoints using purchase-based audience insights. The retailer also highlighted closed-loop measurement capabilities that connect advertising directly to sales performance. ... The launch is another sign of how quickly retail media continues to evolve. Rather than treating in-store displays as standalone advertising, retailers are now connecting digital signage with websites, mobile apps, and customer data to create measurable campaigns that follow shoppers from online browsing to in-store purchases."
IMPACT RETAIL,,,,Exa,BlissClub raises ₹160 crore from Singularity AMC to expand omnichannel retail and product innovation - The HinduBusinessLine,www.thehindubusinessline.com (BL Bengaluru Bureau),2026-08-07T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-07 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.thehindubusinessline.com/companies/blissclub-raises-160-crore-led-by-singularity-amc-to-accelerate-omnichannel-expansion/article71317048.ece,"BlissClub raises ₹160 crore from Singularity AMC to expand omnichannel retail and product innovation - The HinduBusinessLine ... # BlissClub raises ₹160 crore led by Singularity AMC to accelerate omnichannel expansion ... ## Fresh capital will fuel retail expansion, new product development, hiring and the brand’s next phase of omnichannel growth across India ... Founded in 2020, BlissClub reported a 47 per cent rise in FY25 revenue to ₹135.3 crore while reducing losses by 54 per cent to ₹20 crore, as it continues its transition into an omnichannel apparel brand. ... D2C athleisure brand BlissClub has raised ₹160 crore in a new funding round led by Singularity AMC, with founder Minu Margeret and her partner, Vidit Aatrey, also making significant personal investments in the round. ... Existing investors Elevation Capital and Eight Roads Ventures have doubled down on the company. The fresh capital will be used to accelerate product innovation, expand the company’s offline retail network, strengthen product development and hire for its next stage of growth. ... Founded in 2020, BlissClub has evolved into an omnichannel apparel business. Its revenue grew 47% year-on-year to ₹135.3 crore in FY25, while its loss narrowed 54% to ₹20 crore, according to data from market intelligence platform Tracxn. The company has also said revenue has grown more than 60% year-on-year consistently over the past two years. Its retail footprint has expanded to more than 40 stores, while it forayed into menswear this year as part of its category expansion strategy. ... The round comes as BlissClub seeks to build on its transition from a digital-first brand to a broader omnichannel player, with its founders and existing institutional investors increasing their commitment to the business."
IMPACT RETAIL,,,,Exa,"Electronics Mart Sees Record Q1 Profit Surge of 458% with 39% Revenue Growth, ETRetail",retail.economictimes.indiatimes.com (www.ETRetail.com),2026-08-07T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-07 00:00:00+00:00,,https://retail.economictimes.indiatimes.com/news/consumer-durables-and-information-technology/consumer-electronics/electronics-mart-q1-profit-jumps-over-four-fold-as-revenue-rises-39-per-cent/133032789,"Electronics Mart Sees Record Q1 Profit Surge of 458% with 39% Revenue Growth, ETRetail ... # Electronics Mart Q1 profit jumps over four-fold as revenue rises 39 per cent During the quarter, the company added four stores, taking its retail network to 227 stores across more than 100 cities. Large appliances contributed 48% of revenue, followed by mobile phones at 39 per cent and small appliances, IT products and other categories at 13 per cent. - ... Electronics Mart India (EMIL) reported its highest-ever quarterly performance in the first quarter of FY27, with revenue from operations rising 39% year-on-year to Rs 2,419 crore, driven by strong same-store sales growth and improving profitability across regions. Profit after tax surged 458% to Rs 121 crore, while EBITDA more than doubled to Rs 239 crore, with the EBITDA margin expanding to 9.9 per cent. Gross profit increased 65 per cent year-on-year to Rs 417 crore, supported by an improved product mix and operating leverage. Same-store sales growth (SSSG) stood at 34.2 per cent during the quarter. The South cluster posted 40 per cent revenue growth, while the North cluster recorded 29 per cent revenue growth and improved its EBITDA margin to 4.9 per cent as stores continued to mature. During the quarter, the company added four stores, taking its retail network to 227 stores across more than 100 cities. Large appliances contributed 48% of revenue, followed by mobile phones at 39 per cent and small appliances, IT products and other categories at 13 per cent. Electronics Mart said, ""Tt will continue its cluster-based expansion strategy and plans to enter West Bengal, while remaining focused on disciplined store expansion, working capital efficiency and enhancing customer experience through the remainder of FY27."" - Published ... , 2026 at 05:10 PM IST"
IMPACT RETAIL,,,,Exa,CCC Group to launch ShockPrice retail chain in Romania - Business Review,business-review.eu (Newsroom),2026-08-07T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-07 00:00:00+00:00,,https://business-review.eu/business/retail/ccc-group-to-launch-shockprice-retail-chain-in-romania-299264,"CCC Group to launch ShockPrice retail chain in Romania - Business Review ... # CCC Group to launch ShockPrice retail chain in Romania Newsroom 07/08/2026 | 12:29 Romania is part of the expansion plans of Polish retailer Modivo, part of the CCC Group, which is preparing to launch its new ShockPrice store brand after announcing the concept earlier this year. ... The company will open the first ShockPrice store in Poland this month and has also signed a lease for a 3,000 sqm space at the Agora shopping centre in Arad. Modivo is already recruiting a store manager for the Romanian location. ... ShockPrice is Modivo’s second off-price retail concept after HalfPrice, which is already present on the Romanian market. ... Modivo officials have said ShockPrice stores will primarily target smaller cities and retail parks, with the new format expected to have faster inventory turnover. The ... previously indicated that individual stores would generally cover between ... 1,200 and 2,500 sqm. ... Modivo announced this spring that it would increase its focus on the off-price segment through the launch of the new brand. ... The company expects around 65% of its retail space expansion this year to come from the off-price model. Under its strategy, Modivo plans to open around 100 off-price stores per year, with more than half located outside Poland. The group sees the format as relatively resilient to market downturns, macroeconomic volatility and changes in consumer purchasing power. ... The group’s overall sales in Romania declined slightly during the first three months of its current financial year, which began on February 1. HalfPrice was the exception, recording a 60% increase in Romanian sales, from around EUR 5 million in February-April 2025 to almost EUR 8 million in the same period of 2026, according to company financial data. CCC sales in Romania declined by 7.5% over the three-month period, while Modivo sales fell by 20%. Overall sales across the group’s Romanian operations decreased by 2%."
IMPACT RETAIL,,,,Exa,Ringy acquires UPGREAT to scale the Baltics' circular electronics infrastructure - ArcticStartup,arcticstartup.com,2026-08-07T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-07 00:00:00+00:00,,https://arcticstartup.com/ringy-acquires-upgreat/,"Ringy acquires UPGREAT to scale the Baltics' circular electronics infrastructure - ArcticStartup ... # Ringy acquires UPGREAT to scale the Baltics’ circular electronics infrastructure August 7, 2026 ... Ringy has acquired Baltic refurbished electronics retailer UPGREAT in a deal backed by an undisclosed funding round led by Trind VC, creating a larger player focused on expanding the region’s circular electronics market. Financial terms of both the acquisition and investment were not disclosed, but the combined company expects to generate €26 million in revenue in 2026. Founded in 2021, Estonia-based Ringy operates a recommerce platform that sources, evaluates and resells used electronics across nearly 40 markets, while UPGREAT is the Baltics’ largest refurbished electronics retailer with retail stores in Estonia and Latvia and in-house refurbishment capabilities. UPGREAT will continue operating under its existing brand and team, as the combined business plans to expand refurbishment capacity, broaden its product offering and strengthen trade-in, repair and resale infrastructure across the Baltics. Trind VC’s latest investment will support Ringy’s strategy to build an end-to-end recommerce platform covering the full lifecycle of electronic devices. ... “Circularity requires more than consumer demand. It needs convenient take-back channels, professional testing and repair, reliable warranties and efficient routes for devices to reach their next owners,” said Kristo Kraft, CEO of Ringy. “By joining forces with UPGREAT, we can strengthen that infrastructure across the Baltics and return significantly more electronics to productive use.” ... UPGREAT operates three stores—at Lasnamäe Centrum and T1 in Tallinn and at the SĀGA shopping centre in Riga. Its devices undergo comprehensive technical inspection by the company’s Apple-certified repair technicians and are sold with warranties of up to two years. ... “UPGREAT and Ringy share the same mission: to make refurbished electronics as natural a consumer choice as buying a pre-owned car,” said Kristjan Nemvalts, CEO of UPGREAT. “Together, we can offer customers a larger and more diverse selection of professionally inspected devices while creating a strong foundation for regional growth.” ... Refurbished electronics remain a single-digit share of the Baltic device market, according to Ringy’s estimates, leaving considerable room for growth. The combined company plans to expand its product selection, refurbishment capacity and regional reach while extending the useful life of more electronic devices. ... “Having seen the successful collaboration between UPGREAT and Ringy over the past several years, we’re excited to see the two companies join forces. Together, they’re uniquely positioned to cover the entire value chain of second-hand mobile electronics and accelerate the transition towards a more circular economy,” said Ivar Siimar, Partner at Trind VC. ... Trind VC has backed Ringy for several years. The new investment supports Ringy’s ambition to build a scalable recommerce platform covering the device lifecycle from trade-in and evaluation to repair, resale and reuse."
IMPACT RETAIL,,,,Exa,Amazon’s Rumored Buy of Omnivore Data Is Rattling the DTC World – Online Store News,onlinestorenews.com (Daniel Ortega),2026-08-07T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-07 00:00:00+00:00,,https://onlinestorenews.com/amazons-rumored-buy-of-omnivore-data-is-rattling-the-dtc-world/,"It started as a whisper at a closed-door dinner on the sidelines of eTail East in Boston last month. By the first week of August, it had become the most-discussed rumor in DTC Slack channels and agency group chats from Austin to Amsterdam: Amazon is allegedly in advanced conversations to acquire Omnivore Analytics, the Seattle-adjacent retail data platform that quietly powers the competitive intelligence stacks of several hundred mid-market merchants on Shopify, BigCommerce, and WooCommerce. ... Neither Amazon nor Omnivore Analytics has confirmed anything publicly. But sources close to the matter — including two agency principals who claim to have direct knowledge of the conversations — say a term sheet has reportedly changed hands and that an announcement could come as early as Q4 2026. Online Store News has not been able to independently verify the existence of a signed term sheet, and all details should be treated as unconfirmed at this stage. ... Omnivore Analytics isn’t a household name, but in merchant circles it carries serious weight. The platform aggregates anonymized SKU-level sell-through data, pricing signal feeds, and inventory velocity metrics from opted-in retailers across Shopify, WooCommerce, and Magento storefronts. Its client list reportedly includes several eight-figure DTC brands in the apparel, home goods, and consumer electronics categories. The company raised a $34 million Series B in late 2024 led by Andreessen Horowitz’s growth fund, according to Crunchbase. ... For Amazon, the alleged appeal is straightforward and, to independent merchants, deeply alarming: Omnivore’s data set would theoretically give the retail giant granular, near-real-time visibility into what competitor products are moving fastest off digital shelves before those trends become public knowledge. ... deal is real, it’s not a data acquisition — it’s a competitive moat play,” said Jason Greenfield, managing director at the ... Greenfield’s characterization is blunt, but it tracks with a broader pattern that analysts have flagged throughout 2026: Amazon’s increasing appetite for third-party data infrastructure that enhances its first-party retail and advertising businesses simultaneously. ... The reaction in merchant communities has ranged from skeptical to outright panicked, depending largely on how reliant a given operator is on competitive intelligence tooling. In several Discord servers dedicated to eight-figure Shopify operators, members have been openly discussing whether to pull their data from any third-party analytics vendor that doesn’t have an explicit non-sale data clause in its terms of service. ... - Several Shopify Plus merchants have reportedly begun auditing their data-sharing agreements with third-party analytics platforms. - At least two mid- market brands on BigCommerce have allegedly paused renewals with Omnivore pending clarification on the acquisition rumors. - Agency operators are fielding increased client questions about what SKU-level data is flowing through connected commerce tools. - Some WooCommerce store owners running their own data pipelines say they feel insulated, but admit they likely pipe into aggregated benchmarks that could be implicated. ... The anxiety isn’t entirely theoretical. Omnivore’s terms of service, as of its last public update in March 2026, include a clause permitting data use by acquirers in the event of a merger or acquisition — standard legal boilerplate that suddenly feels a lot less standard when the alleged acquirer is Amazon. ... Here is where the story gets genuinely complicated. Sources familiar with antitrust thinking in Washington say the FTC under Chair Andrew Ferguson has shown a willingness to scrutinize data acquisitions that don’t meet traditional revenue-threshold notification requirements. Omnivore’s revenue is reportedly in the $40–60 million annual range, which would likely put it below the Hart-Scott-Rodino filing threshold — meaning a deal could theoretically close without mandatory regulatory review. ... But that calculus may be changing. A bipartisan bill introduced in the Senate Commerce Committee in April 2026 — the Data Acquisition Transparency Act, or DATA Act — would expand merger notification requirements to cover acquisitions where the primary asset is a proprietary data set exceeding 50 million consumer or merchant records. Omnivore’s data set, if the platform’s own marketing claims are accurate, would almost certainly qualify. ... That sense of urgency may explain what multiple sources describe as an unusually compressed negotiation timeline — reportedly initiated by Amazon’s corporate development team in late June and allegedly accelerating through July. ... Independent of whether the Omnivore deal closes, the rumor itself appears to be accelerating a structural shift in how DTC merchants think about third-party data vendors. The conversation is no longer just about feature sets and pricing — it’s about data sovereignty and the downstream risk of a vendor exit. ... Several agency owners who spoke to Online Store News described advising clients to prioritize competitive intelligence platforms with explicit contractual protections against data sale to marketplace operators. A handful of Shopify-native tools — including Particl, which focuses on competitor benchmarking, and DataHawk, which skews toward Amazon-side analytics — have reportedly seen an uptick in inbound interest over the past three weeks that their teams have quietly attributed to the Omnivore chatter. ... - Particl CEO James Thompson has not commented publicly, but sources say the company’s sales team is actively fielding calls from merchants seeking Omnivore alternatives. - Numerator, the larger consumer intelligence firm backed by Vista Equity Partners, is reportedly watching the situation closely as a potential signal for its own product positioning. - At least one Shopify app developer building a competitive benchmarking tool told this publication they moved up their public launch timeline specifically in response to the rumors. ... Not everyone inside Amazon’s orbit is reportedly enthusiastic about the acquisition. Sources described as close to Amazon’s advertising business — which operates as a largely separate P&L and competes internally for executive attention — allegedly view the Omnivore acquisition as a distraction from more pressing priorities, including Amazon’s push to deepen its retail media network integrations with off-Amazon publishers. ... There is also said to be internal debate about whether aggregated merchant-level data from Omnivore would actually integrate cleanly with Amazon’s existing first-party retail intelligence infrastructure, or whether it would require a costly data harmonization effort that eats into the deal’s strategic rationale. ... One source characterized the internal dynamic as “a deal being pushed by the corporate development team that the actual business units haven’t fully signed off on” — a dynamic that, if accurate, could slow or ultimately derail any agreement. This characterization is entirely unconfirmed and should be treated with appropriate skepticism. ... Regardless of how the Omnivore situation resolves, the practical takeaway for store operators is clear: the data you share with third-party platforms carries M&A risk that your contracts may not adequately protect against. Several steps are worth taking now, independent of any single deal outcome. ... The broader story here isn’t really about Amazon or Omnivore specifically — it’s about the growing tension between the data-sharing economy that powers modern ecommerce tooling and the competitive realities of operating independently on platforms that share an ecosystem with the world’s largest retailer. That tension was always present. If the Omnivore rumors are accurate, it just became considerably harder to ignore."
IMPACT RETAIL,,,,Exa,Trent Q1 profit rises 22% to ₹518 crore as Zudio and Westside expansion boosts growth - The HinduBusinessLine,www.thehindubusinessline.com (BL Mumbai Bureau),2026-08-06T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-06 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.thehindubusinessline.com/companies/trent-q1-net-profit-rises-22-to-518-crore-as-zudio-westside-expansion-drives-growth/article71314092.ece,"Trent Q1 profit rises 22% to ₹518 crore as Zudio and Westside expansion boosts growth - The HinduBusinessLine # Trent Q1 net profit rises 22% to ₹518 crore as Zudio, Westside expansion drives growth ## Retail expansion, higher operating profitability and growing online sales supported the Tata Group retailer’s strong quarterly performance across key business segments. ... Trent, the Tata Group’s retail arm, reported a 22% year-on-year rise in June quarter net profit to ₹518 crore, while revenue grew 18% to ₹5,755 crore and EBITDA increased 33%. | Photo Credit: PRIYANSHU SINGH Trent, the retail arm of the Tata Group, reported that its net profit in the June quarter rose 22 per cent to ₹518 crore from ₹425 crore in the same period last year. ... Revenue increased 18 per cent to ₹5,755 crore (₹4,883 crore). EBITDA was up 33 per cent to ₹848 crore. ... The food and grocery business under Star gained traction, adding 5 new stores during the quarter. The overall count of stores under Star increased to 86 across 12 cities. ... Going ahead, the company will continue to invest in expanding its footprint, with a focus on building scale and strengthening its position across select markets, said Tata. ... Expansion of its Westside and Zudio brands beyond metropolitan cities helped Trent outweigh pressure from rising raw material costs. ... In Q1FY27, it opened 1 Westside and 22 Zudio stores (including 1 in the UAE) and consolidated 3 Zudio stores, expanding its presence to 9 new cities. As of June 30, the retailer’s store portfolio included 301 Westside, 982 Zudio (including seven stores in the UAE), and 29 stores across other lifestyle concepts. ... “We now operate a significant portfolio of over 1,300 ‘large-box’ fashion stores, with presence across 330 cities (including 3 in the UAE),” the company stated. ... Emerging categories, including beauty and personal care, innerwear, and footwear, accounted for over 21 per cent of its revenues. Westside online, together with its proposition on the Tata Neu platform, continues to witness traction and grow profitably. ... In Q1FY27, online revenues accounted for over 6 per cent of Westside’s revenue, the company said."
IMPACT RETAIL,,,,Exa,"Croma Unveils 'Edge' – A Digital-First Neighbourhood Retail Format in Mumbai, ETRetail",retail.economictimes.indiatimes.com (www.ETRetail.com),2026-08-06T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-06 00:00:00+00:00,,https://retail.economictimes.indiatimes.com/news/consumer-durables-and-information-technology/croma-launches-digital-first-neighbourhood-retail-format-edge-opens-first-store-in-mumbai/132998230,"Croma Unveils 'Edge' – A Digital-First Neighbourhood Retail Format in Mumbai, ETRetail ... # Croma launches digital-first neighbourhood retail format 'Edge'; opens first store in Mumbai The new format will focus on personal technology categories such as smartphones, wearables and accessories, while also providing customers access to Croma's broader product portfolio. The stores will offer services including financing, exchange, device setup and after-sales support, alongside in-store product guidance. - ... Croma, the electronics retail chain operated by Infiniti Retail, has launched Edge by Croma, a digital-first neighbourhood retail format aimed at strengthening its omnichannel strategy and expanding its presence in India's fast-growing personal technology market. The first Edge by Croma store has opened in Ghatkopar East, Mumbai, with the company planning to expand the format to other urban markets, including Bengaluru and Pune. The new format will focus on personal technology categories such as smartphones, wearables and accessories, while also providing customers access to Croma's broader product portfolio. The stores will offer services including financing, exchange, device setup and after-sales support, alongside in-store product guidance. Shibashish Roy, CEO & MD, Infiniti Retail (Croma) said, ""The initiative is designed to combine the convenience of neighbourhood retail with the brand's omnichannel capabilities as demand for personal technology continues to grow, driven by shorter device replacement cycles and changing consumer shopping preferences."" Operated by Infiniti Retail , Croma currently has more than 560 stores across over 200 cities in India. The company said the Edge format will play a key role in expanding its retail footprint while making its technology offerings and services more accessible to customers in urban neighbourhoods."
IMPACT RETAIL,,,,Exa,"Emami Agrotech Launches WeMe to Dominate India's Snacking Market with ₹750 Crore Investment, ETRetail",retail.economictimes.indiatimes.com (www.ETRetail.com),2026-08-06T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-06 00:00:00+00:00,,https://retail.economictimes.indiatimes.com/news/food-entertainment/personal-care-pet-supplies-liquor/emami-agrotech-enters-indias-snacking-war-with-weme-targets-rs-1000-crore-brand-with-rs-750-crore-investment-push/132995829,"Emami Agrotech Launches WeMe to Dominate India's Snacking Market with ₹750 Crore Investment, ETRetail ... # Emami Agrotech enters India’s snacking war with WeMe, targets Rs 1,000 crore brand with Rs 750 crore investment push ... Emami Agrotech Limited is launching WeMe, a digital-first snacking brand, with a substantial investment. The company plans to build a Rs 1,000 crore brand over the next seven years. WeMe enters the market with three initial categories and an aggressive innovation pipeline. The brand leverages quick commerce and social media for consumer engagement and feedback. Emami expects WeMe to create over 1,000 direct and 3,000 indirect jobs. ... Emami Agrotech Limited (EAL), the branded foods business of the Rs. 30,000 crore Emami Group, is making a major play in India’s fast-growing packaged snacking market with the launch of WeMe, a digital-first food brand backed by a planned investment of Rs 750 crore and an ambition to build a Rs 1,000 crore brand over the next five to seven years.As part of its long-term growth strategy, the company is also evaluating a dedicated manufacturing facility, which could entail an additional investment of around Rs 400 crore over the next two years to support scale-up across categories and markets.""WeMe is not just another product launch; it is a new platform for our foods business. We have committed nearly Rs 750 crore towards building the brand, and our objective is to create a Rs 1,000 crore snacking brand in the next five to seven years,"" Aditya V Agarwal, Director, Emami Group, told ETRetail. ... The investment will be deployed across manufacturing, brand building, marketing, distribution expansion and talent.Currently, Emami is following a hybrid model with third-party manufacturing and selective machinery investments. However, if the brand scales as planned, the company expects to invest around Rs 400 crore in its own plant and machinery over the next two years.The launch marks Emami Agrotech’s expansion beyond edible oils and pantry staples under its Healthy & Tasty portfolio into the high-growth snacking segment, driven by premiumisation, evolving consumption habits and the rise of quick commerce. ... WeMe has entered the market with three categories, namely, Choco Hazelnut Spread, Jhuri Aloo Bhaja and Potato Chips. However, the company is planning an aggressive innovation pipeline, with 27 SKUs already under the brand and another 22-25 SKUs expected to launch over the next three to four months.One of the key innovations is a portable chiplet-format Choco Hazelnut Spread, which the company claims is India’s first in the category. Priced at Rs 10 per sachet, the product is targeted at children and families and designed for everyday consumption with bread, rotis, pancakes, fruits and other foods. ... Unlike traditional FMCG launches that typically prioritise general trade, Emami is positioning WeMe as a digital-first brand, leveraging creators, social media communities and quick commerce to drive discovery and consumer engagement.The brand is currently available on Blinkit across Kolkata, Delhi NCR, Mumbai, Bengaluru and Ahmedabad, while offline distribution has initially been limited to Kolkata and parts of West Bengal. The company plans to expand into more markets based on consumer response.""We call it digital-first because consumers today discover products through conversations, creators and communities. Quick commerce enables us to reach consumers immediately while giving us real-time feedback to innovate faster,"" Agarwal said.Emami is also building a separate distribution network for WeMe rather than leveraging its existing edible oil infrastructure. The company is creating a dedicated sales team as snacking requires a wider retail footprint, including pan, B and C-class outlets.Emami expects WeMe to become a significant growth driver for its foods business while creating employment opportunities. The company estimates the initiative will generate over 1,000 direct and nearly 3,000 indirect jobs as it expands nationally."
IMPACT RETAIL,,,,Exa,BGF Retail also performed well in the aftermath of the cargo solidarity strike in the first half of .. - MK,www.mk.co.kr (변덕호),2026-08-06T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-06 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.mk.co.kr/en/economy/12119316,"also performed well in the aftermath of the cargo solidarity strike in the first half of .. ... Sales of KRW 2.4268 trillion and operating profit of KRW 84.9 billion Performance improvement despite the April Cargo Solidarity strike ""Hot weather and summer product sales increase"" 사진 확대 BGF Retail, which operates CU, announced on the 13th that it has opened its first store in Hawaii, the U.S., 'CU Downtown'. [Courtesy of BGF Retail] BGF Retail also performed well in the aftermath of the cargo solidarity strike in the first half of this year. Initially, a slowdown in second-quarter earnings was expected due to increased logistics costs and one-time costs, but sales recovery due to hot weather and high profit margins offset this. ... BGF Retail announced on the 6th that its consolidated operating profit in the second quarter of this year was 84.9 billion won, a 22.3% increase from the same period last year. During the same period, sales rose 6% and 7.6% to 2.4268 trillion won and net profit to 56.8 billion won, respectively. ... The cumulative sales in the first half of the year were 4.5472 trillion won and operating profit was 123 billion won, up 5.6% and 33.7%, respectively, from the same period last year. Its net profit rose 30.1% to 86.1 billion won. ... Earlier this year, the supply of fresh food and convenience food was temporarily disrupted by the blockade of the distribution center and strike issues of the Korea Confederation of Trade Unions' public transport union's cargo alliance headquarters, but both sales and operating profit increased. ... BGF Retail said, ""Friendly weather conditions such as fewer precipitation days and higher average temperature compared to the previous year, recovery of consumer sentiment, the effect of high oil price damage subsidies, and an increase in foreign tourists contributed to the main momentum of earnings growth."" ... On the other hand, demand for summer products increased as the weather got hotter quickly this year, and more consumers chose domestic travel rather than overseas travel due to the high exchange rate, which positively affected the expansion of sales. ... In line with the increase in foreign tourists, it also expanded the customer inflow route by introducing specialized tourism products for foreigners visiting Korea and overseas food service stores for foreigners living in Korea. ... As demand for summer products such as beverages and ice cream increased and 'Eating Dessert' and 'PBICK The Kitchen', its own brand (PB), became popular, it led to improved sales and profit margins. ... The operation of ""grocery specialized stores"" aimed at one or two-person households, specialized stores for foreign tourists, and overseas food ingredients operation stores for foreigners living in Korea also affected customer diversification. ... Increased sales of food, which is relatively more profitable than tobacco, also affected profitability improvement. The share of food and processed foods in total sales rose 0.4 percentage points and 0.9 percentage points, respectively, compared to the same period last year. As a result, the average product profit margin has also improved."
IMPACT RETAIL,,,,Exa,Flipkart's super.money Debuts SplitStore as Parliament Ends Free UPI Era,"www.techtimes.com (Collin Mercer Published: Aug 06 2026, 2:04 PM EDT)",2026-08-06T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-06 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.techtimes.com/articles/323386/20260806/flipkarts-supermoney-debuts-splitstore-parliament-ends-free-upi-era.htm,"Flipkart's fintech arm super.money on Thursday launched SplitStore, an in-app shopping marketplace that lets users purchase products in zero-interest installments without a credit card, marking the company's most consequential move to turn India's dominant payments infrastructure into a profitable credit business. The launch was confirmed by multiple outlets including Bloomberg, Storyboard18, and NewsBytesApp on August 6, 2026. ... The launch coincides with India's Parliament passing legislation to amend the Payment and Settlement Systems Act, creating the first legal framework since 2020 to allow possible merchant fees on Unified Payments Interface (UPI) transactions — ending a six-year-old zero-fee guarantee that had structured every Indian fintech's business model. Parliament passed the bill on August 6, the same day as SplitStore's launch, after its introduction in the Lok Sabha on August 4. Whether or not merchant fees ultimately return, super.money will now earn through credit — a bet that makes SplitStore's timing as strategic as its technology. ... Launched in June 2024 as a ""scan-and-pay"" UPI application, super.money has grown in under two years into India's fifth-largest UPI platform by transaction volume, surpassing CRED, WhatsApp Pay, Amazon Pay, and BHIM. The app climbed the UPI rankings by offering cashback of up to 5% on merchant transactions and targeting digitally savvy first-time earners. As of September 2025, the app processed more than 256 million UPI transactions worth ₹9,852 crore (approximately $1.03 billion) per month, with more than 15 million users. Flipkart invested $30 million more in a September 2025 funding round, following an initial $20 million at spinoff. ... Super.money was already generating roughly $3 million per month in revenue from secured credit cards, fixed deposits, and lending partnerships, per TechCrunch's October reporting— an annualized run rate of approximately $36 million. SplitStore is designed to add the credit-marketplace layer that turns a payments network into a full-stack lending platform. ... SplitStore's technical foundation arrived in February 2025 when super.money bought checkout platform BharatX, a Y Combinator-backed Bengaluru startup that had spent four years building documentation-free, credit-score-agnostic checkout financing. ... This is a deliberate structural choice. Super.money operates as a Technology Service Provider (TSP), not as an NBFC — it arranges credit through regulated partners rather than lending from its own balance sheet. That positioning keeps super.money outside the scope of direct lending regulation while still earning origination revenue, and it means the company can scale without absorbing credit risk directly. ""We are focused on product innovation in partnership with lenders, rather than taking on underwriting risk directly,"" Sikaria said. The TSP model allows super.money to benefit from BharatX's risk architecture without holding loans on its own books. ... The regulatory development that frames SplitStore's timing is the August 6 passage of India's Taxation and Other Laws (Amendment) Bill, 2026 through the Lok Sabha. Parliament removed the blanket MDR prohibition, replacing it with a framework giving the central government authority to exempt specific payment modes from MDR charges by executive order — meaning that for the first time since 2020, merchant fees on UPI are legally possible. The amendment does not impose fees immediately, but the structural guarantee is gone. ... A second shift is regulatory consolidation. The Reserve Bank of India's Digital Lending Directions issued in May 2025 effectively winnowed the standalone BNPL sector: direct lending now requires regulated-entity partners, loan disbursement must flow directly to the borrower, and lending service providers face strict compliance requirements. Several early BNPL pioneers— ZestMoney and LazyPay among them — have pivoted or exited under this tightened regime. What remains is precisely the architecture super.money has chosen: platform-embedded credit, operated as a TSP, with banks and NBFCs as the underwriting partners. ... ""The way we think about BNPL or EMIs is that it becomes an introductory product for a ... The Indian BNPL market is projected to reach$30.45 billion in 2026, up from $24.86 billion in 2025, and is forecast to reach $62.61 billion by 2031. But the competitive field has thinned and consolidated. ... Snapmint, the most direct structural parallel to SplitStore — UPI-first, no-credit-card-required, Tier 2 and Tier 3 focus — turned profitable in FY2025 with revenue reaching ₹158.5 crore(approximately $16.6 million) and then raised $125 million from General Atlantic in October 2025. Co-founder Nalin Agrawal projected a doubling of revenue in FY2026 — which means super.money is entering a market where proof of concept exists and the field is capitalized. ... Amazon moved faster on the institutional side: it completed the acquisition of Axio(formerly Capital Float) in September 2025 for a sum exceeding $150 million, giving Amazon Pay a direct NBFC lending license, an established 15-million-customer credit base, and a checkout financing capability it is integrating across its commerce ecosystem. ... Walmart-backed Flipkart has committed a total of$50 million to super.money since its spinoff — an initial $20 million at launch followed by an additional $30 million announced in September 2025. The company has publicly targeted$100 million annual revenue by 2026, with a lean team of approximately 130–150 people. ... SplitStore is the credit piece of a three-pillar architecture Sikaria has described repeatedly: savings (via superFD fixed deposits at competitive interest rates), credit (now via SplitStore's embedded buy-now-pay-later), and eventually stock trading — all sitting on top of UPI's instant payment rails. The fixed-deposit product establishes a liability relationship with the user; SplitStore establishes the credit relationship; stock trading would complete the wealth-management layer. Each product cross-sells the others, and each is designed to raise the lifetime value of a user the app acquired cheaply through a UPI cashback offer. ... India's largest cohort of new digital financial services users — the hundreds of millions who use UPI daily but have never swiped a credit card — now has a frictionless on-ramp to formal consumer credit inside an app they already open to pay for groceries. Whether super.money can profitably underwrite their installment purchases, at scale, under India's evolving regulatory framework, is the test that SplitStore's launch has just begun. ... India's Parliament passed legislation on August 6, 2026 creating the legal framework for the government to restore merchant fees on UPI transactions for large merchants by executive order — though no fees have been imposed yet. If fees return, platform-embedded fintech companies like super.money would likely benefit relative to standalone payment apps: they earn through credit products regardless of whether the payment layer generates transaction revenue, whereas pure payments apps lose the subsidy model with no alternative revenue. The fee debate accelerates the strategic logic behind every major Indian fintech's pivot toward credit, wealth management, and insurance. ... ZestMoney and LazyPay were standalone BNPL brands that operated without a captive payment distribution network. When the RBI tightened digital lending regulations in 2022 and further in 2025, they faced compliance burdens and cost structures without the customer acquisition advantage of an embedded UPI application. ZestMoney effectively exited the market; LazyPay pivoted its product mix. SplitStore enters a market that regulatory consolidation has cleared, with a distribution advantage — 15 million existing UPI users — that a standalone BNPL startup cannot replicate without building the payment app first."
IMPACT RETAIL,,,,Exa,"Reliance Retail Expands AI Capabilities with Furrl Acquisition, ETRetail",retail.economictimes.indiatimes.com (www.ETRetail.com),2026-08-05T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-05 00:00:00+00:00,,https://retail.economictimes.indiatimes.com/news/apparel-fashion/apparel/reliance-retail-acquires-ai-powered-fashion-discovery-platform-furrl/132903363,"Reliance Retail Expands AI Capabilities with Furrl Acquisition, ETRetail ... # Reliance Retail acquires AI-powered fashion discovery platform Furrl Following the acquisition, Furrl's technology will be integrated into Reliance Retail's ecosystem to serve a significantly larger consumer base. The startup's team will work on scaling its AI capabilities while contributing to Reliance Retail's broader commerce and artificial intelligence initiatives. - ETRetail Desk ... Reliance Retail has acquired Furrl, the AI-powered fashion discovery platform focused on Indian direct-to-consumer (D2C) brands, with the startup's entire team joining the retail major as part of the transaction. Founded four years ago, Furrl built a marketplace for independent fashion brands while developing AI-powered shopping tools aimed at improving fashion discovery. Among its key technologies is an AI styling engine that transforms catalogue images into complete, personalised and shoppable looks based on individual user preferences. Following the acquisition, Furrl's technology will be integrated into Reliance Retail's ecosystem to serve a significantly larger consumer base. The startup's team will work on scaling its AI capabilities while contributing to Reliance Retail's broader commerce and artificial intelligence initiatives. ... Esha Tiwary, founder of Furrl, said, ""The company began by solving the challenge of discovering unique Indian D2C fashion but gradually shifted its focus towards AI-led fashion discovery."" She added that discussions with Jeyandran Venugopal, CEO, Reliance Retail, reinforced the company's belief that its technology could create greater impact at scale. Tiwary has also taken on a new role at Reliance Retail, where she will lead high-impact AI initiatives focused on enhancing consumer experiences across the company's commerce platforms. No financial details of the acquisition were disclosed. -"
IMPACT RETAIL,,,,Exa,"Croma launches Edge digital-first retail format, opens first neighbourhood store in Mumbai - The Times of India","timesofindia.indiatimes.com (TOI Tech Desk / TIMESOFINDIA.COM / Aug 05, 2026, 23:15 IST)",2026-08-05T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-05 00:00:00+00:00,,https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/technology/tech-news/croma-launches-edge-digital-first-retail-format-opens-first-neighbourhood-store-in-mumbai/articleshow/132930181.cms,"Croma launches Edge digital-first retail format, opens first neighbourhood store in Mumbai - The Times of India ... # Croma launches Edge digital-first retail format, opens first neighbourhood store in Mumbai ... Croma has launched Edge, a new digital-first retail format. The Tata Group electronics retailer has opened the first Edge by Croma neighbourhood store in Ghatkopar East, Mumbai, marking the next phase of its omnichannel expansion. The company said it plans to expand the format to other urban markets, including Bengaluru and Pune. Explaining the move, Shibashish Roy, CEO, Infiniti Retail Ltd, said, ""The way India shops for personal technology is changing. Consumers increasingly expect the speed and convenience of neighbourhood retail without compromising on trust, expertise or service. Edge by Croma has been built around this changing consumer behaviour. It extends our omnichannel strategy by bringing the Croma experience closer to where our customers live, while strengthening our leadership in one of the fastest-growing categories in consumer technology.""​ ... ​ The new retail format focuses on personal technology products and is designed to serve customers through neighbourhood stores. The outlets offer a curated range of smartphones, wearables, accessories and other personal technology products while also providing access to Croma's wider catalogue. Customers can also avail services, including financing, exchange, product setup, and after-sales support, at the stores. ... | Feature | Details | | --- | --- | | Retail format | Digital-first neighbourhood store | | First store location | Ghatkopar East, Mumbai | | Expansion plans | Bengaluru and Pune | | Product categories | Smartphones, wearables, accessories and personal technology | | Additional access | Wider Croma product portfolio | | Services | Financing, exchange, product setup and after-sales support | ... According to the company, the new format has been introduced as demand for personal technology products continues to grow, with consumers increasingly opting for neighbourhood shopping while expecting access to digital services and after-sales support. Edge by Croma combines neighbourhood retail with access to Croma's product portfolio and services through its omnichannel network. The company said the format will support its expansion in the personal technology segment while increasing its presence across urban markets.​"
IMPACT RETAIL,,,,Exa,Maceoo acquires WHSmith fashion division - Inside Retail US,insideretail.us (Kaycee Enerva),2026-08-05T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-05 00:00:00+00:00,,https://insideretail.us/maceoo-acquires-whsmith-fashion-division/,"Maceoo acquires WHSmith fashion division - Inside Retail US ... Luxury menswear brand Maceoo has acquired WHSmith’s fashion division, including 13 stores across Nevada, Florida, Washington, New Jersey, and New York. ... CEO and founder Mehdi Raad said the acquisition builds on a 16-year relationship with WHSmith, as the menswear brand expands into travel retail. ... “We’re thrilled for this next stage in our growth that further taps into international travel shoppers,” he said. ... Founded in 1792, WHSmith is a British retailer best known for its travel retail business, operating stores in airports, railway stations, hospitals, and motorway service areas worldwide, alongside its UK high street business. ... Raad said plans for the acquired stores are still being finalized, with some to remain as multi-brand retailers, while others will be converted into Maceoo boutiques. ... The stores will carry Maceoo’s full range of menswear, including leisurewear, dress shirts, tech shirts, polo shirts, blazers, jackets, sweaters, footwear, socks, underwear, and swimwear. ... “This will involve major renovations to reflect the brand’s vision of experiential retail, combining fashion, entertainment, and lifestyle,” he said. ... Maceeo said renovations are expected to take 12 to 18 months and cost an estimated $17 million."
IMPACT RETAIL,,,,Exa,"Meesho: Elevation Capital, Peak XV Partners sell 2.3% stake in Meesho for Rs 1,949 crore; stock falls - The Economic Times",economictimes.indiatimes.com (ET),2026-08-05T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-05 00:00:00+00:00,,https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/tech/technology/elevation-capital-peak-xv-partners-sell-2-3-stake-in-meesho-for-rs-1949-crore-stock-falls/articleshow/132894509.cms,"Meesho: Elevation Capital, Peak XV Partners sell 2.3% stake in Meesho for Rs 1,949 crore; stock falls - The Economic Times ... Peak XV Partners sell ... 2.3% stake in ... for Rs 1 ... 949 crore; stock ... # Elevation Capital, Peak XV Partners sell 2.3% stake in Meesho for Rs 1,949 crore; stock falls ... Following the stake sale, Meesho's shares fell nearly 1 per cent on Wednesday to trade at Rs 190.50 apiece on the National Stock Exchange (NSE). ... ETMarkets.comMeesho in focus as early investors plan Rs 1,900 crore sale ... Private equity firms Elevation Capital and Peak XV Partners have divested a combined 2.3 per cent stake in e-commerce marketplace Meesho for Rs 1,949 crore through open market transactions, according to block deal data on the NSE.Following the stake sale, Meesho's shares fell nearly 1 per cent on Wednesday to trade at Rs 190.50 apiece on the National Stock Exchange (NSE).Elevation Capital's affiliate Elevation Capital V Ltd and Peak XV Partners' arm Peak XV Partners Investments V, together sold 10.48 crore equity shares, representing nearly a 2.3 per cent equity stake in Bengaluru-based Meesho. ... The transactions were executed on Tuesday at an average price of Rs 186 apiece, taking the aggregate deal size to Rs 1,949.28 crore, as per the data.After the latest transaction, Elevation Capital's holding in Meesho fell to 10.91 per cent from 12.04 per cent, while Peak XV Partners stake declined to 8.93 per cent from 10.06 per cent.Despite the divestment, Elevation Capital and Peak XV Partners continue to remain among the largest public shareholders in Meesho, along with Naspers and SoftBank. ... Naspers holds an 11.27 per cent stake, while tech investor Softbank through its arm SVF II Meerkat (DE) LLC owned a 8.60 per cent holding in the company.The buyers include a mix of domestic mutual funds, insurance companies and global institutional investors.Domestic investors comprised UTI Mutual Fund (MF), Kotak Mahindra MF, HSBC MF, Franklin Templeton MF, Aditya Birla Sun Life MF, Axis MF, and Edelweiss MF.Among insurance firms, Factory Mutual Insurance Company, and ICICI Prudential Life Insurance purchased shares of Meesho.Foreign investors participating in the transaction included Societe Generale, Prudential Hong Kong, Morgan Stanley, Goldman Sachs, Susquehanna International Group, Fidelity, Abu Dhabi Investment Authority, and Dendana Investments Mauritius.Separately, Gurugram-based Elevation Capital on Tuesday sold 10.20 lakh shares in One 97 Communications, the parent of financial services firm Paytm, for over Rs 139 crore through an open market transaction. PTI"
IMPACT RETAIL,,,,Exa,"Nexus Select Trust Reports 17% Growth in Retail Consumption for Q1 FY27, ETRetail",retail.economictimes.indiatimes.com (www.ETRetail.com),2026-08-04T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-04 00:00:00+00:00,,https://retail.economictimes.indiatimes.com/news/industry/nexus-select-trust-reports-17-per-cent-consumption-growth-in-q1/132849725,"Nexus Select Trust Reports 17% Growth in Retail Consumption for Q1 FY27, ETRetail ... # Nexus Select Trust reports 17 per cent consumption growth in Q1 ... The company declared a distribution of Rs 370 crore, equivalent to Rs 2.442 per unit, representing 10 per cent year-on-year and 7 per cent quarter-on-quarter growth. This marks the 12th consecutive quarter of distributing 100 per cent of available cash to unitholders. ... Nexus Select Trust, India's first listed retail REIT, reported a strong first quarter for FY27, driven by double-digit growth in retail consumption, net operating income(NOI) and distributions to unitholders.For the quarter ended June 30, 2026, the REIT recorded retail consumption of Rs 3,850 crore, up 17 per cent year-on-year, with growth witnessed across all retail categories. Net Operating Income (NOI) increased 11 per cent to Rs 510 crore, supported by higher consumption and continued leasing momentum.The company declared a distribution of Rs 370 crore, equivalent to Rs 2.442 per unit, representing 10 per cent year-on-year and 7 per cent quarter-on-quarter growth. This marks the 12th consecutive quarter of distributing 100 per cent of available cash to unitholders. ... Dalip Sehgal, executive director and chief executive officer, Nexus Select Trust, said, ""We delivered robust double-digit consumption growth despite heightened global uncertainties. The quarter also saw 11 per cent growth in NOI and a 10% increase in distribution per unit, reflecting the resilience of our portfolio and disciplined operational execution.""During the quarter, Nexus Select re-leased 0.4 million square feet of retail space at double-digit rental spreads, including 0.2 million square feet renewed ahead of lease expiries at spreads exceeding 20 per cent. The portfolio also added new brands such as Lego, Kurt Geiger and Harajuku Bakehouse, strengthening its premium retail mix. ... The REIT announced the acquisition of Diamond Plaza Mall in Kolkata, with the transaction expected to close in the first half of FY27. It also said it has built an acquisition pipeline of eight retail assets, with two assets currently under due diligence, as part of its strategy to double its portfolio by 2030.Nexus Select maintained a loan-to-value (LTV) ratio of 18 per cent, an AAA/Stable credit rating, and reduced its average debt cost to 7.2 per cent, down 30 basis points year-on-year.Since its listing, the REIT has distributed more than Rs 4,080 crore to unitholders and delivered an internal rate of return (IRR) of 25 per cent, according to the company. Its portfolio comprises 19 Grade-A retail consumption centres, three hotels, and three office assets across 15 Indian cities."
IMPACT RETAIL,,,,Exa,"Mintoak acquires rewards fintech ICC Loyalty to expand bank platform in West Asia, Africa - The Economic Times",economictimes.indiatimes.com (ET),2026-08-04T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-04 00:00:00+00:00,,https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/tech/startups/mintoak-acquires-rewards-fintech-icc-loyalty-to-expand-bank-platform-in-west-asia-africa/articleshow/132851431.cms,"Mintoak acquires rewards fintech ICC Loyalty to expand bank platform in West Asia, Africa - The Economic Times ... Business News Tech Startups Mintoak acquires rewards fintech ICC Loyalty to expand bank platform in West Asia, Africa ... # Mintoak acquires rewards fintech ICC Loyalty to expand bank platform in West Asia, Africa ... Bengaluru-based Mintoak has acquired Dubai's ICC Loyalty for consumer rewards technology. This acquisition was largely funded by Mintoak's cash reserves and a small debt. ICC Loyalty manages credit card reward points for thirty-four banks and eleven million customers. The combined entity now offers a full-stack solution for bank clients. This strategic move aims to strengthen their presence in Africa and West Asia. ... Bengaluru: Merchant payments software company Mintoak has acquired Dubai-headquartered ICC Loyalty in a largely cash-funded deal, adding consumer rewards technology to its bank platform as it expands in West Asia and Africa. The companies did not disclose transaction terms.The acquisition was funded mainly from Mintoak’s cash reserves, with a small debt component, cofounder and chief executive Raman Khanduja told ET. It forms part of the Rs 200-crore pool Mintoak earmarked last year for acquisitions adjacent to payments.ICC manages the earning, redemption and expiry of credit-card reward points and targeted campaigns for 34 banks. It serves more than 11 million customers across over 10 countries. ... Mintoak operates on the merchant, or acquiring, side of banking, supplying white-label software that lets lenders accept payments and cross-sell loans and insurance to small businesses. ICC operates on the consumer, or issuing, side by powering card loyalty programmes.“We are on the merchant side and they are on the consumer side, while the economic buyer in the bank is the same,” Khanduja said. “We sit over the bank’s processing system and power engagement and monetisation. Processing itself is largely becoming a commodity.”The combined business has about $30 million in annual revenue and an operating profit margin of around 30%, Khanduja said. Mintoak recorded FY25 revenue of Rs 106 crore and profit of Rs 32.6 crore. Its FY26 revenue grew about 25%, though the accounts are still being audited, Khanduja said. ... Mintoak said its platform powers close to Rs 50,000 crore of monthly payment volume for various banks, including HDFC Bank, Axis Bank and SBI Payments. ICC’s clients include Emirates Islamic and RAKBANK.ICC cofounder and chief executive Amit Narang said combining the acquiring and issuing products would allow the companies to sell more services to their existing bank clients. “It becomes a full-stack solution,” he said, adding that the offering would help win customers in Africa, where both companies are still expanding.Founded in 2017 by former Visa executives, Mintoak acquired payments startup Digiledge in March 2025. It raised $20 million in 2023 in a round led by PayPal Ventures; other backers include HDFC Bank, British International Investment, Pravega Ventures and Z3Partners."
IMPACT RETAIL,,,,Exa,Nexus Select eyes eight retail acquisitions as expansion gathers pace | Company Business News,www.livemint.com (Madhurima Nandy),2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-03 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.livemint.com/companies/news/nexus-select-eyes-eight-retail-acquisitions-as-expansion-gathers-pace-11785750073502.html,"Nexus Select eyes eight retail acquisitions as expansion gathers pace | Company Business News ... Nexus Select eyes eight retail acquisitions as expansion gathers pace ... Nexus Select Trust, India's only publicly listed retail real estate investment trust (Reit), is lining up its next phase of expansion, with plans to acquire eight retail assets across multiple cities as it pushes into eastern India and strengthens its footprint in existing markets. ... The acquisition pipeline spans shopping centres in the capital cities of states including Bihar and Jharkhand, one asset in southern India, and additional land and built-up space at two locations where the Reit already operates in north India, according to people familiar with the plans. Two of the transactions are currently in the due diligence stage. ... Among the deals, the Blackstone Group-backed Reit is in the process of acquiring a 500,000 sq. ft shopping centre in Guwahati as part of a brownfield development where construction is at an advanced stage. The project will also include a 164-key hotel. ... The expansion comes as Nexus continues to report steady operating performance, giving it financial headroom to pursue acquisitions. ... On Monday, the Reit reported an 11% year-on-year increase in net operating income (NOI) to ₹ 510 crore for the April-June quarter, driven by strong consumption and leasing momentum. It declared a distribution of ₹ 370 crore ( ₹ 2.44 per unit), up 10% from a year earlier, marking its 12th consecutive quarter of a 100% distribution payout to unitholders. ... Since listing in 2023, the company has distributed more than ₹ 4,080 crore and delivered an internal rate of return (IRR) of 25%, underscoring its commitment to delivering consistent returns to unitholders, the company said. ... “Backed by a strong balance sheet, low leverage, and nearly $1 billion of available debt headroom, we are well-positioned to capitalize on inorganic growth opportunities and continue scaling our platform,” Dalip Sehgal, executive director and chief executive at Nexus Select Trust, told Mint. ... Nexus Reit currently owns 19 shopping centres across 15 cities, along with three hotel assets (450 keys) and three office assets. ... The acquisition pipeline aligns with the Reit's strategy of doubling its portfolio by 2030. It recently announced the acquisition of Diamond Plaza mall in Kolkata, with the transaction expected to close in the first half of FY27. ... Beyond acquisitions, Nexus Select Trust has also partnered with real estate developer Runwal Enterprises to develop a shopping mall spanning more than 700,000 sq. ft in Dombivli, a suburban Mumbai neighbourhood. ... The project marks the Reit's first partnership in an under-construction development since listing. The two partners will jointly develop Nexus Runwal Gardens Mall with a total investment value of more than ₹ 900 crore. ... “Last year, footfall growth was not great, but there has been a recovery. Our NOI has grown, so has profitability and consumption. Occupancy remains high at 96%. We have got a strong playbook in terms of what we are doing. The strategy of acquiring and turning assets around, investing in them, increasing the categories and brands, bringing in more brands, has been validated,” Sehgal said."
IMPACT RETAIL,,,,Exa,Couche-Tard agrees Żabka acquisition in 32.62bn-zloty deal,www.retail-insight-network.com (Shubhendu Vimal),2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-03 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.retail-insight-network.com/news/couche-tard-agrees-zabka-acquisition/,"Couche-Tard agrees Żabka acquisition in 32.62bn-zloty deal ... Canadian convenience store retailer Alimentation Couche-Tard has agreed to acquire the entire issued and outstanding share capital of Polish convenience retailer Żabka Group in a transaction with equity value of 32.62bn zlotys ($8.74bn). ... The Canadian convenience and mobility operator will make a voluntary tender offer through its wholly owned subsidiary Circle K Polska. ... Under the offer, shareholders would receive 32 zlotys per share in cash. ... Couche-Tard said the deal is its “largest” acquisition to date. ... Shareholders representing approximately 57% of Żabka’s shares, including CVC Capital Partners and Partners Group, have signed hard irrevocable undertakings to tender their stakes under the offer. ... Żabka’s senior executives have also agreed to sell their shares and intend to reinvest a significant portion of the proceeds into Couche-Tard equity. ... The deal comes after Japanese retailer Seven & i Holdings ended discussions last month regarding a potential investment in Żabka. ... For the 12 months to 31 March 2026, Żabka recorded revenue of $7.4bn, adjusted EBITDA [earnings before interest, taxes, depreciation and amortisation] of $1.1bn and net profit of nearly $0.3bn. ... On a pro forma basis, the combined business would have last-12-month revenue of about $83.9bn, adjusted EBITDA of roughly $7.8bn and an adjusted EBITDA margin of close to 9.3%, before synergies. ... Couche-Tard identified possible cost and revenue synergies of about $250m, with full realisation targeted by the third year after completion. ... The company said the transaction is expected to increase adjusted EBITDA margin immediately and raise earnings per share from year two onward. ... The acquisition is subject to a number of regulatory approvals. ... These include merger control clearance from either the European Commission or Poland’s UOKiK, foreign direct investment approval from Romania’s screening authority, and clearance under the EU’s Foreign Subsidies Regulation. ... Poland’s Financial Supervision Authority must also review the offer document. ... The tender period is expected to start around 26 August 2026 and continue for an initial 30 days. ... If there are no delays, completion is expected by December 2026 at the latest. ... If Couche-Tard obtains at least 95% of Żabka’s voting rights, it intends to carry out a compulsory squeeze-out of remaining shareholders and remove the company from the Warsaw Stock Exchange. ... The deal adds to Couche-Tard’s presence in Central and Eastern Europe and comes alongside its existing network of nearly 400 Circle K stations in Poland."
IMPACT RETAIL,,,,Exa,Beyond expansion: Quick commerce seeks more growth from existing assets | Company News - Business Standard,www.business-standard.com (Business Standard),2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-02 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.business-standard.com/companies/news/beyond-expansion-quick-commerce-seeks-more-growth-from-existing-assets-126080200252_1.html,"Beyond expansion: Quick commerce seeks more growth from existing assets | Company News - Business Standard ... # Beyond expansion: Quick commerce seeks more growth from existing assets ... ## Even as they continue adding dark stores, quick-commerce firms are focusing on higher utilisation of their existing assets, better unit economics and improved margins ... Quick commerce are focusing on extracting more orders, higher basket values and better operational ... from existing dark stores, ... India's quick-commerce companies have spent the past few years racing to expand dark-store networks, enter more neighbourhoods and promise ever-faster deliveries. But that strategy may now be evolving. According to estimates by Bernstein, the country's five largest quick-commerce players added nearly 900 dark stores between April and July, with Blinkit adding 289 stores to take its network to 2,511. Flipkart Minutes added 262 stores, crossing the 1,000-store mark, while Amazon Now nearly doubled its footprint to an estimated 600-700 dark stores. Swiggy's Instamart, meanwhile, operated 1,171 dark stores across 131 cities at the end of the June quarter and plans to add another 75 stores during the September quarter. However, alongside network expansion, companies are increasingly seeking to raise the productivity of each store and improve unit economics. Swiggy recently said its existing Instamart network can support more than double its current gross order value (GOV) without significant expansion by increasing the operational efficiency of its dark stores already in place. It plans to widen its product assortment and encourage customers to place larger orders through initiatives such as Maxxsaver. ... Now, the challenge is making those networks more productive. According to Malpani, while geographic expansion will continue in underserved micro markets, companies can improve their economics by increasing the number of orders processed every hour, handling more orders every day, raising average basket values, expanding product assortment, improving warehouse picking efficiency and increasing delivery density. These measures allow fixed infrastructure costs to be spread across more orders, improving profitability without proportionately increasing capital expenditure. ... According to the consulting firm Redseer's estimates, India's quick commerce market has crossed ₹11,000 crore in monthly Gross Merchandise Value (GMV), growing about 100 per cent year-on-year, with non-grocery categories contributing an increasingly larger share of sales, reflecting changing consumer behaviour beyond emergency grocery purchases. ... Malpani believes three additional operational levers will increasingly separate winners from the rest: - smarter assortment optimisation by focusing on fast-moving products while continuously removing slow-moving inventory. - basket expansion by encouraging consumers to combine routine grocery purchases with impulse buying, thereby increasing average order values without materially raising fulfilment costs. - warehouse productivity through faster picking, better inventory accuracy and improved replenishment planning. He also identified advertising and monetised digital shelf space as an emerging source of high-margin revenue that does not require additional physical infrastructure. ... ## Investor focus shifts to capital efficiency and unit economics According to Malpani, during the industry's early expansion phase, metrics such as dark-store additions, city expansion and GMV growth dominated investor presentations. As networks mature, analysts increasingly expect attention to shift towards measures that reflect capital efficiency and unit economics. These include GOV generated per dark store, orders processed per store, contribution margins, asset utilisation, inventory productivity, fulfilment efficiency, customer retention and return on invested capital. ... Redseer says India's quick commerce sector has evolved from being a convenience-led grocery proposition into an increasingly important retail channel for brands, with categories such as beauty, home products and general merchandise becoming larger contributors to sales. As product assortment widens and repeat customer behaviour strengthens, profitability will increasingly depend on operational execution rather than network expansion alone."
IMPACT RETAIL,,,,Exa,"Insight Cosmetics Aims for Rs 500 Crore Revenue in 18 Months with Skincare Expansion, ETRetail",retail.economictimes.indiatimes.com (www.ETRetail.com),2026-07-31T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-31 00:00:00+00:00,,https://retail.economictimes.indiatimes.com/news/health-and-beauty/insight-cosmetics-targets-rs-500-crore-revenue-in-12-18-months-bets-on-skincare-and-retail-expansion/132767033,"Insight Cosmetics Aims for Rs 500 Crore Revenue in 18 Months with Skincare Expansion, ETRetail ... # Insight Cosmetics targets Rs 500 crore revenue in 12-18 months; bets on skincare and retail expansion ... Insight Cosmetics aims for Rs 500 crore revenue within eighteen months. The company is expanding its omnichannel presence and strengthening its skincare portfolio. Online sales currently contribute thirty-five percent of overall revenue. Insight Cosmetics plans to increase its company-owned retail outlets significantly. The brand will also enter the haircare market in the coming quarters. ... Insight Cosmetics, one of India’s homegrown colour cosmetics manufacturers, is targeting Rs 500 crore revenue in the next 12-18 months as it accelerates its omnichannel expansion, strengthens its skincare portfolio and expands its offline footprint.The company reported revenue from operations of Rs 251.99 crore in FY26, registering a 29.1 per cent growth over Rs 195.17 crore in FY25. Total income increased 27.9 per cent to Rs 257.85 crore, while profit before tax surged 64.1 per cent to Rs 44.48 crore. PAT stood at Rs 34.12 crore during the year.“We are a healthy, profitable company. Last year’s revenue was around Rs 300 crore, and we are a 15-18 per cent EBITDA company. In the next 12-18 months, we plan to grow till Rs 500 crore,” said Mihir Jain, Director, Insight Cosmetics. ... Led by second-generation entrepreneur, the brand is transitioning from an offline-focused cosmetics player into a pan-India beauty company.“Earlier, we were focused more on the offline market and our presence was heavily dominated in West and North India. But slowly, we have penetrated across India and are currently present in 26 states,” Jain said.Online channels currently contribute around 35 per cent of overall sales, while the brand has witnessed strong momentum on quick commerce platforms.“We are recording 100 per cent plus growth year-on-year across all quick commerce platforms,” he added. ... Insight Cosmetics currently has 30,000-35,000 active retail counters, 360 modern trade outlets and presence across around 15 online platforms. The company is also scaling its direct-to-consumer retail model through company-owned kiosks.Currently operating 25 kiosks across malls, the brand plans to expand this network to 60 exclusive brand outlets by the end of FY27.“All our kiosks are company-owned and company-operated. The kiosk size ranges from 50 sq ft to 100 sq ft, with an investment of approximately Rs 8-10 lakh per kiosk,” Jain said.The brand is looking to strengthen its presence in underserved markets including the Seven Sisters, Jammu, Jharkhand and Chhattisgarh. It currently works with around 400-500 distributors and plans to add another 100-150 distributors in the next two-three quarters. ... While colour cosmetics remain its core business, Insight is increasingly focusing on skincare and adjacent categories. The company currently has around 350 products and 2,800-3,000 SKUs across colour cosmetics, skincare and professional ranges.“The face category is the biggest category for us. Concealers and primers are our best-selling products and contribute roughly 35 per cent of our revenue,” Jain said.The brand has recently launched skincare products including face masks and face sheets and plans to expand the portfolio further.“Skincare is going to be the next growth lever for Insight Cosmetics. We are also increasing launches in lips and eyes categories, and will focus on launching more skincare products in the coming months,” he added.The brand also plans to enter haircare in the next three quarters.Insight Cosmetics operates three manufacturing facilities in Maharashtra and manufactures all products locally, including formulation and research. The company is currently operating at around 70 per cent capacity utilisation and plans to expand capacity, especially for lip and eye categories.“We manufacture, formulate and conduct research in India. We plan to expand our capacity soon as we continue to scale our launches,” Jain concluded."
IMPACT RETAIL,,,,Exa,"Noice: Instamart's Game-Changer in Quick Commerce, ETRetail",retail.economictimes.indiatimes.com (www.ETRetail.com),2026-07-30T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-30 00:00:00+00:00,,https://retail.economictimes.indiatimes.com/news/food-entertainment/personal-care-pet-supplies-liquor/one-year-of-noice-why-it-could-be-instamarts-smartest-bet-yet/132737324,"# One year of Noice: Why it could be Instamart's smartest bet yet Its portfolio has expanded to over 400 products across more than 20 categories, ranging from breads and dairy to snacks, beverages and desserts. But the bigger story isn't the scale of the portfolio. It is what Noice represents for Instamart at a time when India's quick commerce players are searching for new ways to stand apart. - Published On Jul 30, 2026 at 04:29 PM IST - Read by 100 Professionals ... Bengaluru: When Swiggy launched Noice last year, it entered one of the toughest segments in the consumer business. Unlike staples such as rice or pulses, packaged foods are categories where taste, quality and brand loyalty often outweigh price. Winning consumers over is difficult. Doing so with a private label is even harder. One year later, Noice appears to be emerging as more than just another in-house brand. Its portfolio has expanded to over 400 products across more than 20 categories, ranging from breads and dairy to snacks, beverages and desserts. But the bigger story isn't the scale of the portfolio. It is what Noice represents for Instamart at a time when India's quick commerce players are searching for new ways to stand apart. ... As Blinkit, Zepto, Flipkart Minutes, Amazon Now and Instamart continue to compress delivery times and expand assortments, logistics is becoming less of a differentiator. Swiggy's answer appears to be building products consumers cannot buy elsewhere. That strategy recently received independent validation. Brokerage Bernstein, which initiated coverage on Swiggy with an 'Outperform' rating, subjected Noice products to blind taste tests against established FMCG and direct-to-consumer brands. The outcome surprised even the brokerage. Across seven categories, Noice ranked first in three, second in two and third in two, with only marginal gaps separating it from category leaders in the latter categories. The findings suggest that consumers judged the products on merit rather than simply because they were sold on Instamart. ... For Bernstein, however, taste was only part of the story. The brokerage argues that Noice represents a strategic shift in how private labels could evolve within quick commerce. Traditionally, retailer-owned brands have been built to improve margins by offering lower-cost alternatives to national brands. Noice, in contrast, is attempting to compete in premium packaged foods—categories where consumers are far more discerning and brand loyalty is significantly stronger. That distinction matters because the economics of quick commerce are changing. The next phase of competition is unlikely to be won solely through faster deliveries. Most leading platforms already offer comparable delivery speeds, similar assortments and overlapping promotional campaigns. As these advantages become easier to replicate, exclusive brands offer something competitors cannot immediately copy. ... ""If consumers can access a product only through one platform, it changes the switching equation,"" Bernstein argues. Rather than functioning purely as a margin lever, Noice could become a customer-retention tool that strengthens loyalty over time. Yet the brokerage is equally clear about the limits of the opportunity. ""Noice, while very good, by itself, is not enough to turn Instamart's fortunes. It may be a small building block to retain quality-conscious customers in the long run,"" Bernstein said. Instead, the firm's investment thesis continues to hinge on much larger operational challenges. Instamart customers currently place 2.8 orders per month, compared with 3.6 orders per month for Blinkit customers, according to Bernstein. Closing that engagement gap, improving dark-store monetisation and deciding whether incremental contribution margins should be reinvested into growth or retained to improve profitability remain the bigger priorities for Swiggy's quick commerce business. That context is what makes Noice strategically interesting. Swiggy is not merely launching another private label. It is attempting to create a food brand that consumers actively choose, even when competing against established FMCG names. If successful, Noice could offer Instamart something that is becoming increasingly valuable in quick commerce: differentiation that extends beyond delivery speed. The challenge, however, will be sustaining that differentiation. Established FMCG companies have spent decades earning loyalty through consistent quality, distribution and recall. Replicating that trust inside a quick commerce platform will require far more than a successful first year. Still, Bernstein believes Noice has demonstrated something important: Swiggy can build products capable of competing with established brands in categories where quality matters most. Whether that eventually translates into stronger customer loyalty and ultimately a stronger Instamart business will determine if Noice becomes a successful private brand or something much bigger. - Published"
IMPACT RETAIL,,,,Exa,"Unlock Flexible Credit Options with Flipkart Pay Later, ETRetail",retail.economictimes.indiatimes.com (www.ETRetail.com),2026-07-30T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-30 00:00:00+00:00,,https://retail.economictimes.indiatimes.com/news/e-commerce/e-tailing/flipkart-launches-pay-later-with-flexible-credit-options-across-its-shopping-platforms/132729938,"# Flipkart launches Pay Later with flexible credit options across its shopping platforms Flipkart said the credit product is designed to support both everyday transactions across categories including grocery, fashion, beauty and home, as well as larger purchases that require structured repayment. - ETRetail Desk ... ETRetail - Published On Jul 30, 2026 at 12:09 PM IST - Read by 100 Professionals ... Flipkart Group, in partnership with PayU Finance, has launched Flipkart Pay Later, a new embedded credit offering that enables customers to access flexible repayment options across Flipkart, Myntra, and Flipkart Minutes. The offering includes three repayment options: Pay Later, which allows repayment within up to 30 days for everyday purchases; Pay in 3, which splits payments into three instalments; and EMI plans ranging from three to 12 months for higher-value purchases such as mobiles, electronics, appliances and furniture. Flipkart said the credit product is designed to support both everyday transactions across categories including grocery, fashion, beauty and home, as well as larger purchases that require structured repayment. ... To power the offering, Flipkart Finance has partnered with PayU Finance, the non-banking financial company (NBFC) arm of PayU, as its first lending partner. While PayU Finance will serve as the licensed lender, Flipkart Finance will manage customer relationships and leverage its commerce data to support credit underwriting. The company said additional financial institution partnerships will be added as the programme expands. Vishal Ahuja, executive director, Flipkart Finance, said, ""Affordability remains the core driver of how millions in India engage with digital commerce. For these consumers, aspiration has never been a constraint, access has. With Flipkart Pay Later, we are putting a decade of commerce signals to work, building an underwriting model that sees these customers clearly and extends them credit at the moment it matters the most. This is not a payment feature. It is an important step in building a financial services capability that we believe will fundamentally change who gets to participate in India's digital economy."" ... Deepak Mendiratta, CEO, PayU Finance, said, ""As digital commerce continues to evolve, the need for responsible and accessible credit has never been greater. Through our partnership with Flipkart Finance, we are combining technology, data-driven underwriting, and strong risk management to deliver seamless credit experiences at scale. Flipkart Pay Later is another step towards expanding financial inclusion while powering the next phase of India's digital commerce growth."" Flipkart said credit decisions will be based on its proprietary commerce intelligence, including transaction history, purchase behaviour and platform activity, combined with PayU Finance's lending and risk management capabilities. The company added that multiple data sources will be used to assess customer eligibility and ensure responsible lending. -"
IMPACT RETAIL,,,,Exa,Snitch Acquires D2C Women’s Fashion Brand Berrylush,inc42.com (Shrishti Bisht),2026-07-28T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-28 00:00:00+00:00,,https://inc42.com/buzz/snitch-acquires-d2c-womens-fashion-brand-berrylush-to-expand-beyond-menswear/,"Snitch Acquires D2C Women’s Fashion Brand Berrylush ... Snitch has acquired women’s apparel brand Berrylush, marking its entry into women’s fashion. Deal terms were not disclosed ... The deal lets Snitch expand beyond menswear using Berrylush’s existing brand, customer base and product portfolio instead of building from scratch ... Berrylush will retain its identity and team, while Snitch will support its product, tech, supply chain, marketing and omnichannel expansion ... Snitch has acquired a 100% stake in D2C women’s fashion brand Berrylush to expand beyond menswear as it seeks to build a broader portfolio of fashion brands. The financial terms of the deal were not disclosed. ... Following the acquisition, Berrylush will retain its brand identity and continue operating with its existing team, including cofounder Anusha Chandrashekar. Snitch will support the brand across product development, technology, supply chain, marketing, and omnichannel retail. ... “Our ambition is to build Berrylush into a ₹1,000 Cr women’s fashion brand over the coming years, while staying focused on product, customer experience and execution,” Snitch founder and CEO Siddharth Dungarwal said. ... The acquisition gives Snitch an immediate foothold in India’s women’s apparel market, which is projected to reach over $121 Bn by 2034, growing at a CAGR of 2.71% between 2026 and 2034. ... Instead of launching a new label, the startup has opted to acquire an established brand with an existing customer base and market presence. ... Snitch chose the acquisition route because Berrylush had already built customer trust and a strong brand identity, he said, adding that the objective is to scale the business while preserving the brand’s positioning. ... Over the coming months, Snitch will work with Chandrashekar and the existing Berrylush team on what it calls ‘BERRYLUSH 2.0’. The next phase will focus on faster product development, improving customer experience, expanding its omnichannel presence, and driving long-term growth. ... The startup currently operates 115 stores across India, with online channels contributing about 60% of its revenue and offline stores accounting for the remaining 40%. ... Snitch closed FY26 with operating revenue of ₹900 Cr, up about 80% from ₹498 Cr a year earlier, Dungarwal told Inc42 earlier. Its unaudited EBITDA margin stood at around 2-3%, implying EBITDA of roughly ₹18-27 Cr. ... The startup is also expanding its quick commerce presence through Snitch Quick, which currently operates in Bengaluru, Delhi, Gurugram and Ahmedabad. The channel already contributes about 10% of its online revenue. ... Snitch is targeting revenue of around ₹1,400 Cr in FY27 as it continues to expand its store network and online business."
IMPACT RETAIL,,,,Exa,"Action Reports Sales, Customer Number Growth In H1 2026 | ESM Magazine",www.esmmagazine.com (Lisa Edelbrock),2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-27 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.esmmagazine.com/retail/action-reports-14-net-sales-growth-in-h1-2026-amid-rising-customer-numbers-317514,"Action Reports Sales, Customer Number Growth In H1 2026 | ESM Magazine ... # Action Reports 14% Net Sales Growth In H1 2026 Amid Rising Customer Numbers ... Dutch non-food discounter Action has reported a 14% increase in net sales, to €8.3 billion, in the first half of 2026, driven by an increase in customer numbers. ... Average weekly customer numbers increased by 12%, year on year, to 22.7 million, while like-for-like net sales rose by 3.6%. ... Hajir Hajji, CEO, commented, “In line with our formula, we reduced prices by passing on cost savings. ... lower prices are resonating with ... , as reflected in ... growing customer base and ... ### Expansion Strategy ... To support its growing customer base, Action also invested in network expansion, opening 121 new stores across Europe. ... The company now operates 3,423 stores and aims to add a minimum of 400 new ones in 2026. ... As part of its expansion strategy, Action opened its first three stores in Sicily, increasing the total number of stores in Italy to 241, while the company also marked the opening of its 50th store in Slovakia in 2026. ... The expansion strategy also supported the creation of 4,464 jobs across stores and distribution centres, increasing Action’s workforce by 6%. ... ### Seasonal, Sustainable Products ... Action highlighted an increased demand for seasonal products, particularly summer essentials, driven by warmer weather across Europe. ... The demand for seasonal products also increased across its online platforms in the Netherlands and Belgium. ... In addition, the company noted its focus on sustainable products, highlighting its sun cream brand, Zenova. ... Action also supports Fairtrade’s Living Income Reference Price across its own-brand cocoa range, which has helped support projects in Côte d’Ivoire, while the company also works with the Johan Cruyff Foundation to support local communities in Spain and the Netherlands."
IMPACT RETAIL,,,,Exa,The Works CEO on its performance since scrapping ecommerce - Retail Gazette,www.retailgazette.co.uk (Eloise Hill),2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-27 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.retailgazette.co.uk/blog/2026/07/the-works-ceo-stores/,"Discount retailer The Works saw profits soar 47 per cent to £14 million in its latest full year results, as stronger sales, improved margins and cost savings helped to offset its higher wage and tax costs. ... The brand attributed its performance to its “screen-free” strategy paying off. It also noted that supplier negotiations, tighter stock controls, improved promotional markdowns and a more favourable product mix helped to boost its margins. ... The results come after The Works opted to axe its ecommerce operations earlier this year, choosing to instead double-down on its store-led strategy. ... Unsurprisingly, he assures that “at the moment” the brand has no plans to return to ecommerce. ... “We never say never, but certainly for the next several years we’re focused on everything else we’ve got to do as part of our strategy,” he says. ... “Opening new stores, optimising stores that we’ve got through space and improving the proposition, the brand, and the systems work. ... “At some point in the future we might look at bringing back click and collect or marketplaces or other things. But that’s ... in the near ... In terms of its new store launches since ditching ecommerce, the exec says that he is “pretty proud of all the stores that we’ve opened”. ... However, he highlights its new retail park store near Newport as performing “particularly well”. ... He notes that it has opened stores in “quite a few retail parks” such as Deepdale Shopping Park in Preston and Glasgow Fort. ... “They’re big high turnover openings for us. But another one that’s gone particularly well is in Arnold, which is a suburb of Nottingham. That has massively outperformed what we thought it would do,” he says. ... “We’ve had a few retail parks that have taken slightly longer to mature, but certainly the ones we’ve opened this spring have gone really well.” ... With ecommerce out of the picture, The Works has plenty of chance to make improvements to its physical store estate. ... Peck explains that the company is “just about to roll out customer experience training for all of our store colleagues at absolutely every level”. ... “That’s through a program called Fame, which is find a moment every time, which is just really about expectations for base customer experience,” he says. ... “For us we want the customer experience to be a key part of that. I think we already have fantastic colleagues giving great experience, but we want to lift that level and improve the consistency of it.” ... He also notes that it is “starting to try and give customers more of the ranges that they want in their local stores”. ... “At the minute, pretty much everybody gets the same range, irrespective of the demographics in that location and what sells in that location,” he says. ... “We need a system to do that long term and properly, but in the short term we’re looking at stores that do particularly well for our books where there’s a lot of BookTokers. ... “Looking at how we can increase the space and the amount of book stock going to those stores.” ... He adds: “Around our stores located near the beach, we’re also looking at how we can send more of our out to play summer play ranges. ... “And like many other retailers, we’re continuing to focus on the kids’ pocket money trend, with the particular trend at the moment being those squishy dumplings. ... “We’re just continuing to really drive that newness in, particularly for kids’ pocket money toys so that when kids come in there’s something that they want and that will keep them coming back.” ... Discount retailer The Works saw profits soar 47 per cent to £14 million in its latest full year results, as stronger sales, improved margins and cost savings helped to offset its higher wage and tax costs. ... The brand attributed its performance to its “screen-free” strategy paying off. It also noted that supplier negotiations, tighter stock controls, improved promotional markdowns and a more favourable product mix helped to boost its margins. ... The results come after The Works opted to axe its ecommerce operations earlier this year, choosing to instead double-down on its store-led strategy. ... Unsurprisingly, he assures that “at the moment” the brand has no plans to return to ecommerce. ... “We never say never, but certainly for the next several years we’re focused on everything else we’ve got to do as part of our strategy,” he says. ... “Opening new stores, optimising stores that we’ve got through space and improving the proposition, the brand, and the systems work. ... “At some point in the future we might look at bringing back click and collect or marketplaces or other things. But that’s not in the near term.” ... In terms of its new store launches since ditching ecommerce, the exec says that he is “pretty proud of all the stores that we’ve opened”. ... However, he highlights its new retail park store near Newport as performing “particularly well”. ... He notes that it has opened stores in “quite a few retail parks” such as Deepdale Shopping Park in Preston and Glasgow Fort. ... “They’re big high turnover openings for us. But another one that’s gone particularly well is in Arnold, which is a suburb of Nottingham. That has massively outperformed what we thought it would do,” he says. ... “We’ve had a few retail parks that have taken slightly longer to mature, but certainly the ones we’ve opened this spring have gone really well.” ... With ecommerce out of the picture, The Works has plenty of chance to make improvements to its physical store estate. ... Peck explains that the company is “just about to roll out customer experience training for all of our store colleagues at absolutely every level”. ... “That’s through a program called Fame, which is find a moment every time, which is just really about expectations for base customer experience,” he says. ... “For us versus some of the general merchandise discount retailers like B&M, Home Bargains and The Range, they sell some of what we sell. ... “For us we want the customer experience to be a key part of that. I think we already have fantastic colleagues giving great experience, but we want to lift that level and improve the consistency of it.” ... He also notes that it is “starting to try and give customers more of the ranges that they want in their local stores”. ... “At the minute, pretty much everybody gets the same range, irrespective of the demographics in that location and what sells in that location,” he says. ... “We need a system to do that long term and properly, but in the short term we’re looking at stores that do particularly well for our books where there’s a lot of BookTokers. ... “Looking at how we can increase the space and the amount of book stock going to those stores.” ... He adds: “Around our stores located near the beach, we’re also looking at how we can send more of our out to play summer play ranges. ... “And like many other retailers, we’re continuing to focus on the kids’ pocket money trend, with the particular trend at the moment being those squishy dumplings. ... “We’re just continuing to really drive that newness in, particularly for kids’ pocket money toys so that when kids come in there’s something that they want and that will keep them coming back.”"
IMPACT RETAIL,,,,Exa,Schnucks' health-tracking AI assistant is a model for other retailers,www.modernretail.co (Mitchell Parton),2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-27 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.modernretail.co/technology/schnucks-new-health-tracking-ai-assistant-is-a-model-for-other-retailers/,"St. Louis-based regional grocer Schnucks just launched a new AI assistant, but behind it is a larger idea around how agents could eventually take over the shopping process and help people eat healthier. ... Schnucks, which operates 112 grocery stores in Missouri, Illinois and Indiana, announced its partnership with the Boston-based agentic commerce tech company VitalityIP on July 22. Through this deal, it plans to launch an AI shopping assistant for nutrition guidance, meal ideas and product recommendations. It will be found in the Schnucks Rewards app and Schnucks’ website later this summer. ... The grocer and startup co-developed the technology behind the assistant using customer and store inventory data as well as VitalityIP’s AI and proprietary database of health insights and ingredient-level product data. Schnucks is the first to debut VitalityIP’s platform, but it was designed for white-label integration by retailers nationwide. ... Executives at both Schnucks and VitalityIP told Modern Retail that the platform is just the beginning of a transition from customer-initiated shopping to one in which AI agents take control of people’s eating habits. ... The field of AI agents in retail is quickly becoming crowded; 21% of retailers in 2026 have generative AI chatbots, up from just 5% in 2025, according to Gartner research. Greg Carlucci, a senior director analyst for Gartner who consults CMOs as well as digital commerce and marketing leaders, said VitalityIP’s platform could have a competitive advantage because it enables consumers to shop based on nutritional needs or goals rather than just by asking general questions or for pricing. ... Paul Zwillenberg, CEO of Kantar, said the Schnucks launch signals a shift in retail where AI replaces static product aisles with goal-driven health and meal engines. He added that this will mean that, in the future, brands won’t have to compete as much for shelf space or search rankings, but instead for the most trusted, credible answer from AI agents. ... “The path to purchase is no longer shaped solely by consumers. It’s increasingly mediated by AI,” Zwillenberg said in an email. “As agents move upstream to recommend what shoppers should buy, not just where to find it, brands must compete for algorithmic trust as much as consumer attention. The winners will be the brands that are most credible, relevant and easy for AI to recommend.” ... Hoit said the sophistication of Schnucks’ product data made the retailer an ideal partner to show what’s possible with agentic shopping. But, she said, VitalityIP can also work with grocers that are smaller or less technologically equipped. She said the company is beginning to sign contracts with other retailers. ... ’s AI shopping assistant has ads at launch ... Kroger made advertising part of its AI shopping assistant on day one, following the implementation of advertising features on competitors' chatbots. ... #### Schnucks’ new health-tracking AI assistant is a model for other retailers ... Schnucks’ new health-tracking AI assistant is a model for other retailers ... St. Louis-based regional grocer Schnucks just launched a new AI assistant, but behind it is a larger idea around how agents could eventually take over the shopping process and help people eat healthier."
IMPACT RETAIL,,,,Exa,Retail Media: Vusion Acquires In-Store Media | invidis,invidis.com (Florian Rotberg),2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-27 00:00:00+00:00,,https://invidis.com/news/2026/07/retail-media-vusion-acquires-in-store-media/,"Retail Media: Vusion Acquires In-Store Media | invidis ... The ESL market leader is moving beyond store infrastructure. By acquiring Spanish retail media specialist In-Store Media, Vusion gains access to 90 retailers, 1,600 brands and a €120 million revenue business – accelerating its ambition to monetize physical stores at scale. ... Vusion Group(formerly SES Imagotag) is taking a strategic step beyond electronic shelf labels and connected store infrastructure. The French retail technology company has signed an agreement to acquire In-Store Media (ISM), one of Europe’s largest specialists in in-store retail media. ... Founded in Barcelona in 1998, ISM has built a substantial international business around retail advertising and shopper marketing. The company operates in nine countries across Europe, Latin America and Asia, serving more than 90 retailers and working with over 1,600 consumer brands. ISM generated approximately EUR 120 million in revenue in 2025 with strong profitability. ... The transaction remains subject to regulatory approval and customary closing conditions. Vusion plans to finance the acquisition through debt. ... The acquisition marks an evolution of Vusion’s business model. For years, the public company partly owned by BOE has successfully transformed itself from an ESL vendor into a broader connected-store platform provider. Its technology portfolio now includes ESLs, large epaper displays, digital signage displays, computer vision, store analytics and cloud-based retail management solutions. ... Rather than merely supplying the digital infrastructure, the French company is positioning itself as an enabler – and potentially operator – of retail media networks inside physical stores. The acquisition adds advertising expertise, media inventory management and long-established relationships with both retailers and brand advertisers. ... Retail media has become one of the fastest-growing segments in advertising over the past five years. Most investments have flowed into online retail platforms, where retailers monetize their websites, apps and e-commerce marketplaces. ... ISM brings extensive experience in this area. The company offers more than 50 retail media formats, covering both traditional and digital in-store advertising solutions across supermarkets, hypermarkets, shopping centers, department stores and specialty retail environments. ... The company already provides much of the hardware and software infrastructure necessary for digital retail media deployments. ISM contributes the missing layer: media sales expertise, campaign execution capabilities and advertiser relationships. ... Together, the two companies aim to create a platform that connects retailers, brands and shoppers through measurable in-store activation. ... Vusion is facing growing competition in the ESL market. While the French retail technology provider – with French and Austrian roots – remains the undisputed market leader, having deployed more than 600 million electronic shelf labels worldwide, the company understands that future growth will not come from hardware alone. ... Both at NRF in New York and Euroshop in Düsseldorf, Vusion demonstrated how far its portfolio has evolved beyond electronic shelf labels. Alongside e-paper posters and category signage, the company is increasingly investing in software, data services, computer vision, and AI-powered retail solutions. The Vusion-vision is clear: transform stores from operational environments into connected digital platforms. ... Against this backdrop, the acquisition of Spanish retail media specialist In-Store Media (ISM) is a logical next step. ... With approximately €120 million in annual revenue and an extensive network of retailers and advertising brands, ISM adds a new dimension to Vusion’s hardware business. Compared to Vusion’s 2025 revenue of around €1.5 billion, the acquisition is financially manageable, yet strategically significant. More importantly, it brings capabilities that Vusion could not easily build organically: media sales expertise, advertiser relationships, and operational know-how in monetizing physical retail environments. ... The transactions shows, that the traditional boundaries between ESL/display networks, data platforms, store infrastructure, and advertising ecosystems are rapidly disappearing. Retailers increasingly view displays, ESLs, and in-store communication systems not only as tools for pricing and operations but as potential media channels capable of generating incremental revenue. ... In this environment, ownership of the network becomes more valuable than ownership of the hardware. The real prize is no longer the screen on the shelf edge but the ability to control, manage, and monetize shopper attention across thousands of locations. ... By acquiring ISM, Vusion gains access not only to retail media expertise but also to a mature commercial platform operating across Europe, Latin America, and Asia. The acquisition strengthens the company’s position against both traditional ESL and digital signage vendors as well as a growing group of retail media specialists competing for a share of retailers’ advertising budgets. ... Ultimately, the transaction suggests that Vusion’s ambitions extend well beyond connected store infrastructure. The company increasingly wants to participate in the economics of retail media itself."
IMPACT RETAIL,,,,Exa,Offline Zinda Hai: Why retailers are betting big on outlets - The Economic Times,economictimes.indiatimes.com (Indrishka Bose),2026-07-24T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-24 00:00:00+00:00,,https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/industry/services/retail/retailers-shift-focus-why-indias-offline-stores-are-thriving-amidst-e-commerce-growth/articleshow/132598243.cms,"India's largest retailers are opening thousands of new physical stores across the nation. This expansion targets emerging consumer markets beyond the major metropolitan areas. While e-commerce grows, physical stores remain crucial for reaching new customers. These outlets also serve as hubs for online order fulfillment and customer experiences. Retailers are strategically investing in offline presence for future consumption growth. ... For years, the narrative around Indian retail has been that e-commerce is growing rapidly, and more consumers are shopping on their phones than ever before.Yet India's largest retailers are doing something that seems to go against that trend. They are opening thousands of new physical stores.Also Read: Top retailers raise Rs 4,000 crore as store expansion hits four-year high ... According to an Economic Times report, the country's 10 largest listed retailers added a net 2,182 stores in FY26, 25% more than the previous year, taking their combined network to more than 31,000 outlets. Reliance Retail, DMart, Trent and More Retail have also raised or announced plans to raise over Rs 4,000 crore this financial year to fund expansion. Reliance Retail alone increased its non-current borrowings to Rs 22,521 crore in FY26 from Rs 14,809 crore a year earlier.At first glance, it may seem like retailers are simply responding to stronger consumer demand. But industry experts say that is only part of the story. ... Retailers are seeing healthy demand, especially during wedding and festive seasons. But existing stores are not necessarily selling dramatically more than before.""It is only partly demand-driven. While strong wedding season and festive discounting supported consumption in the latest quarter, same-store sales growth across most large chains remained modest. That suggests retailers are not just opening more stores because existing outlets are seeing significantly higher demand. They are also looking to expand their footprint, enter underpenetrated markets and be closer to the next set of consumers,"" said Poonam Upadhyay, Director at Crisil Ratings.That shift is important.Instead of opening more stores in cities where they already have a strong presence, retailers are increasingly moving into new catchments where organised retail is still limited. The objective isn't just to serve existing demand, it's to make sure they are present when demand picks up.Also Read: IKEA expands Delhi footprint with second city store in Saket, deepens NCR growth strategy""Consumption determines whether a new store can work; market share is what the retailer earns if it executes well,"" said Anand Ramanathan, Partner and Consumer Industry Leader at Deloitte South Asia.In other words, retailers are following where consumption is headed rather than simply trying to outdo competitors. ... The numbers explain why.India's retail market is expected to grow from about $1.06 trillion in 2024 to $1.93 trillion by 2030. E-commerce will also expand rapidly during this period, reaching around $250 billion, said Ramanathan.But even then, online shopping will account for only about 13% of the country's retail market.That means the overwhelming majority of purchases will still happen through physical stores or a mix of online and offline channels.""The expansion is demand-led, but growth in online commerce is reinforcing, not replacing, the need for stores,"" Ramanathan said.Instead of competing with e-commerce, stores are increasingly becoming part of it.Also Read: Tata’s fashion brand Westside plans biggest push yet with 100 annual stores Many now double up as fulfilment centres, places where customers can experience products before buying, or locations that support online deliveries and returns.According to Deloitte, nine out of ten consumers prefer a seamless experience between online discovery and offline purchase. Integrating stores with digital platforms can also generate nearly 20% incremental revenue.The next growth story is outside the metrosMuch of the expansion is happening beyond India's biggest cities.According to another Economic Times report, retailers are accelerating store openings in smaller towns where organised retail still has significant room to grow.Reliance Retail added a net 820 stores in FY26 after rationalising its network the previous year. V-Mart Retail CEO Lalit Agarwal earlier told the Economic Times that the shift from unorganised to organised retail continues to create opportunities for expansion.Upadhyay said Tier-II and Tier-III cities are emerging as the biggest growth markets, helped by lower rentals and relatively low organised retail penetration.Value fashion and grocery retailers are leading store additions in these markets, while jewellery and premium lifestyle brands are opening larger stores to cater to rising incomes.The opportunity, therefore, is less about taking customers away from competitors and more about reaching consumers who have so far had limited access to organised retail. ... The push isn't limited to grocery and fashion chains.Reuters recently reported that jewellery retailer BlueStone plans to more than double its store network to 705 outlets by FY30, with most of the expansion happening on high streets rather than malls because premium retail space remains scarce. Brands such as Wooden Street and Asics have also continued to expand their physical presence despite highlighting the shortage of quality retail space.Also Read: For mall outlets, summer's time for some cool business Across categories, retailers increasingly see physical stores as more than just places to make a sale. They are becoming important for customer acquisition, brand building and supporting omnichannel operations, complementing rather than competing with online channels.Even so, the pace of expansion is expected to become more measured. Ramanathan expects retailers to be more selective about where they deploy capital, with greater emphasis on store productivity, catchment-level demand, omnichannel integration and returns on investment rather than simply adding more outlets.Upadhyay also expects offline expansion to remain a key growth driver for organised retailers, supported by low organised retail penetration and the continued shift towards organised retail.Also Read: India’s retail market set to reach ₹21.5 lakh crore by 2035, driven by AI and consumption surge: Report However, she said the pace of store additions will increasingly depend on same-store sales growth, rental trends in smaller cities and the extent to which quick commerce reshapes buying behaviour in categories such as groceries and electronics.India's retail story is no longer about choosing between online and offline. The two are increasingly working together. And for the country's biggest retailers, opening a new store today is as much about expanding their footprint into tomorrow's growth markets as it is about serving customers walking in today."
IMPACT RETAIL,,,,Exa,Pepkor doubles down on informal economy with plans for fintech listing,www.businessday.co.za (Nompilo Zulu),2026-07-23T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-23 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.businessday.co.za/companies/2026-07-23-pepkor-doubles-down-on-informal-economy-with-plans-for-fintech-listing/,"Pepkor doubles down on informal economy with plans for fintech listing ... # Pepkor doubles down on informal economy with plans for fintech listing ... ## Retailer invests R1.57bn to acquire a controlling stake in Shop2Shop in bid to expand its footprint in township market ... South African retail major Pepkor has launched its most daring vote of confidence in the informal economy to date with the planned listing of its fintech business within the next three years. ... The mooted listing comes after the JSE-listed retailer, valued at about R80bn, on Wednesday deepened its fintech ambitions by investing R1.57bn to acquire a controlling 57.1% stake in Shop2Shop. ... It would combine Shop2Shop with Pepkor’s Flash payments and value-added services platform to create a R21.3bn fintech business that processes more than R200bn in transactions annually. ... Pepkor said the combined business, which it refers to as FintechCo, will become the vehicle for a future listing as the retailer seeks to unlock the value of one of its fastest-growing businesses while expanding its footprint in South Africa’s informal market. ... The retailer said that its planned bank, with code name PlusB, will not form part of the listed fintech business. The bank will remain a separate legal entity because of regulatory requirements, though it is expected to work closely with FintechCo by allowing customers to move more easily between formal banking services and the merchant network served by Flash and Shop2Shop. ... The transaction comes as competition for South Africa’s informal and small business market intensifies, with banks and fintech companies investing heavily in digital payments, merchant acquisition and lending. ... Last year, Nedbank completed its R1.65bn acquisition of merchant payments business iKhokha, strengthening its position in the small business market. ... Lesaka, which completed its acquisition of Adumo in 2024, now processes more than R250bn in annual payment volumes across its merchant and consumer businesses. Meanwhile, Capitec has been expanding lending to small businesses, including credit based on card payment turnover, while also launching banking products aimed at informal businesses. ... Pepkor’s move is taking place against the backdrop of a rapidly growing township economy. Standard Bank estimates South Africa’s township economy generates more than R900bn in economic activity each year, while the informal economy accounts for almost one-fifth of total employment. ... Though banking penetration is relatively high, ... business owners continue to rely on personal bank accounts, cash transactions and manual bookkeeping, creating opportunities ... businesses offering integrated payments, lending and merchant services. ... Pepkor believes combining Flash and Shop2Shop positions it to capture more of that opportunity. ... Flash has become the group’s largest fintech business since being acquired in 2021, accounting for roughly two-thirds of fintech revenue. The platform enables merchants to sell airtime, prepaid electricity, data and vouchers, as well as to effect bill payments and money transfers. Shop2Shop specialises in merchant payments, cash management, supplier payments and trade enablement. ... The businesses have limited overlap, with only about 20%-30% of traders using both platforms. Bringing them together will allow merchants to access a broader range of services through a single platform, including ordering stock, paying suppliers, accepting card payments, digitising cash transactions and accessing working capital. ... According to Pepkor, the combined business serves about 177,000 merchants through more than 114,000 payment devices and almost 3,000 cash-counting safes. Management said about three-quarters of the R172bn flowing through the Shop2Shop ecosystem is paid back to suppliers, reflecting the role the platform plays in supporting working capital for small businesses. ... The transaction also builds on Pepkor’s broader financial services expansion. Flash processed R34.7bn in transaction value during the six months to end-March, up 20.3% from a year earlier. Over the same period, its network of active traders grew to 176,000. ... Capfin’s unsecured lending book increased to R5.3bn, while FoneYam expanded its active smartphone rental customer base to 2.4-million. Abacus Insurance doubled its revenue as insurance products were rolled out across more of the group’s retail brands. ... Pepkor recently received regulatory approval to establish a bank, with its first products expected to launch later this year. The group has previously said it serves about 26-million customers, more than 12-million of whom are active on its digital platforms. ... Financially, both businesses entering the transaction have recorded strong growth. Flash generated revenue of R11.15bn and earnings before interest, taxes, depreciation and amortisation (ebitda) of R900m in the year to September 2025, while Shop2Shop reported revenue of R9.33bn and ebitda of R661m in the year to June 2025. Shop2Shop’s ebitda has grown at a compound annual rate of 85% over the past three years. ... The transaction remains subject to competition and other regulatory approvals, with Pepkor expecting implementation in roughly nine months. ... in the pound seats as group plans to list fintech business ... ### Pepkor flexes financial services muscle ahead of bank launch ... plans for debit card"
IMPACT RETAIL,,,,Exa,Kathmandu powers KMD Brands as Rip Curl stumbles - Inside Retail Australia,insideretail.com.au (Kaycee Enerva),2026-07-22T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-22 00:00:00+00:00,,https://insideretail.com.au/business/kathmandu-powers-kmd-brands-as-rip-curl-stumbles-202607,"Kathmandu powers KMD Brands as Rip Curl stumbles - Inside Retail Australia ... KMD Brands expects higher sales and earnings in FY26, with strong momentum at Kathmandu offsetting softer trading at Rip Curl. ... The retailer forecasts group sales of $863.2 million to $866.5 million, up 5 per cent from last year, while underlying EBITDA (earnings before interest, taxes, depreciation and amortisation) is expected to more than double to $31.5 million to $34 million. ... Kathmandu continued to outperform in the second half, with direct-to-consumer same-store sales, including online, rising 4.8 per cent in the 24 weeks to July 19. ... Demand for rainwear, fleece, and base layers was said to remain strong, although sales of insulated products were affected by an “unseasonably warm winter” on Australia’s east coast. Trading in New Zealand also outperformed Australia. ... In contrast, Rip Curl’s same-store sales dropped by 2.8 per cent, reflecting weaker consumer demand, increased promotional activity, and a smaller benefit from favourable foreign exchange movements. ... Meanwhile, Oboz returned to year-on-year sales growth in the fourth quarter, supported by online demand and new product launches. ... Separately, the company will also divest its manufacturing facility in Thailand, with production to be phased out over the next 12 months. ... The sale is expected to generate $4.2 million to $5.8 million in net property proceeds and release around $5 million in working capital, strengthening the group’s balance sheet."
IMPACT RETAIL,,,,Exa,"Reliance Retail revenue up 7.4% in Q1 FY27, profit falls 14.2%",www.retail-insight-network.com (Shubhendu Vimal),2026-07-20T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-20 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.retail-insight-network.com/news/reliance-retail-revenue-q1-fy27-revenue/,"Reliance Retail revenue up 7.4% in Q1 FY27, profit falls 14.2% ... India’s Reliance Retail has reported consolidated gross revenue of Rs904.08bn ($9.38bn) for the quarter ended 30 June 2026, marking a 7.4% year-on-year (YoY) increase. ... The company attributed top line growth to double-digit gains in grocery, fashion and lifestyle, and consumer electronics. ... Adjusted for the demerger of its consumer brands business, revenue growth stood at 11.6% YoY. ... Profit after tax fell 14.2% YoY to Rs28.06bn while EBITDA from operations declined 1.8% YoY to Rs59.35bn, translating to an operating EBITDA margin of 7.4%. ... The retailer expanded its footprint with 252 new stores during the quarter, taking its total network to 20,169 outlets across 78.4 million square feet, a 1% rise YoY. ... Registered customers grew 10.6% YoY to 396 million, and transaction volumes rose 46.0% YoY to 568 million. ... By segment, consumer electronics recorded like-for-like (LFL) growth of 16% YoY, driven by demand across air conditioners, laptops, mobile phones and small appliances, while its ResQ services arm grew revenue by 27% YoY. ... Fashion and lifestyle posted LFL growth of 4% YoY, with own brands’ share up 380 basis points and digital commerce contributing 27.3% of apparel & footwear revenue in the segment. ... Grocery LFL growth reached 7% YoY, led by staples, dairy and frozen & bakery. ... The company noted that customers shopping across both online and offline channels spent 2.7 times more than offline-only shoppers. ... The B2B business saw average bill value rise 15% YoY. ... On digital operations, JioMart now serves approximately 5,500 pin codes, with more than 2,500 digital and fashion and lifestyle stores offering two-hour delivery. ... Average daily orders on grocery digital commerce climbed 116% YoY, and digital’s share of grocery B2C revenue rose 160 basis points to 13.4%. ... Reliance Retail executive director Isha Ambani said: “Our continued investment in digital commerce underscores the transformative power of our digital platforms. ... “Our expanding customer base, widest store network, and growing omni-channel capabilities position us well to continue fulfilling every need, every dream, for every Indian, every day.”"
IMPACT RETAIL,,,,Exa,"Reliance Retail Unveils Ambitious Plan for Online Growth with JioMart and Dark Stores by FY27, ETRetail",retail.economictimes.indiatimes.com (www.ETRetail.com),2026-07-20T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-20 00:00:00+00:00,,https://retail.economictimes.indiatimes.com/news/industry/reliance-retail-maps-online-business-growth-with-jiomart-dark-stores-in-fy27/132505072,"Reliance Retail Unveils Ambitious Plan for Online Growth with JioMart and Dark Stores by FY27, ETRetail ... # Reliance Retail maps online business growth with JioMart, dark stores in FY27 ... Reliance Retail plans rapid online business scaling and dark store expansion. The company will invest in JioMart and omni-channel reach across platforms. Operational metrics will improve with a market-by-market focus on positive unit economics. Reliance Retail aims for 2x Operating EBITDA by FY28 and FY29. This scale will create value through improved margins and stronger cash generation. ... Reliance Retail plans rapid online business scaling and dark store expansionNew Delhi: Reliance Retail Ventures Ltd (RRVL) will 'rapidly' scale its online business, expand dark stores and strengthen omni-channel capabilities, as the retailer expects these investments to translate into higher margins and earnings growth, a top company official said.Outlining a three-year roadmap, Reliance Retail CFO Dinesh Taluja said the company will invest in the infrastructure of JioMart and omni-channel reach across platforms, even as margins have come under pressure from rising technology and dark-store investments. ... ""We are looking at growing our online businesses pretty rapidly during this year. We will expand dark stores. We will grow our omni-channel platforms. We will grow JioMart,"" Taluja said in the company's earnings call.Besides, it will also focus on improving the operational metrics around availability, speed, and reliability with a market-by-market focus on achieving positive unit economics.""Each market, the unit economics, we need to have a clear path to positive unit economics. Accordingly, we are evaluating each and every market and focusing our investments in that manner,"" Taluja said.By FY28 and FY29, the company expects to convert scale into value by increasing repeat rates, basket size and customer lifetime value. In the next three years, Reliance Retail has an ambition of ""2x Operating EBITDA"". ... EBITDA refers to earnings before interest, taxes, depreciation and amortisation.According to him, the scale being built this year is expected to create value through improved margins and stronger cash generation over the next two years as customer acquisition, repeat purchases and basket sizes improve.The company also plans to improve profitability through a higher share of private labels, increased monetisation opportunities and greater marketplace income.""We will use all these levers to improve economics, which will start reflecting meaningfully in the numbers over the next two years,"" he said. ... Taluja said the current year will be focused on laying a strong foundation while pursuing disciplined growth, with emphasis on customer quality rather than merely chasing volumes.The company has set internal targets around metrics such as order density at dark stores, repeat purchase rates, fulfilment costs and contribution margins, and will calibrate investments based on performance.""We will grow quite quickly, but we will also look at the quality of business, not just the volume,"" he said.As benefits from higher order density, better product mix, productivity gains, improved inventory turns and monetisation initiatives begin to accrue, Reliance Retail expects returns on capital to improve, with EBITDA and cash generation accelerating in the coming years, Taluja added.The company is also witnessing strong traction from its omni-channel strategy. According to Taluja, omni-channel customers spend about 2.7 times more than pure offline customers, while their spending has grown 20-25 per cent year-on-year.He said Reliance Retail's fashion quick-commerce offering, AJIO Rush, has also recorded strong momentum, with order volumes rising 136 per cent sequentially during the quarter.Taluja noted that the service, which offers fashion deliveries within two to four hours, was launched only a few quarters ago and is still in the early stages of scaling up.Now AJIO, AJIO Rush and the store network are being managed as one omni-channel proposition. AJIO Luxe now offers 1,000+ brands, expanding participation in premium consumption, said an earnings presentation of Reliance Industries.Moreover, average daily digital orders more than doubled, growing 116 per cent year-on-year during the quarter.Reliance Retail is the largest quick commerce player in the country, which operates through its digital retail arm JioMart, and has a wide geographical coverage.JioMart utilises a hybrid model of 3,100+ physical stores and 600+ dark stores to service more than 1,200 cities and cover 5,100 pin codes."
IMPACT RETAIL,,,,Exa,THE ICONIC rebrands retail media business to ICONIC Media - retailbiz,www.retailbiz.com.au (Celene Ignacio),2026-07-20T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-20 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.retailbiz.com.au/online-retailing/the-iconic-rebrands-retail-media-business-to-iconic-media/,"THE ICONIC rebrands retail media business to ICONIC Media - retailbiz ... THE ICONIC has rebranded its unified retail media offering to ICONIC Media, consolidating its advertising, first-party data and marketing services under a single brand for more than 1,500 fashion and lifestyle partners. ... The retailer said the rebrand is designed to provide brands with a full-funnel retail media offering, combining onsite advertising, customer insights and campaign opportunities aimed at driving awareness, product discovery and sales. ... At the centre of the offering is THE ICONIC’s proprietary self-service advertising platform, which allows brands to create, manage and optimise onsite advertising campaigns through a centralised dashboard. Partners can also access first-party customer insights and campaign performance metrics, including return on investment and halo attribution. ... According to THE ICONIC, its advertising platform recorded 160 per cent year-on-year revenue growth during the first half of 2026, while Sponsored Products achieved an average return on ad spend (ROAS) of 390 per cent. The platform currently supports more than 290 brands. ... ICONIC Media also provides brands with the opportunity to participate in THE ICONIC’s marketing calendar through influencer campaigns, content initiatives, offsite media placements, creative campaigns and customer activations. ... Over the past 12 months, THE ICONIC said it has delivered campaigns including Nike’s Outrun the Delivery Van, influencer campaigns with Polo Ralph Lauren, onsite activations with PayPal and integrated flyer campaigns with Uber. ... ICONIC Media is available to all of THE ICONIC’s fashion and lifestyle brand partners, as well as strategic partners."
