NECTAR 360 SERVICES,,,,Tavily,What Salesforce's ‘No Browser Required’ Moment Means For Enterprise Software,,,,,https://www.forbes.com/councils/forbestechcouncil/2026/08/05/what-salesforces-no-browser-required-moment-means-for-enterprise-software,"API-first platforms, built without a legacy UI model, were already routing around Salesforce's perimeter. If Benioff had not opened Headless 360, the market would have eventually moved to platforms that were natively agent-compatible. Salesforce would have found itself a database pretending to be a workflow engine, while lighter and more composable systems took the real work. So, the strategic logic is sound: Cannibalize your own licensing model before someone else does it for you. [...] We see this at the implementation level. The companies extracting real value from agentic systems are not the ones that deployed the most agents. They're the ones that invested in data quality, permission architecture and workflow governance that make agents reliable rather than just active. ## The Trojan Horse Risk There's another consequence of Headless 360 that deserves more attention. By exposing the platform through MCP, APIs and CLI, Salesforce isn't only making Agentforce more useful—it's al"
