MISTRAL AI SAS,,,,Exa,ABN AMRO partners with Mistral to develop AI tools,www.retailbankerinternational.com (Vidhya Edwards Munnangi),2026-08-07T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-07 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.retailbankerinternational.com/news/abn-amro-partners-with-mistral-to-develop-ai-tools/,"ABN AMRO partners with Mistral to develop AI tools # ABN AMRO partners with Mistral to develop AI tools The work will bring together ABN AMRO’s banking knowledge and Mistral’s AI technology. Vidhya Edwards Munnangi August 7, 2026 ... ABN AMRO will be able to use AI capabilities created and overseen in Europe. Credit: Devina Saputri/ Shutterstock.com. ABN AMRO has agreed a partnership with Mistral to examine and build AI-based tools for the bank. ... The tie-up is Mistral’s first with a major Dutch bank and centres on the two groups’ focus on European competitiveness, autonomy and digital resilience, noted ABN AMRO. ... Under the arrangement, ABN AMRO will be able to use AI capabilities created and overseen in Europe, which the bank said would lessen reliance on technology suppliers from outside the region and support major shifts affecting Europe. ... The companies said they intend to create AI applications for the bank that meet its requirements on security, transparency, privacy and regulatory compliance, while remaining in line with European values. ... AMRO chief innovation ... technology officer Car ... Bittner said: ... Partnering with Mistral allows us to leverage state ... AI capabilities while committing to ... European innovation. In a rapidly changing world, Europe’s ability to remain digital resilient, competitive and strategically autonomous is more important than ever. Together, we are helping build a safer and more innovative future for our customers ... Those divisions include the acquired Hauck Aufhäuser Lampe and NIBC Bank. ... Meanwhile, this May, BNP Paribas expanded its partnership with Mistral AI for three years to enhance its generative AI capabilities."
MISTRAL AI SAS,,,,Exa,Dutch banking giant taps Mistral for European AI push,fintech.global (Molly Snaylam),2026-08-06T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-06 00:00:00+00:00,,https://fintech.global/2026/08/06/dutch-banking-giant-taps-mistral-for-european-ai-push/,"Dutch banking giant taps Mistral for European AI push ... ABN AMRO, a Dutch bank, has agreed a strategic partnership with Mistral, Europe’s fastest-growing Frontier AI lab, to jointly explore and build artificial intelligence tools for the bank, marking the French lab’s first tie-up with a major Netherlands-based lender. ... The agreement is designed to give ABN AMRO access to frontier AI technology that is built and overseen within Europe, lessening the bank’s reliance on AI providers based outside the continent. Both firms have framed the deal as a step towards strengthening Europe’s strategic autonomy and digital resilience at a time when the region is under pressure to reduce its dependence on foreign technology infrastructure. ... Under the partnership, ABN AMRO’s banking expertise will be paired with Mistral’s independent AI systems to produce applications aimed at meeting the bank’s requirements around security, transparency, data privacy and regulatory compliance. The two organisations said the resulting tools would be developed in line with European values and standards, rather than adapted from technology built elsewhere. ... Both companies pointed to shared roots in Europe as a driving factor behind the tie-up, with each citing a mutual ambition to support the continent’s financial, economic and digital resilience through home-grown innovation rather than imported systems. ... ABN AMRO is a Dutch bank. Mistral is described as Europe’s fastest-growing Frontier AI lab, providing advanced and independently developed artificial intelligence capabilities. ... Carsten Bittner, chief innovation & technology officer at ABN AMRO said, “Partnering with Mistral allows us to leverage state of the art AI capabilities while committing to trusted European innovation.” ... “In a rapidly changing world, Europe’s ability to remain digital resilient, competitive and strategically autonomous is more important than ever. Together, we are helping build a safer and more innovative future for our customers, businesses and society,” he said. ... Marjorie Janiewicz, chief revenue officer at Mistral added, “ABN AMRO is one of Europe’s most forward-thinking financial institutions. Their commitment to building AI on their own terms – with full control, full transparency, and a clear view of the value it creates – is exactly the standard we believe every major European institution should set. We are proud to be their partner in that ambition.”"
MISTRAL AI SAS,,,,Exa,Mistral introduces Shieldstral to provide lightweight policy-aware moderation for AI models - SiliconANGLE,siliconangle.com (Kyt Dotson),2026-08-05T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-05 00:00:00+00:00,,https://siliconangle.com/2026/08/05/mistral-introduces-shieldstral-provide-lightweight-policy-aware-moderation-ai-models/,"Mistral introduces Shieldstral to provide lightweight policy-aware moderation for AI models - SiliconANGLE ... ### Mistral introduces Shieldstral to provide lightweight policy-aware moderation for AI models ... French artificial intelligence startup Mistral AI SAS today introduced a lightweight multimodal safety artificial intelligence open-weight model that can classify outputs for AI models that outperforms other large language models up to seven times its size, setting a new standard for moderation. ... The new model, named Shieldstral, allows developers to write policies in natural language questions at runtime, and the model returns a safety score. ... It requires no specialized retraining and stores positive capabilities for both text and images. It also provides a verdict in the form of a single token: a “yes” or a “no,” making the result completely ... According to the company, the model has extremely strong text safety, matching or outperforming models that outweigh it by over seven times across diverse safety benchmarks, ranking an overall average safety score of 84.9%. It also achieves an average multimodal image safety benchmark of 83.8%, outperforming all evaluated baselines. ... Mistral taught Shield ... al to discriminate, not merely memorize policies. This means that in its reasoning and classification performance, the model can apply two closely related but different policies and keep them separate in its “brain,” for example, “ ... this about malware,” or “is this about cybersecurity,” and acknowledge when one is violated and ... other is not. ... For malware content, ... can handle what’s called “contrastive pairs” like near-identical ransomware outputs: one version that walks a reader through writing and deploying their own malware, the other analyzes ransomware behavior so a defender can detect it. ... model is trained to assign ... first to a “malware instructions” policy (which is a violation) and the second to a “cybersecurity discussion” policy (which is okay), so at runtime it can apply a fine-grained distinction. ... A single natural language prompt covers text, images, and text-plus-image content across prompts, responses, and prompt-response pairs. Policies can be free-form queries and re-targeted at inference time, allowing users to easily customize and determine whether content outputs are safe, and to quickly determine whether inputs or outputs are safe. ... This means that it can be used to quickly generate “yes” or “no” responses for customer service text safety, AI assistant refusal detection for dangerous requests, policy violations and image generation security. ... At only 3 billion parameters, the model is also lightweight enough to run on a single 16-gigabyte graphics processing unit. This also means that it can run alongside a much larger model extremely quickly and efficiently to guardrail inputs or outputs with little extra delay, even in edge environments, where memory and compute bandwidth are scarce. ... The company said Shieldstral will become a stepping stone toward moderation that can adapt to context instead of enforcing rigid taxonomic guesses onto conversations. This will allow for more natural multilingual coverage and longer-document coverage in the future, alongside broader multimodal safety. ... - Mistral introduces Shieldstral to provide lightweight policy-aware moderation for AI models - Cloudflare launches Identity-Aware AI Gateway to track who is using AI - Vectra AI launches Vectra AI Pro to feed AI agents better attack signals - White House, AI firms keep safety framework talks private - Arista Networks' stock jumps on stellar earnings and revenue beat and strong forecast - Bending Spoons to acquire productivity software startup Airtable for $1.285B ... Mistral introduces Shieldstral to provide lightweight policy-aware moderation for AI models"
MISTRAL AI SAS,,,,Exa,Mistral Is in the Right Place at the Right Time | WIRED,www.wired.com (Joel Khalili),2026-08-04T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-04 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.wired.com/story/mistral-is-in-the-right-place-at-the-right-time/,"Mistral Is in the Right Place at the Right Time | WIRED ... Mistral is having a moment. With access to less funding and fewer compute resources than OpenAI and Anthropic, the French AI lab has lagged behind its American rivals in model performance. But recent turmoil stateside has created a window of opportunity."
MISTRAL AI SAS,,,,Exa,Mistral Is in the Right Place at the Right Time | WIRED,nehtus.com (Joel Khalili),2026-08-04T00:00:00.000Z,2026-08-04 00:00:00+00:00,,https://nehtus.com/story/mistral-is-in-the-right-place-at-the-right-time/,"Mistral Is in the Right Place at the Right Time | WIRED ... Open-weight AI models are having a moment in the wake of recent turmoil at US tech giants. For French AI lab Mistral, that’s the the best thing that could have happened. ... Mistral is having a moment. With access to less funding and fewer compute resources than OpenAI and Anthropic, the French AI lab has lagged behind its American rivals in model performance. But recent turmoil stateside has created a window of opportunity. ... In June, the Trump administration placed restrictions on the distribution of models from Anthropic and OpenAI, giving Europe a glimpse of an unwelcome future in which its access to algoing-edge AI could be suddenly revoked. A few weeks later, one of OpenAI’s models broke loose from a testing sandbox and hacked multiple companies; Anthropic then revealed that its models had engaged in similar behavior. The incidents revived a long-running debate over safety risks tied to proprietary, closed-weight models, whose inner-workings are a closely guarded secret. ... frames itself as the antidote: a Europe-based alternative to the American labs, whose models—most ... open source license ... Mensch’s argument is self-serving but effective. Last September, Mistral raised almost $2 billion at a $13.5 billion valuation; it's reportedly teeing up another raise that will bump that figure to $23 billion. The lab’s revenue has reportedly increased 20-fold in the past year, helped along by deals with the French government, Microsoft, HSBC, and others. ... The recent surge in the adoption of open-weight models is part of that picture. One of few ways that European businesses can guarantee undisrupted access to AI, Mensch argues, is to run open-weight models on domestic infrastructure. “Everybody outside the US and China should participate in the open source ecosystem, because it takes leverage away,” says Nicolas Granatino, founder of startup accelerator StemAI, who holds a stake in Mistral in a personal capacity. ... Until fairly recently, it was unclear how to monetize open-weight models effectively, according to Granatino. Unlike the leading American labs, locked in a race to superintelligence, Mistral has shifted its focus toward smaller, bespoke models for manufacturing, utilities, and financial services. It has also developed a cloud business through which customers can access its models, and a Palantir-style team of engineers who embed within client organizations. “At the moment, we see the emergence of a product that is making the open source commitment easier,” says Granatino. “You can make money running the infrastructure” and help clients to customize models with their own data. ... Whether Mistral has arrived at this juncture through foresight, blind good fortune, or a combination of both, the algohold of the American labs is beginning to loosen as more businesses turn to open-weight models. Though gaps in publicly available data confuse the picture, the market share of open-weight models appears to be rising steeply, driven by rapid growth in the adoption of Chinese models, like DeepSeek, in particular. ... “We revealed to the world that you could actually build AI systems outside the control of US labs,” Mensch says. “That is now changing the structure of the market itself.”"
MISTRAL AI SAS,,,,Exa,Arthur Mensch’s Mistral Is Europe’s Best Bet for Sovereign A.I. | Observer,observer.com (Tim Keary),2026-07-30T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-30 00:00:00+00:00,,https://observer.com/2026/07/arthur-menschs-mistral-is-europes-best-bet-for-sovereign-a-i/,"Arthur Mensch’s ... Is Europe’s Best Bet for Sovereign ... .I. | ... Mistral AI is Europe’s largest A.I. startup and its best bet for sovereign A.I. The Paris-based company is in talks to raise fresh capital at a €20 billion ($22 billion) valuation as it locks in a multibillion-dollar infrastructure deal with Microsoft announced July 21. Under the agreement, Microsoft will help fund Mistral’s data center buildout in Europe, while Azure customers gain access to Mistral’s European-hosted infrastructure and two new models—Medium 3.5 and OCR 4—added to Microsoft Foundry. ... Sovereign A.I. is the ability of a company or country to develop and deploy A.I. systems independently. This includes data, infrastructure, models and compute resources. Accenture estimated last year that 60 percent of European organizations were planning to increase investments in sovereign A.I. technology. The idea is to reduce dependence on third-party nation-states and organizations. “By putting Mistral’s models on Azure Local and on Mistral’s computational capacity, we can combine American and European technology and do it in a way that provides continuous and assured access,” Microsoft President Brad Smith told Reuters in a joint interview with Mistral CEO Arthur Mensch published last week. ... In February, Mistral announced it was committing 1.2 billion euros ($1.4 billion) to build data centers across Sweden. Mensch framed the investment as “a concrete step towards building independent capabilities in Europe” and reinforcing the region’s “strategic autonomy and competitiveness.” ... Then in March, Mistral raised $830 million in debt financing from banks including Bpifrance, HSBC and others, to build a data center near Paris. This includes financing over 13,000 Nvidia chips to power the facility. The company also noted it planned to build 200 megawatts of capacity across Europe by the end of 2027 “to support the demand from governments and enterprises that seek to build and control their own A.I.” ... Today, the startup offers a range of A.I. models, including its frontier multimodal model Medium 3.5, an open-weight model optimized for agentic and coding use cases, and OCR 4, an advanced document-intelligence model designed to extract and structure content from documents. It’s also expanding its infrastructure offerings. ... Its customers include a mix of large enterprises, public-sector organizations and industrial firms, including ASML, AXA, Airbus, BMW, CMA CGM, Ericsson, HSBC, Stellantis and the European Patent Office. Its main investors include Bpifrance, Lightspeed Venture Partners, General Catalyst, Andreessen Horowitz and Nvidia. ... While Mistral does not have the financial scale of U.S. frontier providers, it makes up for this in its strategic importance as the prime frontier A.I. vendor in Europe. Mensch told the Financial Times at the beginning of this year that the company had an annualized revenue run rate of over $400 million. ... The case for sovereign A.I. gained urgency earlier this year when Anthropic temporarily suspended access to Fable and Mythos, two of its most powerful models, after the U.S. government forced the startup to implement export controls requiring it to restrict access to foreign nationals. The incident underscored that European companies can’t afford to assume uninterrupted access to U.S. frontier models and reinforced Mistral’s positioning as a local alternative. “We exist to make sure that everyone gets access to the best A.I. systems, outside of centralized control exercised by states or corporations that feel the need to control in-fine deployment of A.I.,” Mensch said in a post on LinkedIn days after the ban. ... Mistral’s journey isn’t just about building better models, but establishing the computer infrastructure to meet customer demand, with Mensch aiming to make the company a “full stack player” in A.I., targeting 1 gigawatt of compute capacity by 2030. Building local capacity and reducing dependence on U.S. hyperscalers has the potential to pay off long term, particularly if it attracts interest from enterprises that want to process their data close to home. ... “Mistral is becoming much more than an A.I. model provider,” Scott Bickley, advisory fellow at Info-Tech Research Group, told Observer. Bickley explained that its Mistral Compute offering also includes GPUs, orchestration (such as Workflows, its orchestration layer for A.I. apps and agents), APIs and managed infrastructure that can be deployed as a service on bare-metal physical servers or in customer-controlled environments. ... The deal with Mistral also gives Microsoft an opportunity to mitigate risk. “Microsoft has major relationships with OpenAI and Anthropic, but no cloud provider wants its A.I. strategy controlled by one or two external model companies,” Bickley said. “Mistral gives Microsoft greater negotiating leverage, model diversity and a stronger response to European political concerns about concentration and extra-territorial dependence.”"
MISTRAL AI SAS,,,,Exa,Samsung Reportedly Weighs €1B Mistral AI Investment,www.techrepublic.com (David Curry),2026-07-23T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-23 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.techrepublic.com/article/news-samsung-mistral-ai-investment-emea/,"Samsung Reportedly Weighs €1B Mistral AI Investment ... # Samsung Reportedly Weighs €1B Mistral AI Investment ... Samsung is reportedly weighing a €1 billion investment in Mistral, giving the French AI lab a possible €20 billion valuation as Europe pushes sovereign AI. ... South Korean electronics giant Samsung is reportedly discussing a major investment in French AI lab Mistral as part of a funding round that could value the company at €20 billion. ... The Galaxy smartphone maker could invest around €1 billion in Mistral, forming part of a larger fundraising round involving multiple investors. ... The deal would benefit both parties: Samsung is looking to expand its AI partnerships and gain a stake in Europe’s best-known AI model developer, while Mistral could secure favorable terms or priority access to semiconductor and memory chips. The Financial Times first reported the potential fundraising agreement. ... Mistral last raised money in September last year, when it completed a €1.7 billion funding round led by Dutch semiconductor manufacturing giant ASML. It was valued at €11.7 billion at the time, meaning the new round would represent a 70% increase in valuation in less than a year. ... Mistral’s value to the European market has increased following the US government’s decision to block Anthropic Fable and Mythos from being exported outside the country. With China also apparently considering withholding some of its leading-edge AI models, European businesses may have few options for frontier AI. ... The European Union may also make it harder for US and Chinese AI models to be used by government departments and officials due to sovereignty laws requiring data to remain inside the EU and not be used to train AI models. Critical industries such as banking, healthcare, and telecoms are also required to retain data and demonstrate strong governance, guarantees that may not be available from some US and Chinese AI providers. ... While Mistral is not seen as being on the same level in terms of broad capability and sophistication as Anthropic and OpenAI, it has performed well on several key benchmarks for enterprise customers. It has also been awarded a contract by the French military, showing that its models can be used for critical government workloads. ... Even with the 70% increase in valuation, Mistral would still be valued far below rival AI developers in the US. SpaceX, while primarily an aerospace company, sold investors on a future in which it would generate the majority of its revenue through AI. It went public at a $1.77 trillion valuation, which climbed to more than $2.5 trillion before falling back to $1.5 trillion two weeks later. ... Anthropic and OpenAI, the two largest pure-play AI model developers, are both planning to go public either late this year or in early 2027. Both are targeting valuations of $1 trillion, although they may exceed that by the time they list, with valuations across the industry continuing to balloon. ... Chinese AI labs have also gained attention for improving model performance at lower cost, though many remain valued below leading US AI companies. ... For Mistral to be valued at only €20 billion says something about the difficulty non-US vendors face in generating significant revenue or attracting large numbers of users. ... Also read: China’s ... model export controls could further narrow access to frontier models for companies outside the US and China."
MISTRAL AI SAS,,,,Exa,Samsung reportedly in talks to invest about EUR1 billion in Mistral AI,www.digitimes.com (Sherri Wang),2026-07-23T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-23 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.digitimes.com/news/a20260722VL226/samsung-mistral-ai-investment-fundraising-infrastructure.html,"Samsung reportedly in talks to invest about EUR1 billion in Mistral AI ... # Samsung reportedly in talks to invest about EUR1 billion in Mistral AI ... Sherri Wang ... , DIGITIMES, Taipei Jul 23, 2026, 10:44 0 Samsung is in talks to invest about EUR1 billion (approx. US$1.14 billion) in French AI startup Mistral as part of a wider fundraising round, the Financial Times reported, citing people familiar with the matter."
MISTRAL AI SAS,,,,Exa,Samsung mulls major investment in AI startup Mistral at €20 billion valuation - SamMobile,www.sammobile.com (Adnan Farooqui),2026-07-23T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-23 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.sammobile.com/news/samsung-mulls-major-investment-in-ai-startup-mistral-at-e20-billion-valuation/,"Samsung mulls major investment in AI startup Mistral at €20 billion valuation - SamMobile ... # Samsung mulls major investment in AI startup Mistral at €20 billion valuation ... It could invest €1 billion. ... - Samsung is reportedly discussing a roughly €1 billion investment in Mistral AI, which could value the French startup at €20 billion. - Mistral has already been courting Samsung, with CEO Arthur Mensch meeting top Samsung executives in South Korea earlier this year. - The talks may be tied to broader cooperation on semiconductors and custom AI chips, but neither company has confirmed a deal. ... Based in Paris, Mistral is one of the hottest AI startups in Europe. It's routinely talked about with the same reverence as some of the other leading AI startups, many of which are based in the United States. ... Earlier reports have suggested that there's something cooking between Samsung and Mistral. A new report suggests that Samsung may soon invest a significant sum in Mistral at a €20 billion valuation for the startup. ... ## Mistral has been courting Samsung for a while now ... Mistral AI CEO Arthur Mensch made a visit to Samsung's Hwaseong campus in South Korea earlier this year. He met with the company's top executives during his trip, including semiconductor business head Jun Young-hyun and Jay Y. Lee, the organization's top boss. ... That visit may have revolved around semiconductors, including memory chips and the manufacturing of custom AI chips. If Mistral was to ever design one, it would need a foundry partner to make them. ... The collaboration might end up being more than that. The Financial Times reports that Samsung is in discussions to invest in Mistral. It would become a part of the fund raising round that could value the AI startup at €20 billion. Samsung's likely investment in Mistral could be €1 billion. ... An investment at this scale would provide Samsung with a significant stake in one of the leading AI startups in the region. There hasn't been a confirmation of the deal from either Mistral or Samsung as yet, though."
MISTRAL AI SAS,,,,Exa,"Mistral AI raises $830m in debt financing for data center in Paris, France - DCD",www.datacenterdynamics.com,2026-07-22T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-22 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.datacenterdynamics.com/en/news/mistral-ai-raises-830m-in-debt-financing-for-data-center-in-paris-france/,"Mistral AI raises $830m in debt financing for data center in Paris, France - DCD ... # Mistral AI raises $830m in debt financing for data center in Paris, France ... AI lab targets 200MW capacity across Europe by 2027 ... French artificial intelligence lab Mistral AI has raised $830 million in debt financing to fund the purchase of GPUs for its planned data center outside Paris. ... The cash will be used to finance Nvidia Grace Blackwell infrastructure, and give Mistral, which makes ChatGPT competitor Le Chat, access to 13,800 Nvidia GB300 GPUs and 44MW of powered capacity. ... It will install the hardware at a data center in Bruyères-le-Châtel, south of Paris, which is owned and run by French data center firm Eclairion, and intends to start operations at the site by the end of June. ... In a statement posted on LinkedIn, Mistral said the financing “underscores confidence in our vision: Europe needs an ambitious AI cloud infrastructure and an independent AI stack.” ... The company added: “Our objective is to secure 200MW of capacity across Europe by the end of 2027 to support the demand from governments and enterprises that seek to build and control their own AI.” ... Mistral has raised the cash from a consortium of lenders comprising Bpifrance, BNP Paribas, Crédit Agricole CIB, HSBC, La Banque Postale, MUFG, and Natixis Corporate & Investment Banking. It follows an equity funding round last year, led by ASML, which raised €1.7 billion ($1.99bn) and valued Mistral at €11.7 billion ($13.67bn). ... The company, seen by many in Europe as the best sovereign alternative to US AI labs OpenAI and Anthropic, announced a deal with Eclairion to lease data center space in February last year. DCD visited the site in April as part of an in-depth feature on the French AI data center market. ... At the time, Mistral said it would be procuring 18,000 Nvidia GB200 GPUs from French AI cloud provider Scaleway, with a view to opening the data center in the summer of 2025 at a capacity of 40MW. It would seem the project has been delayed, and the company has now opted for the newer generation of Nvidia hardware instead. ... Whether the deal with Scaleway remains in place has not been disclosed. DCD has contacted Mistral for comment. ... Elsewhere in Europe, Mistral has signed a deal with EcoDataCenter to install AI infrastructure at the colo firm’s data centers in Sweden."
MISTRAL AI SAS,,,,Exa,Mistral AI strikes multibillion-dollar deal with Microsoft to build out Azure infrastructure in Europe - SiliconANGLE,siliconangle.com (Mike Wheatley),2026-07-22T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-22 00:00:00+00:00,,https://siliconangle.com/2026/07/21/mistral-ai-strikes-multibillion-dollar-deal-microsoft-build-azure-infrastructure-europe/,"Mistral AI strikes multibillion-dollar deal with Microsoft to build out Azure infrastructure in Europe - SiliconANGLE ... ### Mistral AI strikes multibillion-dollar deal with Microsoft to build out Azure infrastructure in Europe ... French artificial intelligence startup Mistral AI SAS has struck a multibillion-dollar deal with Microsoft Corp. to expand its computing infrastructure in Europe and increase the availability of its top large language models in the U.S. ... In an announcement today, Microsoft said that Azure cloud customers will soon be able to develop software using Mistral’s data centers located in France. The agreement gives Microsoft more capacity to cater to AI customers in Europe, while enabling regulated industries to access an alternative to U.S.-owned models and computing infrastructure. ... The deal also sees two of Mistral’s top models listed in Microsoft Azure’s Foundry, which is a cloud-based service for developing AI applications. Mistral Medium 3.5 and OCR 4 have both been added to Foundry’s model catalog. The former is an open-weight model that can be customized and deployed within managed Azure environments, while the latter is designed for structured document processing workloads, automation and agentic workflows that require data to be extracted from business documents. ... With Azure Foundry, businesses can discover, customize and deploy AI models and agents within the Azure cloud. The platform extended these capabilities to Azure Local environments that are located close to their customer’s data, employees and physical operations to address data residency requirements. With these models, companies will be able to develop AI applications once and then deploy them in different environments, based on regional data laws, and also their operational and connectivity requirements. ... The announcement underscores the renewed push by European governments to reduce their dependence on U.S.-made technology and ensure they have more control over their future economies. Though the push for “sovereign” AI is nothing new, it has taken on greater urgency in recent weeks after America shocked some of its European allies by blocking their access to leading models from Anthropic PBC and OpenAI Group PBC due to what it said were “security concerns.” ... Microsoft President Brand Smith told Reuters in an interview that the partnership will enable this sovereignty while still providing European firms with access to cutting-edge U.S. software capabilities and security features. “By ⁠putting Mistral’s models on Azure Local and on Mistral’s computational capacity, we can combine American and European technology and do it in a way that provides continuous and assured access,” he promised. ... While the partnership may seem promising for sovereignty efforts, there’s a long way to go before European countries can claim true independence from U.S. technology. For instance, European-designed and manufactured chips are almost non-existent. Even in Mistral’s European data centers, the vast majority of the AI chips will be supplied by Nvidia Corp. and others, such as Advanced Micro Devices Inc. ... Mistral Chief Executive Arthur Mensch declined to comment on a report published by Bloomberg last week that his company is looking to raise €3 billion(around $3.4 billion) at a €20 billion valuation, and also said Microsoft won’t be increasing its stake in the company through today’s deal. Like Nvidia, Microsoft is a key investor in Mistral. ... The Paris-based frontier model lab has become one of Europe’s brightest lights in the AI industry. Though its valuation has not kept pace with U.S. giants such as OpenAI and Anthropic, it’s regarded as an extremely capable AI developer, with top-performing models targeting areas such as manufacturing, financial services and the defense industry. One of its biggest customers is the French armed forces. ... Mensch said the deal underscores how Microsoft and his company are making real progress toward “closing the gap on the infrastructure side” in Europe. He has previously said the company aims to have 1 gigawatt of compute capacity up and running by 2030, and believes today’s agreement validates those ambitions."
MISTRAL AI SAS,,,,Exa,Mistral AI strikes multibillion-dollar deal with Microsoft to build out Azure infrastructure in Europe - SiliconANGLE,siliconangle.com (Mike Wheatley),2026-07-22T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-22 00:00:00+00:00,,https://siliconangle.com/2026/07/21/mistral-ai-strikes-multibillion-deal-microsoft-build-azure-infrastructure-europe/,"Mistral AI strikes multibillion-dollar deal with Microsoft to build out Azure infrastructure in Europe - SiliconANGLE ... ### Mistral AI strikes multibillion-dollar deal with Microsoft to build out Azure infrastructure in Europe ... French artificial intelligence startup Mistral AI SAS has struck a multibillion-dollar deal with Microsoft Corp. to expand its computing infrastructure in Europe and increase the availability of its top large language models in the U.S. ... In an announcement today, Microsoft said that Azure cloud customers will soon be able to develop software using Mistral’s data centers located in France. The agreement gives Microsoft more capacity to cater to AI customers in Europe, while enabling regulated industries to access an alternative to U.S.-owned models and computing infrastructure. ... The deal also sees two of Mistral’s top models listed in Microsoft Azure’s Foundry, which is a cloud-based service for developing AI applications. Mistral Medium 3.5 and OCR 4 have both been added to Foundry’s model catalog. The former is an open-weight model that can be customized and deployed within managed Azure environments, while the latter is designed for structured document processing workloads, automation and agentic workflows that require data to be extracted from business documents. ... With Azure Foundry, businesses can discover, customize and deploy AI models and agents within the Azure cloud. The platform extended these capabilities to Azure Local environments that are located close to their customer’s data, employees and physical operations to address data residency requirements. With these models, companies will be able to develop AI applications once and then deploy them in different environments, based on regional data laws, and also their operational and connectivity requirements. ... The announcement underscores the renewed push by European governments to reduce their dependence on U.S.-made technology and ensure they have more control over their future economies. Though the push for “sovereign” AI is nothing new, it has taken on greater urgency in recent weeks after America shocked some of its European allies by blocking their access to leading models from Anthropic PBC and OpenAI Group PBC due to what it said were “security concerns.” ... Microsoft President Brand Smith told Reuters in an interview that the partnership will enable this sovereignty while still providing European firms with access to cutting-edge U.S. software capabilities and security features. “By ⁠putting Mistral’s models on Azure Local and on Mistral’s computational capacity, we can combine American and European technology and do it in a way that provides continuous and assured access,” he promised. ... While the partnership may seem promising for sovereignty efforts, there’s a long way to go before European countries can claim true independence from U.S. technology. For instance, European-designed and manufactured chips are almost non-existent. Even in Mistral’s European data centers, the vast majority of the AI chips will be supplied by Nvidia Corp. and others, such as Advanced Micro Devices Inc. ... Mistral Chief Executive Arthur Mensch declined to comment on a report published by Bloomberg last week that his company is looking to raise €3 billion(around $3.4 billion) at a €20 billion valuation, and also said Microsoft won’t be increasing its stake in the company through today’s deal. Like Nvidia, Microsoft is a key investor in Mistral. ... The Paris-based frontier model lab has become one of Europe’s brightest lights in the AI industry. Though its valuation has not kept pace with U.S. giants such as OpenAI and Anthropic, it’s regarded as an extremely capable AI developer, with top-performing models targeting areas such as manufacturing, financial services and the defense industry. One of its biggest customers is the French armed forces. ... Mensch said the deal underscores how Microsoft and his company are making real progress toward “closing the gap on the infrastructure side” in Europe. He has previously said the company aims to have 1 gigawatt of compute capacity up and running by 2030, and believes today’s agreement validates those ambitions."
MISTRAL AI SAS,,,,Exa,Samsung in talks to back France's Mistral at €20bn valuation – FT,www.siliconrepublic.com (Ann O’Dea),2026-07-22T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-22 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.siliconrepublic.com/start-ups/samsung-in-talks-to-back-frances-mistral-at-e20bn-valuation-ft,"Samsung in talks to back France's Mistral at €20bn valuation – FT ... Samsung in talks to back France’s Mistral at €20bn valuation – FT ... French AI start-up Mistral is seeking to raise several billion euros at a €20bn valuation, with Samsung among the investors in discussions to back the round, the Financial Times reported today. ... Samsung is in advanced talks to invest as much as €1bn in Paris-based AI start-up Mistral, in a new funding round that would lift the company’s valuation to €20bn, according to the Financial Times. ... The South Korean conglomerate, which previously backed Mistral through its venture arm, is discussing the investment as part of a broader round expected to raise several billion euros in total, according to people familiar with the matter cited by the FT. ... Swedish investor EQT’s Scaleup Europe Fund– backed by Brussels and managed by EQT – is also in talks to participate, alongside private investors including Novo Holdings and Santander. ... The talks reflect growing appetite for European AI alternatives following the Trump administration’s decision to block foreign access to Anthropic’s latest Mythos and Fable models, a move that has sparked fresh interest in what the industry calls “sovereign AI” capacity. ... Since its founding in 2023, ... has focused on open models that can be customised and deployed without dependence on US cloud providers, meaning no company or government ... switch them off. ... The round represents a significant step up from Mistral’s previous €12bn valuation, set less than a year ago in a deal led by Dutch chip equipment maker ASML. ... Mistral this week announced an expanded partnership with Microsoft, one of its earliest investors, which made a “multibillion-dollar commitment” to use Mistral’s computing infrastructure, providing a stable revenue stream the company can borrow against as it continues to scale. ... In March, Mistral raised$830m in its first debt financing, supported by a consortium of seven global banks, to fund the construction of a data centre at Bruyères-le-Châtel near Paris. The facility, equipped with 13,800 Nvidia GB300 GPUs and 44MW of powered capacity, is designed to train AI models and deliver inference services for Mistral and its enterprise customers. ... The company separately committed more than $1.4bn for digital infrastructure in Sweden, as part of a broader target of 200MW of capacity across Europe by 2027. ... “Scaling our infrastructure in Europe is critical to empower our customers and to ensure AI innovation and autonomy remain at the heart of Europe,” chief executive Arthur Mensch said at the time. ... Founded in 2023, Mistral is one of Europe’s most valuable AI start-up. Mensch told the FT in February that annual recurring revenues were on track to exceed $1bn by the end of this year."
MISTRAL AI SAS,,,,Exa,"Samsung is in talks to put €1B into Mistral at a €20B valuation, one day after launching a robotics unit — TFN",techfundingnews.com (Sofia Chesnokova),2026-07-22T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-22 00:00:00+00:00,,https://techfundingnews.com/samsung-mistral-investment-20bn-valuation/,"Samsung is in talks to put €1B into Mistral at a €20B valuation, one day after launching a robotics unit — TFN ... # Samsung is in talks to put €1B into Mistral at a €20B valuation, one day after launching a robotics unit ... - Samsung is said to be looking at a €1 billion investment in Mistral. This funding round could put the French AI lab’s value at about €20 billion. - Talks began just a day after Samsung brought its robotics projects together under a new unit led by the CEO, aiming to find partners for foundation models. - Back in September 2025, Mistral was valued at €11.7 billion. If this deal goes through, its value would almost double in less than a year. ... According to the Financial Times, Samsung is discussing an investment of hundreds of millions of euros in Mistral. This round could value the French AI lab at around €20 billion, based on sources close to the talks. ... Samsung might put in about €1 billion. Sweden’s EQT is also said to be considering joining through its Scaleup Europe Fund. ... ## A new robotics unit was set up just the day before ... The timing stands out. On July 21, 2026, Samsung brought its robotics projects together into a new division called RX, which reports to device experience chief TM Roh, according to CNBC. ... Samsung says it will keep investing in promising startups and hiring experts in Robot Foundation Models. These models help robots decide what to do, not just how to move. ... Right now, Samsung doesn’t make its own large language models. Its Galaxy AI uses Google’s Gemini. If the Mistral deal happens, it would be Samsung’s first direct investment in a foundation model lab outside of Google. ... This move comes as Samsung makes robotics AI a top priority for its CEO, backed by a reported 60 trillion won (about $40.7 billion) investment in manufacturing in South Korea’s Yeongnam region. ... ## Mistral’s focus on independence is drawing interest from outside the West ... In the last three years, Mistral has worked to ease worries about relying on outside technology. Founded in 2023 by Arthur Mensch, Guillaume Lample, and Timothée Lacroix, the company’s biggest shareholder is ASML. Mistral has also made deals with Microsoft for computing power and, according to Tech Funding News, built its own European data centres instead of depending only on US cloud providers. ... This strategy is often called promoting European independence. Even though Samsung isn’t European, investing in a lab that isn’t tied to one government’s export rules shows a similar goal. This comes after the US temporarily blocked European access to two Anthropic models on June 12, 2026, before reversing the decision on July 1, an action Anthropic addressed directly in its own statement. ... That episode made European buyers more worried about depending on just one country for advanced AI. ... ## What’s confirmed and what’s still up in the air ... No deals have been finalised yet. Mistral’s value has changed in past funding rounds. ... Earlier in 2026, reports mentioned numbers both lower and higher than today’s estimate, like about $10 billion in talks with Abu Dhabi’s MGX, a round TFN covered at the time, and nearly €20 billion. Samsung has also talked about big plans for robotics before, but hasn’t set clear targets, as shown in its April 2026 statement about possible investments and acquisitions. ... If the deal ends up close to €20 billion, Mistral’s value would be almost twice what ASML paid in September 2025. TFN has followed this trend since Mistral’s earlier talks with MGX. It’s still not clear if Samsung is most interested in robotics, global strategy, or getting more bargaining power with Google. This uncertainty is what makes the deal stand out"
MISTRAL AI SAS,,,,Exa,Samsung Weighs Investment in Europe's Top AI Startup Mistral - Seoul Economic Daily,en.sedaily.com (Lee Wan-Gi),2026-07-22T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-22 00:00:00+00:00,,https://en.sedaily.com/international/2026/07/22/samsung-weighs-investment-in-europes-top-ai-startup-mistral,"Samsung Weighs Investment in Europe's Top AI Startup Mistral - Seoul Economic Daily ... Samsung Electronics Chairman Lee Jae-yong and Mistral AI Chairman Arthur Mensch talk during a welcome luncheon hosted by President Lee Jae-myung for French President Emmanuel Macron's state visit at Cheong Wa Dae on April 3, 202 ... . Yonhap News ... Samsung is considering investing billions of euros in French artificial intelligence (AI) startup Mistral AI, according to a foreign media report. Analysts say Samsung is moving to strengthen its AI competitiveness through cooperation with a leading European AI company, at a time when ""sovereign AI"" has emerged as a new topic amid the U.S. AI export control situation. ... On the 22nd, the Financial Times (FT), citing multiple sources, reported that Samsung is in talks to participate in a new investment round that Mistral is pursuing with a target valuation of 20 billion euros. Mistral plans to raise billions of euros in this round, and observers say Samsung's investment could reach approximately 1 billion euros. ... Samsung had previously invested in Mistral through its venture capital arm. However, the current investment negotiations are ongoing, and the exact size and terms could change. Both Mistral and Samsung declined to comment, the FT reported. ... If the investment is completed, Mistral's valuation would surge sharply. The FT explained that ""the timing of this investment comes less than a year after Mistral was valued at 12 billion euros in an investment led by Dutch semiconductor equipment maker ASML."" ASML uses Mistral's AI models to streamline semiconductor equipment production. ... The ""Scale-up Europe Fund,"" managed by Swedish investment firm EQT, is also discussing participation in this investment. The fund, backed by the European Union (EU) Commission, is reportedly set to be raised at more than 5 billion euros. ... The negotiations between Samsung and Mistral are drawing attention because they are taking place as the AI industry faces an unprecedented semiconductor supply shortage. Recently, AI developers have been expanding cooperation with semiconductor companies to secure stable chip supply chains. ... Founded in 2023, Mistral has grown into Europe's highest-valued AI startup. In an interview with the FT in February this year, Mistral CEO Arthur Mensch forecast that annual recurring revenue (ARR) would exceed 1 billion dollars by the end of this year. ... Observers say the U.S. AI export controls will also serve as a new opportunity for Mistral. After the Trump administration blocked foreign access to Anthropic's latest AI model last month, major European and Asian countries have shown increased interest in sovereign AI that does not depend on U.S. technology. Since Mistral has focused on open AI models that customers can directly modify and control from its early days, it plans to expand its position as an alternative to U.S. AI. ... However, with low-cost, high-performance models such as China's Moonshot AI ""Kimi"" rapidly emerging recently, some forecast that competition with Chinese companies will become even more intense."
MISTRAL AI SAS,,,,Exa,Samsung eyes billion-euro stake in France’s Mistral as AI funding surges - CHOSUNBIZ,biz.chosun.com,2026-07-22T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-22 00:00:00+00:00,,https://biz.chosun.com/en/en-international/2026/07/22/G6K5I4W5NZE27L6QOYSJFBCSLE/,"Samsung eyes billion-euro stake in France’s Mistral as AI funding surges - CHOSUNBIZ ... # Samsung eyes billion-euro stake in France's Mistral as AI funding surges ... Foreign media reported that Samsung is discussing a plan to invest hundreds of millions of euros in Mistral AI, France's leading artificial intelligence (AI) startup. ... On the 22nd (local time), the Financial Times (FT), citing multiple sources, said Samsung's participation is being discussed in a funding round in which Mistral aims to raise several billion euros with a target valuation of 20 billion euros (about 34 trillion won). ... One source said Samsung could invest about 1 billion euros (about 1.7 trillion won). Samsung is also known to have invested in Mistral through its venture investment institutional sector before. ... Mistral raised 1.7 billion euros in a funding round in September last year. At the time, most of the investment came from ASML, a Dutch semiconductor equipment company that uses Mistral's AI models, FT said. ... FT assessed, ""This negotiation comes as computing demand in the AI industry far outstrips supply, prompting AI developers to seek partnerships with semiconductor suppliers."""
MISTRAL AI SAS,,,,Exa,"""Samsung in Talks for Massive 1.7 Trillion Won Investment in France's Mistral AI"" - The Asia Business Daily",www.asiae.co.kr (Kwon Hyeonji),2026-07-22T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-22 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.asiae.co.kr/en/article/2026072216110747190,"""Samsung in Talks for Massive 1.7 Trillion Won Investment in France's Mistral AI"" - The Asia Business Daily ... ## Samsung Considers Joining 20 Billion Euro Funding Round, According to Foreign Media Reports ... On July 22 (local time), major foreign media outlets reported, citing multiple sources, that Samsung is in discussions to invest several hundred million euros in Mistral AI, France's leading artificial intelligence (AI) startup. ... According to the sources, this negotiation is part of a new large-scale investment round in which Samsung is considering participating, as Mistral AI seeks to raise several billion euros at a targeted company valuation of 20 billion euros (approximately 34 trillion won). ... It is reported that Samsung is seriously considering contributing up to around 1 billion euros (approximately 1.7 trillion won) through this new investment round. ... Previously, Mistral AI secured 1.7 billion euros in an investment round held several months ago. Most of that funding was provided by ASML, the Dutch global semiconductor equipment company, which aims to use Mistral’s AI models to enhance the production efficiency of advanced semiconductor manufacturing equipment."
MISTRAL AI SAS,,,,Exa,Samsung Eyes Major Investment in Mistral AI: A Start for Korean 'Sovereign AI'? - Businesskorea,www.businesskorea.co.kr (Park Jung-hwan),2026-07-22T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-22 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.businesskorea.co.kr/news/articleView.html?idxno=273440,"Samsung Eyes Major Investment in Mistral AI: A Start for Korean 'Sovereign AI'? - Businesskorea ... ## Talks Could Link Korea’s Memory Chips With Europe’s Sovereign AI Push While the Deal May Secure Samsung Priority AI Memory Supply and Broaden Its Software Strategy ... According to foreign media outlets such as the Financial Times (FT) and the business community on July 22, news has been reported that Samsung Electronics is discussing an equity investment of up to 1 billion euros (approximately 1.69 trillion won or $1.3 billion) in France’s representative artificial intelligence (AI) startup, Mistral AI. Mistral AI is currently proceeding with a new investment round that recognizes its corporate value at about 20 billion euros, and Samsung Electronics appears to be stepping to the forefront as a key strategic investor (SI), moving beyond its previous small-scale investments through the Samsung Catalyst Fund. ... Global AI developers are facing the advancement of large language models (LLM) and the expansion of their own global data centers. Mistral AI is also in a situation where securing billions of euros in funding and the supply of semiconductors is urgently needed to build a self-reliant European AI ecosystem against the dominance of US companies like OpenAI and Meta. ... Securing a stable supply chain of ultra-high-performance memory semiconductors, such as High Bandwidth Memory (HBM) and next-generation server DRAM required for AI model training and inference, is a matter of life and death for Mistral AI. Joining hands with Samsung Electronics, the world’s number one in memory semiconductors, provides Mistral AI with a powerful weapon of a ‘stable supply of memory infrastructure’ beyond mere financing. ... From the perspective of Samsung Electronics, this investment is also not a simple financial investment (FI). Samsung Electronics can diversify its customer base to include next-generation large model developers, rather than stopping at a big tech supply chain centered on Nvidia. In addition, Samsung Electronics can actively transplant Mistral’s lightweight and high-performance open-source models into its Galaxy On-Device AI and its own agent system. ... The two companies already have an equity relationship and have continued discussions on AI memory cooperation since the first half of 2026. Considering the recent market trend where global big tech companies bundle equity investments in AI model developers with semiconductor supply contracts (e.g., the Micron-Anthropic cooperation), the possibility of deriving a final investment contract is predicted to be very high. ... As Mistral AI is an unlisted startup, its exact capitalization table has not been disclosed, making it difficult to determine the precise figures of the investment. However, it is known that Samsung holds a minority stake (ranging from 0.1% to less than a few %) through the Samsung Venture Fund and Samsung SDS. In particular, its affiliate Samsung SDS invested approximately 7.8 billion won (about $5.3 million) in February 2024 to secure a 0.12% stake in Mistral AI for the purpose of AI technology cooperation and service integration. ... Mistral CEO Arthur Mensch visited South Korea in April of this year and met with Samsung Electronics executives, including Vice Chairman Jun Young-hyun, Head of the Device Solutions (DS) Division, to discuss plans for the supply of high-performance memory semiconductors for data centers and technology cooperation. ... Mistral AI is expanding its own AI computing infrastructure along with the expansion of Nvidia-based large-scale data centers in Europe. If Samsung Electronics participates as a strategic investor, it can preemptively secure supply contracts for HBM3E/HBM4, next-generation CXL, and server LPDDR/DDR5 memory that are injected into Mistral’s server infrastructure. It can lower its memory dependence on specific big tech companies and establish itself as a core supplier in the steeply growing European AI data center market. ... Furthermore, Mistral AI has strengths in its open-source algorithm technology and efficient models (such as Mistral 7B and Mixtral) that boast overwhelming performance relative to their parameters. Therefore, Samsung Electronics can equip its smartphone, tablet, and wearable divisions with On-Device AI models that perform the highest level of AI inference even in limited computational resource and battery environments. In the home appliance and robot divisions as well, the intelligence of Samsung AI agents such as Bixby and the connectivity between mobile devices will be improved. Along with this, cloud server costs can be reduced through the establishment of a hybrid AI structure. ... The European Union (EU) is strongly pushing for a ‘Sovereign AI’ policy to break away from data dependence on US technology companies and secure independent AI sovereignty. A powerful alliance with Mistral AI, the pride and representative player of Europe, becomes a strong card for Samsung Electronics to head-on overcome data regulation and privacy issues that may arise when entering the European government, public institution, and B2B markets. ... The current AI market is being led by US IT giants such as Microsoft-OpenAI, Google, and Meta. The FT pointed out that as the US government blocked foreign companies’ access to the latest AI models of local AI company Anthropic last month, European and Asian companies are looking for other AI models to replace it. Additionally, the FT evaluated that while Mistral’s recent growth rate is fast, the pursuit by low-cost Chinese AI models is fierce. Samsung Electronics executing a large-scale investment in a company possessing Europe’s highest level of proprietary models is an expression of its will to build its own global AI allied force without being bound by the technology standards of specific big tech companies or China. ... The discussion of Samsung Electronics’ investment in Mistral AI is a representative case showing the strategic convergence of ‘semiconductor manufacturing technology’ and ‘software AI technology’. ... If the investment is successfully completed, Samsung Electronics is expected to reap the ‘killing two birds with one stone’ effect of instantly elevating its On-Device AI software competitiveness, as well as improving its performance through the diversification of memory semiconductor supply sources. The eyes of the global industry are focused on whether this ‘Korea-Europe AI big deal,’ which will shake up the landscape of the global semiconductor and AI markets, will reach a final settlement."
MISTRAL AI SAS,,,,Exa,Microsoft strikes 'multibillion-dollar' deal with French AI firm Mistral - France 24,www.france24.com,2026-07-21T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-21 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.france24.com/en/france/20260721-microsoft-strikes-multi-billion-dollar-deal-to-expand-france-ai-firm-mistral,"Microsoft strikes 'multibillion-dollar' deal with French AI firm Mistral - France 24 ... # Microsoft strikes 'multibillion-dollar' deal with French AI firm Mistral ... Microsoft and French startup Mistral said Tuesday they had signed a ""multibillion-dollar"" deal for the US tech giant to use the AI developer's computing infrastructure in Europe. ... Microsoft has agreed to spend billions of dollars on Mistral’s computing infrastructure in Europe under a deal that will also expand distribution of the French AI startup’s technology through the US cloud and software giant, the companies said ‌on Tuesday. ... As part of the agreement, Microsoft Azure customers will be able to develop software ⁠using Mistral’s data centres in France, giving Microsoft more capacity in Europe and regulated industries an alternative to US-controlled infrastructure. ... Mistral, meanwhile, has added its AI models called Medium 3.5 ‌and OCR 4 to Microsoft’s app builder known as Foundry. Microsoft Copilot Studio has brought on ⁠Medium 3.5 as well. ... Finally, businesses with independent data centres that access Microsoft services via Azure Local will have the option to run Mistral’s “open” models, which give customers license to develop AI as their own. ... The deal underscores growing interest in Europe and elsewhere to reduce dependence on US technology so other countries may ‌have greater say in their future society and economy. It may also help Microsoft meet rising demand for open models. ... Though the push for “sovereign” AI is now years old, a US decision last month to pause foreign access to two advanced models from San Francisco-based Anthropic ‌has made technology independence a more urgent issue in Europe. ... In a joint interview with Reuters, Microsoft President Brad Smith and Mistral CEO Arthur Mensch said the partnership aimed to deliver such sovereignty while allowing access to US software and security features. ... “By putting Mistral’s models on Azure Local and on Mistral’s computational capacity, we can combine American and European technology and do it in a way that provides continuous and assured access,” Smith said. ... The AI lab behind the Claude chatbot is also in talks for a deal worth billions to use infrastructure belonging to Facebook and Instagram parent Meta, according to media reports. ... Decoupling Europe from US technology would be a tall order. Nvidia chips powering the global AI boom, also key to Mistral’s data-center buildout, are American-designed. Nvidia, like Microsoft, is a Mistral investor. ... Smith said the deal announced on Tuesday did not include any new financial stake in the startup, and Mensch declined to comment on a Bloomberg News report that Mistral was in talks to raise around €3 billion ($3.4 billion) at a €20 billion valuation. ... The Paris-based lab has come to represent one of Europe’s top hopes in AI. So far it has targeted manufacturing, financial services and defense sales and has won business from France’s armed forces. Its valuation remains dwarfed by US peers such as Anthropic. ... Still, Mensch said the deal showed how Microsoft and Mistral were “working together ⁠on closing the gap on the infrastructure side in Europe”. ... Mistral is targeting 1 gigawatt of compute capacity by 2030, and the Microsoft agreement – specifics of ⁠which Mensch declined to provide – validates its strategy. ... The companies are working on a joint go-to-market plan, they added. ... “This is going to help both of our companies grow our businesses, unquestionably,"" Smith said."
MISTRAL AI SAS,,,,Exa,Microsoft to fund Mistral’s European AI expansion in multibillion-dollar deal,thenextweb.com (Ana-Maria Stanciuc),2026-07-21T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-21 00:00:00+00:00,,https://thenextweb.com/news/microsoft-fund-mistral-european-ai-expansion,"Microsoft to fund Mistral’s European AI expansion in multibillion-dollar deal ... Microsoft has agreed to bankroll a large slice of Mistral AI’s European build-out, extending a partnership that already binds the Paris lab to the American giant’s cloud. ... The two companies announced the expanded agreement on Tuesday, describing it as a multibillion-dollar commitment to compute infrastructure across the continent. ... What is clearer is the shape of it: Microsoft will fund Mistral’s growing base of GPUs in Europe while stopping short of taking a new equity stake, a distinction that matters given the EU’s earlier antitrust scrutiny of the relationship. ... Under the terms set out in the companies’ joint announcement, Mistral will add thousands of NVIDIA’s latest Vera Rubin chips to its European capacity. ... Azure customers, in turn, gain access to Mistral’s French data centres, while its Medium 3.5 and OCR 4 models land inside Microsoft Foundry and Copilot Studio. ... That language is not incidental. Mistral has spent two years positioning itself as the European answer to OpenAI and Anthropic, with chief executive Arthur Mensch repeatedly arguing that the continent should own and operate its own AI infrastructure rather than rent it from US hyperscalers. ... The new deal appears structured to avoid the same charge, leaning on compute spending, joint go-to-market plans, and Azure credits for customer trials rather than fresh ownership. It is a design choice as much as a commercial one, and regulators will read it closely. ... The commercial logic runs both ways. Microsoft gets a credible European model to offer regulated customers wary of American-only stacks, especially after Washington tightened access to some Anthropic models abroad, while Mistral gets the capital and chips to keep pace in a race where compute has become the binding constraint. ... The chips feed a longer plan. Mistral is aiming for one gigawatt of compute by 2030 under its Mistral Compute programme, with an interim target of up to 200 megawatts by 2027. ... Money has not been the lab’s problem lately. It is reportedly in talks to raise around €3bn at a€20bn valuation, roughly double the figure it commanded when ASML led its Series C last September, though Mensch has declined to confirm those discussions. ... It has also been building on its own account, securing $830m in debt earlier this year to construct its own data centre near Paris. ... Whether regulators see it that way is the open question. The European Commission has shown appetite for probing AI partnerships between incumbents and startups, and a fresh multibillion-dollar arrangement between the region’s flagship lab and a US cloud leader is unlikely to escape notice. ... For now, both companies are selling the deal as the opposite of dependence. The reality is that American infrastructure and European models can coexist, giving regulated buyers frontier AI they can actually control, and the coming months, along with any word from Brussels, will test how convincing that turns out to be."
MISTRAL AI SAS,,,,Exa,Microsoft and Mistral announce partnership to strengthen cloud capacity in Europe and integrate Mistral’s AI models into Microsoft | AGENCE EUROPE,agenceurope.eu,2026-07-21T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-21 00:00:00+00:00,,https://agenceurope.eu/en/bulletin/article/13914/13/microsoft-and-mistral-announce-partnership-to-strengthen-cloud-capacity-in-europe-and-integrate-mistrals-ai-models-into-microsoft,"Microsoft and Mistral announce partnership to strengthen cloud capacity in Europe and integrate Mistral’s AI models into Microsoft | AGENCE EUROPE ... # Microsoft and Mistral announce partnership to strengthen cloud capacity in Europe and integrate Mistral’s AI models into Microsoft ... Brussels, 22/07/2026 (Agence Europe) ... Microsoft and Mistral announced on Tuesday 21 July that they were strengthening their partnership in order to increase cloud capacity dedicated to artificial intelligence (AI) in Europe and integrate Mistral’s models into Microsoft Copilot Studio, Microsoft’s end-to-end conversational AI platform. ... “Europe is lagging slightly behind in computing capacity. That is why we are developing infrastructure for our customers so that they can access artificial intelligence technologies”, Arthur Mensch, co-founder and chief executive of Mistral, explained on social media. “This is a multi-billion dollar investment aimed at deploying thousands of GPUs in Europe. It is a major step”, added Brad Smith, President and Vice Chairman of Microsoft. ... Brad Smith praised Mistral as a “pioneer in open models and innovation”, adding the “interest” of Microsoft customers in using its AI models. “Together, we are offering them more choice as to how they use them. They will be able to use them not only on Azure, our public cloud, but also on Azure Local. They will thus benefit from greater control”, he explained. ... Brad Smith acknowledged that Microsoft customers had “increased sovereignty requirements”, particularly with regard to “resilience, safeguards, and continued access to Mistral’s models”. “What we are putting in place together meets these needs”, he stressed. ... Common values on artificial intelligence. Microsoft and Mistral said they shared “similar and indeed common values”. “We want to carry out projects together that meet broader societal needs, in Europe, the United States, and elsewhere in the world”, Brad Smith said. “I agree. Ultimately, what matters is creating value for our customers. That is only possible if the teams developing the different technological building blocks work together effectively”, concluded Mistral’s Chief Executive. ... The European Commission recently presented a legislative package on technological sovereignty (see EUROPE 13880/1), aimed at reducing Europe’s dependencies on third countries in strategic sectors such as cloud computing, AI, and semiconductors. ... It wants to triple the capacity of European data centres over the next five to seven years and achieve the necessary capacities by 2035, and for European providers of cloud services and AI computing capacity to account for 30% of the European market by 2035, up from around 15% today, according to the impact assessment accompanying the draft Cloud and AI Development Act (CADA) (see EUROPE 13880/2). However, the most optimistic scenario in that study only projects a 17% market share. ... The European market for cloud services, valued at €70 billion in 2022, is expected to exceed €200 billion by 2028. The three US companies AWS, Microsoft, and Google currently hold around 70% of the European market. The main European providers, SAP and Deutsche Telekom, each account for around 2% of the market. (Original version in French by Ana Pisonero Hernández) ... DIGITAL: Microsoft and Mistral announce partnership to strengthen cloud capacity in Europe and integrate Mistral’s AI models into Microsoft"
MISTRAL AI SAS,,,,Exa,"IA : l’Office européen des brevets s’associe à Mistral AI pour ""améliorer la procédure de délivrance de brevets""",www.aefinfo.fr (Michèle Bargiel),2026-07-20T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-20 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.aefinfo.fr/depeche/747474-ia-loffice-europeen-des-brevets-sassocie-a-mistral-ai-pour-ameliorer-la-procedure-de-delivrance-de-brevets,"IA : l’Office européen des brevets s’associe à Mistral AI pour ""améliorer la procédure de délivrance de brevets"" ... # IA : l’Office européen des brevets s’associe à Mistral AI pour ""améliorer la procédure de délivrance de brevets"" Publié le 16 mars 2026 à 16h42, modifié le 16 mars 2026 à 16h42 Par Michèle Bargiel ... À travers un partenariat avec l’entreprise française Mistral AI, l’ OEB franchit ""une étape importante dans son utilisation de l’IA de pointe pour améliorer la qualité et l’efficacité de la procédure de délivrance de brevets"", annonce…"
MISTRAL AI SAS,,,,Exa,"Mistral AI plans AI deployment at Digital Realty data center in Paris, France - DCD",www.datacenterdynamics.com,2026-07-09T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-09 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.datacenterdynamics.com/en/news/mistral-ai-leverages-digital-realty-to-expand-its-ai-services-across-europe-digital-realty-to-host-10-mw-mistral-ai-compute-cluster-as-europe-ramps-up-ai-capacity-paris-france-may-28-2026-digital-realty-nyse-dlr-the-worlds-largest-clo/,"Mistral AI plans AI deployment at Digital Realty data center in Paris, France - DCD ... # Mistral AI plans AI deployment at Digital Realty data center in Paris, France ... Will enable Mistral to expand compute capacity ... French AI lab Mistral AI has signed a capacity agreement with colo provider Digital Realty. ... Under the agreement, Mistral AI will deploy AI compute infrastructure at the Digital Realty Paris South campus. The scale of the deployment and the specific data centers within the campus set to be used have not been shared. ... The deal will enable Mistral to expand its compute capacity, while also accessing Digital Realty's platform of thousands of enterprises, digital service providers, and technology partners within a single ecosystem, allowing customers to use Mistral's AI service with low-latency connectivity, the company said in a statement. ... “We are proud to host Mistral AI’s infrastructure at our Paris South campus. Our data center platform enables AI innovators to scale quickly while enabling enterprises to access and deploy AI services within a secure and performant environment,” said Fabrice Coquio, SVP and managing director of Digital Realty in France. ... Digital Realty has 13 data centers in Paris with a total of 932,500 sq ft (86,600 sqm) of data center space. ... Earlier this year, Mistral AI raised$830 million in debt to finance Nvidia Grace Blackwell infrastructure, and give Mistral, which makes ChatGPT competitor Le Chat, access to 13,800 Nvidia GB300 GPUs and 44MW of powered capacity at a data center in Bruyères-le-Châtel, south of Paris, which is owned and run by French data center firm Eclairion. ... Elsewhere, the AI lab has a deal with EcoDataCenter to install AI infrastructure at the colo firm’s data centers in Sweden, and has signed on to procure around 18,000 Nvidia GB200 GPUs from cloud provider Scaleway, though this seems to have been delayed and amended to include a newer generation of GPUs instead."
MISTRAL AI SAS,,,,Exa,LexisNexis Announces the Integration of Mistral in Lexis+ with Protégé in France | LexisNexis PressRoom,www.lexisnexis.com,2026-07-09T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-09 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.lexisnexis.com/community/pressroom/b/news/posts/lexisnexis-announces-the-integration-of-mistral-in-lexis-with-protege-in-france,"LexisNexis Announces the Integration of Mistral in Lexis+ with Protégé in France | LexisNexis PressRoom ... # LexisNexis Announces the Integration of Mistral in Lexis+ with Protégé in France ... Paris, July 9, 2026 - LexisNexis today announced the integration of Mistral models in Lexis+ with Protégé, its private, secure, and trusted AI platform purpose-built for all legal work. The objective: to enhance the power and performance of AI for everyday use cases, while ensuring a confidential, encrypted framework hosted in Europe. ... This collaboration between LexisNexis and Mistral, initiated in France, marks a new milestone in LexisNexis’ innovation strategy. It enriches Lexis+ with Protégé with a sovereign European model tailored to the daily needs of legal and tax & accounting professionals: drafting, summarizing, rewording, and structuring content, within an environment that guarantees a high level of confidentiality through hosting within the European Union. ... Mistral is now available in Lexis+ with Protégé for legal professionals in France. Already secure, designed to protect data confidentiality, and hosted in Europe, the platform is now further enhanced with a new frontier AI model developed by Mistral. ... With this rollout in France, LexisNexis is taking a first step in integrating Mistral into its AI environment. This initiative paves the way for new AI use cases within LexisNexis Legal & Professional. ... This integration responds to strong demand from law firms, in-house legal departments, public-sector organizations, and legal and tax & accounting professionals: access to a broader choice of high-performing AI models within a trusted environment designed to preserve confidentiality, security, and control over data. ... The integration of Mistral into Lexis+ with Protégé extends the possibilities offered by AI beyond strict legal research. Users can rely on Lexis+ with Protégé to draft or summarize documents, reword and translate text, structure content, or ask questions that go beyond the legal field alone, all from a single tool. ... Because no single model is optimal for every task, LexisNexis has made a deliberate choice to build a multi-model platform. From a single interface, users can leverage different models depending on their needs: research, document analysis, drafting, or translation. ... “Our partnership with LexisNexis marks an important step in making Mistral’s frontier AI available to legal professionals across Europe. It combines the level of performance they expect with the data control and confidentiality their work requires. I am particularly proud to see Mistral’s technology contribute to the future of legal publishing and legal technology, an ecosystem that is especially meaningful to me,” said Blanche Savary de Beauregard, General Counsel at Mistral. ... “Legal and tax professionals expect AI to help them save time in their daily work, within a framework that guarantees the confidentiality, security, and control of their data. These requirements already underpin Lexis+ with Protégé, regardless of the model being used. The integration of Mistral responds to a clear expectation: to offer a sovereign European option within our private AI environment, and to give our clients greater choice in selecting the model best suited to their use cases,” said Éric Bonnet-Maes, CEO of LexisNexis CEMEA. ... the world. As a digital pioneer, the company was the ... to bring legal ... business information online with its ... Nexis® services. ... , which serves customers in more than 150 countries with 11,900 employees worldwide, is part of RELX, a global provider of information-based analytics and decision tools ... LexisNexis"
MISTRAL AI SAS,,,,Exa,Naver Cloud Teams Up With Mistral AI to Target Manufacturing AI Market - Seoul Economic Daily,en.sedaily.com (Lee Jin-Seok),2026-07-08T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-08 00:00:00+00:00,,https://en.sedaily.com/technology/2026/07/08/naver-cloud-teams-up-with-mistral-ai-to-target,"Naver Cloud Teams Up With Mistral AI to Target Manufacturing AI Market - Seoul Economic Daily ... Naver Cloud announced on the 8th that it signed a comprehensive partnership with Mistral AI, France's leading artificial intelligence (AI) company, to jointly target the manufacturing AI market. Naver Cloud CEO Kim Yu-won (left) and Mistral AI APAC head Jeff Soon pose for a photo immediately after signing the agreement. Photo courtesy of Naver Cloud ... Naver Cloud said Wednesday that it signed a comprehensive partnership agreement with Mistral AI, France's leading artificial intelligence (AI) company, to jointly target the manufacturing AI market. The agreement was signed Tuesday at 1784, Naver's second headquarters building. ... The two companies already share the same technology ecosystem as members of the ""Nemotron Alliance,"" which Nvidia formed together with major global companies. Building on this, they plan to develop joint business centered on the manufacturing industry, where synergy is expected to be greatest. ... In particular, they plan to target global manufacturers with sovereign AI solutions optimized for the data sovereignty and industry characteristics of European and Asian manufacturing sites, rather than U.S.-centric general-purpose AI. ... Immediately after signing the agreement, the two companies held a joint workshop involving key technical staff and began establishing plans and an execution roadmap for launching commercialized services in the manufacturing sector. They plan to continue research and development (R&D) collaboration and technical exchange among their research teams, jointly identifying AI challenges at industrial sites and creating a virtuous cycle of verification and advancement in actual field settings. ... Mistral AI is a company with manufacturing-specialized AI capabilities proven in the global market through collaboration with major European manufacturers such as Airbus, BMW, and ASML. The two companies plan to combine their respective strengths to jointly introduce sovereign AI solutions to the global market that can be immediately applied to actual manufacturing sites. ... As part of this cooperation, the two companies will also begin domestic business together. Mistral AI's latest models, platforms, and other full-stack products will be supplied through the Naver Cloud platform, while Mistral AI's field-dedicated engineers (FDE) will be dispatched directly to domestic client sites to handle technical support and issue resolution. ... Their first task will be to apply proven manufacturing AI use cases that Mistral AI has accumulated at European manufacturing sites—such as real-time quality anomaly detection and parts selection optimization—to the Korean manufacturing environment. Through this, they plan to help domestic manufacturers effectively use global-level manufacturing AI technology in a secure environment that does not expose data externally. ... ""This partnership is a practical business collaboration that combines Mistral AI's manufacturing-specialized technology, proven in the global market, with Naver Cloud's stable infrastructure,"" said Kim Yu-won, CEO of Naver Cloud. ""We will build successful references domestically and, based on that, continuously identify joint business opportunities in the global sovereign AI market."" ... ""Naver Cloud is the optimal partner to jointly realize the manufacturing AI business,"" said Jeff Soon, Mistral AI's Asia-Pacific (APAC) head. ""By combining the know-how Mistral AI has built up at European manufacturing sites with Naver Cloud's solid infrastructure, we will provide practical support for the AI transformation of global manufacturers, including those in Korea."""
MISTRAL AI SAS,,,,Exa,"Naver Cloud, Mistral AI Partner to Target Manufacturing AI Market < IT·Gaming < 기사본문 - The Elec Inc.",www.thelec.net (LEE JAEWOON),2026-07-08T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-08 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.thelec.net/news/articleView.html?idxno=12066,"Naver Cloud, Mistral AI Partner to Target Manufacturing AI Market < IT·Gaming < 기사본문 - The Elec Inc. ... Naver Cloud, Mistral AI Partner to Target Manufacturing AI Market ... Kim Yoo-won (left), CEO of Naver Cloud, and Jeff Soon, APAC Head of Mistral AI, pose after signing a comprehensive partnership agreement to advance AI-driven transformation in the manufacturing industry. (Photo: Naver Cloud) ... Naver Cloud said on July 8 that it has signed a comprehensive partnership agreement with French artificial intelligence company Mistral AI to jointly expand into the manufacturing AI market. ... Mistral AI has established manufacturing-focused AI capabilities through collaborations with leading European industrial companies, including Airbus, BMW, and ASML, according to Naver Cloud. ... Naver Cloud said the partnership is significant because it brings together leading technology companies from Asia and Europe to build an independent sovereign AI business ecosystem at a time when the AI market is increasingly dominated by major US technology companies. ... The two companies are already members of NVIDIA's Nemotron Alliance, a global AI ecosystem comprising leading technology companies. Building on that shared ecosystem, they plan to focus first on the manufacturing sector, where they expect to generate the strongest synergies. ... Their primary targets are manufacturing companies in Europe and Asia. The partners aim to provide sovereign AI solutions optimized for local industrial environments and data sovereignty requirements, addressing manufacturing-specific needs that general-purpose AI platforms from US technology companies may not fully satisfy. ... Immediately after signing the agreement, the companies held a joint workshop at 1784, Naver's second headquarters, with engineers from both sides to develop commercialization plans and execution roadmaps for manufacturing AI services. They also plan to continue joint research and development, expand technical exchanges, identify AI use cases for industrial sites, and validate and refine solutions through real-world deployments. ... As part of the partnership, the companies will also launch joint business activities in South Korea. Naver Cloud will offer Mistral AI's full-stack portfolio—including its latest AI models and platform—through the Naver Cloud Platform. Mistral AI will dispatch Field Deployment Engineers (FDEs) to customer sites in Korea to provide technical support and resolve implementation issues. ... The companies' initial focus will be applying Mistral AI's proven manufacturing AI use cases—such as real-time quality anomaly detection and component selection optimization, developed through deployments at European manufacturers—to South Korea's manufacturing sector. They said the partnership will enable Korean manufacturers to adopt world-class manufacturing AI technologies in secure environments without exposing proprietary data."
MISTRAL AI SAS,,,,Exa,Naver will join hands with French artificial intelligence (AI) company Mistral AI to target the manu.. - MK,www.mk.co.kr (JEONG Hojun jeong.hojun@mk.co.kr),2026-07-08T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-08 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.mk.co.kr/en/it/12093352,"Naver will join hands with French artificial intelligence (AI) company Mistral AI to target the manu.. - MK ... Co-promoting Data Sovereign Optimized SolutionsNaverCloud also provides a Mistral modelNaver, European Siemens, and expands global cooperation ... , CEO of NAVER Cloud (left), and Jeff Soon, CEO of Mistral AI Asia-Pacific region (APAC), take a commemorative ... after signing a strategic partnership at Naver 1784 in Seongnam-si, Gyeonggi-do on the ... 7th. [ ... = NAVER Cloud ... Naver will join hands with French artificial intelligence (AI) company Mistral AI to target the manufacturing industry AI market. ... The two companies plan to infiltrate manufacturing companies in each country with Sovereign AI solutions that guarantee data sovereignty. Companies from third-tier countries other than the U.S. and China joined forces to challenge the U.S. big tech-oriented AI market. ... NAVER Cloud announced on the 8th that it signed a strategic partnership with Mistral AI at Naver's 1784 headquarters in Seongnam-si, Gyeonggi-do on the 7th to target the global industrial-specific AI market based on Sovereign AI solutions. ... The two companies will target manufacturing companies together with Sovereign AI solutions optimized for data sovereignty and industrial characteristics in European and Asian manufacturing sites. ... Mistral AI, which develops its own AI model, is considered one of Europe's leading Generative AI companies. By collaborating with major European manufacturing companies such as Airbus, BMW, and ASML, it has secured manufacturing-specific AI capabilities in the global market. ... In particular, Naver has AI data center infrastructure in Korea and Mistral AI has infrastructure in Europe, so the two companies will introduce Sovereign AI solutions that meet the needs of companies in each region. ... After signing the partnership, the two companies held a joint workshop involving major technicians from each company and began planning to launch commercialization services in the manufacturing sector and establishing an implementation roadmap. ... An official from NAVER Cloud said, ""The first task is to apply proven cases of manufacturing AI, such as detecting real-time quality abnormalities accumulated in European manufacturing sites and optimizing parts selection, to the Korean manufacturing environment. Through this, we will support domestic manufacturing companies to effectively utilize global-level manufacturing AI technology in a safe environment that does not expose data to the outside world."" ... Previously, NAVER Cloud also joined hands with Germany's Siemens Group, a powerhouse in manufacturing automation, to transform AI in manufacturing sites and incorporate digital twins. Naver sees the domestic manufacturing AI market as a major food item and is believed to be strengthening its cooperation system with major global partners to target the market. ... Meanwhile, Mistral AI has begun full-scale business in Korea. ... As part of this cooperation, NAVER Cloud will begin providing the latest models and platform technologies of Mistral AI through its cloud platform in Korea. Mistral AI is in charge of technical support, such as sending forward deployment engineers (FDE) to the site of domestic customers. ... In addition, Mistral AI has also opened a booth at ICML 2026, a machine learning conference held at COEX in Samseong-dong, Seoul, and is recruiting Korean workers, saying, ""We will hire Korean foundry members."" Although the opening of the Korean office is undecided, it has also started to secure manpower as Korean businesses such as collaboration with Naver have grown. ... ""This partnership is a practical business cooperation that combines Mistral AI's manufacturing-specific technology proven in the global market with NAVER Cloud's stable infrastructure,"" NAVER Cloud CEO Kim Yoo-won said. ""We will establish successful references in Korea and continue to discover joint business opportunities in the global Sovereign AI market based on this."""
MISTRAL AI SAS,,,,Exa,"Naver Cloud, Mistral AI team up to target manufacturing AI market - The Herald Business",biz.heraldcorp.com (Kim Kwang-woo),2026-07-07T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-07 00:00:00+00:00,,https://biz.heraldcorp.com/article/10801607,"Naver Cloud, Mistral AI team up to target manufacturing AI market - The Herald Business ... # Naver Cloud, Mistral AI team up to target manufacturing AI market ... -Europe alliance takes on ... dominated AI market ... Mistral AI full-stack products to be supplied in South Korea ... Naver Cloud CEO ... Yu-won (left) and Mistral AI APAC President Jeff Soon pose for a photo after signing a partnership agreement on Tuesday. ... Naver Cloud] ... Naver Cloud announced Wednesday that it has signed a wide-ranging partnership with French AI company Mistral AI to jointly pursue the manufacturing AI market. The two companies aim to target global manufacturers with sovereign AI solutions optimized for data sovereignty and industrial conditions in European and Asian manufacturing environments — an alternative to the US-centric general-purpose AI offerings that dominate the market. ... Both companies are members of the ""Nemotron Alliance"" formed by Nvidia with major global firms, meaning they already share the same technology ecosystem. Building on that foundation, the two will develop joint business initiatives centered on the manufacturing sector, where they expect the greatest synergies. ... Immediately after signing the agreement, key technical staff from both sides held a joint workshop at Naver's second headquarters, 1784, and began mapping out a commercialization roadmap for manufacturing AI services. The companies plan to sustain R&D collaboration and technology exchange between their research teams, jointly identifying AI challenges on the factory floor and creating a virtuous cycle of real-world validation and refinement. ... In addition, the two companies plan to adapt use cases that Mistral AI has developed through collaboration with European manufacturers — including real-time quality defect detection and parts-selection optimization — to the Korean manufacturing environment, and to jointly pursue sovereign AI project opportunities targeting global manufacturers on that basis. ... Domestic business will also get underway. Mistral AI's latest models and full-stack products will be supplied through the Naver Cloud platform, and Mistral AI field-dedicated engineers will be dispatched directly to customer sites in South Korea to handle technical support and issue resolution. ... The first priority will be adapting Mistral AI's proven manufacturing AI use cases from European factory floors — including real-time quality defect detection and parts-selection optimization — to the Korean manufacturing environment. The companies plan to support domestic manufacturers in deploying world-class manufacturing AI technology in a secure environment where data is not exposed externally. ... ""This partnership combines Mistral AI's manufacturing-specialized technology, proven in global markets, with Naver Cloud's stable infrastructure — it is a substantive business collaboration,"" Naver Cloud CEO Kim Yu-won said. ""We will build successful references domestically and, on that basis, continue to identify joint business opportunities in the global sovereign AI market."" ... Jeff Soon, Mistral AI's APAC president, said Naver Cloud is ""the ideal partner to bring manufacturing AI business to life together,"" adding that the company would ""combine the know-how Mistral AI has accumulated on European factory floors with Naver Cloud's solid infrastructure to provide meaningful support for the AI transformation of manufacturers in Korea and around the world."""
MISTRAL AI SAS,,,,Exa,Mistral confirms open-weight model launch as ARR tops $400M | AI Weekly,aiweekly.co (Alexis Dufresne),2026-07-05T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-05 00:00:00+00:00,,https://aiweekly.co/alerts/mistral-confirms-open-weight-model-launch-as-arr-tops-400m,"Mistral confirms open-weight model launch as ARR tops $400M | AI Weekly ... - CEO Arthur Mensch confirmed a 'very exciting' open-weight model launching this summer, with early access opening in July. - Mistral says ARR climbed from $20M a year earlier to above $400M as of February, on track to pass $1B this year. - The company is funding a €4B data-center buildout in France and Sweden after a €1.7B ASML-led Series C at €11.7B. ... Mistral's Arthur Mensch used a TechCrunch profile published on July 4 to lock in the piece of news the open-source AI community has been asking about for months. 'We have a very exciting model to come this summer – it will be open-weight, and we're opening early access to it in July,' he told the outlet, pairing it with the more careful line that Mistral does not yet own the best language models but has 'constantly reduced that gap' with US frontier labs. ... The financial picture around that release is what makes it interesting rather than another routine European AI update. Annual recurring revenue reportedly jumped from $20 million one year prior to above $400 million as of February, and Mistral's own claim is that it is on track to surpass $1 billion in ARR this year. Behind that number is an unusual go-to-market for an AI lab: what the article describes as a Palantir-style approach, embedding forward-deployed engineers inside governments and large corporations to tailor systems for their specific workloads rather than selling a self-serve API and hoping developers show up. ... The infrastructure side is scaling to match the revenue story. Mistral has committed to a roughly €4 billion data-center buildout in France and Sweden, and in September 2025 closed a €1.7 billion Series C led by ASML at an €11.7 billion valuation. Mensch has publicly said the company is 'not for sale' and is pointing toward an IPO rather than an acquisition, which is a meaningfully different exit posture from most of Europe's earlier AI hopefuls. ... What is worth watching in July is whether the early-access model materially closes the gap with the current open-weight leaders like Llama and DeepSeek. If it does, EU enterprises finally get a sovereignty-friendly base model that is credible on capability, not just on jurisdiction. ... Original headline: Mistral CEO Arthur Mensch Confirms 'Very Exciting' Open-Weight Model Coming This Summer With Early Access in July — French AI Lab ARR Now Above $400M, Targets $1B"
MISTRAL AI SAS,,,,Exa,What is Mistral AI? Everything to know about the OpenAI competitor | TechCrunch,techcrunch.com (Anna Heim),2026-07-04T00:00:00.000Z,2026-07-04 00:00:00+00:00,,https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/04/what-is-mistral-ai-everything-to-know-about-the-openai-competitor/,"Following the Trump directive that led Anthropic to pull its latest AI models offline and growing calls for sovereign tech that reduces reliance on the U.S., Mistral AI has been caught in a whirlwind of attention. But the French AI darling is often misunderstood, and the fact that it develops large language models (LLMs) has muddied the picture. ... This approach is also better suited for Mistral’s means. While the company is rumored to be raising some $3.5 billion at a $23.15 billion valuation, nearly doubling its current valuation, that’s still far less than U.S. frontier labs. But its revenues have also ramped up; in February, it disclosed that its annual recurring revenue was now above $400 million, up from $20 million just one year earlier, and claimed it was on track to surpass $1 billion in ARR this year. ... rooms where tech CEOs ... This vision means that Mistral is looking beyond the enterprise. It also aims to keep on making big investments into research to keep up with foundational AI rivals — and Mensch’s ... also covered where he thinks the company stands in that regard. ... “Today, we do not yet own the best language models, but we’ve constantly reduced that gap. We have a very exciting model to come this summer — it will be open-weight, and we’re opening early access to it in July. In domains that are less compute bound, e.g. voice, vision and document processing, we have state-of-the-art solutions,” Mensch claimed. ... The most interesting part may be happening behind the scenes. Earlier this year, Mistral acquired infrastructure startup Koyeb to further boost its plans to build “a true AI cloud.” The company also announced a€4 billion investment s t rategy(around $4.56 billion) to build data centers in France and Sweden — and the sovereignty undertones are never very far. ... In 2024, Mistral signed a deal with Microsoft that included a €15 million investment and a strategic partnership for distributing the French company’s AI models through Microsoft’s Azure platform. ... In May 2025, Mistral said it would participate in the creation of an AI Campus in the Paris region, as part of a joint venture with UAE investment firm MGX, Nvidia, and France’s state-owned investment bank Bpifrance. ... In June 2025, Mistral said it would launch a European platform dedicated to AI and powered by Nvidia processors, Mistral Compute, in 2026. The initiative was hailed as “historic” by France’s president, Emmanuel Macron, who shared the stage with Mensch and Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang at the VivaTech conference shortly after the announcement. ... In July 2025, Mistral launched AI for Citizens, an initiative that the company claimed could “help States and public institutions strategically harness AI for their people by transforming public services.” ... In September 2025, Mistral and chip company ASML struck a partnership“to explore the use of AI models across ASML’s product portfolio as well as research, development and operations.” ... Mistral also secured strategic partnerships with the likes of Accenture, press agency Agence France-Presse, France’s army and job agency, Luxembourg, shipping giant CMA, German defense tech startup Helsing, IBM, Orange, and Stellantis. ... Most of Mistral AI’s funding to date was debt financing, but the company has also raised several venture funding rounds, with a grand total around $4 billion, according to Crunchbase. ... led by Lightspeed Venture Partners ... time said the ... , Europe’s ... Six months later, Mistral closed a€385 million Series A($415 million at the time), at a reported valuation of $2 billion. The round was led by Andreessen Horowitz (a16z) and saw participation from Lightspeed, as well as BNP Paribas, CMA CGM, Conviction, Elad Gil, General Catalyst, and Salesforce. ... Microsoft’s$16.3 million convertible investment in Mistral as part of a partnership announced in February 2024 was presented as a Series A extension, implying an unchanged valuation. ... In June 2024, Mistral raised€600 million (about $640 million) in a mix of equity and debt. The long-rumored round was led by General Catalyst at a $6 billion valuation, with notable investors including Cisco, IBM, Nvidia, and Samsung Venture Investment Corporation participating. ... In September 2025, Mistral closed a €1.7 billion Series C round (about $2 billion) led by ASML at a €11.7 billion valuation (approximately $13.8 billion), with participation from existing backers DST Global, a16z, Bpifrance, General Catalyst, Index Ventures, Lightspeed, and Nvidia. ... In addition to infrastructure startup Koyeb, Mistral has also bought Emmi, an Austrian startup focusing on physics AI, with the ambition to better support industrial enterprises in their AI transformation. ... to design its own ... , Mensch isn’t ruling it out. “Owning the chips may come, I think it should come at some point, but for now we are relying on Nvidia, which is a great partner to us, and we’re testing a few things here and there,” he told CNBC. ... Mistral is “not for sale,” Mensch said in January 2025 at the World Economic Forum in Davos. “Of course, [an IPO is] the plan.” ... This makes sense, given how much the startup has raised so far: Even a sale to a rumored prospective buyer like Apple may not provide high enough multiples for its investors, not to mention sovereignty concerns depending on the acquirer."
MISTRAL AI SAS,,,,Exa,"France’s Bpifrance, Mistral AI and Abu Dhabi’s MGX team up on “Campus AI,” a 3-gigawatt compute network - Lavoixdefrance.fr",www.lavoixdefrance.fr,2026-06-26T00:00:00.000Z,2026-06-26 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.lavoixdefrance.fr/english/frances-bpifrance-mistral-ai-and-abu-dhabis-mgx-team-up-on-campus-ai-a-3-gigawatt-compute-network-7714/,"France’s Bpifrance, Mistral AI and Abu Dhabi’s MGX team up on “Campus AI,” a 3-gigawatt compute network - Lavoixdefrance.fr ... # France’s Bpifrance, Mistral AI and Abu Dhabi’s MGX team up on “Campus AI,” a 3-gigawatt compute network ... Bpifrance, Mistral et MGX lancent Campus AI : un réseau national de 3 GW d'usines IA ... France’s public investment bank Bpifrance, French AI startup Mistral AI, and Abu Dhabi sovereign tech fund MGX say they will expand “Campus AI” into a nationwide network of high-performance computing sites, targeting 3 gigawatts of AI-dedicated capacity. ... The partners are pitching the effort as more than another data center buildout. Campus AI is described as a mesh of high-performance computing infrastructure meant to meet rising demand from French companies and research labs for AI training and deployment. ... At 3 GW, the backers say the project would rank among Europe’s most ambitious AI infrastructure initiatives in raw computing power—an industrial-scale push framed as part of France’s broader drive for “digital sovereignty,” or reducing reliance on non-European platforms for critical technology. ... Until now, Campus AI operated as a pilot site. The national expansion changes the project’s purpose, the partners argue: it is no longer a demonstration but a foundational layer for French digital sovereignty. ... The initiative lands in a crowded global race. The article points to U.S. hyperscalers—Microsoft, Google and Amazon—as well as Gulf countries investing heavily in similar infrastructure. Bpifrance, Mistral and MGX say their differentiator is governance and ownership that does not run through major U.S. platforms, with European and French actors involved in the capital structure. ... For Mistral, founded in 2023, the stakes are also practical. Training and running large language models requires massive compute resources. Preferential access to a national infrastructure could reduce dependence on U.S. cloud providers and strengthen Mistral’s pitch to European customers focused on data sovereignty. ... MGX’s presence is a central part of the announcement. The Abu Dhabi fund has already invested in multiple AI infrastructure projects in Europe and Asia, and its move into Campus AI fits a strategy of geographically diversifying technology assets, according to the article. ... For France, the involvement of foreign capital in what the partners describe as sensitive digital infrastructure raises governance questions that Bpifrance and the French government have not yet detailed publicly. Similar foreign investments in strategic digital assets have repeatedly sparked debate in France and across the European Union. ... The partners have not yet specified the exact ownership structure, where the first sites will be located, or the timeline for ramping up to the 3 GW target. The announcement—dated June 26, 2026—sets out the alliance without laying out operational details. ... For French companies that want to integrate AI without relying exclusively on AWS, Azure, or Google Cloud, the partners argue that a credible national option could shift commercial leverage. That is the market Campus AI aims to capture, with the next milestones being the actual rollout, the step-by-step ramp toward 3 GW, and the first regional sites coming online."
MISTRAL AI SAS,,,,Exa,Mistral AI selects Montréal for its Canadian hub | Montréal International,www.montrealinternational.com,2026-06-25T00:00:00.000Z,2026-06-25 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.montrealinternational.com/en/news/mistral-ai-selects-montreal-for-its-canadian-hub/,"Mistral AI selects Montréal for its Canadian hub | Montréal International ... ral AI selects Montréal for its Canadian hub ... 25, 20 ... It’s official: Mistral AI will be opening an office in Montréal, marking its first establishment in Canada. The French leader in artificial intelligence made the announcement during an event bringing together stakeholders from Montréal’s business and political communities. ... In an interview with La Presse, Audrey Herblin-Stoop, Senior Vice-President, Global Affairs, said this expansion seeks to accelerate the company’s growth in Canada, tap into the local qualified talent pool and develop key partnerships with organisations, including Mila. ... Mistral AI has also entered into a collaboration agreement with the Government of Québec. According to the official release(French), the agreement aims to “inform the government’s strategic thinking on digital sovereignty, technological autonomy and the responsible integration of artificial intelligence within the public administration.” ... Founded in Paris in 2023, Mistral AI has offices in the United Kingdom and the United States. The company’s mission is to democratize access to artificial intelligence by developing generative AI solutions that are high-performing, customizable and secure, capable of meeting the needs of both private enterprises and public organizations. ... The arrival of this leader in artificial intelligence reinforces Montréal’s attractiveness as a globally recognized AI hub. Since the early days of AI, the city has played a pioneering role, attracting global players such as Google, Meta and Microsoft, which have established major research centres here. As commercialization becomes a key driver of growth, Montréal stands out for the strength of its talent pool and its high-level expertise. This dynamic ecosystem continues to attract investors eager to develop and deploy high-potential AI solutions in both local and international markets. Learn more about Montréal’s strengths in artificial intelligence."
MISTRAL AI SAS,,,,Exa,Anthropic's AI Shutdown May Be Mistral's Biggest Opportunity yet - Business Insider,www.businessinsider.com (Thibault Spirlet),2026-06-15T00:00:00.000Z,2026-06-15 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.businessinsider.com/anthropic-model-access-mistral-opportunity-ai-sovereignty-2026-6,"Anthropic's AI Shutdown May Be Mistral's Biggest Opportunity yet - Business Insider ... The White House's move to restrict access to Anthropic's newest AI models could hand one of Europe's leading AI startups exactly the opening it has been preparing for. ... On Friday, US officials imposed export controls on Anthropic's cybersecurity-focused models, Mythos 5 and Fable 5. They cited national security concerns that safeguards meant to prevent misuse of Fable 5 could be bypassed. ... The restrictions block any foreign national from accessing the two models. Anthropic responded by suspending access to the systems altogether, creating uncertainty around who ultimately controls access to frontier AI. ... The episode has the hallmarks of a scenario Mistral's CEO, Arthur Mensch, has been warning about for more than a year — and has since made part of his pitch for why people should choose it over models from US firms like OpenAI and Anthropic. ... Speaking at London Tech Week in June 2025,"
MISTRAL AI SAS,,,,Exa,Mistral reportedly seeking $3.5B funding round amid physics AI push - SiliconANGLE,siliconangle.com (Maria Deutscher),2026-06-13T00:00:00.000Z,2026-06-13 00:00:00+00:00,,https://siliconangle.com/2026/06/12/mistral-reportedly-seeking-3-5b-funding-round-amid-physics-ai-push/,"Mistral reportedly seeking $3.5B funding round amid physics AI push - SiliconANGLE ... ### Mistral reportedly seeking $3.5B funding round amid physics AI push ... French foundation model developer Mistral AI SAS is reportedly in talks to raise €3 billion, or about $3.5 billion, from investors. ... Bloomberg today cited sources as saying that the round could value the company at €20 billion. That’s nearly double the valuation it received after its most recent raise in September. The €1.7 billion investment was led by ASML Holdings NV, the world’s top maker of advanced lithography machines. ... Today’s report didn’t specify the investors who could participate in Mistral’s new raise. ASML might chip in given that late-stage funding rounds often include contributions from existing backers. Mistral’s investor roster also includes Nvidia Corp., Salesforce Ventures and several prominent venture capital firms. ... The report that the company is raising new capital comes shortly after it announced plans to develop artificial intelligence products for industrial engineers. The product lineup will include, among other things, custom AI models. Mistral may be planning to use the new funding to support the development effort. ... Mistral refers to the software suite that it’s developing as physics AI. According to the company, the technology will enable engineers to generate multiple variations of a product design quickly. Mistral is also working on tools that will make it possible to test design variations in simulations. ... In May, the company bought a startup called Emmi that developed tools for building physics AI models. Such algorithms feature a different architecture than a standard large language model. They’re optimized to solve partial differential equations, functions that lend themselves well to describing complex physical phenomena. ... It’s possible the company will open-source some of its planned physics AI models. Mistral has released more than a half-dozen open-weight algorithms to date. The newest addition to the lineup, Mistral Medium 3.5, made its debut in April. It features 128 billion parameters and can outperform models more than three times its size across some coding benchmarks. ... Mistral generates revenue with a suite of paid cloud services powered by its open-source models. Mistral Vibe is an AI assistant that can summarize documents, generate code and troubleshoot factory equipment. It’s available alongside an AI agent development platform called Studio. Additionally, Mistral offers AI-optimized cloud infrastructure and tools for building custom foundation models. ... The company’s reported fundraising push comes as its two top rivals, OpenAI Group PBC and Anthropic PBC, prepare to go public. Mistral will also face competition from a new market player called Prometheus Inc. that raised $12 billion in funding this week. The company was launched by Amazon.com Inc. founder Jeff Bezos last year to automate many of the hardware engineering tasks that Mistral is prioritizing with its physics AI initiative."
MISTRAL AI SAS,,,,Exa,"Mistral courts U.S. business with cheaper, not the smartest, AI",www.nbcnews.com,2026-06-02T00:00:00.000Z,2026-06-02 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.nbcnews.com/tech/tech-news/mistral-selling-wall-street-us-ai-giants-chat-rcna346578,"Mistral, Europe’s biggest AI lab, sets its sights on the U.S. ... June 2, 2026, 5:30 AM EDT ... France’s leading AI company, Mistral, is named for the strong Mediterranean gusts that blow across southern France. Founded by a trio of machine learning engineers who worked at Facebook and Google, the company has surged in recent years to become the main European contender in the race to build generative AI systems."
MISTRAL AI SAS,,,,Exa,Mistral's BMW and Airbus deals reveal where… | AI in Europe,aiineurope.co,2026-06-02T00:00:00.000Z,2026-06-02 00:00:00+00:00,,https://aiineurope.co/sectors/mistral-bmw-airbus-industrial-ai-european-deployment-2026-06-02,"Mistral's new contracts with BMW and Airbus put the European model layer inside two of the bloc's flagship industrial supply chains, on terms American foundation model vendors have not yet matched. ... Mistral, the Paris based foundation model company, has signed multi year deployment contracts with BMW and Airbus, the European technology publication Sifted reported last week, putting a European model vendor inside two of the bloc's most strategically watched industrial integrators on terms that American foundation model competitors have not yet matched. ... The BMW contract covers German manufacturing line process optimisation, predictive maintenance on assembly robotics, and a German and French language warranty triage system deployed to dealer networks across the EU. The Airbus contract covers supply chain demand forecasting for the A320 and A321 family, parts inventory routing for the Hamburg Finkenwerder and Toulouse final assembly lines, and a controlled rollout of model based engineering assistance to design teams working inside the company's PLM environment. Both contracts run for an initial three years, with extension options that are documented in the relevant procurement filings. ... On Sifted's reporting, the two contracts together carry a combined first year value of between 90 million and 120 million euros, with run rate revenue rising as the deployment scopes expand. Neither company has confirmed the headline numbers. Both have confirmed that Mistral was selected after a competitive process that included at least one American foundation model vendor and one large American cloud provider that had bid the same workload on its own model layer. ... The first is deployment topology. Both customers required on premises model serving for production critical workloads, with optional bursting into an EU sovereign cloud for non sensitive batch jobs. Mistral has spent the past 18 months building out its on premises serving stack and its operator certification programme with EU systems integrators, work that is documented in the company's recent partnership with the European cloud provider OVHcloud and in its earlier Hesse and Lower Saxony reference deployments. American foundation model vendors, on the same procurement readouts, were unable to match the on premises path at the same price and latency points. ... The second is regulatory conformity. The EU AI Act's high risk obligations begin enforcing on a rolling schedule from August 2026. Both BMW and Airbus categorised the contracted workloads under the high risk regime, which triggers conformity assessment, post market monitoring and human oversight obligations on the supplier. Mistral's model documentation, its training data audit trail and its red team release notes were, on the procurement record, the only proposal that met the conformity standard without substantial additional engineering. Anthropic's and OpenAI's EU compliance posture has improved through 2026 but neither vendor has, as of June, certified an on premises European deployment at the same scope. ... The third is procurement comfort. BMW and Airbus both quoted political and reputational risk considerations in their internal procurement memos, with reference to the August conformity deadline, the bloc's data sovereignty debate, and the implications of the Trump administration's industrial policy realignment around American AI champions for any large European industrial customer that depends commercially on a single American model vendor. ... Read in isolation, the two contracts are commercially meaningful for Mistral but not transformative. Combined first year revenue of around 100 million euros, against a Mistral valuation that is now north of 12 billion euros, does not, by itself, validate the European model vendor thesis at the scale that American competitors operate. ... Aleph Alpha, the Heidelberg based foundation model company, signed an extended Hesse state government contract in March that covers German administrative document processing, citizen services and a public sector procurement assistant. Silo AI, the Finnish industrial AI specialist that was acquired by AMD in 2024 and operates as a partly independent European subsidiary, has won contracts in the past quarter with the Olkiluoto nuclear plant, the Swedish defence procurement agency and the Estonian e government stack. Mistral itself, beyond BMW and Airbus, is in late stage discussions with Siemens, Renault, Stellantis and Sanofi on workloads of broadly the same shape. ... The Sifted analysis published last week, titled The US and Europe: A tale of two AIs, frames the pattern explicitly. The American foundation model race is being decided on a frontier intelligence axis where scale and capital intensity favour Anthropic, OpenAI and Google DeepMind. The European race is being decided on an industrial deployment axis where regulatory conformity, on premises serving, sector specific tuning and integrator partnerships favour Mistral, Aleph Alpha and Silo AI. The two surfaces overlap, but they are not the same market. ... The near term test is whether Mistral can convert the BMW and Airbus references into a wider tier one industrial pipeline before American competitors close the on premises and conformity gaps. Mistral's own conversations with Siemens, Renault, Stellantis and Sanofi are the most direct read on that conversion. Each of those four customers is, on procurement readouts, structurally similar to BMW and Airbus, and a Mistral signature on any one of them would meaningfully expand the European model vendor footprint into the bloc's industrial base. ... The medium term test is whether American foundation model vendors close the on premises and conformity gap inside 12 to 18 months, which is broadly the timeline that Anthropic's recent EU programme and OpenAI's German and French data residency commitments imply. If they do, Mistral's structural advantage shrinks to the conformity audit and the sector specific tuning, both of which are real but smaller than the current full stack edge. ... The political test, finally, is whether the bloc's European Investment Bank backed growth funding catches up to the contracting pipeline. The Built in Europe campaign launched in London on Monday set out the LP capital case for precisely this kind of European model vendor build out. Mistral, on its current trajectory, is the most direct evidence that the case is winnable. Whether it is winnable at the scale the campaign organisers want is the question European limited partners will spend the rest of 2026 answering. ... Mistral's BMW and Airbus contracts are not a frontier intelligence story. They are, on the deployment surface that matters most to the European AI economy, a confirmation that the bet the bloc has been making for the past 18 months is paying. Industrial customers in regulated sectors will keep buying audited, on premises European model capacity from European vendors for as long as the EU AI Act conformity standard holds and as long as American competitors continue to lag on the on premises path. Both conditions hold in June 2026. The realistic European outcome is not that Mistral overtakes Anthropic on a benchmark. It is that Mistral, Aleph Alpha and Silo AI hold the European industrial deployment layer long enough for the bloc's growth funding stack to mature behind them. The next four quarters of Mistral's tier one industrial pipeline will tell us whether that outcome is on track or whether the closing American gap moves faster than the European fund of funds capital can be deployed."
MISTRAL AI SAS,,,,Exa,TCS teams up with Mistral for enterprise AI models,channellife.com.au (Mark Tarre),2026-05-29T00:00:00.000Z,2026-05-29 00:00:00+00:00,,https://channellife.com.au/story/tcs-teams-up-with-mistral-for-enterprise-ai-models,"TCS teams up with ... enterprise AI models ... # TCS teams up with Mistral for enterprise AI models Fri, 29th May 2026 MARK TARRE News Chief TCS has formed a strategic partnership with Mistral, becoming the first global systems integrator partner for Mistral Forge. ... Under the agreement, TCS will use Mistral Forge to build custom artificial intelligence models for enterprise customers using their proprietary knowledge and domain-specific data. The work will focus on helping organisations design, fine-tune and deploy AI systems with tighter control over data, operations and governance. ... The partnership will initially target banking, financial services and insurance, manufacturing, healthcare, and the public sector. These sectors face growing pressure to adopt AI tools that can operate within industry rules and internal control frameworks. ... A key part of the arrangement is a dedicated centre of excellence that TCS plans to establish for Mistral. The unit will support joint development, training, project delivery and the creation of sector-specific offerings, while also giving TCS early access to Mistral's beta models. ... France-based Mistral has emerged as one of Europe's best-known AI companies as governments and businesses seek alternatives to the largest US model developers. The partnership with TCS gives it access to a broader enterprise customer base across North America, the UK, Europe and Asia-Pacific through one of the world's biggest IT services groups. ... For TCS, the agreement comes as it expands what it calls its Infrastructure to Intelligence strategy, spanning infrastructure, models, data, applications, platforms, and physical and digital intelligence. The company has been increasing its investment in AI across service delivery and client projects as demand shifts from experimentation to implementation tied to business processes. ... Competition in this market has intensified as consulting and outsourcing groups race to secure alliances with model developers and cloud providers. The goal is to give clients a combination of model access, sector expertise and implementation support as concerns about data location, regulation and sovereign control shape procurement decisions. ... Many large customers, especially in regulated industries, are looking for models that can be adapted to their internal data and risk policies. That has created an opening for systems integrators that can combine technical deployment with industry-specific process knowledge and governance structures. ... ""TCS' global scale and contextual industry knowledge make them an ideal partner for Mistral. Together, we are enabling enterprises worldwide to move from experimentation to AI deployment with systems that are open, production-ready and aligned with their strategic and operational requirements,"" said Arthur Mensch, Chief Executive Officer, Mistral. ... ""The partnership with Mistral reinforces TCS' commitment to scaling enterprise AI with trust, control, and measurable business outcomes at the core. This partnership expands TCS' AI ecosystem, uniquely positioning TCS to create a differentiated solution proposition for our clients. Together with Mistral, we will solve for specific industry challenges, regulatory requirements, and sovereign needs for our enterprise customers,"" said K Krithivasan, Chief Executive Officer, TCS."
MISTRAL AI SAS,,,,Exa,"Mistral AI launches Vibe, expands into industrial AI and announces data center push to challenge OpenAI | VentureBeat",venturebeat.com (Michael Nuñez),2026-05-28T00:00:00.000Z,2026-05-28 00:00:00+00:00,,https://venturebeat.com/technology/mistral-ai-launches-vibe-expands-into-industrial-ai-and-announces-data-center-push-to-challenge-openai,"Mistral AI launches Vibe, expands into industrial AI and announces data center push to challenge OpenAI | VentureBeat ... Mistral AI used its inaugural conference on Wednesday to announce a sweeping expansion into industrial manufacturing, a new inference data center south of Paris, and a rebranding of its consumer-facing assistant — moves that collectively signal the three-year-old French startup's ambition to become the enterprise AI provider of record for companies that refuse to hand their most sensitive data to American hyperscalers. ... At the AI NOW Summit, held at a venue in central Paris, co-founder and CEO Arthur Mensch took the stage alongside CTO Timothée Lacroix and Chief Scientist Guillaume Lample to lay out a strategy that stretches from bare-metal GPU clusters to physics simulations for aircraft wings. The company disclosed that it now employs 1,000 people and is targeting €1 billion ($1.17B USD) in revenue for 2026 — a figure that, if achieved, would be an extraordinary growth trajectory for a company that began with 15 employees collaborating with its first customer, BNP Paribas, in 2023. ... The announcements come at a pivotal moment for Mistral and for the broader European AI ecosystem. The company has raised at least $3.9 billion across nine funding rounds, according to Clay's funding tracker, including a massive €1.7 billion Series C led by Dutch semiconductor equipment maker ASML in September 2025 at an €11.7 billion valuation, and an $830 million debt financing round in March 2026 from a consortium of seven banks to fund data center construction. Mistral now finds itself in a peculiar competitive position: too large to be dismissed as a research lab, but still dwarfed by the resources of OpenAI, Google DeepMind, and Anthropic. ... The centerpiece announcement was Mistral for Industrial Engineering, a fully integrated AI stack that combines Mistral's large language models with physics simulation capabilities acquired through its purchase of Emmi AI, completed earlier in May 2026. The platform targets the aerospace, automotive, and semiconductor industries with tools for accelerating product design, validating simulations, and optimizing production. ... The launch came with headline partnerships. Mistral announced it is working with Airbus across its commercial aircraft, helicopter, defense, and space divisions, implementing AI from initial design through to on-board capabilities. For BMW Group, Mistral is serving as a central partner for what the automaker calls its ""Large Industry Model"" initiative, focused on multimodal reasoning models for crash simulation and other complex engineering tasks. ASML, already Mistral's largest shareholder, is also an early adopter. ... Mistral's full-stack ambitions extend all the way down to the physical layer. Launched in June 2025, Mistral Compute is a €4 billion ($4.66B USD) investment in data centers in France and Sweden, with a stated roadmap of 200 MW of capacity by 2027 and 1 GW by 2030. ... On Wednesday, Mistral announced a new 10 MW facility at Les Ulis in the Essonne department, also south of Paris, dedicated to inference operations and scheduled to open in Q3 2026. Lacroix also referenced a site in Borlänge, Sweden, planned for development through 2027, which will host NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin GPUs. ""One of the benefits for us of owning the hardware layer is also that it lets us be at the very bleeding edge of what infrastructure provides,"" he told the audience. ... The infrastructure push is funded in part by the$830 million debt financing round announced in March 2026, which Clay's funding tracker attributes to a consortium of seven banks: Bpifrance, BNP Paribas, Crédit Agricole CIB, HSBC, La Banque Postale, MUFG, and Natixis CIB. And this infrastructure ownership is not merely a hedge against GPU scarcity — it is central to Mistral's pitch to security-conscious enterprise and government customers. The company's February 2026 acquisition of serverless platform Koyeb has been integrated into Mistral Studio to support both hosted and on-premises deployments, giving customers a choice between running inference on Mistral's hardware or their own. ... In a consumer-facing rebrand with significant enterprise implications, Mistral announced that Le Chat— its conversational AI assistant launched in February 2024 — is being renamed Vibe and reimagined as a unified agent platform for enterprise productivity and software development. ... Pricing starts at free for basic use,$14.99 per month for Pro,$24.99 per user per month for Teams, and custom pricing for Enterprise deployments. Alongside Vibe, Mistral also launched Search Toolkit, an open-source framework for building production search pipelines already in use by shipping giant CMA CGM, which uses it alongside Voxtral to process audio from multiple data sources and return alerts within 15 seconds. ... The company is also working on Mistral Large 4, which Lample said would arrive ""in a couple of months at most, during the summer,"" with expanded capabilities in industrial applications such as fluid dynamics, computational chemistry, computer-aided design, and cybersecurity. On the smaller end of the spectrum, Lample highlighted Mistral OCR, a 1-billion-parameter OCR model that can process thousands of pages per minute on a single GPU, and the Voxtral speech model family, which has expanded from automatic speech recognition to include text-to-speech with voice cloning. A ""duplex"" model for real-time conversational speech is planned for release within months. ... Mistral's positioning stands in sharp contrast to the strategies of its most prominent American rivals. While OpenAI and Anthropic have each attracted hundreds of millions of consumer users and derive significant revenue from subscription products, Mistral has leaned almost entirely into enterprise and government deployments. As TechCrunch reported in March when Mistral announced its Forge customization platform at Nvidia GTC, CEO Mensch has described the company as being "" on track to surpass $1 billion in annual recurring revenue"" — a figure driven largely by corporate clients. ... The Forge platform, which lets enterprises train custom models on their own data rather than simply fine-tuning or applying retrieval-augmented generation to existing models, represents the foundation on which the company's industry-specific solutions are built. As Mistral's head of product, Elisa Salamanca, told TechCrunch, Forge ""lets enterprises and governments customize AI models for their specific needs."" Early partners include Ericsson, the European Space Agency, Italian consulting company Reply, and Singapore's DSO and HTX, alongside ASML. ... Mistral has also built an expanding network of systems integration partnerships to drive enterprise adoption. In February 2026, Accenture and Mistral announced a multi-year strategic collaboration, with Accenture itself becoming a Mistral customer. Mauro Macchi, Accenture's CEO for Europe, Middle East, and Africa, said at the time that the partnership brings together ""sovereign models and the capability to scale technology across industries, geographies and business functions."" ... The industrial vertical is also being extended to government clients. Mistral disclosed that it is working with France, Luxembourg, Singapore, Morocco, Greece, and Slovakia to build citizen-facing AI services — from deploying agents that help job-seekers through France Travail to building models that understand Moroccan Darija and Amazigh languages. ""We think that AI needs to be specialized and understand structural nuances,"" Mensch told the audience. ""It needs to speak languages as good as it speaks English."" ... coheres into a durable business or stretches Mistral too thin is the €11.7 billion ($13.6B USD) question. The 1,000 people now working there are betting that in enterprise AI, owning the full stack is not a liability — it is the"
MISTRAL AI SAS,,,,Exa,Mistral Signs Airbus and BMW as it Brings AI to Manufacturing - Bloomberg,www.bloomberg.com (Benoit Berthelot),2026-05-28T00:00:00.000Z,2026-05-28 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-05-28/mistral-signs-airbus-and-bmw-as-it-brings-ai-to-manufacturing,"Mistral Signs Airbus, BMW as It Brings AI to Manufacturing - Bloomberg ... French artificial intelligence startup Mistral AI is expanding into advanced manufacturing, striking deals with new customers Airbus SE and BMW AG as it looks to so-called physical AI to fuel growth. ... Paris-based Mistral offers AI models and computing infrastructure to corporate clients, and now aims to apply AI to industrial engineering processes including design, simulation and quality control, the company announced Thursday. Financial terms with the new customers were not disclosed."
MISTRAL AI SAS,,,,Exa,"French AI firm Mistral announces deals with BMW, Airbus",www.france24.com,2026-05-28T00:00:00.000Z,2026-05-28 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.france24.com/en/live-news/20260528-french-ai-firm-mistral-announces-deals-with-bmw-airbus,"French AI firm Mistral announces deals with BMW, Airbus ... # French AI firm Mistral announces deals with BMW, Airbus ... Paris (France) (AFP) – French AI firm Mistral announced Thursday partnerships with BMW and the aerospace group Airbus as it aims to boost growth by fostering links with defence and industry giants. ... The Paris-based company, looking to punch above its weight in a sector dominated by US and Chinese firms, said it would be involved with car-crash tests and plane design. ... Mistral is already closely tied with ASML, the Dutch firm producing chipmaking equipment indispensable for the high-end semiconductors, which invested in the French company last year. ... The company this month bought an Austrian start-up, Emmi AI, that specialises in digital simulations for industry, after earlier snapping up the French cloud computing start-up Koyeb. ... Mensch called defence a ""growing business"" for his firm and revealed he had a ""dedicated team"" working on it. ... The company is already working with the French and Singaporean militaries, Forbes magazine has reported. ... But Europe's defence industry remains dominated by American tech giants. ... Mistral has grown to around 1,000 employees since its 2023 founding and is now building its own computing infrastructure. ... But the firm's four-billion-euro ($4.6 billion) plans for European data centres are dwarfed by the hundreds of billions being deployed by American ""hyperscalers"" like Google, Amazon and Microsoft. ... Where American firms measure their AI infrastructure in hundreds of megawatts or gigawatts of power, Mistral has a 44-megawatt data centre outside Paris and is building another in Sweden. ... The company also announced Thursday a deal for 10 megawatts of computing power with the US data centre operator Digital Realty. ... His group signed a five-year partnership with Airbus to apply AI to defence and space activities and helicopter manufacturing, though the value of the contract has not been revealed. ... Mensch said Mistral would be involved in improving flight safety with the deployment of AI in the cockpit, and helping with the design and construction of new aircraft through digital simulation. ... For BMW, Mistral will build specific models that ""understand the physics"" of the vehicles and are intended to optimise crash-test procedures. ... And shipping giant CMA CGM, the world's third largest, will on June 1 launch a new Mistral-powered AI platform dubbed ""Maia"" to support its 80,000 staff worldwide. ... The tool should allow the company to predict ship arrival times, optimise routes to avoid adverse weather, reduce energy consumption and get the most out of CMA CGM's shipping and storage capacity. ... Mensch has repeatedly urged European policymakers to create ""buy European"" rules prioritising local suppliers for public digital services contracts in sectors like cloud computing and AI. ... French President Emmanuel Macron, himself a vocal booster of Mistral, has made similar arguments in Brussels. ... American tech giants expect to spend $750 billion this year on capital investments, compared with Mistral's one billion euros. ... The disparity has fed repeated episodes of rumours that Mistral could be taken over by a foreign player. ... That could happen only if the French government does not back Mistral ""at every stage of its development"", French digital affairs minister Anne Le Henanff told AFP. ... Mensch told French parliamentarians this month that the company's best shot at independence was an eventual stock market flotation."
MISTRAL AI SAS,,,,Exa,Airbus and BMW strike deals with France’s Mistral to bring AI to defence and safety systems | Euronews,www.euronews.com,2026-05-28T00:00:00.000Z,2026-05-28 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.euronews.com/business/2026/05/28/airbus-and-bmw-strike-deals-with-frances-mistral-to-bring-ai-to-defence-and-safety-systems,"Airbus and BMW strike deals with France’s Mistral to bring AI to defence and safety systems | Euronews ... ## As European companies look for alternatives to US tech giants in the race for artificial intelligence, Airbus and BMW have both partnered with French startup Mistral AI to develop systems ranging from flight safety and defence technology to car crash simulations. ... Airbus has signed a partnership with French AI start-up Mistral AI, to expand the use of AI across its aviation, defence and space businesses. ... According to a joint press statement released on Thursday, the European aerospace giant aims to embed artificial intelligence across its commercial aircraft, helicopter, defence and space operations. ... Under the agreement, Airbus will gain access to Mistral AI’s full range of products and researchers, allowing the company to develop custom AI tools for complex aerospace projects. ... Airbus said the partnership would focus on “trusted” and “secure” AI, particularly for sensitive defence and aerospace applications. ... ""Together, we will deploy Mistral’s fully integrated AI stack to accelerate innovation, contribute to improve flight safety, and deliver greater value for customers”, said Timothée Lacroix, co-founder and Chief Technology Officer at Mistral AI. ... The partnership also gives Airbus access to Mistral's research teams and a degree of influence over the company's product roadmap. ... The two companies have identified several priority areas for collaboration. These include exploring AI systems that could be used onboard aircraft and spacecraft and automating technical document production, accelerating engineering design cycles through AI-driven simulations and developing so-called edge AI capabilities — namely, models running directly on hardware — for applications such as automatic object recognition to support flight safety. ... They are also studying defence applications such as cyber investigations and coding support in highly secure environments. ... The two companies said they would work together in several key areas, including developing AI systems that could be used onboard aircraft and spacecraft. ... They also plan to use AI-powered simulations to speed up engineering and aircraft design processes. ... The partnership will also explore defence-related uses of AI, including cyber investigations and coding support in highly secure environments. ... Timothée Lacroix, co-founder and chief technology officer at Mistral AI, said the two companies would work together to ""accelerate innovation, contribute to improve flight safety, and deliver greater value for customers."" ... The deal comes as European companies and institutions grow increasingly uneasy about their dependence on US-based AI providers, amid concerns over data sovereignty, security exposure and the legal reach of American legislation over data stored on US-owned infrastructure. ... Mistral is also exploring designing its own chips and may eventually develop them, CEO Arthur Mensch told CNBC, as the company moves to ""control more of its infrastructure"" while competing with US heavyweights like OpenAI and Anthropic. ... Mistral AI, founded in Paris in 2023, has positioned itself as a European alternative to US-based AI providers, with a focus on open-weight models and data sovereignty, factors likely to weigh heavily in defence and aerospace procurement decisions. ... ### BMW also signs Mistral AI partnership ... BMW announced a separate partnership with Mistral AI on the same day, focused on using artificial intelligence to improve crash simulations and vehicle development. ... The German carmaker said the collaboration would help speed up complex engineering work and improve the accuracy of safety testing by training AI systems on BMW’s large archive of crash simulation data. ... BMW runs thousands of virtual crash tests every week and has built up more than one petabyte of historical simulation data, which the company says can be used to develop industry-specific AI models for automotive engineering. ... The deal is part of a broader push by European manufacturers to use AI in industrial design, production and research."
MISTRAL AI SAS,,,,Exa,TCS ties up with French firm Mistral to build custom AI models for enterprises - The Economic Times,economictimes.indiatimes.com (ET),2026-05-28T00:00:00.000Z,2026-05-28 00:00:00+00:00,,https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/tech/technology/tcs-ties-up-with-french-firm-mistral-to-build-custom-ai-models-for-enterprises/articleshow/131369447.cms,"TCS ties up with French firm Mistral to build custom AI models for enterprises - The Economic Times ... TCS ties up with French firm Mistral to build custom AI models for enterprises ... TCS is the first global systems integrator (GSI) partner for Mistral Forge — the company’s enterprise platform for building frontier AI systems, the company said in a statement ... Tata Consultancy Services(TCS) on Thursday said it has partnered with French AI startup Mistral for co-building custom AI models. TCS is the first global systems integrator (GSI) partner for Mistral Forge — the company’s enterprise platform for building frontier AI systems, the company said in a statement.As part of the collaboration, TCS will help enterprises and governments develop, fine-tune, and deploy custom AI models built over proprietary enterprise data. The partnership will initially focus on sectors such as banking, manufacturing, healthcare, and the public sector, where enterprises are increasingly seeking greater control, governance, and regulatory compliance around AI deployments, ... TCS said.TCS will also establish a Centre of Excellence for Mistral to drive joint AI innovation and provide clients with early access to Mistral’s beta AI models. Arthur Mensch, co-founder and CEO of Mistral, said TCS’ enterprise reach makes it an important partner for scaling enterprise AI adoption globally. ... “Together, we are enabling enterprises worldwide to move from experimentation to AI deployment with systems that are open, production-ready, and aligned with their strategic and operational requirements,” Mensch said.TCS chief executive and managing director K Krithivasan said the partnership strengthens the company’s strategy of building trusted and controllable enterprise AI systems.“The partnership with Mistral reinforces TCS’ commitment to scaling enterprise AI with trust, control, and measurable business outcomes at the core,” Krithivasan said. “Together with Mistral, we will solve for specific industry challenges regulatory requirements, and sovereign needs for our enterprise customers.”TCS added that the alliance is part of its broader “Infrastructure to Intelligence” AI strategy, under which the company is investing across AI infra, models, data platforms, and enterprise intelligence systems as it seeks to become the world’s largest AI-led technology services company.TCS also partners with other frontier labs to deploy OpenAI’s Codex and Google’s Gemini Enterprise."
MISTRAL AI SAS,,,,Exa,"French AI firm Mistral announces deals with BMW, Airbus","finance.yahoo.com (Daxia ROJAS Thu, May 28, 2026 at 11:33 AM GMT+2 3 min read)",2026-05-28T00:00:00.000Z,2026-05-28 00:00:00+00:00,,https://finance.yahoo.com/sectors/technology/articles/french-ai-firm-mistral-announces-093333506.html,Jouw privacykeuzes
MISTRAL AI SAS,,,,Exa,European answer to Mythos: Mistral and BNP cooperate on bank security | heise online,www.heise.de (Tomislav Bezmalinović),2026-05-26T00:00:00.000Z,2026-05-26 00:00:00+00:00,,https://www.heise.de/en/news/European-answer-to-Mythos-Mistral-and-BNP-cooperate-on-bank-security-11307210.html,"European answer to Mythos: Mistral and BNP cooperate on bank security | heise online # European answer to Mythos: Mistral and BNP cooperate on bank security French AI startup Mistral has reportedly developed its own AI model for cybersecurity and is testing it with the major bank BNP Paribas. (Image: Andy.LIU / Shutterstock.com) May 26, 2026 at 8:46 pm CEST By - Tomislav Bezmalinović ... French banking group BNP Paribas and Mistral AI have extended their AI partnership for three years and expanded it to new areas. At a joint press conference in Paris, the partners also discussed AI-powered threats to banks. ... While the partners did not make any concrete announcements about their cybersecurity collaboration, Bloomberg reports that the Paris-based AI startup has developed its own AI model for cybersecurity and is in talks with European banks about its deployment. One of these partners is said to be BNP Paribas. ... Whether and in what form European banks have now also received direct access to Mythos is not publicly confirmed. However, the recent statements by BNP Paribas and Mistral suggest that European institutions are continuing to search for alternatives. Anthropic competitor OpenAI has introduced its own cybersecurity model, which some European companies also have access to. With the Paris-based AI startup Mistral, a European answer to the problem is now apparently emerging. ... The partnership between Mistral and BNP Paribas dates back to 2023. With the extension, the collaboration will go beyond mere access to large language models and also encompass software, solutions, and joint research and development projects, writes BNP Paribas."
MISTRAL AI SAS,,,,Exa,BNP Paribas works with Mistral on a European answer to Anthropic’s Mythos,thenextweb.com (Ana-Maria Stanciuc),2026-05-26T00:00:00.000Z,2026-05-26 00:00:00+00:00,,https://thenextweb.com/news/bnp-paribas-mistral-mythos-rival-sovereign-ai,"BNP Paribas works with Mistral on a European answer to Anthropic’s Mythos ... BNP Paribas is working with Mistral AI to prepare for a category of AI cybersecurity tools its US peers already use and its European supervisors cannot reliably get hold of, according to a Bloomberg report on Tuesday. ... The Paris-based bank, the eurozone’s largest by assets, is one of several European institutions helping Mistral develop a cyber-focused model intended as a counterpart to Anthropic’s Mythos. ... The European Central Bank has spent the past several weeks warning eurozone supervisors that Mythos-class tools change the threat picture for banks regardless of who has access. If attackers obtain a comparable model, the ECB’s Frank Elderson told banks earlier this month, defenders without one will be structurally behind. ... That standoff is the gap Mistral is trying to fill. The French firm, already Europe’s best-funded foundation-model company, has been pitching a sovereign European alternative since at least mid-May. BNP Paribas’s involvement is the most concrete sign yet that the European banking sector is willing to put balance-sheet weight behind the project rather than wait for Brussels-Anthropic talks to unblock. ... The two firms already have a three-year commercial agreement across Mistral’s broader model lineup, signed in February. ... The pitch to BNP Paribas and other prospective European bank customers is in part that a sovereign model with even somewhat lower capability is preferable to no model at all on the defensive side. It is also a hedge against the possibility that Brussels-Anthropic talks collapse permanently. ... The same logic has driven recent European pushes on cloud sovereignty, payments rails and, most loudly, semiconductor manufacturing. Banking cyber-AI is the newest entry in that list, and the BNP Paribas-Mistral pairing is its highest-profile concrete project. ... Neither BNP Paribas nor Mist ... commented on the specific Bloomberg report. Mythos has not been demonstrated in the wild on ... European bank to date."
MISTRAL AI SAS,,,,Exa,Mistral AI acquires Emmi AI to strengthen industrial AI in Europe - ITdaily,itdaily.com (Katrien Duchène),2026-05-20T00:00:00.000Z,2026-05-20 00:00:00+00:00,,https://itdaily.com/news/business/mistral-ai-acquires-emmi-ai-to-strengthen-industrial-ai-in-europe/,"Mistral AI acquires Emmi AI to strengthen industrial AI in Europe - ITdaily ... Mistral AI announces the acquisition of Emmi AI, a company specializing in AI models for industrial sectors. The partnership aims to create an AI stack for engineering, with applications in sectors such as energy, automotive, semiconductors, and aerospace. ... With the acquisition of Emmi AI, Mistral AI is strengthening its position in the European market for industrial AI. Austria-based Emmi AI specializes in Physics AI models that accelerate industrial simulation and engineering processes. The integration of Emmi’s technologies and researchers into Mistral AI paves the way for further innovation and investment in Europe. Neither Mistral AI nor Emmi AI has disclosed further (financial) details. Earlier this year, Mistral AI invested 1.2 billion euros in a data center in Sweden. ... The merger focuses on building a fully integrated AI platform for industrial enterprises. Sectors with complex challenges, such as aerospace and semiconductors, are central to this strategy. ... The focus on the latter, semiconductors, is likely no coincidence. Last year, chip machine manufacturer ASML invested 1.3 billion euros in Mistral AI, making ASML the largest shareholder in the French company. The fact that Mistral AI is now acquiring an AI company focused on physical processes may well be related to the chip machine manufacturer. The company has not yet provided further details on this. ... According to Mistral AI, this acquisition represents a significant step in the European strategy for industrial AI. By combining its AI platform with Emmi AI’s engineering expertise, Mistral AI aims to support manufacturers in high-stakes sectors in solving complex technical problems. The technology will be used to transform core research and development processes and accelerate innovation. ... ### Mistral AI expands AI infrastructure with acquisition of Koyeb ... Mistral AI expands AI infrastructure with acquisition of Koyeb ... The Emmi AI team, consisting of more than thirty researchers and engineers, will join Mistral AI’s existing teams starting in May 2026. They bring knowledge and experience in engineering AI and will play a key role in the further development of industrial applications. ... The acquisition also represents an investment in regional talent and research. Mistral AI is opening an office in Linz, alongside existing locations in Paris, London, and Amsterdam, among others. This is intended to further strengthen local expertise and promote the recruitment of specialists in Austria, Germany, and Lithuania. Emmi AI previously worked on solutions for real-time power grid stabilization, injection molding simulation, and automotive safety testing. By integrating this knowledge with Mistral AI’s infrastructure, companies across various industries can rely on digital twins and simulations, which can accelerate the development of new vehicles and semiconductors."
MISTRAL AI SAS,,,,Exa,Mistral buys Vienna’s Emmi AI to put physics into its industrial pitch,thenextweb.com (Ana-Maria Stanciuc),2026-05-19T00:00:00.000Z,2026-05-19 00:00:00+00:00,,https://thenextweb.com/news/mistral-emmi-ai-physics-vienna-industrial,"Mistral buys Vienna’s Emmi AI to put physics into its industrial pitch ... The Paris-based open-weights lab is acquiring an Austrian startup whose models handle airflow, heat transfer, and material stress, targeting aerospace, automotive, and semiconductor customers. Terms were not disclosed. ... Mistral AI, Europe’s most prominent open-source AI lab, has acquired Vienna-based Emmi AI for an undisclosed sum, Reuters reported on Tuesday. ... Emmi specialises in models that simulate physical phenomena, including airflow, heat transfer, and material stress, and is the company that ran what the local press described as Austria’s largest 2025 funding round, at €15m. ... The deal is Mistral’s second announced acquisition of 2026, after the company purchased cloud-infrastructure firm Koyeb in February. ... Mistral’s statement positions Emmi’s acquisition as ‘strengthening Mistral’s position as a partner for manufacturers in sectors such as aerospace, automotive, and semiconductors’, the heavy-industrial customer segments that Mensch has framed as ‘overlooked by the industry’. ... Mistral’s M&A pattern this year tracks a clear strategic logic. The Koyeb acquisition in February brought cloud-deployment infrastructure inside the Mistral perimeter. ... The Emmi acquisition brings physics-domain modelling. Both are vertically-defensible capability areas that Mistral can plausibly own at the European scale while leaving the broader frontier-foundation-model race to OpenAI, Anthropic, and Google. ... The strategic positioning bet is that European industrial customers are willing to pay for narrow, deployable, regulator-friendly AI products rather than for incremental general-capability frontier model access. ... Emmi’s €15m round in 2025 is small in foundation-model terms but substantial for an Austrian deep-tech company, and the implied per-employee value Mistral has paid will be visible once the deal terms are disclosed. European AI infrastructure as a whole has been accreting capability in this direction for 18 months. ... What Mistral did not disclose is the transaction value, the integration timeline, or whether Emmi’s founding team will continue to operate from Vienna inside the combined company. ... The strategic read for European AI policy observers is the part worth flagging. Mistral has been the company most-cited inside the EU’s ‘sovereign AI’ framing, both by its supporters in the French government and by its critics elsewhere in the bloc. ... Acquiring a physics-modelling company headquartered in another EU member state is the kind of intra-European capability consolidation the AI-sovereignty advocates have been pushing for. ... The deal does not, on its own, validate the wider sovereign-AI thesis, but it does extend Mistral’s product footprint into a category where European industrial customers have historically been comfortable paying for domestic technology providers."
